const PTNK_EXAMS = [{"id":"ptnk-2026-kc","year":2026,"track":"kc","title":"PTNK 2026 · Tiếng Anh Không chuyên","subtitle":"Đề thi tuyển sinh lớp 10 · Trường Phổ thông Năng khiếu · 2026-2027","durationMin":60,"keySource":"solved","source":"2026 - TS10 - Tiếng Anh KC (Đề thi).pdf","passages":["If you think of the jobs robots could never do, you would probably put doctors and teachers at the top of the list. It's easy to imagine robot cleaners and factory workers, but some jobs need human connection and creativity. Are we underestimating what robots can do? In some cases, they already perform better than doctors at diagnosing illness. Also, some patients might feel more comfortable sharing personal information with a machine than a person. Could there be a place for robots in education after all?<br><br>British education expert Anthony Seldon thinks so. He even has a date for the robot takeover of the classroom: 2027. He predicts robots will do the main job of transferring information and teachers will be like assistants. Intelligent robots will read students' faces, movements and maybe even brain signals. Then <b>they</b> will adapt the information to each student. It's not a popular opinion and it may be a challenge for robots to have empathy and the ability to really connect with humans like another human can.<br><br>One thing is certain, though. A robot teacher is better than no teacher at all. In some parts of the world, there aren't enough teachers and 9–16 per cent of children under the age of 14 don't go to school. That problem could be partly solved by robots because they can teach anywhere and won't get stressed, or tired, or move somewhere for an easier, higher-paid job.<br><br>Those negative aspects of teaching are something everyone agrees on. Teachers all over the world are leaving because it is a difficult job and they feel overworked. <b>[I]</b> Perhaps the question is not 'Will robots replace teachers?' but 'How can robots help teachers?' Office workers can use software to do things like organise and answer emails, arrange meetings with clients, and update calendars. <b>[II]</b> Teachers waste a lot of time doing non-teaching work, including more than 11 hours a week marking homework. <b>[III]</b> If robots could cut the time teachers spend marking homework and writing reports, teachers would have more time and energy for the parts of the job humans do best. <b>[IV]</b>","Whether you're travelling to the islands or the mountains of Thailand, you're likely to spend at least one night in its capital city on the way. Bangkok might be noisy and (29)____ but it's also an exciting city with plenty of things to see and do. Why not make it a longer stay?<br><br><b>Where to stay</b><br>The Khao San Road was a famous traveller spot even before Leonardo di Caprio's character in the film <i>The Beach</i> stayed there, but it's noisy, not very pretty and not very Thai. For something more (30)____, Phra Kanong offers an alternative place to stay, with its fantastic street markets where everyday Bangkok people eat, work and live. It's not as convenient for the main tourist sites, but it has a Skytrain station so you can be at the Grand Palace in 20 minutes.<br><br><b>How to get around</b><br>Bangkok's traffic can be a nightmare. Sure, you can easily take a taxi – if you want to spend hours stuck in traffic jams – but there are two much better ways to get around the city. To explore the temples and historical sites, (31)____ an express boat river taxi or a longtail boat along the Chao Phraya river and the canals. For the modern part of the city, the Skytrain is a fast, cheap way to travel from the river to the shopping malls and nightlife of Sukhumvit, and the famous Chatuchak street market.<br><br><b>Where to eat</b><br>The simple answer is: everywhere! Thai street food is among the best in the world, and for around $5 you can eat a filling and delicious meal. Some food stands have little plastic seats where you can sit and eat and they cook the same dish over and over, like fried chicken on rice or Pad Thai noodles. Head for Chinatown – Yaowarat Street – and choose (32)____ looks most interesting from the many excellent Chinese and Thai restaurants and food stands.<br><br><b>What to do</b><br>After you've seen the main sites like the Giant Buddha at the temple of Wat Pho and the spectacular Grand Palace, and shopped at Chatuchak market, check out the snake farm and watch the live snake show. You can even touch a snake yourself if you want to!"],"questions":[{"n":1,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Phonetics","type":"mcq","q":"Choose the word whose underlined part pronounces differently from the other three.","options":["fa<u>c</u>ade","lo<u>c</u>ale","<u>ch</u>oir","me<u>ch</u>anic"],"correct":0,"explanation":"\"façade\" /fəˈsɑːd/ has the /s/ sound; locale /ləʊˈkɑːl/, choir /kwaɪə/ and mechanic /məˈkænɪk/ all have /k/."},{"n":2,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Phonetics","type":"mcq","q":"Choose the word whose underlined part pronounces differently from the other three.","options":["<u>ex</u>ecute","<u>ex</u>periment","<u>ex</u>trovert","<u>ex</u>cavate"],"correct":1,"explanation":"In \"experiment\" /ɪkˈsperɪmənt/ the unstressed prefix is /ɪks/; in execute, extrovert and excavate the stressed first syllable is /eks/."},{"n":3,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Stress","type":"mcq","q":"Choose the word whose primary stress position differs from the other three.","options":["conditioner","advantageous","apprenticeship","unpleasantness"],"correct":1,"explanation":"advan-TA-geous is stressed on the third syllable; con-DI-tioner, ap-PREN-ticeship and un-PLEA-santness on the second."},{"n":4,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Stress","type":"mcq","q":"Choose the word whose primary stress position differs from the other three.","options":["vacuum","vehicle","bother","preserve"],"correct":3,"explanation":"pre-SERVE is stressed on the second syllable; VA-cuum, VE-hicle and BO-ther on the first."},{"n":5,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Rumi: 'Sorry, I couldn't make it to your slumber party because my sister wouldn't give me a ride.'<br>Mira: '______! There was a power cut at my place and it was so hot.'<br>Rumi: 'Oh, really?'","options":["Better luck next time","How dare you","Consider yourself lucky","What a pity"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Mira tells Rumi she missed nothing good — there was a power cut and it was hot — so \"Consider yourself lucky\" (you were fortunate not to come) is the natural reply."},{"n":6,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Since this test does not count toward the final score, nobody is going to revise, ______?","options":["are they","do they","is it","doesn't it"],"correct":0,"explanation":"\"nobody\" is negative and takes the pronoun \"they\" in a question tag, and the verb is the auxiliary of \"is going to\": nobody is going to revise, are they?"},{"n":7,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Since his father passed away in an accident, Tiến is raised by his mother in a(n) ______ family.","options":["nuclear","single parent","extended","adoptive"],"correct":1,"explanation":"A child brought up by only one parent lives in a single-parent family."},{"n":8,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"My teacher is so strict! If you ______ move during his exam, you will receive a penalty.","options":["so much as","as much as","as far as","so far as"],"correct":0,"explanation":"\"so much as\" (= even) emphasises that the smallest action counts: if you so much as move, you will be penalised."},{"n":9,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Thương has a 9-step skincare regimen that she considers part of her nightly ______ before bed.","options":["tradition","ritual","custom","ceremony"],"correct":1,"explanation":"A personal routine done the same way every night is a \"nightly ritual\"; tradition, custom and ceremony describe shared or formal practices."},{"n":10,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"According to my timing, Kim ______ in 2 minutes, so everyone get in position now for the surprise!","options":["must have arrived","should arrive","could be arriving","might arrive"],"correct":1,"explanation":"\"should + verb\" expresses a confident expectation based on evidence (\"according to my timing\"): Kim should arrive in 2 minutes."},{"n":11,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Before you join our team, we need to know if you are committed ______ the project your 100% effort.","options":["in giving","to giving","to give","having given"],"correct":1,"explanation":"\"be committed to + V-ing\": the \"to\" is a preposition, so it is followed by a gerund."},{"n":12,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Laughing at Mario, Bowser said \"You're too slow. When you get to the castle, my minions ______ the Princess away!\"","options":["will have taken","will be taking","will take","would have taken"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The future perfect describes an action completed before a future time point (\"when you get to the castle\"): they will have taken her away by then."},{"n":13,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Either Jim or his children ______ the parcel at the post office today.","options":["has to pick up","has to come across","have to come across","have to pick up"],"correct":3,"explanation":"With \"either … or\" the verb agrees with the nearer subject (\"his children\" → have), and collecting a parcel is \"pick up\", not \"come across\" (find by chance)."},{"n":14,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Once you finish the last test item, it is good practice to ______ the answers at least once before submitting.","options":["brush up on","look back on","go over","touch up"],"correct":2,"explanation":"\"go over\" = check or review carefully; \"brush up on\" is to improve rusty knowledge and \"look back on\" is to remember the past."},{"n":15,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"The presentation ______ I am preparing is very important.","options":["for which","to that","for that","to which"],"correct":0,"explanation":"\"prepare for something\" → \"the presentation for which I am preparing\"; a preposition cannot precede \"that\" in a relative clause."},{"n":16,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Error correction","type":"mcq","q":"(A) Being fined 10 million VND once (B) for drinking and driving, Khoa promises to (C) himself (D) to never visit a bar with his friends again.","options":["Being fined","for","himself","to never visit"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The fine happened once in the past, before the present promise, so the perfect participle is needed: \"Having been fined 10 million VND once …\"."},{"n":17,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Error correction","type":"mcq","q":"Timmy lives so (A) far away from school (B) that (C) his coming to class (D) lately has become an everyday occurrence.","options":["far away","that","his","lately"],"correct":3,"explanation":"\"lately\" means recently; the intended meaning is \"not on time\", which is the adverb \"late\": his coming to class late."},{"n":18,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Error correction","type":"mcq","q":"Many changes (A) were occurred in the city between 2010 (B) and 2020, particularly in the financial district, (C) which saw the construction of multiple (D) high-rises.","options":["were occurred","and","which","high-rises"],"correct":0,"explanation":"\"occur\" is intransitive and has no passive form: many changes occurred."},{"n":19,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Error correction","type":"mcq","q":"Hardly (A) had George spent time (B) with his family (C) than the boss requested him (D) to fly back to the New York office.","options":["had George spent","with","than","to fly"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The pattern is \"Hardly … when\" (\"No sooner … than\"): Hardly had George spent time with his family when the boss requested him to fly back."},{"n":20,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Error correction","type":"mcq","q":"(A) As a parent, you ought (B) to know that it does not teach good behaviour to always have somebody else (C) to clean up (D) after your son.","options":["As","to know","to clean up","after"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Causative \"have somebody do something\" takes the bare infinitive: have somebody else clean up after your son."},{"n":21,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Reading","type":"mcq","passageId":0,"q":"What is the main idea of the passage?","options":["Why experts believe human teachers will be completely replaced by 2027","The reasons why teachers all over the world are leaving their profession","Robots being already more efficient than human doctors and teachers","The potential benefits of integrating robots into the field of education"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The whole passage explores \"a place for robots in education\" — as teachers where there are none, and as helpers that free teachers from non-teaching work."},{"n":22,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Reading","type":"mcq","passageId":0,"q":"According to paragraph 1, why might some patients prefer robots over human doctors?","options":["They feel certain because robots don't get tired or stressed during the session.","They are diagnosed at much higher accuracy compared to human doctors.","They are more easily connected with robot doctors on an emotional level.","They feel more relaxed when they share personal details with a machine."],"correct":3,"explanation":"Paragraph 1: \"some patients might feel more comfortable sharing personal information with a machine than a person\"."},{"n":23,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Reading","type":"mcq","passageId":0,"q":"The word \"<b>they</b>\" in paragraph 2 refers to ______.","options":["intelligent robots","movements","students' faces","brain signals"],"correct":0,"explanation":"\"Intelligent robots will read students' faces … Then they will adapt the information to each student\" — the subject doing the reading and adapting is the robots."},{"n":24,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Reading","type":"mcq","passageId":0,"q":"What is mentioned in paragraph 2 as a likely limitation of robots?","options":["Their inability to adjust educational content to individual students","Their failure to promote genuine connection compared to humans","Their lack of technology to communicate information to students","Their incomprehension of empathy and students' facial movements"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 2: \"it may be a challenge for robots to have empathy and the ability to really connect with humans like another human can\"."},{"n":25,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Reading","type":"mcq","passageId":0,"q":"According to paragraph 3, robots could help solve the teacher shortage because ______.","options":["they can teach children who are not allowed to go to school.","they can teach 9–16 per cent more students than humans.","they are more popular than human teachers among children.","they do not suffer from stress or physical exhaustion."],"correct":3,"explanation":"Paragraph 3: robots \"can teach anywhere and won't get stressed, or tired, or move somewhere for an easier, higher-paid job\"."},{"n":26,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Reading","type":"mcq","passageId":0,"q":"Why does the author mention <b>office workers</b> in the final paragraph?","options":["To imply that robots will eventually replace office workers before they replace teachers","To argue that teachers should be provided with the same software used in offices","To provide a comparison of how automation can handle administrative burdens","To suggest that teaching is a more difficult profession than corporate office work"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Office workers already let software handle emails, meetings and calendars; the author sets this beside the non-teaching work (marking, reports) that robots could take off teachers."},{"n":27,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Reading","type":"mcq","passageId":0,"q":"What word is CLOSEST in meaning to '<b>cut</b>' in the final paragraph?","options":["divide","stop","remove","reduce"],"correct":3,"explanation":"\"cut the time teachers spend marking homework\" = reduce it; the teachers still mark, only less."},{"n":28,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Reading","type":"mcq","passageId":0,"q":"In which place in the final paragraph does the following sentence best fit?<br><b><i>By assigning these tasks to robots, educators could focus on mentoring and inspiring their pupils.</i></b>","options":["[III]","[I]","[IV]","[II]"],"correct":2,"explanation":"\"these tasks\" are the marking homework and writing reports just named, and \"mentoring and inspiring\" spells out \"the parts of the job humans do best\" — so the sentence concludes the paragraph at [IV]."},{"n":29,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":1,"q":"(29)","options":["friendly","threatening","beautiful","polluted"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The blank pairs with \"noisy\" as a drawback contrasted by \"but it's also an exciting city\": noisy and polluted."},{"n":30,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":1,"q":"(30)","options":["accurate","reliable","authentic","well-known"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Khao San Road is \"not very Thai\"; Phra Kanong, \"where everyday Bangkok people eat, work and live\", is the more authentic (genuinely local) alternative."},{"n":31,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":1,"q":"(31)","options":["come","go","catch","hop"],"correct":2,"explanation":"\"catch a boat/bus/train\" takes a direct object; \"hop\" would need \"on\" and \"go/come\" cannot take \"an express boat\" as object."},{"n":32,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":1,"q":"(32)","options":["however","whatever","whoever","wherever"],"correct":1,"explanation":"\"choose whatever looks most interesting\" — \"whatever\" (= anything that) is the subject of \"looks\" and refers to things (restaurants and food stands), not people or places."},{"n":33,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Reading","type":"mcq","passageId":1,"q":"What is the most suitable title for the text?","options":["A Comprehensive Itinerary for a Long Stay in Bangkok","Discovering the Authentic and Modern Faces of Bangkok","A Survivor's Guide to Bangkok's Traffic and Cuisine","Uncovering Khao San Road: The Heart of Thai Culture"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The text pairs the genuinely local (Phra Kanong markets, street food, temples) with the modern (Skytrain, Sukhumvit malls and nightlife); it is a short overview, not a full itinerary, and Khao San Road is dismissed as \"not very Thai\"."},{"n":34,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Reading","type":"mcq","passageId":1,"q":"What is implied about Bangkok's transportation system?","options":["Taxis are among the easiest methods for tourists to get to historical sites quickly.","The Skytrain is exclusively designed to connect the Chao Phraya river to Chatuchak.","Motorized road vehicles are the best way to experience the lively nightlife of the city.","Water-based transport is considered one of the strategic solutions to road congestion."],"correct":3,"explanation":"Taxis mean \"hours stuck in traffic jams\", and one of the \"two much better ways\" offered is the express boat or longtail boat along the river and canals."},{"n":35,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Reading","type":"mcq","passageId":1,"q":"According to the text, what is NOT TRUE about dining and activities in Bangkok?","options":["Street food is a budget-friendly option, with satisfying meals costing around $5.","Yaowarat Street is a prime location to sample both Chinese and Thai cuisine.","Visitors can watch the live snake show but not come in contact with the animals.","Some food stands are highly specialized in cooking specific dishes like Pad Thai."],"correct":2,"explanation":"The last line says the opposite: \"You can even touch a snake yourself if you want to!\""},{"n":36,"part":"PHẦN 2. TỰ LUẬN","section":"Word form","type":"text","q":"His ______ led to several mistakes in the final report. (COMPETENT)","accept":["incompetence"],"answer":"incompetence","explanation":"A noun is needed after \"His\", and the sense is negative (it caused mistakes): incompetence."},{"n":37,"part":"PHẦN 2. TỰ LUẬN","section":"Word form","type":"text","q":"Affordable housing schemes are devised to tackle the issue of ______ in urban areas. (HOME)","accept":["homelessness"],"answer":"homelessness","explanation":"\"the issue of + noun\": the problem that affordable housing solves is homelessness."},{"n":38,"part":"PHẦN 2. TỰ LUẬN","section":"Word form","type":"text","q":"She is a very ______ writer who can create entire worlds filled with mythical creatures. (IMAGINE)","accept":["imaginative"],"answer":"imaginative","explanation":"An adjective describing a person with a lot of imagination: an imaginative writer."},{"n":39,"part":"PHẦN 2. TỰ LUẬN","section":"Word form","type":"text","q":"To be a successful leader, you must be able to handle stressful situations ______. (EFFECT)","accept":["effectively"],"answer":"effectively","explanation":"An adverb is needed to modify the verb \"handle\": handle situations effectively."},{"n":40,"part":"PHẦN 2. TỰ LUẬN","section":"Word form","type":"text","q":"Using audio guides and virtual maps, visitors can take a ______ tour of the castle. (GUIDE)","accept":["self-guided","self guided","guided"],"answer":"self-guided","explanation":"An adjective before \"tour\". \"Using audio guides and virtual maps\" means visitors tour the castle without a human guide, so the intended word is \"self-guided\" (a self-guided tour); the plain \"guided tour\" is grammatical but weaker, since a guided tour is normally led by a guide."},{"n":41,"part":"PHẦN 2. TỰ LUẬN","section":"Word form","type":"text","q":"Even though we are not her friends, we are ______ to the struggles she is facing. (SYMPATHY)","accept":["sympathetic"],"answer":"sympathetic","explanation":"\"be sympathetic to/towards something\" — the adjective is needed after \"are\"."},{"n":42,"part":"PHẦN 2. TỰ LUẬN","section":"Word form","type":"text","q":"Many ______ are working together to study the effects of climate change. (ECOLOGY)","accept":["ecologists"],"answer":"ecologists","explanation":"A plural noun for people after \"Many\" and before \"are working\": ecologists (scientists who study ecology)."},{"n":43,"part":"PHẦN 2. TỰ LUẬN","section":"Word form","type":"text","q":"Many Vietnamese people have the habit of riding motorbikes on the ______ although it is illegal. (WALK)","accept":["sidewalk","sidewalks"],"answer":"sidewalk","explanation":"A noun for the path beside the road that pedestrians use: the sidewalk (British \"pavement\")."},{"n":44,"part":"PHẦN 2. TỰ LUẬN","section":"Sentence transformation","type":"text","q":"If there are any questions, please contact us by email. (SEND)<br>→ Should ______ email.","accept":["you have any questions, please send us an","you have any questions please send us an","you have any questions, send us an","you have any questions send us an","you have any questions, please send an","you have any question, please send us an","there be any questions, please send us an","there be any questions, send us an","there be any questions send us an"],"answer":"you have any questions, please send us an","explanation":"Inverted first conditional \"Should you have …\" (= If you have …); \"contact us by email\" becomes \"send us an email\"."},{"n":45,"part":"PHẦN 2. TỰ LUẬN","section":"Sentence transformation","type":"text","q":"It was a mistake for you to go out in the rain without an umbrella. (SHOULD)<br>→ You ______ in the rain without an umbrella.","accept":["should not have gone out","shouldn't have gone out","should never have gone out","should not have gone","shouldn't have gone"],"answer":"should not have gone out","explanation":"\"should not have + past participle\" criticises a past action that was a mistake: you shouldn't have gone out."},{"n":46,"part":"PHẦN 2. TỰ LUẬN","section":"Sentence transformation","type":"text","q":"We are thinking of appointing Như to the position of Student Body President. (CONSIDERED)<br>→ Như ______ the position of Student Body President.","accept":["is being considered for","is currently being considered for","is now being considered for","is being considered by us for"],"answer":"is being considered for","explanation":"Present continuous passive of \"consider somebody for a position\": Như is being considered for the position."},{"n":47,"part":"PHẦN 2. TỰ LUẬN","section":"Sentence transformation","type":"text","q":"People think that the ancient temple was destroyed by a natural disaster. (THOUGHT)<br>→ The ancient temple ______ by a natural disaster.","accept":["is thought to have been destroyed","is thought to be destroyed"],"answer":"is thought to have been destroyed","explanation":"Impersonal passive with a perfect infinitive for an earlier action: \"is thought to have been destroyed\" (= people think it was destroyed)."},{"n":48,"part":"PHẦN 2. TỰ LUẬN","section":"Sentence transformation","type":"text","q":"\"Don't forget to check the subway schedule before you leave,\" my mother said. (REMINDED)<br>→ My mother ______ before I left.","accept":["reminded me to check the subway schedule","reminded me to check the subway schedules","reminded me to check the subway timetable","reminded me not to forget to check the subway schedule"],"answer":"reminded me to check the subway schedule","explanation":"Reported speech: \"Don't forget to do something\" → \"remind somebody to do something\"; \"you leave\" shifts to \"I left\"."},{"n":49,"part":"PHẦN 2. TỰ LUẬN","section":"Sentence transformation","type":"text","q":"If people have more awareness of environmental issues, they will act more responsibly towards the planet. (THE)<br>→ ______ are, the more responsibly they will act.","accept":["The more environmentally aware people","The more environmentally-aware people","The more aware of environmental issues people","The more aware of environmental issues that people","The more environmentally conscious people","The more aware people","The more environmentally aware that people"],"answer":"The more environmentally aware people","explanation":"Double comparative \"the more …, the more …\": \"have more awareness of environmental issues\" becomes \"the more environmentally aware people are\"."},{"n":50,"part":"PHẦN 2. TỰ LUẬN","section":"Sentence transformation","type":"text","q":"It was a serious moment, but I couldn't stop myself from laughing. (HELP)<br>→ I couldn't ______ it was a serious moment.","accept":["help laughing even though","help laughing although","help laughing though","help laughing even if","help but laugh even though","help but laugh although","help but laugh though","help laughing, even though","help laughing, although"],"answer":"help laughing even though","explanation":"\"can't help + V-ing\" (or \"can't help but + bare infinitive\") = can't stop oneself; the contrast \"but\" becomes \"even though/although\"."}]},{"id":"ptnk-2026-chuyen","year":2026,"track":"chuyen","title":"PTNK 2026 · Tiếng Anh Chuyên","subtitle":"Đề thi tuyển sinh lớp 10 · Trường Phổ thông Năng khiếu · 2026-2027","durationMin":120,"keySource":"solved","source":"2026 - TS10 - Tiếng Anh chuyên (Đề thi).pdf","passages":["What makes people feel nervous about public speaking? Why do their hands shake, their knees quiver, their stomachs (41)____, and their voices rise?<br><br>Researchers find that public-speaking anxiety is both a trait (a general tendency) and a state (anxiety (42)____ by a specific audience or setting). Causes include fears of humiliation, failure, making mistakes, or being unprepared, alongside perfectionism and performance pressure.<br><br>Communication apprehension may have a biological basis; some individuals inherit a tendency to feel anxious. However, speakers can manage this by focusing on changeable behaviors, like enhancing speaking skills, rather than on their (43)____ makeup. Conversely, psychology affects biology. A speaker's perception of the assignment, skills, and self-esteem interact to create anxiety. (44)____ with conflict, the brain triggers a \"fight-or-flight\" (45)____, increasing breathing rates, adrenaline, and blood (46)____. These physiological changes cause a rapid heartbeat, shaking, quivering voice, or stomach \"butterflies\". (47)____, speakers may minimize eye contact, use vocalized pauses (\"um,\" \"ah\"), or speak too rapidly. (48)____ uncomfortable, the body is simply attempting to assist with the task.<br><br>Regardless, apprehension seems to follow a predictable (49)____, with people generally feeling most nervous right before presenting, when uncertainty is highest. The second highest anxiety level occurs when the instructor explains the assignment, while the least anxiety is felt during preparation. Understanding this helps speakers manage anxiety when it (50)____. Because anxiety decreases once speaking begins and diminishes through positive preparation, starting early ensures both a better speech and greater confidence.","In many schools, handwriting has begun to make a modest comeback. For years, it was (1)____ dismissed as a nostalgic skill, likely to be eclipsed by keyboards. Yet recent classroom experiments suggest that putting pen to paper can (2)____ memory in ways typing often cannot. The point is not to (3)____ one tool against the other, but to ask which tool best suits a particular task. When pupils take handwritten notes, they are (4)____ to compress ideas, deciding what matters before it reaches the page. That act of selection can (5)____ understanding. By contrast, laptops make it easy to transcribe a lesson word (6)____ word, a habit that can create the illusion of learning without much processing.<br><br>None of this means schools should turn their backs on digital literacy. A child who cannot search responsibly or revise a shared document is at a (7)____. But a curriculum that treats handwriting as obsolete may be throwing away a simple (8)____ for thought. The best classrooms are not those that (9)____ to old methods or chase every new device; they teach students to choose deliberately. In that sense, the revival of handwriting is less a reactionary move than a reminder that learning is not always (10)____ by speed.","Every museum tells a story, but the most honest ones admit that their story could have been told otherwise. Objects rarely speak for (1)____. They acquire meaning in relation (2)____ the labels, rooms and routes that surround them. A cracked bowl may be presented (3)____ evidence of hardship, ingenuity or trade, (4)____ on the questions curators ask. This does not make museums unreliable; on the (5)____, it makes their responsibility clearer. Visitors need to know not only what is on (6)____ but also what has been left out. In recent years, many museums have begun to invite communities (7)____ histories were once treated as marginal to help decide how collections should be interpreted. Such collaboration is not always easy. There are arguments over ownership, over painful memories, and over (8)____ a national institution can ever speak for those it once ignored. Still, the attempt matters. A museum that refuses to revise its story (9)____ turning heritage into decoration. A museum that listens, by contrast, allows the past to remain alive enough to challenge the present. It asks visitors to see history not as a set of facts over and done with, but as a conversation in which they too have a (10)____.","The latest AI slop to take the internet by (1)____ features anthropomorphic fruit, specifically TikTok-based soap (2)____ videos channeling fruit and their love lives. Created with minimal human input, this low-quality, AI-generated content is as easy to absorb as it is to churn out. While specifics may (3)____, the majority of these clips are telenovela or reality TV-styled, featuring cartoony humanoid fruits with strangely mature (4)____ matter. For instance, viewers might watch a weeping strawberry discover her partner cheating (5)____ her, or two coconuts struggling to feed their children.<br><br>While to adults, the propagation of these fruit videos feels ridiculous on the (6)____, there is an insidious underbelly, namely the exploitation of children's media and its subsequent (7)____ on younger audiences. With bright colors and speedy pacing, these videos mimic the style of (8)____ marketed at children, such as by <i>Cocomelon</i>, albeit with wildly inappropriate themes. While it is true that children consuming age-inappropriate media is not new, AI has accelerated the output rate, (9)____ multiplies exposure frequency and normalizes such content. Frankly, tech corporations, specifically AI companies, ought to be (10)____ accountable for this rampant, uncontrolled spread of harmful AI slop.","<b>The True Price of a Tree</b><br><i>By David Suzuki</i><br><br>We were standing in an ancient forest that was threatened with clear-cut logging. He was the CEO of the company that had been allotted the tree farm license that enabled him to drive a road into the valley and begin the industrial extraction of the trees that would destroy what took millennia to evolve. We had engaged in an animated argument about the fate of that forest while standing face to face less than half a meter apart.<br><br><b>(1)____</b><br><br>“Unless, of course, you tree huggers decide you’ll pay money to save it so you can enjoy it. Think your cronies can raise enough money to save the entire forest? Logging is what keeps the economy of this province growing and makes it possible for you preservationists to wear clothes, drive cars, and watch TV.”<br><br><b>(2)____</b><br><br>But the perspective through which I viewed the forest—of which that tree was a part—was radically different. That one tree was a tiny part of a community of organisms thousands of years in the making. That community is made up of trees (“merchantable timber,” or “fiber,” in the jargon of the industry) that are a tiny minority of the lifeforms that comprise the forest.<br><br><b>(3)____</b><br><br>This is the community that we recognize as a forest, complex and interlinked beyond comprehension and all held together by the air, water, and sunlight that suffuse them.<br><br><b>(4)____</b><br><br>Consequently, companies require geneticists to breed fast-growing strains of commercially valuable trees, tree planters, herbicides to clear out “weed” (i.e., commercially worthless) species, insecticides to eliminate pests, fertilizers to restore nutrition to the soil, and firefighters. Large clear-cuts and the use of heavy machinery expose soil flora and fauna to sunlight, wind, and air. This alters water retention in the streams and rivers that are the lifeblood of the forest, radically transforming the remaining species mix.<br><br><b>(5)____</b><br><br>The entire forest is like the goose that laid golden eggs in the children’s fable; as long as the goose is fat and healthy, it will yield golden eggs indefinitely. In the short-term perspective of global economics, as in the children’s story, forestry companies attempt to gather all of the eggs at once by killing the goose.<br><br><b>(6)____</b><br><br>Returning to the CEO’s statement that the tree only acquires value when it enters our economy by generating revenue, consider what the tree does before humans define its value. Hundreds of years old, that tree has absorbed carbon dioxide (a greenhouse gas) from the air, thereby playing a part in life’s climate engine, and releases oxygen as a byproduct of photosynthesis—not a bad byproduct for all animals like us that are completely dependent on that oxygen for survival.<br><br><b>(7)____</b><br><br>All of these natural services performed by that single standing tree affect human health and survival, yet they are entirely ignored by our economy. It’s long past time when we started lifting our horizons and values beyond the extremely limited perspective of conventional economics.<br><br><b>List of paragraphs:</b><br><br><b>A.</b> As I pointed out in the book <i>Good News for a Change: Hope for a Troubled Planet</i>, by selective logging at or below the growth rate of the trees in a forest, trees can be profitably “harvested” indefinitely instead of once every hundred years or more. Nor is the diversity that is the key to resilience and regeneration sacrificed when trees are selectively removed.<br><br><b>B.</b> Recent studies on urban forestry indicate that compared to natural wild areas, isolated city parks do not offer the same microclimate stability. There, only invasive species and drought-tolerant shrub species thrive rather than native canopy trees. Because of this, urban planners cannot rely solely on localized tree-planting initiatives to emulate the effects of natural forests.<br><br><b>C.</b> The soil is a living organism made up of tens of thousands of species of microorganisms—viruses, bacteria, fungi, protozoa—and larger nematodes, worms, insects, and mites. Plants and animals blanket the forest floor, lichens and mosses coat rocks and decaying wood, snags and fallen logs provide nutrition and protection for countless organisms.<br><br><b>D.</b> “You see that tree over there?” he shouted, pointing to a giant that was probably many centuries old. Without waiting for my response, he continued, “It doesn’t have any value until it’s cut down.” I was dumbstruck by the statement, giving him the opening to carry on.<br><br><b>E.</b> Foresters rationalize these practices as “proper silvicultural management,” as if they know what it is that creates the original forest that was cut down. Of course, they have no idea. They lack both a proper inventory of all the constituent species that make up the forest and a blueprint that explains how all of the components are interconnected.<br><br><b>F.</b> What had made me speechless was the realization that he was right. In the value system inherent in the form of economics our society has embraced, only when money is exchanged for goods and services is the transaction recognized as having economic worth.<br><br><b>G.</b> Likewise, the roots of the tree cling to soil even under the hardest of rains, thereby inhibiting erosion while siphoning vast quantities of water up into the canopy, where it is released through transpiration, hence ameliorating weather. From roots to tips of branches, the tree offers a habitat for countless forms of life, from lichens and fungi to insects, birds, and mammals.<br><br><b>H.</b> What foresters refer to as a “second-growth forest” is not a forest at all but a tree plantation—an attempt to grow trees like a crop of tomato or corn plants. But of course, such a “managed forest,” or “fiber farm,” no longer has the resilience, regenerative capacity, or self-protective devices of a natural forest.<br><br><b>I.</b> Consequently, while carbon offset credits offer a lucrative alternative for developing nations to preserve forest areas, local enforcement often fails to prevent illegal logging operations along unmarked borders. This instability makes it difficult for conservation groups to project the long-term financial viability of these corporate partnerships.","British anthropologist Sir Edward Burnett Tylor espoused the classic definition of culture in 1871, describing it as that complex whole which includes knowledge, belief, arts, morals, law, custom, and any other capabilities and habits acquired by man as a member of society.\" Culture is learned; it is not something that is biologically determined. This is in contrast to our innate ability to acquire language. Regardless, the particular language an individual first learns – their \"mother tongue\" – can \"only be acquired by man as a member of society.\" Language, therefore, is a part of one's culture, but at the same time, language is used to transmit that culture, to teach that culture. In fact, culture and language are interdependent. No type of human society can be maintained if its members do not share a common language. Moreover, language, the most common way people communicate, could not have developed outside a social setting.<br><br>Language both expresses culture and reflects culture. Some linguists and anthropologists argue that different languages produce different ways of thinking. Cultures organize and view the world in diverse ways, and their languages support these differences through grammar and vocabulary. By studying lexical classification in various languages, scientists have been able to show that speakers of different languages perceive and distinguish things differently. The Nuer people of Sudan, for instance, traditionally were cattle herders, and their entire lifestyle centered on cattle, so their language has dozens of words for cattle. Another cultural-dependent concept is time. English speakers divide time into past, present and future, and English grammar has tenses to reflect this view of time. On the other hand, the Hopi, a Native American tribe of the Southwest United States, only make a distinction between events that have existed or exist (English past and present) and those that do not exist or do not yet exist (English future, including the subjunctive case used for possibility). Their language does not differentiate between present and past. Similarly, the complexity of vocabulary in a language to describe family relationships varies depending on the role of kinship in that culture. Color terminology also varies widely by language. Those that involved in areas with a history of using dyes and artificial coloring have the broadest range of color vocabulary.<br><br>Living languages, of course, undergo constant but often gradual transformation, as new words and expressions are added to the lexicon and meanings of words are altered. Different cultures can speak the same basic language, but it will have variations reflecting those different cultures. For instance, Koreans share a common language, but after six decades of being separated by a tightly sealed border, the cultures of North and South Korea have diverged along with the Korean language. If a South Korean asks a North Korean how they are doing, the North Korean would likely reply \"<i>liupsopneda</i>\", an expression that literally means \"not much,\" which has a sense similar to \"I'm fine, thanks.\" Yet, that same expression would mean \"Mind your own business!\" to the South Korean. Both North and South Korean use the word \"<i>mije</i>,\" but in the North it means \"American imperialist,\" while in the South it means, \"Made in the USA.\"<br><br>Within a culture, language can also play an important role in differentiating occupational groups and social status. Many groups have their own \"focal vocabulary,\" that is, a specialized set of terms that are important to them and what they do. On a Hollywood film set, when the gaffer, head electrician, yells to the crew to \"Kill the baby!\" he is not talking about child murder. He is instructing the electricians to turn off a small light. Moreover, people want to improve their social status will try to adopt the habits, attitudes, and behavioural traits of the group to which they aspire. In terms of language, this includes trying to shift how they speak by taking on the speech patterns and vocabulary associated in their culture with the higher social status.<br><br>In any culture, language is more than just a means of conveying information. Significant aspects of a culture are embedded in the structure of its language, in its vocabulary, grammar, and usage. The distinctions a language makes between genders, how it refers to time and space, and the different ways language conveys rank, respect, and love are all related to the culture in which it is used.","British anthropologist Sir Edward Burnett Tylor espoused the classic definition of culture in 1871, describing it as that complex whole which includes knowledge, belief, arts, morals, law, custom, and any other capabilities and habits acquired by man as a member of society.\" Culture is learned; it is not something that is biologically determined. This is in contrast to our innate ability to acquire language. Regardless, the particular language an individual first learns – their \"mother tongue\" – can \"only be acquired by man as a member of society.\" Language, therefore, is a part of one's culture, but at the same time, language is used to transmit that culture, to teach that culture. In fact, culture and language are interdependent. No type of human society can be maintained if its members do not share a common language. Moreover, language, the most common way people communicate, could not have developed outside a social setting.<br><br>Language both expresses culture and reflects culture. Some linguists and anthropologists argue that different languages produce different ways of thinking. Cultures organize and view the world in diverse ways, and their languages support these differences through grammar and vocabulary. By studying lexical classification in various languages, scientists have been able to show that speakers of different languages perceive and distinguish things differently. The Nuer people of Sudan, for instance, traditionally were cattle herders, and their entire lifestyle centered on cattle, so their language has dozens of words for cattle. Another cultural-dependent concept is time. English speakers divide time into past, present and future, and English grammar has tenses to reflect this view of time. On the other hand, the Hopi, a Native American tribe of the Southwest United States, only make a distinction between events that have existed or exist (English past and present) and those that do not exist or do not yet exist (English future, including the subjunctive case used for possibility). Their language does not differentiate between present and past. Similarly, the complexity of vocabulary in a language to describe family relationships varies depending on the role of kinship in that culture. Color terminology also varies widely by language. Those that involved in areas with a history of using dyes and artificial coloring have the broadest range of color vocabulary.<br><br>Living languages, of course, undergo constant but often gradual transformation, as new words and expressions are added to the lexicon and meanings of words are altered. Different cultures can speak the same basic language, but it will have variations reflecting those different cultures. For instance, Koreans share a common language, but after six decades of being separated by a tightly sealed border, the cultures of North and South Korea have diverged along with the Korean language. If a South Korean asks a North Korean how they are doing, the North Korean would likely reply \"<i>liupsopneda</i>\", an expression that literally means \"not much,\" which has a sense similar to \"I'm fine, thanks.\" Yet, that same expression would mean \"Mind your own business!\" to the South Korean. Both North and South Korean use the word \"<i>mije</i>,\" but in the North it means \"American imperialist,\" while in the South it means, \"Made in the USA.\"<br><br>Within a culture, language can also play an important role in differentiating occupational groups and social status. Many groups have their own \"focal vocabulary,\" that is, a specialized set of terms that are important to them and what they do. On a Hollywood film set, when the gaffer, head electrician, yells to the crew to \"Kill the baby!\" he is not talking about child murder. He is instructing the electricians to turn off a small light. Moreover, people want to improve their social status will try to adopt the habits, attitudes, and behavioural traits of the group to which they aspire. In terms of language, this includes trying to shift how they speak by taking on the speech patterns and vocabulary associated in their culture with the higher social status.<br><br>In any culture, language is more than just a means of conveying information. Significant aspects of a culture are embedded in the structure of its language, in its vocabulary, grammar, and usage. The distinctions a language makes between genders, how it refers to time and space, and the different ways language conveys rank, respect, and love are all related to the culture in which it is used.<br><br><b>Language and culture</b><br><br>Although humans possess the (1)____ to learn languages, they cannot effectively do so without belonging to a shared culture. In a similar fashion, cultures can hardly exist without a shared language between its members. This interdependence is demonstrated in multiple aspects. Firstly, vocabulary and grammar reflect the importance and perception of various domains in a culture. For example, cultures that highly regard (2)____ might have a richer vocabulary to denote relationships. Secondly, the (3)____ of any language constantly receives new additions as well as alterations to current words, a process which is tied to historical developments of the culture. Finally, within a society, language can be used to signify social group. This is illustrated by the (4)____ employed only in certain professions with very different meanings from their literal counterparts, while the words and (5)____ of desirable social groups are often copied by people who wish to be part of those groups.","Even in today's ecologically aware world, a declaration of vegetarianism is often met with expressions of incredulity, cynical smirks, and suspicion. While such intolerant attitudes might have been understandable in the previous century—when vegetarianism was a novel ideology closely linked to the youth (1)____ (CULTURE) known for challenging social conventions—they are outdated today. Currently, vegetarians span all walks of life, far (2)____ (NUMBER) the old \"tree-hugging\" stereotypes. As information becomes more accessible, more people are consciously adopting a (3)____ (MEAT) diet for a host of different compelling reasons.<br><br>In a health-obsessed society, eliminating meat is increasingly common among those looking to avoid its links to chronic (4)____ (AIL) like heart disease and arthritis. Furthermore, many are (5)____ (JUST) concerned about the accumulation of chemical treatments and steroids used in intensive industrial farming to make it most (6)____ (PROFIT). Calves and lambs grow at prodigious rates thanks to various treatments, administered both orally and intramuscularly. Studies indicate these substances, which travel up the food chain into human consumers, may trigger serious hormonal (7)____ (BALANCE) and behavioral disorders.<br><br>Concurrently, (8)____ (HIGH) awareness of modern agricultural methods has also led to humanitarian concerns. Many choose to boycott meat because they cannot condone the systemic suffering inflicted upon (9)____ (LIVE)—such as the severe (10)____ (CONFINE) of calves to produce veal or the manipulation of avian ovulation cycles through artificial light to induce egg production. Ultimately, modern vegetarians are not setting themselves up as moral ideals. A vegetarian isn't necessarily a crank, an eccentric, a subversive. A vegetarian is someone who doesn't eat meat.","Identify the FIVE errors in the following passage and correct them. Indicate the line at which mistakes are found, and how to correct them. (Line 1 \"belief\" → \"believed\" is the example.)<br><br>1 &nbsp;It is traditionally <b><u>belief</u></b> that paper was invented in China during the Eastern Han<br>2 &nbsp;Dynasty. There, a resourceful court official with the name of Ts'ai Lun first developed a<br>3 &nbsp;material that was similar to the paper we use today. Although the factual details of his<br>4 &nbsp;story have remained unknown, it is thought that his original ingredients included bits of<br>5 &nbsp;tree bark, old rags, and fishing nets. The legends surrounded this invention speak of the<br>6 &nbsp;inspiration, which Ts'ai Lun apparently drew from watching paper wasps construct thin-<br>7 &nbsp;shelled nests from tiny pieces of wood fibre. Centuries later, the first industrial machine<br>8 &nbsp;for manufacturing paper in a continuing roll was perfected in France in 1799. Since then,<br>9 &nbsp;the world has enjoyed an uninterrupted supply of the material. The wide variety of paper<br>10 options today, ranging from fine white sheets and tough brown wrapping paper, wouldn't<br>11 exist without these monumental advancements centuries before."],"questions":[{"n":1,"part":"Part 1. Language Use — Questions 1-40 Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"The executive requires his assistant to run all sorts of ________ for him, including getting his laundry, picking up his children from school, and walking his dog.","options":["errands","menial tasks","odd jobs","gigs"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The collocation is <b>run errands</b> — short trips to do small jobs such as collecting laundry or picking up children."},{"n":2,"part":"Part 1. Language Use — Questions 1-40 Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"<i>Alfred:</i> \"Her latest album feels completely derivative and uninspired.\"<br><i>Bruce:</i> \"________ It heavily relies on the out-of-fashion Euro-pop sound.\"","options":["My thoughts exactly.","I beg to differ.","That's a bit of a stretch.","Agree to disagree."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Bruce goes on to criticise the album too, so he agrees: <b>My thoughts exactly</b>. The other three all express disagreement."},{"n":3,"part":"Part 1. Language Use — Questions 1-40 Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"The law requires that this factory ________ the machines every week.","options":["have an engineer to inspect","has an engineer to inspect","has an engineer inspect","have an engineer inspect"],"correct":3,"explanation":"After <b>require that</b> the verb is in the subjunctive (base form <b>have</b>), and the causative is <b>have somebody do</b> something, with no <i>to</i>."},{"n":4,"part":"Part 1. Language Use — Questions 1-40 Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"________ a medic present at the scene of the accident, the victim would not have survived.","options":["Had it been for","Were there not","Were it not for","Had there not been"],"correct":3,"explanation":"A past unreal condition (<i>would not have survived</i>) needs an inverted past perfect: <b>Had there not been</b> a medic present = If there had not been a medic."},{"n":5,"part":"Part 1. Language Use — Questions 1-40 Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Sales figures released by the company are suspicious as they do not ________ shipment records.","options":["add up to","square away with","live up to","check out with"],"correct":1,"explanation":"To <b>square with</b> something is to be consistent with it; the figures do not match the shipment records. <i>Add up to</i> means \"total\", <i>live up to</i> means \"meet expectations\"."},{"n":6,"part":"Part 1. Language Use — Questions 1-40 Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"His manuscript is ruined by a highly ________ style with barely coherent digressions.","options":["discursive","pretentious","fastidious","meticulous"],"correct":0,"explanation":"<b>Discursive</b> writing rambles from one topic to another — exactly what \"barely coherent digressions\" describes."},{"n":7,"part":"Part 1. Language Use — Questions 1-40 Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"The famous movie star looks surprisingly casual today; his only ________ is a plain necklace.","options":["decoration","adornment","embellishment","upkeep"],"correct":1,"explanation":"An <b>adornment</b> is something worn to add beauty, such as jewellery. <i>Decoration</i> and <i>embellishment</i> are not used for a piece of jewellery worn by a person."},{"n":8,"part":"Part 1. Language Use — Questions 1-40 Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"His explanation was so ________ that even the experts were unconvinced.","options":["far-fetched","far-flung","far-sighted","far-reaching"],"correct":0,"explanation":"<b>Far-fetched</b> = unlikely, hard to believe; hence nobody was convinced. <i>Far-flung</i> = distant, <i>far-sighted</i> = wise about the future, <i>far-reaching</i> = having wide effects."},{"n":9,"part":"Part 1. Language Use — Questions 1-40 Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Children are often described as ________, easily influenced by their parental and guardian figures.","options":["ephemeral","sporadic","volatile","malleable"],"correct":3,"explanation":"<b>Malleable</b> = easily shaped or influenced, which the sentence itself paraphrases."},{"n":10,"part":"Part 1. Language Use — Questions 1-40 Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Grace was praised for her ________ judgement when she reported the scammer pretending to be a police officer on the phone.","options":["sound","fair","firm","snap"],"correct":0,"explanation":"<b>Sound judgement</b> = sensible, reliable judgement. A <i>snap</i> judgement is a hasty one and would not be praised."},{"n":11,"part":"Part 1. Language Use — Questions 1-40 Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Fans are excited at the prospect of a ________ between two beloved anime franchises.","options":["layover","crossover","runover","spillover"],"correct":1,"explanation":"A <b>crossover</b> is a work in which characters from two different series meet."},{"n":12,"part":"Part 1. Language Use — Questions 1-40 Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"________ a short self-paced online course, one can receive a certificate.","options":["By completing","With the completion","Having completed","On being completed"],"correct":0,"explanation":"<b>By + V-ing</b> expresses the means by which the certificate is obtained. <i>Having completed</i> is also grammatical, but <i>by completing</i> is the natural way to state the method, and the paper's intended answer."},{"n":13,"part":"Part 1. Language Use — Questions 1-40 Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Refrain from consuming cheese or cream if you are lactose ________.","options":["intolerant","indigestible","repellant","averse"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The fixed medical term is <b>lactose intolerant</b> — unable to digest lactose."},{"n":14,"part":"Part 1. Language Use — Questions 1-40 Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"For basing their argument on ________ evidence rather than verified documentation, the plaintiff's civil lawsuit was dismissed.","options":["incidental","hypothetical","anecdotal","accidental"],"correct":2,"explanation":"<b>Anecdotal evidence</b> is based on personal stories rather than verified facts or documents — the contrast the sentence draws."},{"n":15,"part":"Part 1. Language Use — Questions 1-40 Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"If you ________ with the shares that you have, it might be your ticket to early retirement.","options":["stick to your guns","play your cards right","follow your nose","throw your hat in the ring"],"correct":1,"explanation":"<b>Play your cards right</b> = handle a situation skilfully to get the best result; here, managing your shares wisely."},{"n":16,"part":"Part 1. Language Use — Questions 1-40 Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Since the joke never ________ Ricky, we had to explain it to him, which was lame.","options":["squared up to","cottoned on to","muscled in on","pandered to"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The sense is that the joke was never understood: <b>cotton on (to)</b> = understand, \"get\" something. <i>Square up to</i> = confront, <i>muscle in on</i> = force one's way into, <i>pander to</i> = indulge."},{"n":17,"part":"Part 1. Language Use — Questions 1-40 Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"It took the panel quite some time to ________ this conclusion.","options":["land on","reach at","jump to","arrive at"],"correct":3,"explanation":"<b>Arrive at a conclusion</b> is the collocation; <i>reach</i> takes no preposition, and <i>jump to</i> a conclusion means deciding too quickly, contradicting \"quite some time\"."},{"n":18,"part":"Part 1. Language Use — Questions 1-40 Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"________ is comfortable with relocating the fragile fresco.","options":["Both the restoration experts and the museum curator","The restoration experts as well as the museum curator","Either the museum curator or the restoration experts","Neither the restoration experts nor the museum curator"],"correct":3,"explanation":"With <b>neither … nor</b> the verb agrees with the nearer subject, <i>the museum curator</i>, so singular <b>is</b> fits. <i>Both … and</i>, <i>as well as</i> (agreeing with <i>experts</i>) and <i>either … or the experts</i> all need <i>are</i>."},{"n":19,"part":"Part 1. Language Use — Questions 1-40 Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Harry displays an ________ attitude to religion, often joking about heaven and hell.","options":["incorrigible","irreverent","irreconcilable","irrelevant"],"correct":1,"explanation":"<b>Irreverent</b> = showing no respect for things usually taken seriously, such as religion."},{"n":20,"part":"Part 1. Language Use — Questions 1-40 Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"With no major releases ________, the media company decided to lay off a portion of its staff.","options":["in the offing","in the balance","on the back burner","against the grain"],"correct":0,"explanation":"<b>In the offing</b> = likely to happen soon. With nothing coming up, the company cut staff."},{"n":21,"part":"Part 1. Language Use — Questions 1-40 Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"________, the movie was panned by critics as formulaic and lacking in substance.","options":["Commercially successful though was the film","Successfully commercial though the film was","Commercially successful though the film was","Successfully commercial though was the film"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Concessive fronting: <b>Adjective + though + subject + verb</b> — \"Commercially successful though the film was\". No inversion of subject and verb, and the adverb <i>commercially</i> modifies <i>successful</i>."},{"n":22,"part":"Part 1. Language Use — Questions 1-40 Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Jerry's coach ________ for failing to defend his champion title.","options":["phased him out","passed him up","wrote him off","ruled him out"],"correct":2,"explanation":"<b>Write somebody off</b> = decide that they are a failure or no longer worth attention."},{"n":23,"part":"Part 1. Language Use — Questions 1-40 Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Not until the files were declassified decades later ________ the true extent of the criminal operation.","options":["did the public realize","were the public realizing","that the public realized","had the public realized"],"correct":0,"explanation":"After a fronted <b>Not until …</b> clause the main clause is inverted with an auxiliary: <b>did the public realize</b>. The realizing happened after the declassification, so past simple, not past perfect."},{"n":24,"part":"Part 1. Language Use — Questions 1-40 Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"________, I would have visited you a month ago.","options":["Had it been for my fear of flying","Should I not be afraid of flying","Had I not been afraid of flying","Were it not for my fear of flying"],"correct":2,"explanation":"A past unreal result (<i>would have visited … a month ago</i>) needs an inverted past-perfect condition: <b>Had I not been afraid of flying</b>. Option A lacks <i>not</i> and would mean the opposite."},{"n":25,"part":"Part 1. Language Use — Questions 1-40 Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"The journalist refused to disclose her source, even ________ legal action threats.","options":["by virtue of","in the face of","for the sake of","on account of"],"correct":1,"explanation":"<b>In the face of</b> = despite being confronted with (a threat or difficulty)."},{"n":26,"part":"Part 1. Language Use — Questions 1-40 Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"In preparation for a large incoming shipment, warehouse workers were instructed to ________.","options":["bridge the gaps","clear the decks","cut corners","draw lines"],"correct":1,"explanation":"<b>Clear the decks</b> = remove what is not needed so as to be ready for something new — here, making room for the shipment."},{"n":27,"part":"Part 1. Language Use — Questions 1-40 Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Sabrina wrote down \"<i>baking soda for mom</i>\" on her shopping list, lest in her hurry she ________ it.","options":["forgets","will forget","forgot","forget"],"correct":3,"explanation":"<b>Lest</b> is followed by the subjunctive (base form) or <i>should</i>: lest she <b>forget</b> it."},{"n":28,"part":"Part 1. Language Use — Questions 1-40 Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"The boy denied ________ the answer key, but his perfect score makes it hard to believe.","options":["having been stolen","stealing","to steal","to have stolen"],"correct":1,"explanation":"<b>Deny + V-ing</b>: denied stealing. <i>Having been stolen</i> is passive and would make the boy the thing stolen."},{"n":29,"part":"Part 1. Language Use — Questions 1-40 Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"The documentary was criticised not so much for what it said ________ for what it left unsaid.","options":["and","but","as","than"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The structure is <b>not so much X as Y</b>."},{"n":30,"part":"Part 1. Language Use — Questions 1-40 Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Surrounded at Dien Bien Phu with their supply lifelines severed, the French forces were ________.","options":["out on a limb","on a hiding to nothing","in the doldrums","digging their own grave"],"correct":1,"explanation":"<b>On a hiding to nothing</b> = in a situation where failure is certain and success impossible. <i>In the doldrums</i> = inactive or depressed; <i>out on a limb</i> = holding an unsupported position."},{"n":31,"part":"Part 1. Language Use — Questions 1-40 Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Realizing that his matcha-flavored dog food venture had completely failed, the entrepreneur chose to ________.","options":["burn his boats","bide his time","cut his losses","raise his stakes"],"correct":2,"explanation":"<b>Cut one's losses</b> = abandon a failing venture before losing any more."},{"n":32,"part":"Part 1. Language Use — Questions 1-40 Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Only after the committee had reviewed the raw data ________ to publish the preliminary results.","options":["it agreed","did it agree","it had agreed","had it agreed"],"correct":1,"explanation":"A fronted <b>Only after …</b> clause forces inversion in the main clause; the reviewing (past perfect) came before the agreeing (past simple): <b>did it agree</b>."},{"n":33,"part":"Part 1. Language Use — Questions 1-40 Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Growing up in a middle class suburban family, Alice is ________ from the hardships of inner-city children.","options":["restricted","stranded","covered","insulated"],"correct":3,"explanation":"<b>Insulated from</b> = protected from unpleasant experiences."},{"n":34,"part":"Part 1. Language Use — Questions 1-40 Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Nourished by rich silt, the Mekong Delta is a(n) ________ agricultural terrain.","options":["fecund","excessive","abundant","opulent"],"correct":0,"explanation":"<b>Fecund</b> = fertile, producing many crops. <i>Abundant</i> describes quantity, not the productivity of land."},{"n":35,"part":"Part 1. Language Use — Questions 1-40 Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Lowering interest rates is often an economic ________ to encourage spending and investment.","options":["stimulant","stimulus","stimulation","stimuli"],"correct":1,"explanation":"An <b>economic stimulus</b> is a measure to boost activity; <i>stimulant</i> is a drug, <i>stimuli</i> is plural."},{"n":36,"part":"Part 1. Language Use — Questions 1-40 Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"<i>Molly:</i> \"________ our meeting to tomorrow afternoon?\"<br><i>Holly:</i> \"________ Tomorrow afternoon actually works better for me.\"","options":["Would you object to rescheduling / Yes, please!","Do you mind rescheduling / Of course!","Would you mind if I rescheduled / Not at all!","Would you be objected to reschedule / Never mind!"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Holly is happy to move the meeting, so the reply to <i>Would you mind…?</i> must be <b>Not at all</b>. \"Yes, please\" to <i>would you object</i> and \"Of course\" to <i>do you mind</i> would both mean she objects."},{"n":37,"part":"Part 1. Language Use — Questions 1-40 Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Passed down through generations, '<i>Quan Ho</i>' singing requires vocal precision that is ________ to the region's identity.","options":["intrinsic","identical","systemic","ingrained"],"correct":0,"explanation":"<b>Intrinsic to</b> = an essential, inherent part of. <i>Ingrained</i> takes <i>in</i>, not <i>to</i>."},{"n":38,"part":"Part 1. Language Use — Questions 1-40 Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"The university is offering grants in astrophysics, ________ have to be women currently pursuing a PhD program.","options":["the recipients of which","the recipients of those","which the recipients for them","of that the recipients"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Non-defining relative clause with a noun + <b>of which</b>: \"grants, <b>the recipients of which</b> have to be women\"."},{"n":39,"part":"Part 1. Language Use — Questions 1-40 Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Our dean announced extra funding for the faculty, only ________ it withdrawn two weeks later.","options":["having had","by having","having","to have"],"correct":3,"explanation":"<b>only to + infinitive</b> expresses a disappointing outcome; <i>have it withdrawn</i> is the causative/passive \"have something done\"."},{"n":40,"part":"Part 1. Language Use — Questions 1-40 Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"I would rather you ________ the citations of your essay carefully before submitting it tomorrow.","options":["check","checked","would check","had checked"],"correct":1,"explanation":"<b>Would rather + subject + past simple</b> for a present/future wish about someone else's action: I would rather you <b>checked</b>."},{"n":41,"part":"Part 1. Language Use — Questions 41-60 Guided Cloze Test — Guided cloze 1 (41-50)","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":0,"q":"(41) ____","options":["flutter","vibrate","shiver","murmur"],"correct":0,"explanation":"A nervous stomach \"flutters\" (the \"butterflies\" mentioned later in the text); hands shake and knees quiver, but stomachs do not vibrate, shiver or murmur."},{"n":42,"part":"Part 1. Language Use — Questions 41-60 Guided Cloze Test — Guided cloze 1 (41-50)","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":0,"q":"(42) ____","options":["advanced","evoked","sustained","triggered"],"correct":3,"explanation":"A state of anxiety is \"triggered by\" a specific audience or setting — the same verb the passage uses later (\"the brain triggers a fight-or-flight response\")."},{"n":43,"part":"Part 1. Language Use — Questions 41-60 Guided Cloze Test — Guided cloze 1 (41-50)","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":0,"q":"(43) ____","options":["organic","cultural","natural","genetic"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The sentence contrasts changeable behaviours with what people inherit (\"a biological basis\"); the fixed collocation is \"genetic makeup\"."},{"n":44,"part":"Part 1. Language Use — Questions 41-60 Guided Cloze Test — Guided cloze 1 (41-50)","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":0,"q":"(44) ____","options":["Caught","Bound","Dealt","Faced"],"correct":3,"explanation":"\"Faced with conflict\" is the standard participle phrase meaning when confronted with a problem; \"caught\" would need \"up in\" and \"dealt with\" does not fit the meaning."},{"n":45,"part":"Part 1. Language Use — Questions 41-60 Guided Cloze Test — Guided cloze 1 (41-50)","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":0,"q":"(45) ____","options":["reflection","response","reaction","reply"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The set phrase is the \"fight-or-flight response\", the body's automatic reaction to threat."},{"n":46,"part":"Part 1. Language Use — Questions 41-60 Guided Cloze Test — Guided cloze 1 (41-50)","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":0,"q":"(46) ____","options":["surge","stream","flow","pulse"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Adrenaline increases \"blood flow\" — the natural collocation alongside breathing rates; a faster heartbeat would be \"heart rate\" or \"pulse\", not \"blood pulse\"."},{"n":47,"part":"Part 1. Language Use — Questions 41-60 Guided Cloze Test — Guided cloze 1 (41-50)","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":0,"q":"(47) ____","options":["Finally","Interestingly","Consequently","Ironically"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The behaviours (avoiding eye contact, \"um\"/\"ah\", speaking too fast) are the consequences of the physiological changes just described, so \"Consequently\" links them."},{"n":48,"part":"Part 1. Language Use — Questions 41-60 Guided Cloze Test — Guided cloze 1 (41-50)","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":0,"q":"(48) ____","options":["Despite","Unless","Though","Since"],"correct":2,"explanation":"\"Though uncomfortable\" is a reduced concessive clause (= though it is uncomfortable); \"Despite\" needs a noun, and \"Unless\"/\"Since\" give the wrong meaning."},{"n":49,"part":"Part 1. Language Use — Questions 41-60 Guided Cloze Test — Guided cloze 1 (41-50)","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":0,"q":"(49) ____","options":["trend","pattern","schedule","rhythm"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Anxiety follows a \"predictable pattern\": highest just before presenting, next when the assignment is explained, lowest during preparation."},{"n":50,"part":"Part 1. Language Use — Questions 41-60 Guided Cloze Test — Guided cloze 1 (41-50)","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":0,"q":"(50) ____","options":["arouses","rears","arises","raises"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Anxiety \"arises\" (intransitive: appears, comes up); \"arouse\" and \"raise\" need an object, and \"rears\" would need \"its head\"."},{"n":51,"part":"Part 1. Language Use — Questions 41-60 Guided Cloze Test — Guided cloze 2 (51-60)","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":1,"q":"Blank (1): For years, it was ____ dismissed as a nostalgic skill.","options":["totally","utterly","summarily","superficially"],"correct":2,"explanation":"\"Summarily dismissed\" is the set collocation: rejected quickly and without proper consideration — which is what the passage says schools did to handwriting before the recent experiments."},{"n":52,"part":"Part 1. Language Use — Questions 41-60 Guided Cloze Test — Guided cloze 2 (51-60)","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":1,"q":"Blank (2): putting pen to paper can ____ memory in ways typing often cannot.","options":["restore","retrieve","reinforce","recollect"],"correct":2,"explanation":"\"Reinforce memory\" means strengthen it; the other verbs (restore, retrieve, recollect) describe getting something back rather than making it stronger."},{"n":53,"part":"Part 1. Language Use — Questions 41-60 Guided Cloze Test — Guided cloze 2 (51-60)","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":1,"q":"Blank (3): The point is not to ____ one tool against the other.","options":["pit","position","plant","place"],"correct":0,"explanation":"\"Pit X against Y\" is the fixed phrase meaning set them in competition with each other, which the sentence then contrasts with asking which tool suits each task."},{"n":54,"part":"Part 1. Language Use — Questions 41-60 Guided Cloze Test — Guided cloze 2 (51-60)","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":1,"q":"Blank (4): When pupils take handwritten notes, they are ____ to compress ideas.","options":["prompted","permitted","prohibited","forced"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Handwriting is slow, so pupils have no choice but to compress ideas — they are <b>forced</b> to; the contrast with laptops, which let them copy word for word, confirms it."},{"n":55,"part":"Part 1. Language Use — Questions 41-60 Guided Cloze Test — Guided cloze 2 (51-60)","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":1,"q":"Blank (5): That act of selection can ____ understanding.","options":["extend","inflate","deepen","enlarge"],"correct":2,"explanation":"\"Deepen understanding\" is the natural collocation; extend, inflate and enlarge describe size rather than depth of comprehension."},{"n":56,"part":"Part 1. Language Use — Questions 41-60 Guided Cloze Test — Guided cloze 2 (51-60)","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":1,"q":"Blank (6): laptops make it easy to transcribe a lesson word ____ word.","options":["by","over","for","in"],"correct":2,"explanation":"\"Word for word\" means copying exactly, verbatim — the mindless transcription the passage contrasts with selective handwritten notes."},{"n":57,"part":"Part 1. Language Use — Questions 41-60 Guided Cloze Test — Guided cloze 2 (51-60)","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":1,"q":"Blank (7): A child who cannot search responsibly or revise a shared document is at a ____.","options":["drawback","deficit","disadvantage","downfall"],"correct":2,"explanation":"\"To be at a disadvantage\" is the fixed expression; drawback, deficit and downfall do not take \"at a\" in this sense."},{"n":58,"part":"Part 1. Language Use — Questions 41-60 Guided Cloze Test — Guided cloze 2 (51-60)","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":1,"q":"Blank (8): a curriculum that treats handwriting as obsolete may be throwing away a simple ____ for thought.","options":["substitute","shortcut","shelter","scaffold"],"correct":3,"explanation":"A <b>scaffold</b> is a support structure that helps something be built — here handwriting supports thinking; it is not a substitute for thought nor a shortcut past it."},{"n":59,"part":"Part 1. Language Use — Questions 41-60 Guided Cloze Test — Guided cloze 2 (51-60)","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":1,"q":"Blank (9): The best classrooms are not those that ____ to old methods or chase every new device.","options":["commit","cling","affix","abide"],"correct":1,"explanation":"\"Cling to old methods\" means hold on to them stubbornly, the opposite extreme to chasing every new device; \"abide by\" and \"commit to\" do not carry this sense of refusing to let go."},{"n":60,"part":"Part 1. Language Use — Questions 41-60 Guided Cloze Test — Guided cloze 2 (51-60)","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":1,"q":"Blank (10): learning is not always ____ by speed.","options":["exceeded","exacerbated","enriched","accelerated"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The passage argues that slower handwriting can <i>deepen understanding</i> while fast typing gives only \"the illusion of learning without much processing\" — so learning is not always <b>enriched</b> (made richer, deeper) by speed. \"Accelerated by speed\" is a tautology (a trap on the surface word <i>speed</i>); \"exacerbated\" (made worse) and \"exceeded\" make no sense here."},{"n":61,"part":"Questions 61-80 Open Cloze Test — Open cloze 1 (61-70)","section":"Open cloze","passageId":2,"q":"Complete blank (1) with ONE most suitable word.","type":"text","accept":["themselves"],"answer":"themselves","explanation":"\"Objects rarely speak for themselves\" — a fixed expression: the objects do not explain their own meaning; the labels and rooms around them do."},{"n":62,"part":"Questions 61-80 Open Cloze Test — Open cloze 1 (61-70)","section":"Open cloze","passageId":2,"q":"Complete blank (2) with ONE most suitable word.","type":"text","accept":["to"],"answer":"to","explanation":"The collocation is \"in relation to\": objects gain meaning in relation to the labels, rooms and routes around them."},{"n":63,"part":"Questions 61-80 Open Cloze Test — Open cloze 1 (61-70)","section":"Open cloze","passageId":2,"q":"Complete blank (3) with ONE most suitable word.","type":"text","accept":["as"],"answer":"as","explanation":"\"presented as evidence of …\" — <b>present something as</b> means to show it in the role of something."},{"n":64,"part":"Questions 61-80 Open Cloze Test — Open cloze 1 (61-70)","section":"Open cloze","passageId":2,"q":"Complete blank (4) with ONE most suitable word.","type":"text","accept":["depending"],"answer":"depending","explanation":"\"depending on the questions curators ask\" — the bowl can be shown as hardship, ingenuity or trade, according to what curators ask."},{"n":65,"part":"Questions 61-80 Open Cloze Test — Open cloze 1 (61-70)","section":"Open cloze","passageId":2,"q":"Complete blank (5) with ONE most suitable word.","type":"text","accept":["contrary"],"answer":"contrary","explanation":"\"On the contrary\" introduces the opposite of what was just denied: it does not make museums unreliable; instead it makes their responsibility clearer."},{"n":66,"part":"Questions 61-80 Open Cloze Test — Open cloze 1 (61-70)","section":"Open cloze","passageId":2,"q":"Complete blank (6) with ONE most suitable word.","type":"text","accept":["display","show","view"],"answer":"display","explanation":"\"What is on display\" (also on show / on view) contrasts with \"what has been left out\"."},{"n":67,"part":"Questions 61-80 Open Cloze Test — Open cloze 1 (61-70)","section":"Open cloze","passageId":2,"q":"Complete blank (7) with ONE most suitable word.","type":"text","accept":["whose"],"answer":"whose","explanation":"A relative pronoun of possession is needed: communities <b>whose</b> histories were once treated as marginal."},{"n":68,"part":"Questions 61-80 Open Cloze Test — Open cloze 1 (61-70)","section":"Open cloze","passageId":2,"q":"Complete blank (8) with ONE most suitable word.","type":"text","accept":["whether"],"answer":"whether","explanation":"\"arguments … over whether a national institution can ever speak for …\" — <b>whether</b> introduces an indirect yes/no question after the preposition \"over\"."},{"n":69,"part":"Questions 61-80 Open Cloze Test — Open cloze 1 (61-70)","section":"Open cloze","passageId":2,"q":"Complete blank (9) with ONE most suitable word.","type":"text","accept":["risks","means"],"answer":"risks","explanation":"A verb is needed for the subject \"A museum that refuses to revise its story\": it <b>risks</b> turning heritage into decoration (verb + -ing)."},{"n":70,"part":"Questions 61-80 Open Cloze Test — Open cloze 1 (61-70)","section":"Open cloze","passageId":2,"q":"Complete blank (10) with ONE most suitable word.","type":"text","accept":["say","voice","part","role","stake"],"answer":"say","explanation":"\"Have a say\" = have the right to take part in deciding something; history becomes a conversation in which visitors also have a say."},{"n":71,"part":"Questions 61-80 Open Cloze Test — Open cloze 2 (71-80)","section":"Open cloze","type":"text","passageId":3,"q":"Complete blank (1) with ONE most suitable word.","accept":["storm"],"answer":"storm","explanation":"Idiom: <b>take (something) by storm</b> = become suddenly and hugely popular; the AI slop swept the internet."},{"n":72,"part":"Questions 61-80 Open Cloze Test — Open cloze 2 (71-80)","section":"Open cloze","type":"text","passageId":3,"q":"Complete blank (2) with ONE most suitable word.","accept":["opera"],"answer":"opera","explanation":"<b>Soap opera</b> = a melodramatic serial about people's relationships; the videos are about fruit \"and their love lives\", later called \"telenovela\"-styled."},{"n":73,"part":"Questions 61-80 Open Cloze Test — Open cloze 2 (71-80)","section":"Open cloze","type":"text","passageId":3,"q":"Complete blank (3) with ONE most suitable word.","accept":["vary","differ"],"answer":"vary","explanation":"\"While specifics may <b>vary</b>, the majority of these clips are…\" — a concession contrasting individual differences with the general pattern."},{"n":74,"part":"Questions 61-80 Open Cloze Test — Open cloze 2 (71-80)","section":"Open cloze","type":"text","passageId":3,"q":"Complete blank (4) with ONE most suitable word.","accept":["subject"],"answer":"subject","explanation":"Collocation: <b>subject matter</b> = the topic or content of a work; the cartoony fruits deal with \"strangely mature\" themes (cheating, struggling to feed children)."},{"n":75,"part":"Questions 61-80 Open Cloze Test — Open cloze 2 (71-80)","section":"Open cloze","type":"text","passageId":3,"q":"Complete blank (5) with ONE most suitable word.","accept":["on"],"answer":"on","explanation":"<b>Cheat on someone</b> = be unfaithful to one's partner; the weeping strawberry discovers her partner cheating on her."},{"n":76,"part":"Questions 61-80 Open Cloze Test — Open cloze 2 (71-80)","section":"Open cloze","type":"text","passageId":3,"q":"Complete blank (6) with ONE most suitable word.","accept":["surface"],"answer":"surface","explanation":"<b>On the surface</b> = when looked at superficially; it contrasts with the \"insidious underbelly\" hidden beneath."},{"n":77,"part":"Questions 61-80 Open Cloze Test — Open cloze 2 (71-80)","section":"Open cloze","type":"text","passageId":3,"q":"Complete blank (7) with ONE most suitable word.","accept":["impact","effect","influence","effects"],"answer":"impact","explanation":"\"its subsequent <b>impact</b> on younger audiences\" — a noun meaning effect, followed by the preposition <b>on</b>."},{"n":78,"part":"Questions 61-80 Open Cloze Test — Open cloze 2 (71-80)","section":"Open cloze","type":"text","passageId":3,"q":"Complete blank (8) with ONE most suitable word.","accept":["content","shows","programmes","programs","cartoons","videos","media","entertainment"],"answer":"content","explanation":"\"the style of <b>content</b> marketed at children, such as by Cocomelon\" — the bright colours and fast pacing copy children's media/shows like Cocomelon."},{"n":79,"part":"Questions 61-80 Open Cloze Test — Open cloze 2 (71-80)","section":"Open cloze","type":"text","passageId":3,"q":"Complete blank (9) with ONE most suitable word.","accept":["which"],"answer":"which","explanation":"A sentential relative clause: \", <b>which</b> multiplies exposure frequency…\" refers back to the whole preceding idea (AI accelerating the output rate)."},{"n":80,"part":"Questions 61-80 Open Cloze Test — Open cloze 2 (71-80)","section":"Open cloze","type":"text","passageId":3,"q":"Complete blank (10) with ONE most suitable word.","accept":["held"],"answer":"held","explanation":"Collocation: <b>be held accountable for</b> = be made responsible for; passive after \"ought to be\"."},{"n":81,"part":"Part 2. Reading — Questions 89-95 Reading Passage 2 (The True Price of a Tree) — gapped paragraphs A-I","section":"Reading","passageId":4,"q":"Seven paragraphs have been removed from Reading Passage 2. Choose from the texts A – I the one which fits each gap. There are TWO extra paragraphs you do not need. Gap (1)____ (Question 89): which paragraph (A–I) fits here?","type":"text","accept":["D","Paragraph D","D."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Paragraph D gives the CEO’s remark “It doesn’t have any value until it’s cut down” and ends with him getting “the opening to carry on”, which leads straight into his next words, “Unless, of course, you tree huggers decide you’ll pay money…”."},{"n":82,"part":"Part 2. Reading — Questions 89-95 Reading Passage 2 (The True Price of a Tree) — gapped paragraphs A-I","section":"Reading","passageId":4,"q":"Seven paragraphs have been removed from Reading Passage 2. Choose from the texts A – I the one which fits each gap. There are TWO extra paragraphs you do not need. Gap (2)____ (Question 90): which paragraph (A–I) fits here?","type":"text","accept":["F","Paragraph F","F."],"answer":"F","explanation":"“What had made me speechless” picks up “I was dumbstruck”, and F concedes that in our economic value system the CEO was right — which the next paragraph contrasts: “But the perspective through which I viewed the forest… was radically different.”"},{"n":83,"part":"Part 2. Reading — Questions 89-95 Reading Passage 2 (The True Price of a Tree) — gapped paragraphs A-I","section":"Reading","passageId":4,"q":"Seven paragraphs have been removed from Reading Passage 2. Choose from the texts A – I the one which fits each gap. There are TWO extra paragraphs you do not need. Gap (3)____ (Question 91): which paragraph (A–I) fits here?","type":"text","accept":["C","Paragraph C","C."],"answer":"C","explanation":"After “trees… are a tiny minority of the lifeforms that comprise the forest”, C lists the other lifeforms (soil microorganisms, plants, lichens, mosses, logs) and the following paragraph sums up: “This is the community that we recognize as a forest.”"},{"n":84,"part":"Part 2. Reading — Questions 89-95 Reading Passage 2 (The True Price of a Tree) — gapped paragraphs A-I","section":"Reading","passageId":4,"q":"Seven paragraphs have been removed from Reading Passage 2. Choose from the texts A – I the one which fits each gap. There are TWO extra paragraphs you do not need. Gap (4)____ (Question 92): which paragraph (A–I) fits here?","type":"text","accept":["H","Paragraph H","H."],"answer":"H","explanation":"H says a “second-growth forest” is really a tree plantation grown like a crop, and the next paragraph follows logically: “Consequently, companies require geneticists… herbicides… insecticides… fertilizers.”"},{"n":85,"part":"Part 2. Reading — Questions 89-95 Reading Passage 2 (The True Price of a Tree) — gapped paragraphs A-I","section":"Reading","passageId":4,"q":"Seven paragraphs have been removed from Reading Passage 2. Choose from the texts A – I the one which fits each gap. There are TWO extra paragraphs you do not need. Gap (5)____ (Question 93): which paragraph (A–I) fits here?","type":"text","accept":["E","Paragraph E","E."],"answer":"E","explanation":"“Foresters rationalize these practices” refers back to the list of plantation practices (herbicides, clear-cuts, heavy machinery) just described, and E’s point that foresters cannot recreate the original forest leads into the goose-that-laid-golden-eggs analogy."},{"n":86,"part":"Part 2. Reading — Questions 89-95 Reading Passage 2 (The True Price of a Tree) — gapped paragraphs A-I","section":"Reading","passageId":4,"q":"Seven paragraphs have been removed from Reading Passage 2. Choose from the texts A – I the one which fits each gap. There are TWO extra paragraphs you do not need. Gap (6)____ (Question 94): which paragraph (A–I) fits here?","type":"text","accept":["A","Paragraph A","A."],"answer":"A","explanation":"After the forest is compared to a goose that could “yield golden eggs indefinitely” but is killed by short-term logging, A offers the alternative — selective logging so trees can be “harvested indefinitely” — before the writer says “Returning to the CEO’s statement…”."},{"n":87,"part":"Part 2. Reading — Questions 89-95 Reading Passage 2 (The True Price of a Tree) — gapped paragraphs A-I","section":"Reading","passageId":4,"q":"Seven paragraphs have been removed from Reading Passage 2. Choose from the texts A – I the one which fits each gap. There are TWO extra paragraphs you do not need. Gap (7)____ (Question 95): which paragraph (A–I) fits here?","type":"text","accept":["G","Paragraph G","G."],"answer":"G","explanation":"“Likewise, the roots of the tree cling to soil…” continues the list of what the tree does (after absorbing CO2 and releasing oxygen), and the next paragraph refers to “All of these natural services performed by that single standing tree.”"},{"n":88,"part":"Part 2. Reading — Questions 96-105 Reading Passage 3 (Language and culture) — True/False/Not Given 96-100, summary completion 101-105","section":"Reading","type":"text","passageId":5,"q":"Do the following statements agree with the information given in the passage? Write <b>TRUE</b> if the statement agrees with the information, <b>FALSE</b> if the statement contradicts the information, <b>NOT GIVEN</b> if there is no information on this: Each person is born with specific cultural traits.","accept":["FALSE","F","False"],"answer":"FALSE","explanation":"Paragraph 1: \"Culture is learned; it is not something that is biologically determined\" — cultural traits are acquired \"as a member of society\", not present at birth."},{"n":89,"part":"Part 2. Reading — Questions 96-105 Reading Passage 3 (Language and culture) — True/False/Not Given 96-100, summary completion 101-105","section":"Reading","type":"text","passageId":5,"q":"Do the following statements agree with the information given in the passage? Write <b>TRUE</b> if the statement agrees with the information, <b>FALSE</b> if the statement contradicts the information, <b>NOT GIVEN</b> if there is no information on this: Certain animals can be referred to with different words in Nuer culture.","accept":["TRUE","T","True"],"answer":"TRUE","explanation":"Paragraph 2: the Nuer \"were cattle herders … so their language has dozens of words for cattle\"."},{"n":90,"part":"Part 2. Reading — Questions 96-105 Reading Passage 3 (Language and culture) — True/False/Not Given 96-100, summary completion 101-105","section":"Reading","type":"text","passageId":5,"q":"Do the following statements agree with the information given in the passage? Write <b>TRUE</b> if the statement agrees with the information, <b>FALSE</b> if the statement contradicts the information, <b>NOT GIVEN</b> if there is no information on this: The Hopi people's expression of time is more limited compared to English speakers.","accept":["TRUE","T","True"],"answer":"TRUE","explanation":"Paragraph 2: English divides time into past, present and future, whereas the Hopi \"only make a distinction\" between what exists/has existed and what does not, and their language \"does not differentiate between present and past\" — fewer distinctions."},{"n":91,"part":"Part 2. Reading — Questions 96-105 Reading Passage 3 (Language and culture) — True/False/Not Given 96-100, summary completion 101-105","section":"Reading","type":"text","passageId":5,"q":"Do the following statements agree with the information given in the passage? Write <b>TRUE</b> if the statement agrees with the information, <b>FALSE</b> if the statement contradicts the information, <b>NOT GIVEN</b> if there is no information on this: A casual exchange from a North Korean may sound unfriendly to a South Korean.","accept":["TRUE","T","True"],"answer":"TRUE","explanation":"Paragraph 3: the North Korean reply \"liupsopneda\" (meaning roughly \"I'm fine, thanks\") \"would mean 'Mind your own business!' to the South Korean\"."},{"n":92,"part":"Part 2. Reading — Questions 96-105 Reading Passage 3 (Language and culture) — True/False/Not Given 96-100, summary completion 101-105","section":"Reading","type":"text","passageId":5,"q":"Do the following statements agree with the information given in the passage? Write <b>TRUE</b> if the statement agrees with the information, <b>FALSE</b> if the statement contradicts the information, <b>NOT GIVEN</b> if there is no information on this: Some Hollywood terms are derived from murder mystery films.","accept":["NOT GIVEN","NG","Not given","Not Given"],"answer":"NOT GIVEN","explanation":"Paragraph 4 gives the film-set term \"Kill the baby!\" as an example of focal vocabulary but says nothing about where such terms come from."},{"n":93,"part":"Part 2. Reading — Questions 96-105 Reading Passage 3 (Language and culture) — True/False/Not Given 96-100, summary completion 101-105","section":"Reading","type":"text","passageId":6,"q":"Complete the summary below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS from the passage for each answer. Blank (1) in the summary <b>Language and culture</b>.","accept":["innate ability","ability"],"answer":"innate ability","explanation":"Paragraph 1: \"our innate ability to acquire language\" — humans are born able to learn language, but the mother tongue can \"only be acquired … as a member of society\"."},{"n":94,"part":"Part 2. Reading — Questions 96-105 Reading Passage 3 (Language and culture) — True/False/Not Given 96-100, summary completion 101-105","section":"Reading","type":"text","passageId":6,"q":"Complete the summary below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS from the passage for each answer. Blank (2) in the summary <b>Language and culture</b>.","accept":["kinship"],"answer":"kinship","explanation":"Paragraph 2: \"the complexity of vocabulary … to describe family relationships varies depending on the role of kinship in that culture\"."},{"n":95,"part":"Part 2. Reading — Questions 96-105 Reading Passage 3 (Language and culture) — True/False/Not Given 96-100, summary completion 101-105","section":"Reading","type":"text","passageId":6,"q":"Complete the summary below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS from the passage for each answer. Blank (3) in the summary <b>Language and culture</b>.","accept":["lexicon"],"answer":"lexicon","explanation":"Paragraph 3: \"new words and expressions are added to the lexicon and meanings of words are altered\"."},{"n":96,"part":"Part 2. Reading — Questions 96-105 Reading Passage 3 (Language and culture) — True/False/Not Given 96-100, summary completion 101-105","section":"Reading","type":"text","passageId":6,"q":"Complete the summary below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS from the passage for each answer. Blank (4) in the summary <b>Language and culture</b>.","accept":["focal vocabulary"],"answer":"focal vocabulary","explanation":"Paragraph 4: groups have their own \"focal vocabulary\", e.g. \"Kill the baby!\" on a film set means turning off a small light, not its literal meaning."},{"n":97,"part":"Part 2. Reading — Questions 96-105 Reading Passage 3 (Language and culture) — True/False/Not Given 96-100, summary completion 101-105","section":"Reading","type":"text","passageId":6,"q":"Complete the summary below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS from the passage for each answer. Blank (5) in the summary <b>Language and culture</b>.","accept":["speech patterns"],"answer":"speech patterns","explanation":"Paragraph 4: people aspiring to higher status take on \"the speech patterns and vocabulary associated … with the higher social status\"."},{"n":98,"part":"Part 3. Writing — Questions 106-115 Word Formation","section":"Word form","passageId":7,"type":"text","q":"closely linked to the youth (1)____ known for challenging social conventions. (CULTURE)","accept":["counterculture","subculture","counter-culture"],"answer":"counterculture","explanation":"A noun is needed after <b>youth</b>: the movement of the 1960s-70s that challenged social conventions is the youth <b>counterculture</b> (a culture opposing the mainstream); <b>subculture</b> is also acceptable."},{"n":99,"part":"Part 3. Writing — Questions 106-115 Word Formation","section":"Word form","passageId":7,"type":"text","q":"vegetarians span all walks of life, far (2)____ the old \"tree-hugging\" stereotypes. (NUMBER)","accept":["outnumbering"],"answer":"outnumbering","explanation":"A present participle is needed to describe <b>vegetarians</b>; they now far <b>outnumber</b> (are more numerous than) the old stereotypes, so <b>outnumbering</b>."},{"n":100,"part":"Part 3. Writing — Questions 106-115 Word Formation","section":"Word form","passageId":7,"type":"text","q":"more people are consciously adopting a (3)____ diet for a host of different compelling reasons. (MEAT)","accept":["meatless","meat-free"],"answer":"meatless","explanation":"An adjective before <b>diet</b> meaning \"without meat\": <b>meatless</b> (suffix -less = without); <b>meat-free</b> also fits."},{"n":101,"part":"Part 3. Writing — Questions 106-115 Word Formation","section":"Word form","passageId":7,"type":"text","q":"avoid its links to chronic (4)____ like heart disease and arthritis. (AIL)","accept":["ailments"],"answer":"ailments","explanation":"A plural noun after <b>chronic</b>, illustrated by heart disease and arthritis: <b>ailments</b> (illnesses)."},{"n":102,"part":"Part 3. Writing — Questions 106-115 Word Formation","section":"Word form","passageId":7,"type":"text","q":"Furthermore, many are (5)____ concerned about the accumulation of chemical treatments and steroids. (JUST)","accept":["justifiably"],"answer":"justifiably","explanation":"An adverb modifying the adjective <b>concerned</b>: <b>justifiably</b> (with good reason)."},{"n":103,"part":"Part 3. Writing — Questions 106-115 Word Formation","section":"Word form","passageId":7,"type":"text","q":"steroids used in intensive industrial farming to make it most (6)____. (PROFIT)","accept":["profitable"],"answer":"profitable","explanation":"After <b>make it most</b> an adjective is needed in the superlative: most <b>profitable</b> (producing the most profit)."},{"n":104,"part":"Part 3. Writing — Questions 106-115 Word Formation","section":"Word form","passageId":7,"type":"text","q":"these substances may trigger serious hormonal (7)____ and behavioral disorders. (BALANCE)","accept":["imbalances","imbalance"],"answer":"imbalances","explanation":"A noun with the negative prefix is needed: the substances cause hormonal <b>imbalances</b> (a lack of balance), parallel to <b>disorders</b>."},{"n":105,"part":"Part 3. Writing — Questions 106-115 Word Formation","section":"Word form","passageId":7,"type":"text","q":"Concurrently, (8)____ awareness of modern agricultural methods has also led to humanitarian concerns. (HIGH)","accept":["heightened"],"answer":"heightened","explanation":"An adjective before <b>awareness</b> meaning \"increased\": <b>heightened</b> (past participle of the verb heighten)."},{"n":106,"part":"Part 3. Writing — Questions 106-115 Word Formation","section":"Word form","passageId":7,"type":"text","q":"they cannot condone the systemic suffering inflicted upon (9)____. (LIVE)","accept":["livestock"],"answer":"livestock","explanation":"A noun for the animals that suffer on farms, exemplified by calves and hens: <b>livestock</b> (farm animals)."},{"n":107,"part":"Part 3. Writing — Questions 106-115 Word Formation","section":"Word form","passageId":7,"type":"text","q":"such as the severe (10)____ of calves to produce veal. (CONFINE)","accept":["confinement"],"answer":"confinement","explanation":"A noun after <b>the severe</b> and before <b>of calves</b>: <b>confinement</b> (keeping animals shut in a small space, as with veal crates)."},{"n":108,"part":"Part 3. Writing — Questions 116-120 Error Correction","section":"Error correction","type":"text","passageId":8,"q":"Line 2: \"a resourceful court official <b>with</b> the name of Ts'ai Lun\" — write the correction.","accept":["by","by the name of","with by","with -> by","with → by"],"answer":"by","explanation":"The fixed idiom for naming someone is <b>by the name of</b>: \"an official by the name of Ts'ai Lun\"; \"with the name of\" is not idiomatic English."},{"n":109,"part":"Part 3. Writing — Questions 116-120 Error Correction","section":"Error correction","type":"text","passageId":8,"q":"Line 5: \"The legends <b>surrounded</b> this invention speak of the inspiration\" — write the correction.","accept":["surrounding","legends surrounding","surrounded surrounding","surrounded -> surrounding","surrounded → surrounding","which surround","that surround","which surrounded","that surrounded"],"answer":"surrounding","explanation":"The finite verb of the sentence is \"speak\", so the modifier must be a reduced relative clause with the present participle: \"the legends <b>surrounding</b> this invention\" (= which surround)."},{"n":110,"part":"Part 3. Writing — Questions 116-120 Error Correction","section":"Error correction","type":"text","passageId":8,"q":"Line 8: \"manufacturing paper in a <b>continuing</b> roll\" — write the correction.","accept":["continuous","continuous roll","continuing continuous","continuing -> continuous","continuing → continuous"],"answer":"continuous","explanation":"A roll of paper with no breaks is a <b>continuous</b> roll (unbroken in space); \"continuing\" means going on in time and does not collocate with \"roll\"."},{"n":111,"part":"Part 3. Writing — Questions 116-120 Error Correction","section":"Error correction","type":"text","passageId":8,"q":"Line 10: \"ranging from fine white sheets <b>and</b> tough brown wrapping paper\" — write the correction.","accept":["to","from to","and to","and -> to","and → to","ranging from to","sheets to tough"],"answer":"to","explanation":"The structure is <b>range from X to Y</b>: \"ranging from fine white sheets <b>to</b> tough brown wrapping paper\"."},{"n":112,"part":"Part 3. Writing — Questions 116-120 Error Correction","section":"Error correction","type":"text","passageId":8,"q":"Line 11: \"these monumental advancements centuries <b>before</b>\" — write the correction.","accept":["ago","centuries ago","before ago","before -> ago","before → ago","earlier","centuries earlier"],"answer":"ago","explanation":"Measured back from the present (\"options today ... wouldn't exist\"), a time in the past is expressed with <b>ago</b>: \"centuries ago\"; \"before\" is used only relative to another past point."},{"n":113,"part":"Part 3. Writing — Questions 121-130 Sentence Transformation","section":"Sentence transformation","type":"text","q":"Complete the second sentence so that it has a similar meaning to the first sentence, using the word given in the brackets. Do NOT make any changes to the word given. You must write from THREE to EIGHT words, including the given word.<br><br>The fraudster was given the maximum prison sentence as a warning to others. (EXAMPLE)<br>→ The fraudster ______ with the maximum prison sentence.","accept":["was made an example of","has been made an example of"],"answer":"was made an example of","explanation":"<b>Make an example of somebody</b> = punish someone as a warning to others; in the passive: \"The fraudster was made an example of with the maximum prison sentence.\""},{"n":114,"part":"Part 3. Writing — Questions 121-130 Sentence Transformation","section":"Sentence transformation","type":"text","q":"Complete the second sentence so that it has a similar meaning to the first sentence, using the word given in the brackets. Do NOT make any changes to the word given. You must write from THREE to EIGHT words, including the given word.<br><br>The children were so tired that they fell asleep almost immediately. (EXHAUSTION)<br>→ Such ______ like a light.","accept":["was the children's exhaustion that they went out","was the children's exhaustion that they were out"],"answer":"was the children's exhaustion that they went out","explanation":"Inversion <b>Such was + noun + that…</b> replaces \"so tired that\"; <b>go out like a light</b> = fall asleep almost immediately."},{"n":115,"part":"Part 3. Writing — Questions 121-130 Sentence Transformation","section":"Sentence transformation","type":"text","q":"Complete the second sentence so that it has a similar meaning to the first sentence, using the word given in the brackets. Do NOT make any changes to the word given. You must write from THREE to EIGHT words, including the given word.<br><br>It seemed to me that they already knew each other. (ACQUAINTED)<br>→ I was under ______.","accept":["the impression that they were already acquainted","the impression they were already acquainted","the impression that they had already been acquainted","the impression that they were acquainted already"],"answer":"the impression that they were already acquainted","explanation":"<b>Be under the impression that…</b> = it seemed to me that…; <b>be acquainted (with each other)</b> = know each other."},{"n":116,"part":"Part 3. Writing — Questions 121-130 Sentence Transformation","section":"Sentence transformation","type":"text","q":"Complete the second sentence so that it has a similar meaning to the first sentence, using the word given in the brackets. Do NOT make any changes to the word given. You must write from THREE to EIGHT words, including the given word.<br><br>Catherine never thought of paying for her medical bills with her children's money. (COVER)<br>→ It never crossed ______ with her children's money.","accept":["Catherine's mind to cover her medical bills","Catherine's mind to cover the medical bills"],"answer":"Catherine's mind to cover her medical bills","explanation":"<b>It never crossed somebody's mind to do something</b> = somebody never thought of doing it; <b>cover the bills</b> = pay for them."},{"n":117,"part":"Part 3. Writing — Questions 121-130 Sentence Transformation","section":"Sentence transformation","type":"text","q":"Complete the second sentence so that it has a similar meaning to the first sentence, using the word given in the brackets. Do NOT make any changes to the word given. You must write from THREE to EIGHT words, including the given word.<br><br>With multiple delays, I fear this project cannot be saved. (LIKELY)<br>→ Having been ______ in the water.","accept":["delayed multiple times, this project is likely dead","delayed many times, this project is likely dead","delayed several times, this project is likely dead","delayed repeatedly, this project is likely dead"],"answer":"delayed multiple times, this project is likely dead","explanation":"Perfect participle clause \"Having been delayed multiple times\" = with multiple delays; <b>dead in the water</b> = having no chance of succeeding, so \"is likely dead in the water\" = I fear it cannot be saved."},{"n":118,"part":"Part 3. Writing — Questions 121-130 Sentence Transformation","section":"Sentence transformation","type":"text","q":"Complete the second sentence so that it has a similar meaning to the first sentence, using the word given in the brackets. Do NOT make any changes to the word given. You must write from THREE to EIGHT words, including the given word.<br><br>It is typical for teenagers to be defiant towards their parents. (REBELLING)<br>→ Teenagers ______ the course.","accept":["rebelling against their parents is par for","rebelling against their parents is just par for","rebelling against their parents is simply par for"],"answer":"rebelling against their parents is par for","explanation":"<b>Be par for the course</b> = be what is typical or expected; \"Teenagers rebelling against their parents\" is the gerund subject meaning teenagers being defiant."},{"n":119,"part":"Part 3. Writing — Questions 121-130 Sentence Transformation","section":"Sentence transformation","type":"text","q":"Complete the second sentence so that it has a similar meaning to the first sentence, using the word given in the brackets. Do NOT make any changes to the word given. You must write from THREE to EIGHT words, including the given word.<br><br>Leon was suspended for one week for breaking the rules repeatedly. (EARNED)<br>→ Repeatedly running ______ suspension.","accept":["afoul of the rules earned Leon a one-week","foul of the rules earned Leon a one-week","afoul of the rules earned Leon a week's","foul of the rules earned Leon a week's"],"answer":"afoul of the rules earned Leon a one-week","explanation":"<b>Run afoul of / run foul of the rules</b> = break the rules; <b>earn somebody a one-week suspension</b> = result in their being suspended for one week."},{"n":120,"part":"Part 3. Writing — Questions 121-130 Sentence Transformation","section":"Sentence transformation","type":"text","q":"Complete the second sentence so that it has a similar meaning to the first sentence, using the word given in the brackets. Do NOT make any changes to the word given. You must write from THREE to EIGHT words, including the given word.<br><br>The construction has not seen much progress since the start of the year. (HEADWAY)<br>→ Little ______ since the start of the year.","accept":["headway has been made in the construction","headway has been made on the construction","headway has been made with the construction","headway has the construction made"],"answer":"headway has been made in the construction","explanation":"<b>Make headway</b> = make progress; \"Little headway has been made in the construction\" = the construction has not seen much progress."},{"n":121,"part":"Part 3. Writing — Questions 121-130 Sentence Transformation","section":"Sentence transformation","type":"text","q":"Complete the second sentence so that it has a similar meaning to the first sentence, using the word given in the brackets. Do NOT make any changes to the word given. You must write from THREE to EIGHT words, including the given word.<br><br>Americans are disappointed with the fact that their taxes are paying for foreign wars. (DISMAY)<br>→ Much to the Americans' ______ the bill for foreign wars.","accept":["dismay, their taxes are footing","dismay, their taxes foot","dismay, their taxes are paying","dismay, their taxes pay"],"answer":"dismay, their taxes are footing","explanation":"<b>Much to somebody's dismay</b> = to their disappointment; <b>foot the bill for something</b> = pay for it."},{"n":122,"part":"Part 3. Writing — Questions 121-130 Sentence Transformation","section":"Sentence transformation","type":"text","q":"Complete the second sentence so that it has a similar meaning to the first sentence, using the word given in the brackets. Do NOT make any changes to the word given. You must write from THREE to EIGHT words, including the given word.<br><br>Plastic surgery changed Jim's looks to the point that we do not recognize him anymore. (RECOGNITION)<br>→ Jim's looks ______ to plastic surgery.","accept":["have changed beyond recognition due","have changed beyond recognition thanks","have changed beyond recognition owing","changed beyond recognition due","changed beyond recognition thanks","changed beyond recognition owing","have been changed beyond recognition due","were changed beyond recognition due","have altered beyond recognition due","have been altered beyond recognition due"],"answer":"have changed beyond recognition due","explanation":"<b>Change beyond recognition</b> = change so much that one can no longer be recognised; <b>due to / thanks to / owing to</b> plastic surgery gives the cause."}],"omitted":["không sao chép được: Part 2. Reading — Questions 81-88 Reading Passage 1 (A librarian reflects on the value of libraries) (8 câu)"]},{"id":"ptnk-2025-kc","year":2025,"track":"kc","title":"PTNK 2025 · Tiếng Anh Không chuyên","subtitle":"Đề thi tuyển sinh lớp 10 · Trường Phổ thông Năng khiếu · 2025-2026","durationMin":60,"keySource":"solved","source":"2025 - Tiếng Anh (Không Chuyên) - Đề thi.pdf","passages":["1. The entire population of Gaza is facing acute food insecurity, with nearly half a million people now suffering catastrophic hunger, according to a new report from the Integrated Food Security Phase Classification (IPC). Since all humanitarian aid was blocked on 2 March, malnutrition and hunger have worsened dramatically. The IPC estimates that 71,000 children and over 17,000 mothers urgently need treatment for acute malnutrition, a sharp rise from earlier in the year. Without immediate intervention, food insecurity, malnutrition, and mortality levels are expected to exceed famine thresholds in the coming months.<br><br>2. The crisis is being fueled by ongoing conflict, a complete blockade, and the total closure of border crossings, which have now been shut for over two months, the longest in Gaza's history. Food prices in local markets have soared to unaffordable levels, leaving even basic items out of reach for most families. Despite this, more than 116,000 metric tons of food aid, enough to feed one million people for four months, remain stuck at aid corridors, along with hundreds of pallets of life-saving nutrition treatments.<br><br>3. UN agencies like the World Food Programme (WFP) and UNICEF continue to sound the alarm. <b>[I]</b> \"Families in Gaza are starving while food sits at the border,\" said WFP Executive Director Cindy McCain. <b>[II]</b> \"If we wait until famine is declared, it will be too late for many.\" <b>[III]</b> Both leaders stress that this crisis could have been prevented and call on all parties to allow immediate access for humanitarian aid. <b>[IV]</b><br><br>4. The situation is especially dire for children. Seventeen UN agencies and NGOs confirm that extreme food deprivation is widespread among Gaza's youngest residents. Combined with the collapse of health services and lack of clean water, this has triggered rapid increases in acute malnutrition, particularly in the North Gaza, Gaza, and Rafah governorates. UNICEF warns that its remaining supplies for treating malnutrition are critically low, and WFP has already exhausted its food stocks. All 25 WFP-supported bakeries closed weeks ago, and food parcels and meal support programs have ended.<br><br>5. Despite these <b><i>grim</i></b> conditions, aid agencies remain ready to act. Both WFP and UNICEF are on the ground, prepared to deliver life-saving assistance as soon as borders reopen. <b>They</b> urge all parties to prioritize the needs of civilians and respect international humanitarian law, stressing that urgent action is the only way to prevent a full-scale famine in Gaza.","Being healthy isn't just about eating vegetables or going to the gym. True health includes your body, your mind, and your relationships. For students, understanding health in a (29)____ way can help you manage stress, stay focused, and feel better overall. It's not about being perfect. It's about making choices that support your well-being every day.<br><br>Physical health means giving your body what it needs to function well. That includes eating a (30)____ diet, drinking enough water, staying active, and getting 8–10 hours of sleep each night. You don't need to follow strict routines. Just find ways to move your body and rest regularly. Treating your body with care helps you stay energized, concentrate in class, and avoid burnout.<br><br>Mental and emotional health is just as important. School can be stressful, and it's normal to feel anxious or down at times. Learning how to manage those emotions, (31)____ by talking to someone, journaling, or simply taking a break, is a key part of staying healthy. If things ever feel too overwhelming, asking for help is not a weakness but a sign of strength.<br><br>Finally, social health involves your relationships. Having people you trust and feel safe with can (32)____ a big difference. Healthy communication, respect, and support help you grow emotionally and feel connected.<br><br>Being healthy means caring for your whole self: body, mind, and heart, so you can live meaningfully and confidently, both at school, at home, or anywhere else."],"questions":[{"n":1,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Phonetics","type":"mcq","q":"Choose the word whose underlined part pronounces differently from the other three.","options":["skipp<u>ed</u>","liv<u>ed</u>","tick<u>ed</u>","laugh<u>ed</u>"],"correct":1,"explanation":"\"lived\" ends in the voiced /v/, so -ed is /d/; after the voiceless /p/, /k/ and /f/ of skipped, ticked and laughed it is /t/."},{"n":2,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Phonetics","type":"mcq","q":"Choose the word whose underlined part pronounces differently from the other three.","options":["thr<u>ough</u>","alth<u>ough</u>","thor<u>ough</u>","borr<u>ow</u>"],"correct":0,"explanation":"\"through\" is /θruː/ with the /uː/ sound; although, thorough and borrow all end in the /əʊ/ (or unstressed /ə/–/oʊ/) vowel."},{"n":3,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Stress","type":"mcq","q":"Choose the word whose primary stress position differs from the other three.","options":["disaster","important","volunteer","electric"],"correct":2,"explanation":"vo-lun-TEER is stressed on the third syllable; di-SAS-ter, im-POR-tant and e-LEC-tric on the second."},{"n":4,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Stress","type":"mcq","q":"Choose the word whose primary stress position differs from the other three.","options":["manifest","theory","kilogram","disbelief"],"correct":3,"explanation":"dis-be-LIEF is stressed on the last syllable; MA-ni-fest, THE-o-ry and KI-lo-gram on the first."},{"n":5,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Tam: 'Hey Huy! I just came back from the Philippines. We were on vacation.'<br>Huy: '______'","options":["Have a wonderful trip!","Tell me all about it!","And what about it?","Glad you enjoyed it."],"correct":1,"explanation":"Tam has just returned from a trip, so the natural reply is to ask to hear about it; \"Have a wonderful trip\" is said before a trip and \"Glad you enjoyed it\" needs Tam to have said she enjoyed it."},{"n":6,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"_____ every student in my university class is Vietnamese, but there are also a few students from Laos.","options":["Almost","Most of","Mostly","The most of"],"correct":0,"explanation":"\"Almost every student\" — \"almost\" modifies \"every\"; \"most of\" would need \"the students\", not \"every student\"."},{"n":7,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Because the show _____ at 4:30 p.m, we have about one hour to get to the theater.","options":["is going to start","will start","will have started","starts"],"correct":3,"explanation":"A fixed timetable or scheduled event (a show at 4:30) takes the present simple, and time clauses with \"because\" of a schedule do not use \"will\"."},{"n":8,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"The police is talking to my neighbors, none of _____ could give them any useful information.","options":["which","that","who","whom"],"correct":3,"explanation":"After a preposition (\"none of\") the relative pronoun for people must be the object form \"whom\"."},{"n":9,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"It's a good sign that your daughter _____ her friends at the new school.","options":["takes after","looks down upon","comes across","gets on with"],"correct":3,"explanation":"\"get on with somebody\" = have a friendly relationship with; that is the \"good sign\" at a new school."},{"n":10,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"He can't legally drive without a valid driving _____.","options":["degree","license","permit","certificate"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The fixed collocation is \"driving license\" (the official document allowing you to drive)."},{"n":11,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"A friend of _____ asked to share my apartment, but I don't think we are close enough to do that.","options":["mine","I","me","myself"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The double-possessive pattern is \"a friend of mine\" — \"of\" + possessive pronoun."},{"n":12,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Mr. White _____ something in the kitchen because it smells great.","options":["should cook","must be cooking","must cook","should be cooking"],"correct":1,"explanation":"\"must be + V-ing\" expresses a confident deduction about something happening right now — the smell is the evidence."},{"n":13,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Marie Curie is one of _____ female scientists who have won the Nobel Prize in science.","options":["a few","the few","the fewest","few"],"correct":1,"explanation":"\"one of the few + plural noun + who…\" refers to a specific small group defined by the relative clause, so the definite article is needed."},{"n":14,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"My grandfather forgets _____ to Tasmania in his youth. He has no memory of it!","options":["to being","to be","being","having been"],"correct":3,"explanation":"\"forget + V-ing\" refers to a past experience one no longer remembers, and \"have been to a place\" = have visited it, so the perfect gerund \"having been\" is required."},{"n":15,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Some students often learn long definitions by _____ without truly understanding what they mean.","options":["rote","heart","word","parrot"],"correct":0,"explanation":"\"learn by rote\" = memorise through repetition without understanding — exactly what \"without truly understanding what they mean\" describes."},{"n":16,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Error correction","type":"mcq","q":"His family wanted (A) to know where (B) was he last night (C) when they could not reach him (D) by phone.","options":["to know","was he","when","by"],"correct":1,"explanation":"In an indirect question the word order is subject + verb: \"where he was last night\", not \"where was he\"."},{"n":17,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Error correction","type":"mcq","q":"Molly speaks (A) so good Mandarin (B) that some people (C) have mistaken her (D) for a native speaker.","options":["so","that","have mistaken","for"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Before an adjective + noun (\"good Mandarin\") the pattern is \"such … that\", not \"so … that\": \"speaks such good Mandarin that…\"."},{"n":18,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Error correction","type":"mcq","q":"If Arthur (A) had arrived (B) at the theater sooner, he (C) would catch his girlfriend (D) flirting with her fellow cast members.","options":["had arrived","at","would catch","flirting"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Third conditional: \"If + past perfect, would have + past participle\" — \"he would have caught his girlfriend\"."},{"n":19,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Error correction","type":"mcq","q":"The man admitted to (A) steal the luxurious watch, (B) but he denied (C) knowing anything (D) related to the kidnapping.","options":["steal","but","knowing","related"],"correct":0,"explanation":"\"admit (to) + V-ing\": \"admitted to stealing the luxurious watch\"."},{"n":20,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Error correction","type":"mcq","q":"Aurora borealis, or the Northern Lights, (A) is a beautiful natural phenomenon (B) occurring in some Northern countries, (C) that many people want to experience at least once in (D) their lifetime.","options":["is","occurring","that","their"],"correct":2,"explanation":"A non-defining relative clause after a comma cannot use \"that\"; it must be \"which many people want to experience\"."},{"n":21,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Reading","type":"mcq","passageId":0,"q":"What is the main idea of the passage?","options":["Gaza's infrastructure has been completely destroyed after the conflict.","Food prices in Gaza can be controlled with proper measures.","Aid agencies are withdrawing from Gaza due to ongoing conflict.","Gaza is on the brink of famine due to conflict and blockade."],"correct":3,"explanation":"Every paragraph builds the same point: hunger is \"expected to exceed famine thresholds\", the crisis \"is being fueled by ongoing conflict, a complete blockade\", and urgent action is needed \"to prevent a full-scale famine\"."},{"n":22,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Reading","type":"mcq","passageId":0,"q":"What is <b>TRUE</b> according to <b>paragraph 1</b>?","options":["The IPC suggests relocating people from Gaza to receive aid.","It might take over a year for the situation in Gaza to become critical.","There has been a clear increase in malnourished people in Gaza.","People in Gaza still managed to receive some aid after March."],"correct":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 1: \"71,000 children and over 17,000 mothers urgently need treatment for acute malnutrition, a sharp rise from earlier in the year\"; aid has been fully blocked since 2 March, and famine is expected \"in the coming months\", not a year away."},{"n":23,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Reading","type":"mcq","passageId":0,"q":"What is the main idea of <b>paragraph 2</b>?","options":["Aid for Gaza is ready, but the people cannot receive it.","Most people in Gaza find market items too expensive.","Multiple factors are worsening the food situation in Gaza.","Gaza has started producing its own food to reduce dependence on aid."],"correct":2,"explanation":"The topic sentence lists several causes — \"ongoing conflict, a complete blockade, and the total closure of border crossings\" — and the paragraph then adds soaring prices and aid stuck at corridors; B and A are only single details."},{"n":24,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Reading","type":"mcq","passageId":0,"q":"It can be inferred from <b>paragraph 3</b> that …","options":["Aid agencies are exaggerating the crisis to gain attention.","Gaza's situation was unpredictable and took everyone by surprise.","UN agencies are not doing enough to aid the people of Gaza.","If humanitarian access is granted, the crisis can be averted."],"correct":3,"explanation":"The leaders \"stress that this crisis could have been prevented and call on all parties to allow immediate access for humanitarian aid\" — i.e. access would avert it; \"could have been prevented\" also rules out B."},{"n":25,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Reading","type":"mcq","passageId":0,"q":"The word '<b>They</b>' in <b>paragraph 5</b> refers to …","options":["WFP and UNICEF","Border control agencies","IPC","Civilians in Gaza"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The preceding sentence is \"Both WFP and UNICEF are on the ground, prepared to deliver life-saving assistance\" — they are the ones who \"urge all parties\"."},{"n":26,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Reading","type":"mcq","passageId":0,"q":"What is <b>NOT TRUE</b> according to <b>paragraph 4</b>?","options":["WFP's food parcels and meal programs have ended.","Malnutrition is especially a problem in North Gaza, Gaza City, and Rafah.","Children in Gaza are particularly at risk of malnutrition.","There are no more UNICEF supplies for treating malnutrition."],"correct":3,"explanation":"UNICEF's supplies are \"critically low\", not gone — it is WFP that \"has already exhausted its food stocks\"; A, B and C are all stated in the paragraph."},{"n":27,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Reading","type":"mcq","passageId":0,"q":"What word is <b><i>CLOSEST</i></b> in meaning to '<b>grim</b>' in <b>paragraph 5</b>?","options":["interesting","horrible","potential","surprising"],"correct":1,"explanation":"\"grim conditions\" are very bad, bleak conditions — the famine, exhausted stocks and closed bakeries just described — so \"horrible\" is the closest."},{"n":28,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Reading","type":"mcq","passageId":0,"q":"In which place in <b>paragraph 3</b> can the following sentence best fit?<br><i>UNICEF Executive Director Catherine Russell added, \"Famine doesn't arrive suddenly; it grows in places where food is blocked, health systems collapse, and children are left with nothing.\"</i>","options":["[II]","[I]","[III]","[IV]"],"correct":2,"explanation":"\"added\" means Russell speaks after McCain's two quoted sentences, and she must come before \"Both leaders stress…\", which needs two leaders already quoted — so [III]."},{"n":29,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":1,"q":"(29)","options":["well-made","well-developed","well-known","well-rounded"],"correct":3,"explanation":"\"understanding health in a well-rounded way\" = considering all its sides (body, mind, relationships), as the passage goes on to do."},{"n":30,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":1,"q":"(30)","options":["imbalance","balanced","balancing","balance"],"correct":1,"explanation":"An adjective is needed before \"diet\", and the fixed collocation is \"a balanced diet\"."},{"n":31,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":1,"q":"(31)","options":["either","if","or","whether"],"correct":3,"explanation":"\"whether … or …\" introduces the list of alternative ways (talking, journaling, or taking a break); \"either\" pairs only two items, and \"if\"/\"or\" cannot open the phrase."},{"n":32,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":1,"q":"(32)","options":["form","make","get","cause"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The fixed collocation is \"make a (big) difference\"."},{"n":33,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":1,"q":"What is the most suitable title for the passage?","options":["How to perform better at schools","Being healthy as students","The best diet for students","Unhealthy habits to avoid for students"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The passage is addressed to students and covers all three sides of health — physical, mental and social — so \"Being healthy as students\" fits; diet and school performance are only details."},{"n":34,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":1,"q":"According to the text, what is <b>TRUE</b> about physical health?","options":["You have to follow strict exercise routines.","Diet is the most important component.","Being active is not as important as getting rest.","It can help you perform better in class."],"correct":3,"explanation":"\"Treating your body with care helps you stay energized, concentrate in class, and avoid burnout\"; the text explicitly says \"You don't need to follow strict routines\" and ranks no component above the others."},{"n":35,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":1,"q":"What is <b>NOT TRUE</b> about mental health?","options":["As students, it's normal to not feel good sometimes.","There are many ways to approach mental self-care.","Strong people do not ask for help.","Managing your emotions is an important part of staying healthy."],"correct":2,"explanation":"The text says the opposite: \"asking for help is not a weakness but a sign of strength\"; the other three are stated in paragraph 3."},{"n":36,"part":"PHẦN 2. TỰ LUẬN","section":"Word form","type":"text","q":"We live in a ______ society where people buy things they don't need just to impress others or feel good about themselves. (CONSUMER)","accept":["consumerist","consumer"],"answer":"consumerist","explanation":"An adjective describing a society obsessed with buying: \"a consumerist society\" (\"consumer society\" is also a recognised phrase)."},{"n":37,"part":"PHẦN 2. TỰ LUẬN","section":"Word form","type":"text","q":"Ariana Grande's voice is so ______ that you can recognize her singing instantly. (DISTINCT)","accept":["distinctive"],"answer":"distinctive","explanation":"\"distinctive\" = having a quality that makes something easy to recognise — the clue is \"you can recognize her singing instantly\"."},{"n":38,"part":"PHẦN 2. TỰ LUẬN","section":"Word form","type":"text","q":"Scientists discovered ______ cave paintings that are believed to be over 10,000 years old. (HISTORY)","accept":["prehistoric"],"answer":"prehistoric","explanation":"Cave paintings over 10,000 years old date from before written history, so the adjective is \"prehistoric\"."},{"n":39,"part":"PHẦN 2. TỰ LUẬN","section":"Word form","type":"text","q":"My ______ family taught me a lot about Thai customs and cuisine during my exchange program. (HOME)","accept":["homestay"],"answer":"homestay","explanation":"On an exchange program you live with a \"homestay family\" — the local family that hosts you."},{"n":40,"part":"PHẦN 2. TỰ LUẬN","section":"Word form","type":"text","q":"She has a ______ fear of failure and it makes her nervous before every exam. (ROOT)","accept":["deep-rooted","deeply rooted","deeply-rooted","deep rooted"],"answer":"deep-rooted","explanation":"A compound adjective before \"fear\": \"a deep-rooted fear\" = one that is firmly established and hard to remove."},{"n":41,"part":"PHẦN 2. TỰ LUẬN","section":"Word form","type":"text","q":"We prefer learning science through ______ experiments rather than through theories. (HAND)","accept":["hands-on","hands on"],"answer":"hands-on","explanation":"\"hands-on experiments\" = practical, doing things yourself — contrasted with \"theories\"."},{"n":42,"part":"PHẦN 2. TỰ LUẬN","section":"Word form","type":"text","q":"In ______ to free daily guided tours, the museum also offers audio guides for visitors who want to explore by themselves. (ADD)","accept":["addition"],"answer":"addition","explanation":"The fixed phrase \"in addition to\" = as well as; the noun form of ADD is needed after \"In\"."},{"n":43,"part":"PHẦN 2. TỰ LUẬN","section":"Word form","type":"text","q":"Jack is terrified of ______, so he is always busying himself with work. (BORE)","accept":["boredom"],"answer":"boredom","explanation":"A noun is needed after \"terrified of\": \"boredom\" — the state of being bored, which he avoids by keeping busy."},{"n":44,"part":"PHẦN 2. TỰ LUẬN","section":"Sentence transformation","type":"text","q":"It was not wise of you to use the word before checking its definition. (LOOKED)<br>→ You ______ to using it.","accept":["should have looked up its definition prior","should have looked its definition up prior","should have looked up the definition prior","should have looked up the word's definition prior","should have looked up its meaning prior","should have looked the word up prior","should have looked up the word prior"],"answer":"should have looked up its definition prior","explanation":"\"should have + past participle\" criticises a past action, \"look up\" = check in a dictionary, and \"prior to + V-ing\" is needed because the sentence ends in \"to using it\"."},{"n":45,"part":"PHẦN 2. TỰ LUẬN","section":"Sentence transformation","type":"text","q":"No other student studied as hard as the competition winner. (WON)<br>→ The student ______ hardest.","accept":["who won the competition studied the","who won the competition studied","that won the competition studied the","that won the competition studied"],"answer":"who won the competition studied the","explanation":"\"No other … as hard as X\" = X studied (the) hardest; \"the competition winner\" becomes the relative clause \"the student who won the competition\"."},{"n":46,"part":"PHẦN 2. TỰ LUẬN","section":"Sentence transformation","type":"text","q":"Sherlock Holmes managed to catch the murderer even with little evidence. (STOP)<br>→ The lack ______ catching the murderer.","accept":["of evidence did not stop Sherlock Holmes from","of evidence didn't stop Sherlock Holmes from","of evidence did not stop Holmes from","of evidence didn't stop Holmes from","of evidence did not stop him from","of evidence didn't stop him from","of evidence could not stop Sherlock Holmes from","of evidence couldn't stop Sherlock Holmes from"],"answer":"of evidence did not stop Sherlock Holmes from","explanation":"\"stop somebody from + V-ing\": the lack of evidence did not stop Sherlock Holmes from catching the murderer."},{"n":47,"part":"PHẦN 2. TỰ LUẬN","section":"Sentence transformation","type":"text","q":"Our teacher said, \"Your critical thinking will improve if you read nonfiction books.\" (BETTER)<br>→ Our teacher recommended ______.","accept":["reading nonfiction books to better our critical thinking","reading nonfiction books to better your critical thinking","reading nonfiction books to better critical thinking","reading nonfiction books for better critical thinking","reading nonfiction books for our better critical thinking"],"answer":"reading nonfiction books to better our critical thinking","explanation":"\"recommend + V-ing\", and \"better\" is used as a verb meaning \"improve\": reading nonfiction books to better our critical thinking."},{"n":48,"part":"PHẦN 2. TỰ LUẬN","section":"Sentence transformation","type":"text","q":"Jerry was punished by his teacher for bullying his friends. (RECEIVED)<br>→ Since Jerry ______ from the teacher.","accept":["bullied his friends, he received punishment","bullied his friends, he received a punishment","had bullied his friends, he received punishment","had bullied his friends, he received a punishment","was bullying his friends, he received punishment","was bullying his friends, he received a punishment"],"answer":"bullied his friends, he received punishment","explanation":"\"Since\" here means \"because\": the reason (bullying his friends) comes first, and the passive \"was punished\" becomes \"received (a) punishment from the teacher\"."},{"n":49,"part":"PHẦN 2. TỰ LUẬN","section":"Sentence transformation","type":"text","q":"\"I'll visit your mother next week, for sure.\" Sammy said. (PAY)<br>→ Sammy promised ______ week.","accept":["to pay my mother a visit the following","to pay my mother a visit the next","to pay my mother a visit next","to pay a visit to my mother next"],"answer":"to pay my mother a visit the following","explanation":"\"promise + to-infinitive\", \"pay somebody a visit\" = visit somebody, and in reported speech \"next week\" becomes \"the following week\"."},{"n":50,"part":"PHẦN 2. TỰ LUẬN","section":"Sentence transformation","type":"text","q":"I regret revising for the test last minute. (ONLY)<br>→ ______ earlier.","accept":["If only I had revised for the test","If only I'd revised for the test","If only I had revised for it","If only I'd revised for it"],"answer":"If only I had revised for the test","explanation":"\"If only + past perfect\" expresses regret about the past: If only I had revised for the test earlier."}]},{"id":"ptnk-2025-chuyen","year":2025,"track":"chuyen","title":"PTNK 2025 · Tiếng Anh Chuyên","subtitle":"Đề thi tuyển sinh lớp 10 · Trường Phổ thông Năng khiếu · 2025-2026","durationMin":120,"keySource":"solved","source":"2025 - Tiếng Anh (Chuyên) - Đề thi.pdf","passages":["<i>Black Mirror</i>, created by British writer and satirist Charlie Brooker, is a critically (41)____ anthology series that examines the (42)____ of technology, society, and human psychology. First (43)____ in 2011, the show quickly gained (44)____ for its hauntingly plausible depictions of near-future worlds dominated by technology. Brooker has described the series as inspired by <i>The Twilight Zone</i>, but reframed for a digital age, with each episode serving as a (45)____ exploration of how modern innovations might twist human behavior in unexpected ways. The title <i>Black Mirror</i> refers to the reflective surface of a (46)____ screen – phones, televisions, tablets – symbolizing both a literal object and a metaphor for introspection in the age of technology. (47)____ offering grand dystopias, the series often focuses on intimate stories: a woman resurrecting her partner through AI, or a social credit system that dictates a person's rights. The tone is contemplative, with a narrative rhythm that (48)____ stillness and reflection over sensationalism. Its cultural significance lies in the way it (49)____ invisible anxieties visible, (50)____ the cost of progress without rejecting the values of technology. Through minimalism and emotional depth, each episode offers viewers a quiet meditation on a possible tomorrow.","The Mohana fishermen of central Pakistan are a living link with some of the earliest moments of human history. (51)____ from change by the inhospitability of their environment, they live a life which has (52)____ altered in five millennia. Some call them Noah's children.<br><br>They live in floating villages on the (53)____ of the Indus, in the flood plain of Sind, where for 200 miles the river cuts through what is (54)____ desert wasteland. Few other living creatures survive there, (55)____ the climate is harsh and unpredictable. The river, up to three miles wide at points, is too treacherous for (56)____ the Mohana to navigate.<br><br>They live as they (57)____, each person's role rigidly predetermined. There are three castes, based on the three traditional river trades: fishing, boat building and ferrying. The fishermen, or <i>shikari</i>, are the most unusual. They fish the shallow waters at the edge of the river, where the alluvial currents make it difficult to locate fish.<br><br>The solution is to use (58)____ herons. Endowed with oils on the surface of their eyes which act as colour filters, these birds can (59)____ the tiniest movements in the murky waters of the water and (60)____ detect the fish below, which they then dive for and catch. For the <i>shikari</i>, these herons mean the difference between life and death.","African American newspapers in the 1930s faced many hardships. For (61)____, knowing that buyers of African American papers also bought general-circulation papers, advertisers of consumer products often ignored African American publications. On the positive side, advertisers' discrimination (62)____ free the African American press (63)____ advertiser domination since editors could print (64)____ charged material more readily than (65)____ the large national dailies, which (66)____ on advertisers' ideological approval to secure revenues. Unfortunately, it also made the selling price of Black papers much higher than (67)____ of general-circulation dailies. Often as (68)____ as two-thirds of publication costs had to come from subscribers or subsidies from community politicians and other interest (69)____. And (70)____ their editorial freedom, African American publishers often felt compelled to print a disproportionate amount of sensationalism, sports, and society news to boost circulation.","Historians have long recognized the traditional Japanese sword, or <i>nihonto</i>, as one of the finest cutting weapons (71)____ produced, but it has even been (72)____ a spiritual entity. The <i>adage</i> \"the sword is the soul of the samurai\" reflects the sword's psychic importance, not (73)____ to its wielder, but also to its creator, the master smith. Though (74)____ classically regarded as artists, master smiths nevertheless exerted great care in the process of creating swords, no two of (75)____ were forged exactly the same way. Over hundreds of hours, two types of steel were repeatedly heated, hammered, and folded together (76)____ thousands of imperceptible layers, yielding (77)____ a razor-sharp, durable edge and a flexible, shock-absorbing blade. Commonly, though optionally, the smith physically \"signed\" the blade using their secret forging (78)____ to leave an idiosyncratic structural signature. (79)____ unique finished product reflected the smith's personal honor and devotion (80)____ the craft, and today, the Japanese sword is valued for its artistic merit as well as historical significance.","<b>The Science Behind 'Cute Aggression'</b><br><i>Your urge to 'gobble up those chubby cheeks' has an evolutionary purpose.</i><br><br><b>A.</b> If you've ever had the desire to hug a cute baby or animal <i>super tight</i>, you're part of the approximately 50% of people who experience what psychologists call \"cute aggression.\" A physically enthusiastic response to a chubby one-year-old or a new puppy is just one example of what psychologists call a \"dimorphous expression,\" or an incongruent display of emotion. Without proper context, these urges might seem puzzling, but recent studies show they not only make sense psychologically, but may also serve as an important protective mechanism.<br><br><b>B.</b> Oriana Aragon, PhD, assistant professor of psychology at Clemson University, became interested in the scientific underpinnings of cute aggression in 2011 after she saw model Leslie Bibb say on Conan O'Brien that a puppy was \"so cute she wanted to bash it.\" Aragon, at the time a graduate student at Yale University studying social psychology, called her dad to tell him about Bibb's aggressive response to cuteness. He asked a question that set years of future research into motion: Really, how different is wanting to bash a puppy than grandpa squeezing the baby's cheeks or grandma saying she wants to eat you up? \"That's when it clicked,\" Aragon says. \"We do all these weird things, like crying when we're happy or acting aggressively when we think something's cute.\"<br><br><b>C.</b> Aragon began researching dimorphous expression in 2015, testing the hypothesis that people grimace or weep during happy moments instinctively to regulate positive emotions that overwhelm them. Since feeling emotionally and physically overwhelmed isn't sustainable, researchers have long presumed that the brain may respond with a seemingly contradictory expression to 'balance' out or shift the individual away from the other, all-encompassing emotion. The sudden desire to squeeze the baby could help regulate that sense of overwhelming cuteness, essentially bringing you down from the \"high\".<br><br><b>D.</b> In a separate study in 2018, Aragon found another byproduct of dimorphous responses: something called \"motivational orientation,\" which is our unconscious way of showing not just our emotions, but our intentions to the people around us. Expressing and understanding intent is important because it can help us coordinate actions toward people around us. For example, if you see a cute baby and respond with clenched fists and a quick approach, a mother may perceive you as overly excited and decide that this hyped-up interaction might be too much for her baby. She can then set a boundary by holding the baby herself. \"Knowing someone's motivation helps the mom engage with the approacher's orientation,\" says Aragon.<br><br><b>E.</b> Both Aragon and Stavroupoulous believe cute aggression and other expressions that don't necessarily match the scenario at hand may have important social and evolutionary implications. \"When you think about small social groups and how they used to share caretaking of infants, it makes sense that we have these really distinct signals about how we are going to treat someone's baby,\" she says. Aragon also says it's possible our variety of reactions when we encounter a baby could teach babies about the nuance of emotional expression. \"Babies are always decoding facial expressions, trying to map which expressions go in which situations,\" she says. \"Receiving a variety of expressions in a variety of contexts could teach them that these expressions can occur even in positive situations.\"<br><br><b>F.</b> Stavropoulous thinks there could be a more evolutionarily significant purpose behind the desire to squeeze a baby. Researchers found in a 2009 study that women who viewed images of cute babies exhibited more careful, slow, and deliberate behavior afterward, which Stavropoulous says could build on the idea that cute aggression is an adaptive mechanism to activate caretaking behaviors, which ultimately helps babies survive. \"Maybe this feeling of 'I want to squeeze it' serves to remind us that this baby or animal is super fragile, and we are big adults, so we actually need to move carefully,\" Stavropoulous says. \"It might be an odd but effective way to remind us that you could squish this, so don't do it.\"","<b>Why we find human-like robots and dolls so creepy</b><br><i>It seems obvious that the more human robots are, the more familiar we find them. But it's only true up to a point – then we find them disturbing</i><br><br>The \"uncanny valley\" is a characteristic dip in emotional response that happens when we encounter an entity that is almost, but not quite, human. It was first hypothesised in 1970 by the Japanese roboticist Masahiro Mori, who identified that as robots became more human-like, people would find them to be more acceptable and appealing than their mechanical counterparts. But this only held true to up a point. When they were close to, but not quite, human, people developed a sense of discomfort. If human likeness increased beyond this point, and they became very close to human, the emotional response returned to being positive. It is this distinctive dip in the relationship between human likeness and emotional response that is called the \"uncanny valley\".<br><br>Anything with a highly human-like appearance can be subject to this peculiar effect, but common examples are androids, computer game characters and life-like dolls. However, not all near-human robots are eerie, and the perception of eeriness varies from person to person. So what evidence exists for the effect and what properties of near-humans might make us feel so uncomfortable?<br><br>Since 1970, the uncanny valley effect has been explored from many perspectives, from the practical interest of roboticists to theoretical approaches from philosophers and experimental studies conducted by psychologists. Research started in earnest from 2005, when Karl MacDorman and Takashi Minato translated Mori's original paper into English. Interest in the area has since expanded quickly: so far, 510 academic papers reference the effect in 2015, compared to just 35 in 2004. Regardless, while the effect is easy to describe, and feels intuitively \"right\", it is notoriously difficult to research such a subjective concept. Academics are even engaged in an active debate as to whether the uncanny valley exists at all – Jari Kätsyri and colleagues recently reviewed the evidence and drew the conclusion that the effect remains hard to pin down.<br><br>Either way, the debate has not yet been won – although perhaps the most compelling evidence for the existence of the uncanny valley has just been published. Maya Mathur and David Reichling studied 80 real-world robots and found a clear valley effect in how much people liked and were willing to trust them. Their data showed the classic rise-dip-rise that Mori originally predicted. This was found with the original robots and also with CGI images built to systematically vary in human likeness. The challenge now is to explain just why this might be happening. At the moment, there are three theories that seem particularly promising.<br><br>First, the uncanny valley might occur at the boundary where something moves from one category to another, in this case, between non-human and human. Christine Looser and Thalia Wheatley looked at mannequin faces that were morphed into human faces and found a valley at the point where the inanimate face started to look alive. Second, a valley may also occur if we're able to believe that near-human entities possess a mind, as we do. A study by Kurt Gray and Daniel Wegner found that robots were only unnerving when people believed that they could think and experience things, and robots that did not seem to possess a mind were not frightening.<br><br>A final compelling area for future research is that the uncanny valley occurs because of mismatches between aspects of the robot's appearance and/or behaviour. Angela Tinwell's work has looked at many types of mismatch, including speech synchronisation, speech speed and facial expressions. In one 2013 study, near-human agents that reacted to a startling noise by showing surprise in the lower part of their face, but not the upper part, were found to be particularly eerie. The study suggested that this may even be reminiscent of the pattern of expressive behaviour exhibited by humans with psychopathic traits.<br><br>Android science progresses rapidly and is creating increasingly realistic robots. The uncanny valley would not exist if a robot were indistinguishable from a human because there would no longer be a relative dip in emotional response. One theory, that of an uncanny wall, suggests that it will always be possible to tell artificial from human because as robots become more realistic, we will also become more sensitive and will always be able to tell that something is not right. However, we're certainly not there yet, and we could eventually find that the uncanny valley effect was an artefact of this particular period in the history of artificial humans, when representations were easily distinguishable from human. Speaking as a researcher of the uncanny valley, I plan to enjoy its sense of subtle eeriness while it lasts.","<b>SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 7, 1942</b><br><i>Dearest Kitty,</i><br><br>Mother's nerves are on edge again, and that never bodes well for me. Is it just coincidence that Father and Mother never scold Margot but always blame me?<br><br><b>(99)____</b><br><br>Then Father walked in, didn't ask what happened, and instantly sided with Margot: <i>\"I'd like to see how you'd feel if Margot took one of your books!\"</i> I put the book down immediately and left, which they called \"in a huff.\"<br><br><b>(100)____</b><br><br>They always take Margot's side. I'm used to it now – so used to it that I've become indifferent to Mother's scolding and Margot's moods. I love them because they are my mother and sister, but not for who they are. I don't care for them as people. As far as I'm concerned, they can jump in a lake.<br><br><b>(101)____</b><br><br>I've always been the clown, the one who gets double punishment: scolding and then having to deal with my own guilt. I want something more from Father, something he doesn't seem able to give.<br><br><b>(102)____</b><br><br>But Father won't let me talk about Mother—he avoids it. Yet she's the one I clash with most. I don't know how to deal with her sarcasm, her coldness, her carelessness. I can't bring it up directly, but I also can't keep accepting all the blame.<br><br><b>(103)____</b><br><br>I keep telling myself to focus on her good qualities, to find in myself what she lacks. But it never works. And the worst part is – they don't see it. They don't realize how much they've let me down. Do any parents truly make their children happy?<br><br><b>(104)____</b><br><br>But for the time being, who else can I turn to but you, Kitty? Who else hears me out without interrupting? I need consolation often, because I fail often. But I try, every day, to do better.<br><br>So I write. At night, so many things fill my head. By day, I deal with people who misunderstand me. And I come back to you, Kitty, because you're patient. I promise you I'll keep going, I'll find my own way, and I'll swallow my tears.<br><br><b>(105)____</b><br><br>So don't condemn me, Kitty. Think of me as a girl who sometimes reaches her breaking point.<br><br><i>Yours,<br>Anne</i>","Artificial intelligence is revolutionizing mass surveillance and modern policing. China, for instance, has deployed AI-powered systems with facial recognition to monitor public spaces, using networks of millions of cameras. These tools have significantly improved the efficiency of (106)____ (HUNT), such as when Chinese authorities successfully tracked a fugitive in a crowd of 60,000 (107)____ (CONCERT) within minutes.<br><br>On another front, the U.S. National Security Agency (NSA) employs AI to analyze vast amounts of global digital communication, identifying potential threats related to espionage and (108)____ (TERROR). AI systems detect linguistic patterns and unusual metadata in emails or phone calls that human analysts might (109)____ (LOOK), bolstering a nation's information defense amidst international tensions.<br><br>AI also supports (110)____ (COVER) police operations. Law enforcement in the UK has used AI to assist in fighting (111)____ (CRIME), such as online sexual harassment and grooming. Algorithms are used to monitor and detect alarming patterns on messaging platforms and chatrooms. Sometimes, they even accurately predict potential threats.<br><br>However, along with its just use, there are a plethora of ethical concerns. In (112)____ (TOTAL) regimes, AI may be used to stem dissent, turning even a trusted (113)____ (CONFIDE) like close friends and family into (114)____ (KNOW) spies for the police. Furthermore, the growing popularity of generative art and deep-fakes assist the (115)____ (FORGE) of evidence, which muddles critical judicial decisions. Therefore, as AI grows more powerful, societies must navigate the fine line between security and civil liberty, ensuring that the technology serves justice, not undermines it.","Identify the FIVE errors in the following passage and correct them. (Line 1 \"for\" → \"of\" is the example.)<br><br>1 &nbsp;The introduction <b><u>for</u></b> stricter environmental laws has been crucial in reducing pollution<br>2 &nbsp;in many countries. While industries often resist to new regulations, evidence suggests<br>3 &nbsp;that such policies can drive innovation and improve public health. In some cases,<br>4 &nbsp;however, enforcement is inconsistent, resulting in significant gaps between policy and<br>5 &nbsp;practice. Environmental activists argue that governments should increase investment<br>6 &nbsp;in renewable energy and phase fossil fuel subsidies. Without comprehensive approach,<br>7 &nbsp;efforts to mitigate climate changes are unlikely to succeed in the long term. International<br>8 &nbsp;cooperation, including knowledge-sharing and joint research initiatives, plays a vital role<br>9 &nbsp;to address global challenges. The effectiveness of environmental policy ultimately<br>10 depends on both political willness and public support."],"questions":[{"n":1,"part":"PART 1. LANGUAGE USE","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"______ her better judgement, my mother invested all her retirement savings in cryptocurrency.","options":["Against","Notwithstanding","Without","In spite of"],"correct":0,"explanation":"\"Against one's better judgement\" is the fixed phrase for doing something you sense is unwise."},{"n":2,"part":"PART 1. LANGUAGE USE","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Burglars ______ with not only valuables but also sensitive company documents.","options":["made off","cleared out","took off","bowed out"],"correct":0,"explanation":"\"Make off with something\" = steal it and escape; the other phrasal verbs do not take \"with\" + stolen goods."},{"n":3,"part":"PART 1. LANGUAGE USE","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Please do not do me any favors. I do not like to be ______ to anyone.","options":["befriended","bewildered","beholden","begrudged"],"correct":2,"explanation":"\"Beholden to someone\" = owing them a debt of gratitude, which is exactly what accepting favors creates."},{"n":4,"part":"PART 1. LANGUAGE USE","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Jimmy was sent to prison for 3 years just for possession of ______ drugs.","options":["elicit","explicit","illicit","implicit"],"correct":2,"explanation":"\"Illicit\" = illegal; \"elicit\" is a verb and \"explicit/implicit\" do not describe drugs."},{"n":5,"part":"PART 1. LANGUAGE USE","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"With the passing of the Equality Act, the government wants to show its ______ commitment to women's rights.","options":["unblinking","unwavering","unquestioned","unquestioning"],"correct":1,"explanation":"\"Unwavering commitment\" = steady, firm commitment; it is the standard collocation."},{"n":6,"part":"PART 1. LANGUAGE USE","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Her mother has warned her ______ not to respond to messages from strangers online.","options":["most times","many a time","most of the time","a many times"],"correct":1,"explanation":"\"Many a time\" = many times, a formal way of saying \"often\"; \"a many times\" is ungrammatical and \"most of the time\" does not fit a warning that is repeated."},{"n":7,"part":"PART 1. LANGUAGE USE","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Just ______! Life is miserable when you have to watch out for everything and everyone.","options":["throw in the towel","throw yourself a bone","throw a spanner in the works","throw caution to the wind"],"correct":3,"explanation":"\"Throw caution to the wind\" = stop being careful and take a risk — the opposite of watching out for everything."},{"n":8,"part":"PART 1. LANGUAGE USE","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Jimmy received a court order for tax evasion offense, but with his reckless business practice, he ______.","options":["had seen it coming","would get his coming","would not have come","had it coming"],"correct":3,"explanation":"\"Have it coming\" = deserve the bad thing that happens to you; his reckless practice made the court order deserved."},{"n":9,"part":"PART 1. LANGUAGE USE","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"CEOs need to be ______ accountable for enabling the exploitation of the working class.","options":["forced","made","held","turned"],"correct":2,"explanation":"\"Hold somebody accountable (for)\" is the fixed collocation."},{"n":10,"part":"PART 1. LANGUAGE USE","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Be careful and ______. You might not be aware of what you are signing up for.","options":["read the runes","read the fine print","read between the lines","read the room"],"correct":1,"explanation":"\"Read the fine print\" = check the small details of a contract before signing — the context is signing up for something."},{"n":11,"part":"PART 1. LANGUAGE USE","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"While your essay is satisfactory, there are some points that need to be ______ with evidence and reasoning.","options":["worn out","fleshed out","muscled out","ironed out"],"correct":1,"explanation":"\"Flesh out\" = add more detail to an idea or argument; \"iron out\" is for problems, not points needing evidence."},{"n":12,"part":"PART 1. LANGUAGE USE","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Now is a good time to ______ your French before you transfer to the Paris office at the end of this year.","options":["dust off","scrub off","sweep away","brush up on"],"correct":3,"explanation":"\"Brush up on a language\" = practise it again to regain skill."},{"n":13,"part":"PART 1. LANGUAGE USE","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"______ an ancient battlefield, the cave mural was painted using various pigmented minerals.","options":["Exhibiting","Demonstrating","Displaying","Depicting"],"correct":3,"explanation":"A mural \"depicts\" (portrays) a scene; exhibiting/displaying/demonstrating do not describe what a picture shows."},{"n":14,"part":"PART 1. LANGUAGE USE","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"______ modelled after the symbol animal of France.","options":["Situated atop the church spire is a brass figure of a rooster","Is atop the church spire situated a brass figure of a rooster","A brass figure of a rooster is situated atop the church spire","Atop the church spire situated is a brass figure of a rooster"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Fronting the participle phrase inverts subject and verb: \"Situated atop the spire is a brass figure of a rooster [which is] modelled after…\"; in C \"modelled\" would wrongly attach to \"spire\"."},{"n":15,"part":"PART 1. LANGUAGE USE","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"We adopted a ______ to keep our older dog company.","options":["little brown playful poodle puppy","playful little brown poodle puppy","brown little playful poodle puppy","playful little poodle brown puppy"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Adjective order is opinion (playful) → size (little) → colour (brown) → type/noun modifier (poodle) + noun."},{"n":16,"part":"PART 1. LANGUAGE USE","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Since our budget is shrinking, we have to ______. Let's start with the office massage chairs.","options":["cut corners","split hairs","cut ourselves some slack","trim the fat"],"correct":3,"explanation":"\"Trim the fat\" = cut unnecessary spending; the massage chairs are the first luxury to go. \"Cut corners\" means to do things cheaply and badly."},{"n":17,"part":"PART 1. LANGUAGE USE","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"No sooner ______ John burst into tears.","options":["did he hear the voice of his love when","had he heard the voice of his love when","had he heard the voice of his love than","did he hear the voice of his love than"],"correct":2,"explanation":"\"No sooner + had + S + past participle … than\" — inversion with the past perfect and \"than\", never \"when\"."},{"n":18,"part":"PART 1. LANGUAGE USE","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"You are always expected to go the extra ______, but never with any extra pay.","options":["mile","road","length","step"],"correct":0,"explanation":"\"Go the extra mile\" = make more effort than is expected."},{"n":19,"part":"PART 1. LANGUAGE USE","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"You ought to remind your teacher as she ______ about your bonus points.","options":["would not do well to forget","might well have forgotten","as well as forgets","might as well forget"],"correct":1,"explanation":"\"Might well have forgotten\" = it is quite likely that she has forgotten, which is why a reminder is needed."},{"n":20,"part":"PART 1. LANGUAGE USE","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Some schools are coming under ______ pressure from parents to censor content inappropriate for students.","options":["peaking","boiling","mounting","climbing"],"correct":2,"explanation":"\"Mounting pressure\" = increasing pressure; it is the standard collocation."},{"n":21,"part":"PART 1. LANGUAGE USE","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"The security guard gave me a(n) ______ nod after glancing at my ID.","options":["derogatory","indicative","perfunctory","performative"],"correct":2,"explanation":"\"Perfunctory\" = done quickly and without real interest, as a formality — a quick nod after a glance."},{"n":22,"part":"PART 1. LANGUAGE USE","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"All expenses during your trip should be covered ______.","options":["providing you completing the project","providing your completion of the project","providing you complete the project","providing you completed the project"],"correct":2,"explanation":"\"Providing (that)\" = provided that, a conjunction followed by a present-tense clause for a future condition."},{"n":23,"part":"PART 1. LANGUAGE USE","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"After the sudden change in leadership, it's a good idea to ______ before bringing up another major proposal.","options":["let the dust settle","let sleeping dogs lie","let that ship sail","let bygones be bygones"],"correct":0,"explanation":"\"Let the dust settle\" = wait until a situation becomes calm after a big change."},{"n":24,"part":"PART 1. LANGUAGE USE","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"His fiancé has given him a(n) ______ to get married, or she will break off the engagement.","options":["order","ultimatum","stipulation","condition"],"correct":1,"explanation":"An \"ultimatum\" is a final demand with a threat attached (\"… or she will break off the engagement\")."},{"n":25,"part":"PART 1. LANGUAGE USE","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"To combat inflation, the government assembled an economic ______ in order to propose new policies.","options":["think piece","think tank","thought bubble","thought group"],"correct":1,"explanation":"A \"think tank\" is a group of experts assembled to advise on policy."},{"n":26,"part":"PART 1. LANGUAGE USE","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Ms. Sally needs someone to ______ at the school assembly tomorrow as she is quite sick.","options":["pull her through","bring her round to","fill her in on","stand in for her"],"correct":3,"explanation":"\"Stand in for somebody\" = take their place temporarily when they are absent."},{"n":27,"part":"PART 1. LANGUAGE USE","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Any luggage ______ at the airport will be confiscated and searched by police.","options":["leaving unattended","left unattended","leaving unattending","left unattending"],"correct":1,"explanation":"A reduced passive relative clause: luggage (which is) left unattended."},{"n":28,"part":"PART 1. LANGUAGE USE","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"That traitorous snake ______ multiple times for his own benefit.","options":["pulled the plug on people","threw people under the bus","threw people a curveball","pulled the rug out from people's feet"],"correct":1,"explanation":"\"Throw someone under the bus\" = betray them for your own advantage, which is what a \"traitorous snake\" does."},{"n":29,"part":"PART 1. LANGUAGE USE","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Our government is working with foreign construction companies to ______ the urban infrastructure problem.","options":["disintegrate","elevate","alleviate","propagate"],"correct":2,"explanation":"\"Alleviate a problem\" = make it less severe."},{"n":30,"part":"PART 1. LANGUAGE USE","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"______, it started to malfunction just after a drop.","options":["Though was the camera expensive","Expensive that the camera was","Expensive as the camera was","Though the camera be expensive"],"correct":2,"explanation":"\"Adjective + as + S + V\" is the concessive inversion: \"Expensive as the camera was\" = although the camera was expensive."},{"n":31,"part":"PART 1. LANGUAGE USE","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"You need to realize that we are all on the same ______, and either we all survive or no one does.","options":["page","wavelength","cloth","boat"],"correct":3,"explanation":"\"In the same boat\" = in the same (difficult) situation; \"on the same page\" means agreeing, which does not fit \"either we all survive or no one does\"."},{"n":32,"part":"PART 1. LANGUAGE USE","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"The ______ majority of students prefer blended learning to strictly online or face to face.","options":["broad","deep","vast","high"],"correct":2,"explanation":"\"The vast majority\" is the fixed collocation."},{"n":33,"part":"PART 1. LANGUAGE USE","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"The student performer's rapping over the 'Declaration of Independence' raised a few ______ in the audience.","options":["hands","fists","eyebrows","necks"],"correct":2,"explanation":"\"Raise (a few) eyebrows\" = cause surprise or mild disapproval."},{"n":34,"part":"PART 1. LANGUAGE USE","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Receiving warning of a(n) ______ storm, our family quickly devised a shelter plan.","options":["dominant","eminent","prominent","imminent"],"correct":3,"explanation":"\"Imminent\" = about to happen; a storm warning concerns a storm that is coming very soon."},{"n":35,"part":"PART 1. LANGUAGE USE","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"We all paused our climb to admire the ______ beauty of the sun rising over the snow-cloaked peaks.","options":["speechless","ineffable","unwritten","breathless"],"correct":1,"explanation":"\"Ineffable\" = too great to be described in words; \"speechless\" and \"breathless\" describe people, not beauty."},{"n":36,"part":"PART 1. LANGUAGE USE","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"The host would prefer ______ in formal attire.","options":["for her guests to arrive","it if her guests arrived","her guests arriving","that her guests arrive"],"correct":1,"explanation":"\"Would prefer it if + S + past simple\" is the standard pattern for expressing a preference about someone else's action (\"I'd prefer it if you didn't smoke\")."},{"n":37,"part":"PART 1. LANGUAGE USE","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"While doing research for my history assignment, I went ______ reading about medieval conspiracy theories.","options":["on a wild goose chase","down a rabbit hole","under the radar","cold turkey"],"correct":1,"explanation":"\"Go down a rabbit hole\" = get absorbed in something and lose track of time/purpose, e.g. while reading online."},{"n":38,"part":"PART 1. LANGUAGE USE","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"After years of thinking about it, Molly decided to ______ and get a tattoo.","options":["eat her heart out","chew the cud","bite the bullet","sink her teeth into it"],"correct":2,"explanation":"\"Bite the bullet\" = finally do something unpleasant or frightening that you have been putting off."},{"n":39,"part":"PART 1. LANGUAGE USE","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"It is crucial that the evidence ______ by any non-authorized persons.","options":["is not touched","not be touched","be not touched","not to be touched"],"correct":1,"explanation":"After \"It is crucial that\" the subjunctive is used: S + (not) + base verb, so \"not be touched\"."},{"n":40,"part":"PART 1. LANGUAGE USE","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Independent studies are beginning to ______ doubt on the effectiveness of dietary supplements.","options":["cast","shed","throw","bring"],"correct":0,"explanation":"\"Cast doubt on something\" is the fixed collocation (\"shed\" goes with \"light\")."},{"n":41,"part":"PART 1. LANGUAGE USE","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":0,"q":"(41) ______","options":["acclaimed","credited","applauded","commended"],"correct":0,"explanation":"\"Critically acclaimed\" is the fixed collocation for a work praised by critics."},{"n":42,"part":"PART 1. LANGUAGE USE","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":0,"q":"(42) ______","options":["roundabouts","crossroads","intersections","sidewalks"],"correct":2,"explanation":"\"The intersection(s) of technology, society, and human psychology\" = the point where these fields meet and overlap."},{"n":43,"part":"PART 1. LANGUAGE USE","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":0,"q":"(43) ______","options":["being aired","airing","having aired","having been aired"],"correct":1,"explanation":"\"Air\" is intransitive here (a show airs), so the participle clause is \"First airing in 2011, the show quickly gained…\"."},{"n":44,"part":"PART 1. LANGUAGE USE","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":0,"q":"(44) ______","options":["recognition","awareness","momentum","favor"],"correct":0,"explanation":"\"Gain recognition for\" = become known and respected for something."},{"n":45,"part":"PART 1. LANGUAGE USE","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":0,"q":"(45) ______","options":["self-contained","self-serving","self-imposed","self-directed"],"correct":0,"explanation":"An anthology series has \"self-contained\" episodes — each stands alone as a complete story."},{"n":46,"part":"PART 1. LANGUAGE USE","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":0,"q":"(46) ______","options":["broken","switched-off","turned-on","glitching"],"correct":1,"explanation":"A screen is reflective (a \"black mirror\") only when it is switched off."},{"n":47,"part":"PART 1. LANGUAGE USE","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":0,"q":"(47) ______","options":["Besides","Thanks to","With regards to","Rather than"],"correct":3,"explanation":"\"Rather than offering grand dystopias, the series often focuses on intimate stories\" — a contrast between two alternatives."},{"n":48,"part":"PART 1. LANGUAGE USE","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":0,"q":"(48) ______","options":["favors","considers","deepens","invites"],"correct":0,"explanation":"\"Favor X over Y\" = prefer X to Y; the rhythm prefers stillness over sensationalism."},{"n":49,"part":"PART 1. LANGUAGE USE","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":0,"q":"(49) ______","options":["transforms","renders","imagines","forces"],"correct":1,"explanation":"\"Render something visible\" = make it visible (render + object + adjective)."},{"n":50,"part":"PART 1. LANGUAGE USE","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":0,"q":"(50) ______","options":["doubting","suspecting","interrogating","questioning"],"correct":3,"explanation":"\"Questioning the cost of progress\" = examining and challenging it; \"doubting/suspecting\" do not fit \"without rejecting the values of technology\"."},{"n":51,"part":"PART 1. LANGUAGE USE","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":1,"q":"(51) ______","options":["Separated","Independent","Excluded","Isolated"],"correct":3,"explanation":"\"Isolated from change\" = cut off from outside influence, which is why their life has hardly altered."},{"n":52,"part":"PART 1. LANGUAGE USE","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":1,"q":"(52) ______","options":["seemingly","mostly","barely","certainly"],"correct":2,"explanation":"\"A life which has barely altered in five millennia\" — hardly changed at all, matching \"a living link with the earliest moments of human history\"."},{"n":53,"part":"PART 1. LANGUAGE USE","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":1,"q":"(53) ______","options":["shores","currents","banks","edges"],"correct":2,"explanation":"\"On the banks of the river\" is the standard collocation for a river (\"shores\" is for seas and lakes)."},{"n":54,"part":"PART 1. LANGUAGE USE","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":1,"q":"(54) ______","options":["otherwise","thus","unless","however"],"correct":0,"explanation":"\"What is otherwise desert wasteland\" = land that, apart from the river, is desert."},{"n":55,"part":"PART 1. LANGUAGE USE","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":1,"q":"(55) ______","options":["because","so","for","but"],"correct":2,"explanation":"\"…, for the climate is harsh\" — \"for\" introduces the reason in formal written style after a comma."},{"n":56,"part":"PART 1. LANGUAGE USE","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":1,"q":"(56) ______","options":["any but","some of","none but","only"],"correct":0,"explanation":"\"Too treacherous for any but the Mohana\" = too dangerous for anyone except the Mohana."},{"n":57,"part":"PART 1. LANGUAGE USE","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":1,"q":"(57) ______","options":["have always been","have always lived","always had","always lived"],"correct":1,"explanation":"\"They live as they have always lived\" — the present perfect for a way of life continuing from the past to now."},{"n":58,"part":"PART 1. LANGUAGE USE","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":1,"q":"(58) ______","options":["trainee","training","trained","trainer"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Past participle as adjective: \"trained herons\" = herons that have been trained."},{"n":59,"part":"PART 1. LANGUAGE USE","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":1,"q":"(59) ______","options":["dismiss","disclose","discharge","discern"],"correct":3,"explanation":"\"Discern the tiniest movements\" = perceive with difficulty; the oils act as colour filters that help the birds see."},{"n":60,"part":"PART 1. LANGUAGE USE","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":1,"q":"(60) ______","options":["so","thus","that","such"],"correct":0,"explanation":"\"…can discern the tiniest movements and so detect the fish\" — \"and so\" = and as a result."},{"n":61,"part":"PART 1. LANGUAGE USE","section":"Open cloze","type":"text","passageId":2,"q":"(61) ______","accept":["example","instance"],"answer":"example","explanation":"\"For example / for instance\" introduces the hardship that follows."},{"n":62,"part":"PART 1. LANGUAGE USE","section":"Open cloze","type":"text","passageId":2,"q":"(62) ______","accept":["did","helped"],"answer":"did","explanation":"Emphatic \"did free\": \"On the positive side, advertisers' discrimination did free the press from advertiser domination\"."},{"n":63,"part":"PART 1. LANGUAGE USE","section":"Open cloze","type":"text","passageId":2,"q":"(63) ______","accept":["from"],"answer":"from","explanation":"\"Free somebody from something\" = release them from it."},{"n":64,"part":"PART 1. LANGUAGE USE","section":"Open cloze","type":"text","passageId":2,"q":"(64) ______","accept":["politically"],"answer":"politically","explanation":"\"Politically charged material\" = content with strong political content."},{"n":65,"part":"PART 1. LANGUAGE USE","section":"Open cloze","type":"text","passageId":2,"q":"(65) ______","accept":["could","did"],"answer":"could","explanation":"\"More readily than could the large national dailies\" — inverted auxiliary repeating \"could print\"."},{"n":66,"part":"PART 1. LANGUAGE USE","section":"Open cloze","type":"text","passageId":2,"q":"(66) ______","accept":["depended","relied"],"answer":"depended","explanation":"\"Depend / rely on advertisers' approval to secure revenues\"."},{"n":67,"part":"PART 1. LANGUAGE USE","section":"Open cloze","type":"text","passageId":2,"q":"(67) ______","accept":["that"],"answer":"that","explanation":"\"Higher than that of general-circulation dailies\" — \"that\" stands for \"the selling price\"."},{"n":68,"part":"PART 1. LANGUAGE USE","section":"Open cloze","type":"text","passageId":2,"q":"(68) ______","accept":["much"],"answer":"much","explanation":"\"As much as two-thirds of publication costs\" — a large proportion."},{"n":69,"part":"PART 1. LANGUAGE USE","section":"Open cloze","type":"text","passageId":2,"q":"(69) ______","accept":["groups"],"answer":"groups","explanation":"\"Interest groups\" = organised groups with a shared concern."},{"n":70,"part":"PART 1. LANGUAGE USE","section":"Open cloze","type":"text","passageId":2,"q":"(70) ______","accept":["despite","notwithstanding"],"answer":"despite","explanation":"\"Despite their editorial freedom, publishers often felt compelled to print sensationalism\" — concession."},{"n":71,"part":"PART 1. LANGUAGE USE","section":"Open cloze","type":"text","passageId":3,"q":"(71) ______","accept":["ever"],"answer":"ever","explanation":"\"One of the finest cutting weapons ever produced\" — superlative + ever."},{"n":72,"part":"PART 1. LANGUAGE USE","section":"Open cloze","type":"text","passageId":3,"q":"(72) ______","accept":["considered","called","deemed","termed"],"answer":"considered","explanation":"\"It has even been considered a spiritual entity\" — consider + object + noun (no \"as\" needed)."},{"n":73,"part":"PART 1. LANGUAGE USE","section":"Open cloze","type":"text","passageId":3,"q":"(73) ______","accept":["only","just","merely","simply"],"answer":"only","explanation":"\"Not only … but also …\" correlative structure."},{"n":74,"part":"PART 1. LANGUAGE USE","section":"Open cloze","type":"text","passageId":3,"q":"(74) ______","accept":["not"],"answer":"not","explanation":"\"Though not classically regarded as artists, master smiths nevertheless exerted great care\" — the contrast needs a negative."},{"n":75,"part":"PART 1. LANGUAGE USE","section":"Open cloze","type":"text","passageId":3,"q":"(75) ______","accept":["which"],"answer":"which","explanation":"\"Swords, no two of which were forged exactly the same way\" — quantifier + of + which relative clause."},{"n":76,"part":"PART 1. LANGUAGE USE","section":"Open cloze","type":"text","passageId":3,"q":"(76) ______","accept":["into"],"answer":"into","explanation":"\"Folded together into thousands of layers\" — into expresses the result of folding."},{"n":77,"part":"PART 1. LANGUAGE USE","section":"Open cloze","type":"text","passageId":3,"q":"(77) ______","accept":["both"],"answer":"both","explanation":"\"Yielding both a razor-sharp edge and a flexible blade\" — both … and …."},{"n":78,"part":"PART 1. LANGUAGE USE","section":"Open cloze","type":"text","passageId":3,"q":"(78) ______","accept":["techniques","technique","methods","method","process","skills","style"],"answer":"techniques","explanation":"\"Secret forging techniques\" — the way of forging left an idiosyncratic structural signature."},{"n":79,"part":"PART 1. LANGUAGE USE","section":"Open cloze","type":"text","passageId":3,"q":"(79) ______","accept":["The","Each","This","Every"],"answer":"The","explanation":"\"The unique finished product reflected the smith's personal honor\" — a definite article before the noun phrase."},{"n":80,"part":"PART 1. LANGUAGE USE","section":"Open cloze","type":"text","passageId":3,"q":"(80) ______","accept":["to"],"answer":"to","explanation":"\"Devotion to the craft\" — devotion takes the preposition to."},{"n":81,"part":"PART 2. READING","section":"Reading","type":"text","passageId":4,"q":"Reading Passage 1 has six paragraphs <b>A – F</b>. Which paragraph contains the following information? (You may use any letter more than once.) Write the letter.<br>behavioral changes in participants of a study","accept":["F"],"answer":"F","explanation":"Paragraph F: \"a 2009 study … women who viewed images of cute babies exhibited more careful, slow, and deliberate behavior afterward\"."},{"n":82,"part":"PART 2. READING","section":"Reading","type":"text","passageId":4,"q":"Reading Passage 1 has six paragraphs <b>A – F</b>. Which paragraph contains the following information? (You may use any letter more than once.) Write the letter.<br>an educational effect that 'cute aggression' has on human infants","accept":["E"],"answer":"E","explanation":"Paragraph E: \"our variety of reactions when we encounter a baby could teach babies about the nuance of emotional expression\"."},{"n":83,"part":"PART 2. READING","section":"Reading","type":"text","passageId":4,"q":"Reading Passage 1 has six paragraphs <b>A – F</b>. Which paragraph contains the following information? (You may use any letter more than once.) Write the letter.<br>a 'cute aggression' remark made by a celebrity","accept":["B"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Paragraph B: model Leslie Bibb said on Conan O'Brien that a puppy was \"so cute she wanted to bash it\"."},{"n":84,"part":"PART 2. READING","section":"Reading","type":"text","passageId":4,"q":"Reading Passage 1 has six paragraphs <b>A – F</b>. Which paragraph contains the following information? (You may use any letter more than once.) Write the letter.<br>a maternal response to signs of 'cute aggression'","accept":["D"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Paragraph D: a mother who sees clenched fists and a quick approach \"can then set a boundary by holding the baby herself\"."},{"n":85,"part":"PART 2. READING","section":"Reading","type":"text","passageId":4,"q":"Reading Passage 1 has six paragraphs <b>A – F</b>. Which paragraph contains the following information? (You may use any letter more than once.) Write the letter.<br>the likelihood of experiencing 'cute aggression'","accept":["A"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Paragraph A: \"approximately 50% of people who experience what psychologists call 'cute aggression'\"."},{"n":86,"part":"PART 2. READING","section":"Reading","type":"text","passageId":4,"q":"Complete the sentence with the correct ending from the list A – G below. Write the letter.<br><b>People may express a contradictory emotion because …</b><br><b>A.</b> they have to balance out an overwhelming emotion.<br><b>B.</b> they can discourage overreactions.<br><b>C.</b> they need to grow a sense of boundaries.<br><b>D.</b> they can respond in a socially appropriate way.<br><b>E.</b> they have child-rearing instincts.<br><b>F.</b> they do not find the cuteness to be overwhelming.<br><b>G.</b> they want to assert dominance over the baby.","accept":["A","they have to balance out an overwhelming emotion"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Paragraph C: \"the brain may respond with a seemingly contradictory expression to 'balance' out … the other, all-encompassing emotion\"."},{"n":87,"part":"PART 2. READING","section":"Reading","type":"text","passageId":4,"q":"Complete the sentence with the correct ending from the list A – G below. Write the letter.<br><b>We implicitly convey our intentions to people so that …</b><br><b>A.</b> they have to balance out an overwhelming emotion.<br><b>B.</b> they can discourage overreactions.<br><b>C.</b> they need to grow a sense of boundaries.<br><b>D.</b> they can respond in a socially appropriate way.<br><b>E.</b> they have child-rearing instincts.<br><b>F.</b> they do not find the cuteness to be overwhelming.<br><b>G.</b> they want to assert dominance over the baby.","accept":["D","they can respond in a socially appropriate way"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Paragraph D: expressing intent \"can help us coordinate actions toward people around us\" — e.g. the mother can set a boundary."},{"n":88,"part":"PART 2. READING","section":"Reading","type":"text","passageId":4,"q":"Complete the sentence with the correct ending from the list A – G below. Write the letter.<br><b>People's desire to squeeze a baby might indicate that …</b><br><b>A.</b> they have to balance out an overwhelming emotion.<br><b>B.</b> they can discourage overreactions.<br><b>C.</b> they need to grow a sense of boundaries.<br><b>D.</b> they can respond in a socially appropriate way.<br><b>E.</b> they have child-rearing instincts.<br><b>F.</b> they do not find the cuteness to be overwhelming.<br><b>G.</b> they want to assert dominance over the baby.","accept":["E","they have child-rearing instincts"],"answer":"E","explanation":"Paragraph F: cute aggression may be \"an adaptive mechanism to activate caretaking behaviors, which ultimately helps babies survive\"."},{"n":89,"part":"PART 2. READING","section":"Reading","type":"text","passageId":5,"q":"Does the following statement agree with the information given in the passage? Write <b>TRUE</b> if it agrees, <b>FALSE</b> if it contradicts the information, or <b>NOT GIVEN</b> if there is no information on this.<br>The uncanny valley effect is strongest towards human-like robots.","accept":["TRUE","T","True"],"answer":"TRUE","explanation":"The dip in emotional response occurs when robots are \"close to, but not quite, human\"; \"anything with a highly human-like appearance can be subject to this peculiar effect\"."},{"n":90,"part":"PART 2. READING","section":"Reading","type":"text","passageId":5,"q":"Does the following statement agree with the information given in the passage? Write <b>TRUE</b> if it agrees, <b>FALSE</b> if it contradicts the information, or <b>NOT GIVEN</b> if there is no information on this.<br>The study of the uncanny valley effect exclusively attracts roboticists.","accept":["FALSE","F","False"],"answer":"FALSE","explanation":"The effect \"has been explored from many perspectives, from … roboticists to … philosophers and … psychologists\" — not exclusively roboticists."},{"n":91,"part":"PART 2. READING","section":"Reading","type":"text","passageId":5,"q":"Does the following statement agree with the information given in the passage? Write <b>TRUE</b> if it agrees, <b>FALSE</b> if it contradicts the information, or <b>NOT GIVEN</b> if there is no information on this.<br>The majority of research papers on the topic are not in English.","accept":["NOT GIVEN","NG","Not given"],"answer":"NOT GIVEN","explanation":"The passage only says Mori's original paper was translated into English in 2005; it says nothing about the language of the majority of papers."},{"n":92,"part":"PART 2. READING","section":"Reading","type":"text","passageId":5,"q":"Does the following statement agree with the information given in the passage? Write <b>TRUE</b> if it agrees, <b>FALSE</b> if it contradicts the information, or <b>NOT GIVEN</b> if there is no information on this.<br>Mathur and Reichling's experiment yielded results that agree with Mori's theory.","accept":["TRUE","T","True"],"answer":"TRUE","explanation":"\"Their data showed the classic rise-dip-rise that Mori originally predicted.\""},{"n":93,"part":"PART 2. READING","section":"Reading","type":"text","passageId":5,"q":"Complete the summary below. Choose <b>ONE WORD ONLY</b> from the passage for each answer.<br><br>The uncanny valley effect is the feeling of unease felt towards certain robots or characters that bear a strong, but not exact, resemblance to humans. First conceptualized by a Japanese scientist who recorded a (93)____ in human positive reaction when faced with a robot of certain human likeness, the phenomenon has then received much research attention.<br><br>One theory suggests that the uncanny valley effect might be triggered as soon as a robot face begins to look (94)____. Another researcher found that the belief in a robot's ability to (95)____ and feel might increase fear response toward them. Finally, certain contrasts in the way a robot looks and the way it behaves can set off anxiety. For instance, a robot demonstrating its 'startled face' without moving the upper half of its face can be alarming because it reminds people of (96)____ individuals.<br><br>Answer for (93):","accept":["dip"],"answer":"dip","explanation":"Paragraph 1: \"a characteristic dip in emotional response\" — Mori identified the dip."},{"n":94,"part":"PART 2. READING","section":"Reading","type":"text","passageId":5,"q":"Complete the summary below. Choose <b>ONE WORD ONLY</b> from the passage for each answer.<br><br>The uncanny valley effect is the feeling of unease felt towards certain robots or characters that bear a strong, but not exact, resemblance to humans. First conceptualized by a Japanese scientist who recorded a (93)____ in human positive reaction when faced with a robot of certain human likeness, the phenomenon has then received much research attention.<br><br>One theory suggests that the uncanny valley effect might be triggered as soon as a robot face begins to look (94)____. Another researcher found that the belief in a robot's ability to (95)____ and feel might increase fear response toward them. Finally, certain contrasts in the way a robot looks and the way it behaves can set off anxiety. For instance, a robot demonstrating its 'startled face' without moving the upper half of its face can be alarming because it reminds people of (96)____ individuals.<br><br>Answer for (94):","accept":["alive"],"answer":"alive","explanation":"Looser and Wheatley \"found a valley at the point where the inanimate face started to look alive\"."},{"n":95,"part":"PART 2. READING","section":"Reading","type":"text","passageId":5,"q":"Complete the summary below. Choose <b>ONE WORD ONLY</b> from the passage for each answer.<br><br>The uncanny valley effect is the feeling of unease felt towards certain robots or characters that bear a strong, but not exact, resemblance to humans. First conceptualized by a Japanese scientist who recorded a (93)____ in human positive reaction when faced with a robot of certain human likeness, the phenomenon has then received much research attention.<br><br>One theory suggests that the uncanny valley effect might be triggered as soon as a robot face begins to look (94)____. Another researcher found that the belief in a robot's ability to (95)____ and feel might increase fear response toward them. Finally, certain contrasts in the way a robot looks and the way it behaves can set off anxiety. For instance, a robot demonstrating its 'startled face' without moving the upper half of its face can be alarming because it reminds people of (96)____ individuals.<br><br>Answer for (95):","accept":["think"],"answer":"think","explanation":"Gray and Wegner: robots were unnerving \"when people believed that they could think and experience things\"."},{"n":96,"part":"PART 2. READING","section":"Reading","type":"text","passageId":5,"q":"Complete the summary below. Choose <b>ONE WORD ONLY</b> from the passage for each answer.<br><br>The uncanny valley effect is the feeling of unease felt towards certain robots or characters that bear a strong, but not exact, resemblance to humans. First conceptualized by a Japanese scientist who recorded a (93)____ in human positive reaction when faced with a robot of certain human likeness, the phenomenon has then received much research attention.<br><br>One theory suggests that the uncanny valley effect might be triggered as soon as a robot face begins to look (94)____. Another researcher found that the belief in a robot's ability to (95)____ and feel might increase fear response toward them. Finally, certain contrasts in the way a robot looks and the way it behaves can set off anxiety. For instance, a robot demonstrating its 'startled face' without moving the upper half of its face can be alarming because it reminds people of (96)____ individuals.<br><br>Answer for (96):","accept":["psychopathic"],"answer":"psychopathic","explanation":"The 2013 study: surprise shown only in the lower face is \"reminiscent of … humans with psychopathic traits\"."},{"n":97,"part":"PART 2. READING","section":"Reading","type":"mcq","passageId":5,"q":"What can be said about Mathur and Reichling?","options":["They wanted to disprove the existence of the uncanny valley.","They tested participants' response on both real robots and computer images.","Their research was one of the earliest in the field.","They found people were willing to trust robots that they like more."],"correct":1,"explanation":"Their valley effect \"was found with the original robots and also with CGI images built to systematically vary in human likeness\"."},{"n":98,"part":"PART 2. READING","section":"Reading","type":"mcq","passageId":5,"q":"The author believes that the uncanny valley","options":["represents a human weakness.","does not have a meaningful impact on people's lives.","can always be overcome.","might no longer exist one day."],"correct":3,"explanation":"Last paragraph: \"we could eventually find that the uncanny valley effect was an artefact of this particular period … I plan to enjoy its sense of subtle eeriness while it lasts.\""},{"n":99,"part":"PART 2. READING","section":"Reading","type":"text","passageId":6,"q":"Seven pieces of text have been removed from the diary excerpt. Choose from the texts A – J the one which fits gap <b>(99)</b>. There are THREE extra pieces of text that you do not need to use. Write the letter.<br><b>A.</b> We're opposites, she and I. I'm not trying to judge her, but I see that she's not a mother to me. I have to mother myself. I've let go of any hope in her and am charting my own path. I know what a mother should be, and I don't see any of it in her.<br><b>B.</b> Margot never has to explain herself. She could break something or say something cruel, and no one would mind. But when I make the tiniest mistake, all eyes turn on me. I'm tired of tiptoeing around everyone else's moods. Why can't they try to understand mine for once?<br><b>C.</b> But I wasn't angry – just sad. It was unfair of Father to pass judgment without understanding the situation. I would've given the book back on my own, without all the fuss.<br><b>D.</b> They're not consistent with me. One moment I'm called sensible and deserving of honesty, the next a silly goose who thinks she knows it all from books. I'm not the little darling they used to laugh at. I have thoughts, plans, ideals – but I can't yet express them properly.<br><b>E.</b> I sometimes wonder if they'd rather I just disappeared into the walls and stopped being a nuisance altogether.<br><b>F.</b> I just wish – just once – I could see a result. Or hear one word of encouragement from someone who truly loves me.<br><b>G.</b> I'm not jealous of Margot. I don't envy her looks or brains. I just want to feel that I matter, that Father sees me, understands me. But he doesn't see the difference in how he treats us. I hold on to him because my contempt for Mother is growing. It's through him that I try to preserve the last bit of family feeling I still have.<br><b>H.</b> Sometimes I think God is testing me, now and for the future. I'll have to become good without a guide, without advice. Maybe that'll make me stronger in the end.<br><b>I.</b> It's different with Father. I love him more than anyone, and when he praises Margot for being so clever, sweet, and pretty, I feel this ache inside. I want to be loved for who I am – Anne – not just because I'm his child.<br><b>J.</b> Last night proved it again. Margot had been reading a book with beautiful pictures, put it down for a moment, and I picked it up. She returned, saw it in my hands, scowled, and snatched it back, accusingly. I still wanted to look, but she got angrier. Of course, Mother sided with her: <i>\"Margot was reading that; give it back.\"</i>","accept":["J"],"answer":"J","explanation":"Gap 99 needs the book incident that \"Then Father walked in … sided with Margot: 'I'd like to see how you'd feel if Margot took one of your books!'\" refers back to — text J tells it and ends with Mother siding with Margot."},{"n":100,"part":"PART 2. READING","section":"Reading","type":"text","passageId":6,"q":"Seven pieces of text have been removed from the diary excerpt. Choose from the texts A – J the one which fits gap <b>(100)</b>. There are THREE extra pieces of text that you do not need to use. Write the letter.<br><b>A.</b> We're opposites, she and I. I'm not trying to judge her, but I see that she's not a mother to me. I have to mother myself. I've let go of any hope in her and am charting my own path. I know what a mother should be, and I don't see any of it in her.<br><b>B.</b> Margot never has to explain herself. She could break something or say something cruel, and no one would mind. But when I make the tiniest mistake, all eyes turn on me. I'm tired of tiptoeing around everyone else's moods. Why can't they try to understand mine for once?<br><b>C.</b> But I wasn't angry – just sad. It was unfair of Father to pass judgment without understanding the situation. I would've given the book back on my own, without all the fuss.<br><b>D.</b> They're not consistent with me. One moment I'm called sensible and deserving of honesty, the next a silly goose who thinks she knows it all from books. I'm not the little darling they used to laugh at. I have thoughts, plans, ideals – but I can't yet express them properly.<br><b>E.</b> I sometimes wonder if they'd rather I just disappeared into the walls and stopped being a nuisance altogether.<br><b>F.</b> I just wish – just once – I could see a result. Or hear one word of encouragement from someone who truly loves me.<br><b>G.</b> I'm not jealous of Margot. I don't envy her looks or brains. I just want to feel that I matter, that Father sees me, understands me. But he doesn't see the difference in how he treats us. I hold on to him because my contempt for Mother is growing. It's through him that I try to preserve the last bit of family feeling I still have.<br><b>H.</b> Sometimes I think God is testing me, now and for the future. I'll have to become good without a guide, without advice. Maybe that'll make me stronger in the end.<br><b>I.</b> It's different with Father. I love him more than anyone, and when he praises Margot for being so clever, sweet, and pretty, I feel this ache inside. I want to be loved for who I am – Anne – not just because I'm his child.<br><b>J.</b> Last night proved it again. Margot had been reading a book with beautiful pictures, put it down for a moment, and I picked it up. She returned, saw it in my hands, scowled, and snatched it back, accusingly. I still wanted to look, but she got angrier. Of course, Mother sided with her: <i>\"Margot was reading that; give it back.\"</i>","accept":["C"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Text C follows Father's unfair verdict: \"It was unfair of Father to pass judgment … I would've given the book back on my own\" — then \"They always take Margot's side\" continues the theme."},{"n":101,"part":"PART 2. READING","section":"Reading","type":"text","passageId":6,"q":"Seven pieces of text have been removed from the diary excerpt. Choose from the texts A – J the one which fits gap <b>(101)</b>. There are THREE extra pieces of text that you do not need to use. Write the letter.<br><b>A.</b> We're opposites, she and I. I'm not trying to judge her, but I see that she's not a mother to me. I have to mother myself. I've let go of any hope in her and am charting my own path. I know what a mother should be, and I don't see any of it in her.<br><b>B.</b> Margot never has to explain herself. She could break something or say something cruel, and no one would mind. But when I make the tiniest mistake, all eyes turn on me. I'm tired of tiptoeing around everyone else's moods. Why can't they try to understand mine for once?<br><b>C.</b> But I wasn't angry – just sad. It was unfair of Father to pass judgment without understanding the situation. I would've given the book back on my own, without all the fuss.<br><b>D.</b> They're not consistent with me. One moment I'm called sensible and deserving of honesty, the next a silly goose who thinks she knows it all from books. I'm not the little darling they used to laugh at. I have thoughts, plans, ideals – but I can't yet express them properly.<br><b>E.</b> I sometimes wonder if they'd rather I just disappeared into the walls and stopped being a nuisance altogether.<br><b>F.</b> I just wish – just once – I could see a result. Or hear one word of encouragement from someone who truly loves me.<br><b>G.</b> I'm not jealous of Margot. I don't envy her looks or brains. I just want to feel that I matter, that Father sees me, understands me. But he doesn't see the difference in how he treats us. I hold on to him because my contempt for Mother is growing. It's through him that I try to preserve the last bit of family feeling I still have.<br><b>H.</b> Sometimes I think God is testing me, now and for the future. I'll have to become good without a guide, without advice. Maybe that'll make me stronger in the end.<br><b>I.</b> It's different with Father. I love him more than anyone, and when he praises Margot for being so clever, sweet, and pretty, I feel this ache inside. I want to be loved for who I am – Anne – not just because I'm his child.<br><b>J.</b> Last night proved it again. Margot had been reading a book with beautiful pictures, put it down for a moment, and I picked it up. She returned, saw it in my hands, scowled, and snatched it back, accusingly. I still wanted to look, but she got angrier. Of course, Mother sided with her: <i>\"Margot was reading that; give it back.\"</i>","accept":["I"],"answer":"I","explanation":"After \"they can jump in a lake\" (Mother and Margot), text I contrasts: \"It's different with Father. I love him more than anyone…\"; \"I want to be loved for who I am\" leads into \"I want something more from Father\"."},{"n":102,"part":"PART 2. READING","section":"Reading","type":"text","passageId":6,"q":"Seven pieces of text have been removed from the diary excerpt. Choose from the texts A – J the one which fits gap <b>(102)</b>. There are THREE extra pieces of text that you do not need to use. Write the letter.<br><b>A.</b> We're opposites, she and I. I'm not trying to judge her, but I see that she's not a mother to me. I have to mother myself. I've let go of any hope in her and am charting my own path. I know what a mother should be, and I don't see any of it in her.<br><b>B.</b> Margot never has to explain herself. She could break something or say something cruel, and no one would mind. But when I make the tiniest mistake, all eyes turn on me. I'm tired of tiptoeing around everyone else's moods. Why can't they try to understand mine for once?<br><b>C.</b> But I wasn't angry – just sad. It was unfair of Father to pass judgment without understanding the situation. I would've given the book back on my own, without all the fuss.<br><b>D.</b> They're not consistent with me. One moment I'm called sensible and deserving of honesty, the next a silly goose who thinks she knows it all from books. I'm not the little darling they used to laugh at. I have thoughts, plans, ideals – but I can't yet express them properly.<br><b>E.</b> I sometimes wonder if they'd rather I just disappeared into the walls and stopped being a nuisance altogether.<br><b>F.</b> I just wish – just once – I could see a result. Or hear one word of encouragement from someone who truly loves me.<br><b>G.</b> I'm not jealous of Margot. I don't envy her looks or brains. I just want to feel that I matter, that Father sees me, understands me. But he doesn't see the difference in how he treats us. I hold on to him because my contempt for Mother is growing. It's through him that I try to preserve the last bit of family feeling I still have.<br><b>H.</b> Sometimes I think God is testing me, now and for the future. I'll have to become good without a guide, without advice. Maybe that'll make me stronger in the end.<br><b>I.</b> It's different with Father. I love him more than anyone, and when he praises Margot for being so clever, sweet, and pretty, I feel this ache inside. I want to be loved for who I am – Anne – not just because I'm his child.<br><b>J.</b> Last night proved it again. Margot had been reading a book with beautiful pictures, put it down for a moment, and I picked it up. She returned, saw it in my hands, scowled, and snatched it back, accusingly. I still wanted to look, but she got angrier. Of course, Mother sided with her: <i>\"Margot was reading that; give it back.\"</i>","accept":["G"],"answer":"G","explanation":"Text G (\"I'm not jealous of Margot … I hold on to him because my contempt for Mother is growing\") is picked up by \"But Father won't let me talk about Mother\"."},{"n":103,"part":"PART 2. READING","section":"Reading","type":"text","passageId":6,"q":"Seven pieces of text have been removed from the diary excerpt. Choose from the texts A – J the one which fits gap <b>(103)</b>. There are THREE extra pieces of text that you do not need to use. Write the letter.<br><b>A.</b> We're opposites, she and I. I'm not trying to judge her, but I see that she's not a mother to me. I have to mother myself. I've let go of any hope in her and am charting my own path. I know what a mother should be, and I don't see any of it in her.<br><b>B.</b> Margot never has to explain herself. She could break something or say something cruel, and no one would mind. But when I make the tiniest mistake, all eyes turn on me. I'm tired of tiptoeing around everyone else's moods. Why can't they try to understand mine for once?<br><b>C.</b> But I wasn't angry – just sad. It was unfair of Father to pass judgment without understanding the situation. I would've given the book back on my own, without all the fuss.<br><b>D.</b> They're not consistent with me. One moment I'm called sensible and deserving of honesty, the next a silly goose who thinks she knows it all from books. I'm not the little darling they used to laugh at. I have thoughts, plans, ideals – but I can't yet express them properly.<br><b>E.</b> I sometimes wonder if they'd rather I just disappeared into the walls and stopped being a nuisance altogether.<br><b>F.</b> I just wish – just once – I could see a result. Or hear one word of encouragement from someone who truly loves me.<br><b>G.</b> I'm not jealous of Margot. I don't envy her looks or brains. I just want to feel that I matter, that Father sees me, understands me. But he doesn't see the difference in how he treats us. I hold on to him because my contempt for Mother is growing. It's through him that I try to preserve the last bit of family feeling I still have.<br><b>H.</b> Sometimes I think God is testing me, now and for the future. I'll have to become good without a guide, without advice. Maybe that'll make me stronger in the end.<br><b>I.</b> It's different with Father. I love him more than anyone, and when he praises Margot for being so clever, sweet, and pretty, I feel this ache inside. I want to be loved for who I am – Anne – not just because I'm his child.<br><b>J.</b> Last night proved it again. Margot had been reading a book with beautiful pictures, put it down for a moment, and I picked it up. She returned, saw it in my hands, scowled, and snatched it back, accusingly. I still wanted to look, but she got angrier. Of course, Mother sided with her: <i>\"Margot was reading that; give it back.\"</i>","accept":["A"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Text A (\"she's not a mother to me … I know what a mother should be, and I don't see any of it in her\") is followed by \"I keep telling myself to focus on her good qualities, to find in myself what she lacks\"."},{"n":104,"part":"PART 2. READING","section":"Reading","type":"text","passageId":6,"q":"Seven pieces of text have been removed from the diary excerpt. Choose from the texts A – J the one which fits gap <b>(104)</b>. There are THREE extra pieces of text that you do not need to use. Write the letter.<br><b>A.</b> We're opposites, she and I. I'm not trying to judge her, but I see that she's not a mother to me. I have to mother myself. I've let go of any hope in her and am charting my own path. I know what a mother should be, and I don't see any of it in her.<br><b>B.</b> Margot never has to explain herself. She could break something or say something cruel, and no one would mind. But when I make the tiniest mistake, all eyes turn on me. I'm tired of tiptoeing around everyone else's moods. Why can't they try to understand mine for once?<br><b>C.</b> But I wasn't angry – just sad. It was unfair of Father to pass judgment without understanding the situation. I would've given the book back on my own, without all the fuss.<br><b>D.</b> They're not consistent with me. One moment I'm called sensible and deserving of honesty, the next a silly goose who thinks she knows it all from books. I'm not the little darling they used to laugh at. I have thoughts, plans, ideals – but I can't yet express them properly.<br><b>E.</b> I sometimes wonder if they'd rather I just disappeared into the walls and stopped being a nuisance altogether.<br><b>F.</b> I just wish – just once – I could see a result. Or hear one word of encouragement from someone who truly loves me.<br><b>G.</b> I'm not jealous of Margot. I don't envy her looks or brains. I just want to feel that I matter, that Father sees me, understands me. But he doesn't see the difference in how he treats us. I hold on to him because my contempt for Mother is growing. It's through him that I try to preserve the last bit of family feeling I still have.<br><b>H.</b> Sometimes I think God is testing me, now and for the future. I'll have to become good without a guide, without advice. Maybe that'll make me stronger in the end.<br><b>I.</b> It's different with Father. I love him more than anyone, and when he praises Margot for being so clever, sweet, and pretty, I feel this ache inside. I want to be loved for who I am – Anne – not just because I'm his child.<br><b>J.</b> Last night proved it again. Margot had been reading a book with beautiful pictures, put it down for a moment, and I picked it up. She returned, saw it in my hands, scowled, and snatched it back, accusingly. I still wanted to look, but she got angrier. Of course, Mother sided with her: <i>\"Margot was reading that; give it back.\"</i>","accept":["H"],"answer":"H","explanation":"Text H (\"I'll have to become good without a guide, without advice\") is answered by \"But for the time being, who else can I turn to but you, Kitty?\"."},{"n":105,"part":"PART 2. READING","section":"Reading","type":"text","passageId":6,"q":"Seven pieces of text have been removed from the diary excerpt. Choose from the texts A – J the one which fits gap <b>(105)</b>. There are THREE extra pieces of text that you do not need to use. Write the letter.<br><b>A.</b> We're opposites, she and I. I'm not trying to judge her, but I see that she's not a mother to me. I have to mother myself. I've let go of any hope in her and am charting my own path. I know what a mother should be, and I don't see any of it in her.<br><b>B.</b> Margot never has to explain herself. She could break something or say something cruel, and no one would mind. But when I make the tiniest mistake, all eyes turn on me. I'm tired of tiptoeing around everyone else's moods. Why can't they try to understand mine for once?<br><b>C.</b> But I wasn't angry – just sad. It was unfair of Father to pass judgment without understanding the situation. I would've given the book back on my own, without all the fuss.<br><b>D.</b> They're not consistent with me. One moment I'm called sensible and deserving of honesty, the next a silly goose who thinks she knows it all from books. I'm not the little darling they used to laugh at. I have thoughts, plans, ideals – but I can't yet express them properly.<br><b>E.</b> I sometimes wonder if they'd rather I just disappeared into the walls and stopped being a nuisance altogether.<br><b>F.</b> I just wish – just once – I could see a result. Or hear one word of encouragement from someone who truly loves me.<br><b>G.</b> I'm not jealous of Margot. I don't envy her looks or brains. I just want to feel that I matter, that Father sees me, understands me. But he doesn't see the difference in how he treats us. I hold on to him because my contempt for Mother is growing. It's through him that I try to preserve the last bit of family feeling I still have.<br><b>H.</b> Sometimes I think God is testing me, now and for the future. I'll have to become good without a guide, without advice. Maybe that'll make me stronger in the end.<br><b>I.</b> It's different with Father. I love him more than anyone, and when he praises Margot for being so clever, sweet, and pretty, I feel this ache inside. I want to be loved for who I am – Anne – not just because I'm his child.<br><b>J.</b> Last night proved it again. Margot had been reading a book with beautiful pictures, put it down for a moment, and I picked it up. She returned, saw it in my hands, scowled, and snatched it back, accusingly. I still wanted to look, but she got angrier. Of course, Mother sided with her: <i>\"Margot was reading that; give it back.\"</i>","accept":["F"],"answer":"F","explanation":"Text F (\"I just wish … I could see a result. Or hear one word of encouragement\") leads to \"So don't condemn me, Kitty\" after the promise to keep going and swallow her tears."},{"n":106,"part":"PART 3. WRITING","section":"Word form","type":"text","passageId":7,"q":"(106) These tools have significantly improved the efficiency of ______ (HUNT), such as when Chinese authorities successfully tracked a fugitive in a crowd of 60,000 concertgoers within minutes.","accept":["manhunts","manhunt"],"answer":"manhunts","explanation":"Tracking a fugitive in a crowd is a \"manhunt\"; the plural noun is needed after \"the efficiency of\"."},{"n":107,"part":"PART 3. WRITING","section":"Word form","type":"text","passageId":7,"q":"(107) Chinese authorities successfully tracked a fugitive in a crowd of 60,000 ______ (CONCERT) within minutes.","accept":["concertgoers","concert-goers","concert goers"],"answer":"concertgoers","explanation":"A crowd of 60,000 people at a concert = \"concertgoers\"."},{"n":108,"part":"PART 3. WRITING","section":"Word form","type":"text","passageId":7,"q":"(108) The U.S. National Security Agency (NSA) employs AI to analyze vast amounts of global digital communication, identifying potential threats related to espionage and ______ (TERROR).","accept":["terrorism"],"answer":"terrorism","explanation":"A noun parallel to \"espionage\": threats related to \"terrorism\"."},{"n":109,"part":"PART 3. WRITING","section":"Word form","type":"text","passageId":7,"q":"(109) AI systems detect linguistic patterns and unusual metadata in emails or phone calls that human analysts might ______ (LOOK), bolstering a nation's information defense amidst international tensions.","accept":["overlook"],"answer":"overlook","explanation":"\"Overlook\" = fail to notice; AI catches what human analysts might miss."},{"n":110,"part":"PART 3. WRITING","section":"Word form","type":"text","passageId":7,"q":"(110) AI also supports ______ (COVER) police operations.","accept":["undercover"],"answer":"undercover","explanation":"\"Undercover police operations\" = secret operations by disguised officers."},{"n":111,"part":"PART 3. WRITING","section":"Word form","type":"text","passageId":7,"q":"(111) Law enforcement in the UK has used AI to assist in fighting ______ (CRIME), such as online sexual harassment and grooming.","accept":["cybercrime","cybercrimes","cyber-crime","cyber crime"],"answer":"cybercrime","explanation":"Online harassment and grooming are examples of \"cybercrime\"."},{"n":112,"part":"PART 3. WRITING","section":"Word form","type":"text","passageId":7,"q":"(112) However, along with its just use, there are a plethora of ethical concerns. In ______ (TOTAL) regimes, AI may be used to stem dissent.","accept":["totalitarian"],"answer":"totalitarian","explanation":"\"Totalitarian regimes\" = governments with total control that suppress dissent."},{"n":113,"part":"PART 3. WRITING","section":"Word form","type":"text","passageId":7,"q":"(113) AI may be used to stem dissent, turning even a trusted ______ (CONFIDE) like close friends and family into unknowing spies for the police.","accept":["confidant","confidante"],"answer":"confidant","explanation":"A \"confidant\" is a person you confide in — a trusted close friend or family member."},{"n":114,"part":"PART 3. WRITING","section":"Word form","type":"text","passageId":7,"q":"(114) …turning even a trusted confidant like close friends and family into ______ (KNOW) spies for the police.","accept":["unknowing"],"answer":"unknowing","explanation":"\"Unknowing spies\" = people who spy without realising it."},{"n":115,"part":"PART 3. WRITING","section":"Word form","type":"text","passageId":7,"q":"(115) Furthermore, the growing popularity of generative art and deep-fakes assist the ______ (FORGE) of evidence, which muddles critical judicial decisions.","accept":["forgery","forging"],"answer":"forgery","explanation":"A noun after \"the\": \"the forgery of evidence\" = making fake evidence."},{"n":116,"part":"PART 3. WRITING","section":"Error correction","type":"text","passageId":8,"q":"Line 2: \"While industries often <b>resist to</b> new regulations\" — write the correction.","accept":["resist","resist new regulations"],"answer":"resist","explanation":"\"Resist\" is a transitive verb and takes a direct object with no preposition: \"resist new regulations\"."},{"n":117,"part":"PART 3. WRITING","section":"Error correction","type":"text","passageId":8,"q":"Line 6: \"increase investment in renewable energy and <b>phase</b> fossil fuel subsidies\" — write the correction.","accept":["phase out","phase out fossil fuel subsidies"],"answer":"phase out","explanation":"The phrasal verb is \"phase out\" = gradually stop using; \"phase\" alone has no such meaning."},{"n":118,"part":"PART 3. WRITING","section":"Error correction","type":"text","passageId":8,"q":"Line 6: \"<b>Without comprehensive approach</b>, efforts to mitigate…\" — write the correction.","accept":["Without a comprehensive approach","a comprehensive approach","without a comprehensive"],"answer":"Without a comprehensive approach","explanation":"\"Approach\" is a singular countable noun and needs the article: \"without a comprehensive approach\"."},{"n":119,"part":"PART 3. WRITING","section":"Error correction","type":"text","passageId":8,"q":"Line 7: \"efforts to mitigate <b>climate changes</b> are unlikely to succeed\" — write the correction.","accept":["climate change","mitigate climate change"],"answer":"climate change","explanation":"\"Climate change\" is an uncountable noun and is not pluralised."},{"n":120,"part":"PART 3. WRITING","section":"Error correction","type":"text","passageId":8,"q":"Line 8–9: \"plays a vital role <b>to address</b> global challenges\" — write the correction.","accept":["in addressing","in addressing global challenges","role in addressing"],"answer":"in addressing","explanation":"The collocation is \"play a role in + V-ing\": \"plays a vital role in addressing global challenges\"."},{"n":121,"part":"PART 3. WRITING","section":"Error correction","type":"text","passageId":8,"q":"Line 10: \"depends on both political <b>willness</b> and public support\" — write the correction.","accept":["will","political will","willingness","political willingness"],"answer":"will","explanation":"\"Willness\" is not a word; the idiom is \"political will\" (determination of politicians to act)."},{"n":122,"part":"PART 3. WRITING","section":"Sentence transformation","type":"text","q":"Remember to complete all required fields before submitting your assignment. (SHOULD)<br>→ Not ______ submit your assignment.","accept":["until you complete all required fields should you","until all required fields are completed should you","until you have completed all required fields should you","until all required fields have been completed should you","until you have filled in all required fields should you","until you fill in all required fields should you","until all the required fields are completed should you","until you complete all the required fields should you","until all required fields are complete should you","until you complete all required fields, should you"],"answer":"until you complete all required fields should you","explanation":"\"Not until + clause + should + S + V\" — inversion after a fronted negative time clause: \"Not until you complete all required fields should you submit…\"."},{"n":123,"part":"PART 3. WRITING","section":"Sentence transformation","type":"text","q":"Volunteering gives you the opportunities to develop yourself, so don't avoid them. (SHY)<br>→ Try not to ______ opportunities.","accept":["shy away from volunteering","shy away from volunteering as it gives you","shy away from volunteering which gives you","shy away from volunteering because it gives you","shy away from volunteering since it gives you","shy away from volunteering and its","shy away from volunteering and its self-development","shy away from the volunteering","shy away from such volunteering"],"answer":"shy away from volunteering","explanation":"\"Shy away from something\" = avoid it because of nervousness or lack of confidence. The stem already ends in \"opportunities\", so the blank is just \"shy away from volunteering\" → \"Try not to shy away from volunteering opportunities.\""},{"n":124,"part":"PART 3. WRITING","section":"Sentence transformation","type":"text","q":"My mother taught me to be tolerant of others who are different from us. (LET)<br>→ It was from my mother ______ live.","accept":["that I learned to live and let","that I learnt to live and let","I learned to live and let","I learnt to live and let","that I was taught to live and let","that I learned the lesson to live and let","that I learned how to live and let"],"answer":"that I learned to live and let","explanation":"Cleft sentence + the idiom \"live and let live\" (be tolerant of others): \"It was from my mother that I learned to live and let live\"."},{"n":125,"part":"PART 3. WRITING","section":"Sentence transformation","type":"text","q":"They lost twice in a row, so it is certain they would be eliminated. (FOREGONE)<br>→ With two ______ .","accept":["consecutive losses, their elimination was a foregone conclusion","consecutive defeats, their elimination was a foregone conclusion","successive losses, their elimination was a foregone conclusion","successive defeats, their elimination was a foregone conclusion","straight losses, their elimination was a foregone conclusion","straight defeats, their elimination was a foregone conclusion","consecutive losses, their elimination is a foregone conclusion","consecutive defeats, their elimination is a foregone conclusion","consecutive losses, their elimination became a foregone conclusion","losses in a row, their elimination was a foregone conclusion","defeats in a row, their elimination was a foregone conclusion","consecutive losses, their being eliminated was a foregone conclusion"],"answer":"consecutive losses, their elimination was a foregone conclusion","explanation":"\"A foregone conclusion\" = a result that is certain; \"twice in a row\" = \"two consecutive losses\"."},{"n":126,"part":"PART 3. WRITING","section":"Sentence transformation","type":"text","q":"Jack did little besides occasionally visiting his elderly mother's nursing home. (EVERY)<br>→ Visiting her nursing home ______ for his elderly mother.","accept":["every now and then was all Jack did","every now and then was all that Jack did","every so often was all Jack did","every so often was all that Jack did","every once in a while was all Jack did","every once in a while was all that Jack did","every now and again was all Jack did","every now and again was all that Jack did","every now and then was about all Jack did","every now and then was almost all Jack did","every so often was about all Jack did"],"answer":"every now and then was all Jack did","explanation":"\"Every now and then / every so often\" = occasionally; \"was all (that) Jack did\" = he did little besides that."},{"n":127,"part":"PART 3. WRITING","section":"Sentence transformation","type":"text","q":"We did not really expect that our little daughter instantly loved chemistry. (WATER)<br>→ That our little daughter took ______ us by surprise.","accept":["to chemistry like a duck to water took","to chemistry like a duck to water caught","to chemistry like a duck to water really took","to chemistry like a duck to water rather took","to chemistry like a duck to water quite took","to chemistry like a duck to water somewhat took","to chemistry like a duck to water instantly took","to chemistry like a duck to water has taken","to chemistry like a duck to water had taken"],"answer":"to chemistry like a duck to water took","explanation":"\"Take to something like a duck to water\" = learn or start to enjoy it very easily and quickly; the that-clause is the subject of \"took us by surprise\"."},{"n":128,"part":"PART 3. WRITING","section":"Sentence transformation","type":"text","q":"What he said was mostly pseudoscience, but some of it was true. (GRAIN)<br>→ While what he said ______ pseudoscience.","accept":["had a grain of truth, it was mostly","contained a grain of truth, it was mostly","held a grain of truth, it was mostly","had a grain of truth, it was largely","contained a grain of truth, it was largely","had a grain of truth, it was mainly","contained a grain of truth, it was mainly","had a grain of truth in it, it was mostly","contained a grain of truth, most of it was","had a grain of truth, most of it was","had a grain of truth to it, it was mostly"],"answer":"had a grain of truth, it was mostly","explanation":"\"A grain of truth\" = a small amount of truth; \"while\" gives the concession and \"it was mostly pseudoscience\" restates the first clause."},{"n":129,"part":"PART 3. WRITING","section":"Sentence transformation","type":"text","q":"The team did not make it to the finals because they were not performing at their best. (CYLINDERS)<br>→ Had the team been ______ it to the finals.","accept":["firing on all cylinders, they would have made","firing on all cylinders, it would have made","firing on all cylinders, they would've made","firing on all cylinders, they could have made","firing on all cylinders, they might have made","firing on all cylinders, they would have made","firing on all cylinders they would have made"],"answer":"firing on all cylinders, they would have made","explanation":"\"Fire on all cylinders\" = perform at one's best; inverted third conditional \"Had the team been …, they would have made it\"."},{"n":130,"part":"PART 3. WRITING","section":"Sentence transformation","type":"text","q":"Smoking became a habit for him, which severely affected his lungs. (TOLL)<br>→ Becoming ______ his lungs.","accept":["a habitual smoker took a heavy toll on","a habitual smoker took a severe toll on","a habitual smoker took a serious toll on","a habitual smoker took a heavy toll upon","a habitual smoker took a terrible toll on","a habitual smoker took a huge toll on","a habitual smoker took its toll on","a habitual smoker took a toll on","a heavy smoker took a heavy toll on","a heavy smoker took a severe toll on","a heavy smoker took a serious toll on","a heavy smoker took its toll on","a heavy smoker took a toll on","a regular smoker took a heavy toll on","a regular smoker took a severe toll on","a regular smoker took its toll on","a chain smoker took a heavy toll on","a chain smoker took its toll on","a chain smoker took a severe toll on","a smoker took a heavy toll on","a smoker took a severe toll on","a smoker took its toll on","addicted to smoking took a heavy toll on","addicted to smoking took a severe toll on","addicted to smoking took its toll on","hooked on smoking took a heavy toll on","a habitual smoker has taken a heavy toll on","a habitual smoker has taken a severe toll on","a habitual smoker has taken its toll on","a habit, smoking took a heavy toll on","a habit, smoking took a severe toll on","a habit, smoking took a serious toll on","a habit, smoking took its toll on","a habit, smoking took a toll on","a habit, smoking has taken a heavy toll on","a habit, smoking has taken its toll on"],"answer":"a habitual smoker took a heavy toll on","explanation":"\"Take a (heavy) toll on something\" = cause serious harm to it. Two readings fit the stem: \"Becoming a habitual smoker took a heavy toll on his lungs\" (gerund subject), or \"Becoming a habit, smoking took a heavy toll on his lungs\" (participle clause modifying \"smoking\")."},{"n":131,"part":"PART 3. WRITING","section":"Sentence transformation","type":"text","q":"The restaurant owner gave us complimentary champagne. (HOUSE)<br>→ We were ______ restaurant owner.","accept":["given champagne on the house by the","offered champagne on the house by the","served champagne on the house by the","given some champagne on the house by the","offered some champagne on the house by the","treated to champagne on the house by the","given free champagne on the house by the","given champagne, on the house, by the"],"answer":"given champagne on the house by the","explanation":"\"On the house\" = free, paid for by the establishment; passive \"we were given … by the restaurant owner\"."}]},{"id":"ptnk-2024-kc","year":2024,"track":"kc","title":"PTNK 2024 · Tiếng Anh Không chuyên","subtitle":"Đề thi tuyển sinh lớp 10 · Trường Phổ thông Năng khiếu · 2024-2025","durationMin":60,"keySource":"solved","source":"PTNK 2024 - Anh KC - Đề thi.pdf","passages":["<b>BEAUTY</b><br><br><b>1</b> The search for beauty spans centuries and continents. Paintings of Egyptians dating back over 4,000 years show both men and women painting their nails and wearing makeup. In 18th century France, wealthy noblemen wore large wigs of long white hair to make themselves attractive. In cultures throughout the world, people have gone to extreme lengths to achieve the goal of beauty. <b>[A]</b><br><br><b>2</b> Today, people continue to devote a lot of time and money to their appearance. According to a recent report, one out of three consumers globally say they are spending more money today on beauty and health care products than ever before. Worldwide, sales of makeup, dieting, hair and skin-care products, as well as gym memberships and cosmetic surgery, generate billions of dollars every year. Beauty is power. Studies suggest that good-looking people not only make more money, they also get picked first for opportunities and are perceived as friendlier. <b>[B]</b><br><br><b>3</b> But what exactly is beauty? Trying to define it is difficult, and yet we know it when we see it, or so we think. \"Beauty is health,\" says one psychologist. \"It's a billboard saying 'I'm healthy. I can pass on your genes.\" In studies done by psychologists at New Mexico State University, men regularly showed a preference for women with certain features: larger eyes, clear skin, fuller lips, and a smaller nose and chin. Other studies suggest that women prefer men with large shoulders and a narrow waist. According to scientists, the mind tells us that <b>these</b> equal health and genetic well-being. In other words, it's a fundamental part of human nature to look for such qualities in a mate. <b>[C]</b><br><br><b>4</b> Not everyone agrees with this notion, however. \"Our natural hardwired-ness can be altered by all sorts of expectations, predominantly cultural,\" says C. Loring Brace, an anthropologist at the University of Michigan. What is viewed as attractive in one culture might not be in another. Look in most Western fashion magazines, for example, and the women on the pages are thin. But is this the \"perfect\" body type for women worldwide? Beauty standards are subjective and vary around the world. Yu and Shepard found in one study that while being overweight is undesirable in Western cultures, for native peoples in southeast Peru, this is not the case. <b>[D]</b><br><br><b>5</b> For better or worse, beauty plays a role in our lives, but it is extremely difficult to define exactly what makes one person attractive to another. Although there do seem to be certain physical traits that are considered universally appealing, it is also true that beauty does not always conform to a single, uniform standard. A person's cultural background, for example, may influence what they find attractive in others. In the end, beauty really is, as the saying goes, in the eye of the <b>beholder</b>.","Blended learning is an innovative (29)____ approach that combines traditional classroom methods with digital and online media. In this learning environment, students experience the best of both worlds: face-to-face interaction with teachers and peers, and the flexibility of online learning. This method allows for a more personalized learning experience that can cater to individual students' needs. For instance, in a typical blended learning setup, students might attend in-person classes where they engage in discussions or work on group projects and activities. (30)____, at home, they complete online assignments and access resources through the internet.<br><br>This combination not only helps reinforce the material learned in class but also encourages students to be more responsible for their own learning. Additionally, blended learning can be suitable for different learning styles, increasing personal engagement. Visual learners, for example, might benefit from interactive video lessons, (31)____ those who prefer more practice can re-watch learning content and redo exercise as much as they want. Moreover, this approach prepares students for the increasingly digital world they will encounter in higher education and the workforce. (32)____ integrating technology with traditional teaching, blended learning creates a dynamic environment that develops both academic competences as well as digital literacy."],"questions":[{"n":1,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Phonetics","type":"mcq","q":"Choose the word whose underlined part pronounces differently from the other three.","options":["path<u>s</u>","high<u>s</u>","bath<u>s</u>","laugh<u>s</u>"],"correct":1,"explanation":"After the voiced sound in \"high\" the ending is /z/ (highs /haɪz/); after the voiceless /θ/ and /f/ of paths, baths, laughs it is /s/."},{"n":2,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Phonetics","type":"mcq","q":"Choose the word whose underlined part pronounces differently from the other three.","options":["p<u>oo</u>l","sch<u>oo</u>l","w<u>oo</u>l","t<u>oo</u>l"],"correct":2,"explanation":"\"wool\" has the short /ʊ/ (/wʊl/); pool, school and tool all have the long /uː/."},{"n":3,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Stress","type":"mcq","q":"Choose the word whose primary stress position differs from the other three.","options":["animal","hospital","chemical","official"],"correct":3,"explanation":"of-FI-cial is stressed on the second syllable; A-nimal, HOS-pital and CHE-mical are stressed on the first."},{"n":4,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Stress","type":"mcq","q":"Choose the word whose primary stress position differs from the other three.","options":["camel","hotel","hostel","channel"],"correct":1,"explanation":"ho-TEL is stressed on the second syllable; CA-mel, HOS-tel and CHAN-nel on the first."},{"n":5,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"My sister ______ for hours, and I'm worried she will overwork herself.","options":["has studied","will have studied","is studying","has been studying"],"correct":3,"explanation":"An action that started in the past and is still going on (\"for hours\"), with emphasis on its duration, takes the present perfect continuous."},{"n":6,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Thang Long Imperial Citadel is a cultural ____ of historical buildings from the 11<sup>th</sup> century.","options":["monument","landscape","complex","heritage"],"correct":2,"explanation":"A \"complex\" is a group of buildings on one site; \"a cultural complex of historical buildings\" is the natural collocation."},{"n":7,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"According to Vietnamese traditions, children need to care ______ their parents in old age.","options":["with","of","about","for"],"correct":3,"explanation":"\"care for somebody\" means to look after them; \"care about\" only means to be concerned."},{"n":8,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"I think this event will be cancelled because ______ people have shown interests.","options":["a little","few","little","a few"],"correct":1,"explanation":"\"people\" is a plural countable noun, and the negative sense (not enough → cancelled) needs \"few\", not \"a few\"."},{"n":9,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"______ the only authentic ramen restaurant in town, Ittou Ramen is always packed with customers.","options":["To be","Being","It is","Having been"],"correct":1,"explanation":"A present participle clause (\"Being …\") gives the reason for the main clause and shares its subject, Ittou Ramen."},{"n":10,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"It _____ Mrs. Allen that you saw because she passed away last month.","options":["mustn't have been","can't have been","can't be","mustn't be"],"correct":1,"explanation":"\"can't have + past participle\" expresses certainty that something in the past was impossible; \"mustn't have\" is not used for deduction."},{"n":11,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Tam: 'Are you coming to the movie with us this weekend?'<br>Huy: '______.'","options":["That's out of the question.","If I were you, I would.","Thanks, I think it's great.","I wouldn't miss it for anything."],"correct":3,"explanation":"\"I wouldn't miss it for anything\" is the idiomatic enthusiastic \"yes\" to an invitation; the other replies do not answer the question."},{"n":12,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"I _____ a very interesting book in the library, but when I came back the next day it was gone.","options":["searched for","looked up","came across","ran into"],"correct":2,"explanation":"\"come across\" = find by chance; \"run into\" is used for meeting people, and \"search for\"/\"look up\" do not fit finding a book unexpectedly."},{"n":13,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"The ____ shows that we are going to visit Beijing's Forbidden City on the third day of our trip.","options":["itinerary","tour guide","reservation","destination"],"correct":0,"explanation":"An itinerary is the planned route and schedule of a journey, day by day."},{"n":14,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Neither me nor my mom ______ where my dad took the car.","options":["don't know","knows","doesn't know","know"],"correct":1,"explanation":"With \"neither … nor\" the verb agrees with the nearer subject (\"my mom\" → knows); \"neither\" already makes the sentence negative, so no \"don't/doesn't\"."},{"n":15,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Elizabeth participates in many club activities _____ improving her resume for college.","options":["so that","so as to","in order to","with a view to"],"correct":3,"explanation":"\"with a view to + V-ing\" expresses purpose; \"so as to\" and \"in order to\" take a bare infinitive and \"so that\" takes a clause."},{"n":16,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Error correction","type":"mcq","q":"It was (A) reported that people (B) had been queuing in front of the (C) soon opening sneaker's store since (D) yesterday.","options":["reported","had been queuing","soon","yesterday"],"correct":2,"explanation":"(C) is the error: the adverb \"soon\" cannot pre-modify a noun phrase — it should be \"the soon-to-open / upcoming sneaker store\" (or \"the store opening soon\"). \"had been queuing … since yesterday\" is correct: after the past reporting verb \"It was reported that\", the present perfect continuous is backshifted to the past perfect continuous."},{"n":17,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Error correction","type":"mcq","q":"The computer (A) that I (B) had my friend (C) repaired last month just (D) broke down again.","options":["that","had","repaired","broke down"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Causative \"have somebody do something\" takes the bare infinitive: \"had my friend repair\"."},{"n":18,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Error correction","type":"mcq","q":"(A) Would you have any (B) questions, do not hesitate (C) to contact us via email or (D) on social media.","options":["Would","questions","to contact","on"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The inverted conditional \"If you should have any questions\" becomes \"Should you have any questions\", not \"Would\"."},{"n":19,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Error correction","type":"mcq","q":"Children, (A) no matter how young, (B) ought to be taught to help (C) others in times of (D) needs.","options":["no matter","ought to","others","needs"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The fixed expression is \"in times of need\" (uncountable), not \"needs\"."},{"n":20,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Error correction","type":"mcq","q":"All the rats (A) had escaped successfully (B) by the time the cat (C) had realized what (D) was going on.","options":["had escaped","by","had realized","was"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The clause after \"by the time\" takes the simple past (\"realized\"); the past perfect belongs to the earlier action \"had escaped\"."},{"n":21,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Reading","type":"mcq","passageId":0,"q":"Which is the main idea of the passage?","options":["Beauty is becoming more important in today's world.","Scientists have found an explanation for certain beauty standards.","There are practical benefits to being beautiful.","Beauty is important but not easy to define."],"correct":3,"explanation":"The passage opens with beauty's importance across cultures and closes with \"beauty plays a role in our lives, but it is extremely difficult to define\"."},{"n":22,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Reading","type":"mcq","passageId":0,"q":"What is the author's purpose in <b>paragraph 1</b>?","options":["To explain the reason why beauty is so important.","To describe the search for beauty in some cultures.","To question the beauty standards in some cultures.","To illustrate that beauty has been important everywhere for a long time."],"correct":3,"explanation":"\"The search for beauty spans centuries and continents\" — the Egyptian and French examples show beauty has mattered everywhere for thousands of years."},{"n":23,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Reading","type":"mcq","passageId":0,"q":"What is <b>TRUE</b> about the search for beauty in today's world?","options":["People from certain countries care more about beauty.","Being beautiful today mostly benefits a person's career.","The beauty industry is a massive one.","The variety of beauty products and services is increasing."],"correct":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 2: sales of makeup, dieting, hair and skin-care products, gym memberships and cosmetic surgery \"generate billions of dollars every year\"."},{"n":24,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Reading","type":"mcq","passageId":0,"q":"It can be inferred from <b>paragraph 3</b> that …","options":["There may be genetic advantages behind beauty.","Large features are more preferred by both men and women.","Men are more selective than women when it comes to their partner's beauty.","Beautiful people live healthier lives."],"correct":0,"explanation":"\"Beauty is health … I can pass on your genes\" and \"these equal health and genetic well-being\" — attractive features signal genetic advantages."},{"n":25,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Reading","type":"mcq","passageId":0,"q":"The word '<b>these</b>' in <b>paragraph 3</b> refers to …","options":["men and women","male preference","certain features","other studies"],"correct":2,"explanation":"\"these equal health and genetic well-being\" refers back to the physical features listed (larger eyes, clear skin, fuller lips, large shoulders…)."},{"n":26,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Reading","type":"mcq","passageId":0,"q":"What can be inferred from the example in <b>paragraph 4</b>?","options":["Magazines have been responsible for creating some beauty standards.","Western cultures have a great influence on beauty standards worldwide.","Being overweight is attractive in some cultures.","Western beauty standards have recently changed."],"correct":2,"explanation":"Yu and Shepard found that \"while being overweight is undesirable in Western cultures, for native peoples in southeast Peru, this is not the case\"."},{"n":27,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Reading","type":"mcq","passageId":0,"q":"What word is <b><i>CLOSEST</i></b> in meaning to '<b>beholder</b>' in the saying in <b>paragraph 5</b>: <i>'Beauty is in the eye of the beholder?'</i>","options":["owner","culture","public","viewer"],"correct":3,"explanation":"A beholder is the person who looks at something — the viewer; beauty depends on who is looking."},{"n":28,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Reading","type":"mcq","passageId":0,"q":"In which place can the following sentence best fit?<br><b><i>'Take another example: In the Huli culture of Papua New Guinea, men grow their hair long as a symbol of health and strength.'</i></b>","options":["[D]","[C]","[A]","[B]"],"correct":0,"explanation":"\"Take another example\" must follow an explicit example: at [D] it comes straight after \"Yu and Shepard found in one study that … for native peoples in southeast Peru, this is not the case\", and the Huli long-hair custom is another culture-specific beauty standard — exactly paragraph 4's point that what is attractive in one culture might not be in another. At [A] the preceding sentence is a generalisation, not an example."},{"n":29,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":1,"q":"(29) ______","options":["well-educated","education","educator","educational"],"correct":3,"explanation":"An adjective is needed before the noun \"approach\": \"an innovative educational approach\"."},{"n":30,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":1,"q":"(30) ______","options":["Meanwhile","However","Therefore","On the contrary"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The sentence contrasts what happens in class with what happens \"at home\" at the same time — \"Meanwhile\"; there is no contradiction or result."},{"n":31,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":1,"q":"(31) ______","options":["so","but","while","for"],"correct":2,"explanation":"\"while\" contrasts two groups of learners (visual learners … , while those who prefer more practice …) within one sentence."},{"n":32,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":1,"q":"(32) ______","options":["With","By","From","In"],"correct":1,"explanation":"\"By + V-ing\" expresses the means: by integrating technology with traditional teaching, blended learning creates…"},{"n":33,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Reading","type":"mcq","passageId":1,"q":"What is the most suitable title for the passage?","options":["The pros and cons of Blended Learning","An Introduction to Blended Learning","How to maximize the benefits of Blended Learning","Why Blended Learning is the future of education"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The passage defines blended learning, gives an example of how it works and lists its benefits — a general introduction; no drawbacks or maximising tips are given."},{"n":34,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Reading","type":"mcq","passageId":1,"q":"According to the text, what is TRUE about Blended Learning?","options":["The online learning portion gets more focus than traditional learning.","There is more assignment to do at home than normally.","Some types of learner might not like it.","Each student's learning experience might be different from others'."],"correct":3,"explanation":"\"This method allows for a more personalized learning experience that can cater to individual students' needs.\""},{"n":35,"part":"PHẦN 1. TRẮC NGHIỆM","section":"Reading","type":"mcq","passageId":1,"q":"What is NOT an aim of Blended Learning?","options":["Helping students to learn more responsibly","Preparing students for a future digital world","Teaching students necessary skills for the workplace","Equipping students with skills to perform well in school"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The text mentions responsibility for one's own learning, preparation for \"the increasingly digital world\" and \"academic competences\"; it never says blended learning teaches workplace skills — the workforce is only where students will meet that digital world."},{"n":36,"part":"PHẦN 2. TỰ LUẬN","section":"Word form","type":"text","q":"Increased ______ rates helped to eradicate the deadly disease. (VACCINE)","accept":["vaccination"],"answer":"vaccination","explanation":"A noun is needed before \"rates\": vaccination rates."},{"n":37,"part":"PHẦN 2. TỰ LUẬN","section":"Word form","type":"text","q":"The Internet has made education more ______ to people of all economic backgrounds. (ACCESS)","accept":["accessible"],"answer":"accessible","explanation":"\"make something more + adjective\": accessible (easy to reach or use)."},{"n":38,"part":"PHẦN 2. TỰ LUẬN","section":"Word form","type":"text","q":"You can buy kitchen ______ at the electronic store. (APPLY)","accept":["appliances"],"answer":"appliances","explanation":"A plural noun for machines such as fridges and ovens: kitchen appliances."},{"n":39,"part":"PHẦN 2. TỰ LUẬN","section":"Word form","type":"text","q":"Many students ______ applied to help with the charity event. (VOLUNTEER)","accept":["voluntarily"],"answer":"voluntarily","explanation":"An adverb is needed to modify the verb \"applied\": voluntarily."},{"n":40,"part":"PHẦN 2. TỰ LUẬN","section":"Word form","type":"text","q":"It is impossible to finish all these tasks right now, so please ______ the most important ones first. (PRIORITY)","accept":["prioritize","prioritise"],"answer":"prioritize","explanation":"An imperative verb is needed after \"please\": prioritize (deal with the most important first)."},{"n":41,"part":"PHẦN 2. TỰ LUẬN","section":"Word form","type":"text","q":"She was very ______ and kept telling me to quit the competition. (ENCOURAGE)","accept":["discouraging"],"answer":"discouraging","explanation":"Someone who keeps telling you to quit is being discouraging — the -ing adjective describes the effect she has on others, and the sense is negative."},{"n":42,"part":"PHẦN 2. TỰ LUẬN","section":"Word form","type":"text","q":"The mayor is ______ security in the city to prepare for the President's visit. (STRONG)","accept":["strengthening"],"answer":"strengthening","explanation":"\"is + V-ing\" needs the verb strengthen (make stronger): strengthening security."},{"n":43,"part":"PHẦN 2. TỰ LUẬN","section":"Word form","type":"text","q":"______ hopes to educate travellers become more responsible and respectful to nature and the environment. (TOURIST)","accept":["Ecotourism","Eco-tourism"],"answer":"Ecotourism","explanation":"A noun subject is needed, and travel that teaches respect for nature is ecotourism."},{"n":44,"part":"PHẦN 2. TỰ LUẬN","section":"Sentence transformation","type":"text","q":"It was so foggy that we couldn't drive any further. (PREVENTED)<br>→ The thick fog ______ any further.","accept":["prevented us from driving","prevented us from going","prevented us from travelling","prevented us from traveling"],"answer":"prevented us from driving","explanation":"\"prevent somebody from + V-ing\": the thick fog prevented us from driving any further."},{"n":45,"part":"PHẦN 2. TỰ LUẬN","section":"Sentence transformation","type":"text","q":"Katy said to me: \"Henry is not a reliable person.\" (WARNED)<br>→ Katy ______ on Henry.","accept":["warned me not to rely","warned me against relying","warned me not to depend","warned me against depending","warned me not to count"],"answer":"warned me not to rely","explanation":"\"warn somebody not to do / against doing something\"; \"reliable\" becomes the verb \"rely on\"."},{"n":46,"part":"PHẦN 2. TỰ LUẬN","section":"Sentence transformation","type":"text","q":"I couldn't finish the puzzle because I didn't have more time. (HAD)<br>→ If ______ to finish the puzzle.","accept":["I had had more time, I would have been able","I had had more time, I could have been able","I had had more time, I'd have been able","I'd had more time, I would have been able","I'd had more time, I could have been able","I'd had more time, I'd have been able","I had had more time I would have been able","I had had more time I could have been able"],"answer":"I had had more time, I would have been able","explanation":"Third conditional for an unreal past: \"If I had had more time, I would have been able to finish the puzzle.\""},{"n":47,"part":"PHẦN 2. TỰ LUẬN","section":"Sentence transformation","type":"text","q":"Playing in the rain, Tom got a cold. (CAME)<br>→ Tom ______ played in the rain.","accept":["came down with a cold because he","came down with a cold after he","came down with a cold when he","came down with a cold as he","came down with a cold since he"],"answer":"came down with a cold because he","explanation":"\"come down with (an illness)\" = catch it; the participle clause becomes a clause of reason/time: because/after he played in the rain."},{"n":48,"part":"PHẦN 2. TỰ LUẬN","section":"Sentence transformation","type":"text","q":"Your comment during the meeting was unnecessary. (MADE)<br>→ You needn't ______ during the meeting.","accept":["have made that comment","have made the comment","have made your comment","have made a comment","have made any comment","have made such a comment"],"answer":"have made that comment","explanation":"\"needn't have + past participle\" says something that was done in the past was unnecessary: you needn't have made that comment."},{"n":49,"part":"PHẦN 2. TỰ LUẬN","section":"Sentence transformation","type":"text","q":"'Why don't you join our camping trip, Mary?' (PART)<br>→ I suggest ______ our camping trip.","accept":["Mary should take part in","Mary take part in","that Mary should take part in","that Mary take part in","Mary takes part in","that Mary takes part in","Mary taking part in","Mary's taking part in"],"answer":"Mary should take part in","explanation":"\"suggest (that) somebody (should) + bare infinitive\"; \"join\" becomes \"take part in\"."},{"n":50,"part":"PHẦN 2. TỰ LUẬN","section":"Sentence transformation","type":"text","q":"If you have more friends, you will be invited to hang out more. (INVITATIONS)<br>→ The more ______ to hang out you will receive.","accept":["friends you have, the more invitations","friends you have the more invitations","friends you have, the more the invitations"],"answer":"friends you have, the more invitations","explanation":"Double comparative \"the more …, the more …\": the more friends you have, the more invitations to hang out you will receive."}]},{"id":"ptnk-2024-chuyen","year":2024,"track":"chuyen","title":"PTNK 2024 · Tiếng Anh Chuyên","subtitle":"Đề thi tuyển sinh lớp 10 · Trường Phổ thông Năng khiếu · 2024-2025","durationMin":120,"keySource":"solved","source":"PTNK 2024 - Tiếng Anh Chuyên - Đề thi & Phiếu trả lời tự luận.pdf","passages":["'Divorced, Beheaded, Died, Divorced, Beheaded, Survived' goes the famous rhyme that (51)____ the fates of King Henry VIII's six wives. The story of King Henry VIII's six wives has been told for centuries, but never before has it been heard in the way 'SIX' shares it. A new Broadway musical show, 'SIX' gives (52)____ and tune to the women who served as queens in a modern and upbeat way.<br><br>Telling the story of King Henry's wives, some of whom were killed by order of the king himself, the show does not, however, feature a character playing the king. (53)____, it is centered on the women, allowing them to tell their own stories through music.<br><br>The entire 80-minute production takes place on a (54)____ that appears to be a concert stage. The Tudor queens and the band, known as the 'Ladies in Waiting,' are on stage the entire time. Despite being based on historical events and characters, 'SIX's take on the Tudor era is heavily fictionalized. (55)____ is clear the moment the actresses stepped out in bejeweled and glittering outfits, and the stage, with modern (56)____ like bass guitar and keyboard, feels more at home at a pop concert than a historical play.<br><br>\"It can catch you off guard,\" said Sterlyn Termine, a band member. \"As soon as the curtains open and you see guitar, a drum set and keyboards, you will realize that this is going to sound a lot more modern than you thought.\" Still, (57)____ the era and region the storyline takes place in, there are hints of the music from 500 years ago. \"There is harpsichord, but it is a synthesized harpsichord, and one of the songs is polka, German-folk inspired that is accompanied by dubstep and airhorn sounds. It is a creative and clever fusion,\" Termine said. Each queen also has hints of modern-day celebrities, dubbed 'Queenspirations,' with their music, intertwined into their characters and songs, paying (58)____ to 'pop queens' the likes of Beyoncé, Adele, and Ariana Grande.<br><br>The show is high energy from start to finish and does not include an intermission. Many of the (59)____ loved how it was fast-paced and shorter than some other productions, adding that though they didn't know what to expect (60)____ the show, they left highly impressed. \"We thought we booked the wrong show, but as the curtains closed, me and my family were all clapping and cheering.\" Said a member in attendance.<br><br>(Dillon Thomas, for CBS News)","Roughly two-thirds of the world's cocoa is grown in West Africa, (61)____ of it concentrating in Ivory Coast and Ghana, and farmers there have faced extreme weather brought by changing climate (62)____ for a few years: heavy rains and flooding, high winds during the dry season. (63)____ has worsened crop disease and road conditions, which disrupts bean deliveries to ports.<br><br>The International Cocoa Organization forecasts that global cocoa supply will decline by almost 11% this season. Other cocoa-growing countries have been unable to fill the (64)____ because ramping up production is expensive and (65)____; it takes years for newly planted trees to produce cocoa beans. This extensive shortfall of cocoa has (66)____ buyers scrambling, pushing prices up, and the historic rally has drawn in speculators, exacerbating the price volatility. Normally, if prices are very high, people start producing more of that commodity, but that is not the (67)____ with cocoa. Thus, these prices are going to stay higher than they were for a while.<br><br>The damaging weather also compounded other ongoing problems. Trees in West Africa have been aging and yielding less cocoa; farmers in Ghana and Ivory Coast have struggled to battle pests and disease. In fact, those farmers rarely benefit from the surging cost of cocoa on the (68)____, because they typically pre-sell the beans at agreed-upon prices in (69)____. Unfortunately, the amounts they earn for selling their cocoa cannot (70)____ up for the costs to replant, buy fertilizers, buy pesticides, etc. The sourcing of cocoa and the model for producing and trading cocoa in the world is not a sustainable model.","If you've seen the movie or television show M*A*S*H, then you've seen an 'ambulance.' No, not those trucks that bring patients to hospitals. Back then, the field hospital (71)____ was the ambulance. It brought the care to patients, not the other way around. Just look at the word. It has the same (72)____ as words like \"ambulatory\" or \"amble.\" (73)____ essence, the word means \"walking.\" The French term from which the English (74)____ was \"hôpital ambulant,\" but English dropped \"hôpital.\" The emphasis here was on walking and movement.<br><br>(75)____ came the Crimean War (1853–1856). Sometimes considered the first \"modern\" war, this struggle for control of the former Ottoman Empire was the first to use railroads and telegraphs tactically. In (76)____ to that, the Crimean War was when people began to (77)____ to ambulances as the conveyances moving troops from the field to places where their injuries were (78)____. This change likely occurred because the war had so many different theaters. Field hospitals couldn't possibly have (79)____ up with all of the battles. Thus, ambulances took you <i>to</i> the hospital instead of <i>being</i> the hospital. Field hospitals still exist today, of (80)____, but they're no longer called ambulances.","<b>A.</b> Like a naturalist conducting a tour of the jungle, he led the way to a busy intersection in the center of town, where several odd things immediately became clear. Not only was it virtually naked, stripped of all lights, signs, and road markings, but there was no division between road and sidewalk. It was, basically, a bare brick square.<br><br>But in spite of the apparently <b><i>anarchical</i></b> layout, the traffic, a steady stream of trucks, cars, buses, motorcycles, bicycles, and pedestrians, moved along fluidly and easily, as if directed by an invisible conductor. When Mr. Monderman, a traffic engineer and the intersection's proud designer, deliberately failed to check for oncoming traffic before crossing the street, the drivers slowed for him. No one honked or shouted rude words out of the window. <u><b>(1)</b>\"Who has the right of way?\" he asked rhetorically. \"I don't care. People here have to find their own way, negotiate for themselves, use their own brains.\"</u><br><br><b>B.</b> Used by some 20,000 drivers a day, the intersection is part of a road-design revolution pioneered by the 59-year-old Mr. Monderman. His work in Friesland, the district in northern Holland that takes in Drachten, is increasingly seen as the way of the future in Europe.<br><br>His philosophy is simple, if counterintuitive. To make communities safer and more appealing, Mr. Monderman argues, you should first remove the traditional paraphernalia of their roads-the traffic lights and speed signs; the signs exhorting drivers to stop, slow down and merge; the center lines separating lanes from one another; even the speed bumps, speed-limit signs, bicycle lanes, and pedestrian crossings. In his view, it is only when the road is made more dangerous, when drivers stop looking at signs and start looking at other people, that driving becomes safer.<br><br>\"All those signs are saying to cars, 'This is your space, and we have organized your behavior so that as long as you behave this way, nothing can happen to you,'\" Mr. Monderman said. \"That is the wrong story.\" The Drachten intersection is an example of the concept of \"shared space,\" a street where cars and pedestrians are equal, and the design tells the driver what to do. \"It's moving away from regulated, legislated traffic toward space which, by the way it's designed and configured, makes it clear what sort of behavior is anticipated,\" said Ben Hamilton-Baillie, a British specialist in urban design and movement and a proponent of many of the same concepts.<br><br>Highways, where the car is naturally king, are part of the \"traffic world\" and another matter altogether. In Mr. Monderman's view, shared-space schemes thrive only in conjunction with well-organized, well-regulated highway systems. Variations on the shared-space theme are being tried in Spain, Denmark, Austria, Sweden, and Britain, among other places. The European Union has appointed a committee of experts, including Mr. Monderman, for a Europe-wide study.<br><br><b>C.</b> Mr. Monderman is a man on a mission. On a daylong automotive tour of Friesland, he pointed out places he had improved, including a town where he ripped out the sidewalks, signs, and crossings and put in brick paving on the central shopping street. An elderly woman crossed slowly in front of him. <u><b>(2)</b>\"This is social space, so when Grandma is coming, you stop, because that's what normal, courteous human beings do,\"</u> he said.<br><br>Planners and curious journalists are increasingly making pilgrimages to meet Mr. Monderman, considered one of the field's great innovators, although until a few years ago he was virtually unknown outside Holland. Mr. Hamilton-Baillie, whose writings have helped bring Mr. Monderman's work to wider attention, remembers with fondness his own first visit. Mr. Monderman drove him to a small country road with cows in every direction. Their presence was reinforced by a large, standard-issue European traffic sign with a picture of a cow on it.<br><br>He said: \"What do you expect to find here? <i>Wallabies</i>??\" Mr. Hamilton-Baillie recalled. \"They're treating you like you're a complete idiot, and if people treat you like a complete idiot, you'll act like one.\"<br><br><b>D.</b> Here was someone who had rethought a lot of issues from complete scratch. Essentially, what it means is a transfer of power and responsibility from the state to the individual and the community. Dressed in a beige jacket and patterned shirt, with scruffy facial hair and a stocky build, Mr. Monderman has the appearance of a football hooligan, but the temperament of an engineer, which indeed he trained to be. His father was the headmaster of the primary school in their small village; Hans liked to fiddle with machines. \"I was always the guy who repaired the TV sets in our village,\" he said. He was working as a civil engineer building highways in the 1970s when the Dutch government, alarmed at a sharp increase in traffic accidents, set up a network of traffic safety offices. Mr. Monderman was appointed Friesland's traffic safety officer.<br><br>In residential communities, Mr. Monderman began narrowing the roads and putting in design features like trees and flowers, red brick paving stones, and even fountains to discourage people from speeding, following the principle now known as psychological traffic calming, where behavior follows design.<br><br>He made his first nervous foray into shared space in a small village whose residents were upset at its being used as a daily thoroughfare for 6,000 speeding cars. When he took away the signs, lights, and sidewalks, people drove more carefully. Within two weeks, speeds on the road had dropped by more than half. In fact, he said, there has never been a fatal accident on any of his roads.<br><br><b>E.</b> Several early studies bear out his contention that shared spaces are safer. In England, the district of Wiltshire found that removing the center line from a stretch of road reduced drivers' speed without any increase in accidents.<br><br>[...] Mr. Monderman concedes that road design can do only so much. It does not change the behavior, for instance, of the 15 percent of drivers who will behave badly no matter what the rules are. Nor are shared-space designs appropriate everywhere, like major urban centers, but only in neighborhoods that meet particular criteria. Recently a group of well-to-do parents asked him to widen the two-lane road leading to their children's school, saying it was too small to accommodate what he derisively calls \"their huge cars.\" He refused, saying the fault was not with the road, but with the cars. <u><b>(3)</b>\"They can't wait for each other to pass?\"</u> he asked. [...]","<b>'GOOD VIDEO GAMES AND GOOD LEARNING'</b> – James P. Gee<br><br>Video games are a relatively new technology replete with important, and not yet fully understood, implications. Scholars have historically viewed the human mind through the lens of a technology they thought worked like the mind. Locke and Hume, for example, argued that the mind was like a blank slate on which experience wrote ideas, taking the technology of literacy as their guide. Much later, modern cognitive scientists argued that the mind worked like a digital program, calculating generalizations and deductions via a logic-like rule system. More recently, some cognitive scientists, inspired by distributed parallel-processing computers and complex adaptive networks, have argued that the mind works by storing records of actual experiences and constructing intricate patterns of connections among them.<br><br>Human societies get better through history at building technologies that more closely capture some of what the human mind can do and getting these technologies to do mental work publicly. Writing, digital computers, and networks each allow us to externalize some functions of the mind. Though they are not commonly thought of in these terms, video games are a new technology in this same line. They are a new tool with which to think about the mind and through which we can externalize some of its functions. Video games of the sort I am concerned with—games like <i>Half-Life 2, Rise of Nations, Full Spectrum Warrior, The Elder Scrolls III: Morrowind</i>, and <i>World of WarCraft</i>—are what I would call \"action-and-goal-directed preparations for, and simulations of, embodied experience.\"<br><br>Human understanding is not primarily a matter of storing general concepts in the head or applying abstract rules to experience. Rather, humans think and understand best when they can imagine (simulate) an experience in such a way that the simulation prepares them for actions they need and want to take in order to accomplish their goals. Let's take weddings as an example, though we could just as well have taken war, love, inertia, democracy, or anything. You don't understand the word or the idea of weddings by meditating on some general definition of weddings. Rather, you have had experiences of weddings, in real life and through texts and media. On the basis of these experiences, you can simulate different wedding scenarios in your mind.<br><br>You construct these simulations differently for different occasions, based on what actions you need to take to accomplish specific goals in specific situations. You can move around as a character in the mental simulation as yourself, imagining your role in the wedding, or you can \"play\" other characters at the wedding (e.g., the minister), imagining what it is like to be that person. You build your simulations to understand and make sense of things, but also to help you prepare for action in the world. You can act in the simulation and test out what consequences follow, before you act in the real world. You can role-play another person in the model and try to see what motivates their actions or might follow from them before you respond in the real world. So I am arguing that the mind is a simulator, but one that builds simulations to purposely prepare for specific actions and to achieve specific goals.<br><br>Video games turn out to be the perfect metaphor for what this view of the mind amounts to, just as slates and computers were good metaphors for earlier views of the mind. Video games usually involve a visual and auditory world in which the player manipulates a virtual character (or characters). They often come with editors or other sorts of software with which the player can make changes to the game world or even build a new game world. The player can make a new landscape, a new set of buildings, or new characters. The player can set up the world so that certain sorts of actions are allowed or disallowed. The player is building a new world, but is doing so by using and modifying the original visual images that came with the game. One simple example of this is the way in which players can build new skateboard parks in a game like <i>Tony Hawk Pro Skater</i>. The player must place ramps, trees, grass, poles, and other things in space in such a way that players can manipulate their virtual characters to skate the park in a fun and challenging way.<br><br>Even when players are not modifying games, they play them with goals in mind, the achievement of which counts as their \"win state,\" and it's the existence of such win states that, in part, distinguishes games from other simulations. These goals are set by the player, but certainly, in collaboration with the world the game designers have created, and, at least in more open-ended games, players don't just accept developer's goals, they make real choices of their own. Players must carefully consider the design of the world and consider how it will or will not facilitate specific actions they want to take to accomplish their goals. One technical way that psychologists have talked about this sort of situation is through the notion of 'affordances'. An 'affordance' is a feature of the world (real or virtual) that will allow for a certain action to be taken, but only if it is matched by an ability in an actor who has the wherewithal to carry out such an action. For example, in the massive multiplayer game <i>World of WarCraft</i> stags can be killed and skinned (for making leather), but only by characters that have learned the 'Skinning' skill. So a stag is an affordance for skinning for such a player, but not for one who has not acquired said skill. The large spiders in the game are not an affordance for skinning for any players, since they cannot be skinned at all. Affordances are relationships between the world and actors. Playing <i>World of WarCraft</i>, or any other video game, is all about such affordances. The player must learn to <i>see</i> the game world—designed by the developers, but set in motion in particular directions by the players, and, thus, co-designed by them—in terms of such affordances. Broadly speaking, players must utilize their resources and think in terms of \"What are the features of this world that can enable the actions I am capable of carrying out and that I want to carry out in order to achieve my goals?\"<br><br>The view of the mind I have sketched, in fact, argues that the mind works rather like a video game. For humans, effective thinking is more like running a simulation than it is about forming abstract generalizations cut off from experiential realities. Generalizations are formed, when they are, bottom up from experience and imagination of experience. Video games externalize the search for affordances, for a match between character (actor) and world, but this is just the heart and soul of effective human thinking and learning in any situation.","<b>HEART ON YOUR SLEEVE</b><br><i>Fitness trackers that let you self-screen for heart conditions risk doing more harm than good, argues Margaret McCartney.</i><br><br>Self-empowered, self-motivated, self-aware: we have got used to the idea that more knowledge about our health is good for us. This ethos has fuelled an explosion in wearable technologies – fitness trackers, step counters and other gizmos – that give us real-time feedback on key physiological stats such as heart rate.<br><br><b>(100)____ &lt;missing paragraph&gt;</b><br><br>Atrial fibrillation is a relatively common heart condition in which the two atria of the heart – the upper chambers – don't contract regularly. [<b>(101)____ &lt;missing sentence&gt;</b>] It increases the risk of blood clots forming and causing a stroke. Those with the condition may need mediation to thin their blood and allow their hearts to work efficiently.<br><br><b>(102)____ &lt;missing paragraph&gt;</b><br><br>The problem is that this is mass screening via the back door, with all the associated positives and negatives. [<b>(103)____ &lt;missing sentence&gt;</b>] It, however, causes far more damage than the disease itself through false positives and unnecessary worry and treatment.<br><br><b>(104)____ &lt;missing paragraph&gt;</b><br><br>A recent US paper suggests that 10,000 asymptomatic older people would have to be screened to detect 50 people with atrial fibrillation, and all those 50 would have to be treated to prevent one stroke. Meanwhile preliminary results of one study, funded by Apple, find that diagnoses of atrial fibrillation could be confirmed by a subsequent electrocardiogram only in about third of cases.<br><br><b>(105)____ &lt;missing paragraph&gt;</b><br><br>In the UK, the SAFER (Screening for Atrial Fibrillation with ECG to Reduce stroke) study is getting under way to test whether mass screening for atrial fibrillation is useful, with ethics committee oversight and informed consent. In the meantime, if you have symptoms – breathlessness, chest pain – you should see a doctor. If you want to be screened, enter a trial. Just because early detection sounds sensible doesn't mean it is good for us.<br><br><b>List of paragraphs</b><br><b>A.</b> In the UK, the National Health Service follows evidence-based recommendations made by the National Screening Committee. Its current advice is clear: don't screen for atrial fibrillation. That is because we have evidence that treatment works for people with symptoms, or those found to have the condition while being assessed for another condition. There is no evidence that treatment benefits outweigh the risks for a wider, asymptomatic population.<br><b>B.</b> With major breakthroughs in semiconductors and nanotech, personal health appliance industry has mushroomed around the globe. Not only tech giants but also young startups such as Misfit have entered the race and introduced their own wearable products, ranging from smart wristbands to invisible health widgets to the market.<br><b>C.</b> Recently, the makers of the bestselling fitness tracker, the Apple Watch, began to roll out a new feature: the ability to monitor heart rhythm, and specifically to detect atrial fibrillation.<br><b>D.</b> All this matters because the side effects of the blood thinners used to treat atrial fibrillation can be severe, ranging up to bleeding into the brain or gut. Such treatment would of course only be prescribed after consultation with a doctor. But if you are already finding it difficult to get a doctor's appointment, think of the impact of a lot of false positives landing on their desk.<br><b>E.</b> Such contradicting results have raised concerns among health providers and policy makers who ought to watch over the system even from the smallest change. Not to mention the opportunity costs one would have to bear from their individual perspective.<br><b>F.</b> So why wouldn't you want to know if you had it? Certainly, some doctors I have spoken to welcome the diagnostic possibilities that wearables bring; many are enthusiastic users themselves.<br><br><b>List of sentences:</b><br><b>G.</b> They malfunction sometimes, though not impacting the heart.<br><b>H.</b> Arguably, its negatives are evidenced by research and policy practices.<br><b>I.</b> It can be constant, or intermittent, and becomes more common with age.<br><b>J.</b> Individuals may find the health benefits of screening outweigh its drawbacks.<br><b>K.</b> Admittedly, it could facilitate early disease detection to avoid adverse consequences.","<b>PRESCRIPTION ERRORS</b><br><br>In Europe, medical prescriptions were historically written in Latin, for many centuries the universal language among the educated. A prescription for eye drops written in Amsterdam could be filled in Paris, because the abbreviation <i>OS</i> meant \"left eye\" in both places. With the (106)____ (APPEAR) of Latin as a lingua franca, however, these abbreviations can be confusing even to trained professionals. In the early 1990s, two infants died in separate but identical tragedies: they were each administered 5 milligrams of morphine, rather than 0.5, as the (107)____ (DOSE) was written without an initial zero. The naked decimal (.5) was subsequently misread. The personal and economic costs of these (108)____ (INTERPRET) medical prescriptions are hard to quantify, yet anecdotal evidence suggests that they are prevalent. While mistakes will always happen in any human endeavor, taking simple corrective steps and also pushing for additional safeguarding policies would help reduce these unfortunate incidents.<br><br>Certain measures are widely agreed upon. For instance, the American Medical Association has publicly advocated against the use of Latin abbreviations and other relics of historical (109)____ (PHARMACY). As a result, incidents of mixing up <i>qd</i> (every day), <i>qid</i> (four times a day), and <i>qod</i> (every other day) seem to be on the decline. Still, enforcing these changes might be difficult due to the (110)____ (CENTRAL) of the healthcare system where each institution has its own authority. Regulators who (111)____ (SEE) potential areas of confusion, such as drug names have adopted certain changes, such as when the FDA asked a manufacturer to change the name of Levoxine, a thyroid medication, to Levoxyl so that confusion with Lanoxin, a heart failure drug, would be reduced. (112)____ (LIKE), in 1990, the antacid Losec was renamed Prilosec to (113)____ (DIFFERENCE) it from Lasix, a diuretic.<br><br>Other measures are controversial or require significant investment and consensus building. Switching to '(114)____ (COMPUTER) prescriber order entry' systems seems to fix the (115)____ (FAME) problem of illegible handwriting, but many systems still permit naked decimals and other dangerous practices. Moreover, since humans must still enter and retrieve the data, any technological fixes must be accompanied by substantial training. Ultimately, a manifold approach is likely needed to thoroughly address this issue."],"questions":[{"n":1,"part":"I. LANGUAGE USE (4.0 pts) – Questions 1-50 Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"She had prepared extensively for the interview; ______ she didn't get the job.","options":["whereas","hence","albeit","nevertheless"],"correct":3,"explanation":"After a semicolon we need a conjunctive adverb of contrast: <b>nevertheless</b> = in spite of that. \"Whereas\" and \"albeit\" are conjunctions and cannot follow a semicolon this way."},{"n":2,"part":"I. LANGUAGE USE (4.0 pts) – Questions 1-50 Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"He practiced his lines diligently, ______ he forgot them during the performance.","options":["even so","however","yet","still"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Two clauses joined by a comma need a coordinating conjunction: <b>yet</b> = but. \"However\", \"even so\" and \"still\" are adverbs and would need a semicolon."},{"n":3,"part":"I. LANGUAGE USE (4.0 pts) – Questions 1-50 Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"John was determined to finish the marathon, even though he had injured his ankle early on in the race, and he pushed through the pain ______.","options":["regardless","irrespective","notwithstanding","however"],"correct":0,"explanation":"\"Pushed through the pain <b>regardless</b>\" = despite everything, anyway. \"Irrespective\" needs \"of\"; \"notwithstanding\" normally precedes or follows a noun it governs; \"however\" would need a comma."},{"n":4,"part":"I. LANGUAGE USE (4.0 pts) – Questions 1-50 Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"I would rather you ______ my message to him yesterday.","options":["had delivered","delivered","have delivered","will deliver"],"correct":0,"explanation":"\"Would rather + subject + past perfect\" expresses a wish about the past (\"yesterday\")."},{"n":5,"part":"I. LANGUAGE USE (4.0 pts) – Questions 1-50 Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Not only ______ to the party, but he also brought a gift.","options":["comes he","does he come","came he","did he come"],"correct":3,"explanation":"A sentence starting with \"Not only\" takes subject–auxiliary inversion; the past tense (\"brought\") requires <b>did he come</b>."},{"n":6,"part":"I. LANGUAGE USE (4.0 pts) – Questions 1-50 Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Bananas are one of ______ and they are believed to help raise serotonin levels.","options":["the available fruits most nutritious","the fruits most nutritious available","the most nutritious fruits available","the most available nutritious fruits"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Superlative + noun + \"available\" as a post-modifying adjective: \"one of <b>the most nutritious fruits available</b>\"."},{"n":7,"part":"I. LANGUAGE USE (4.0 pts) – Questions 1-50 Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"In no way ______ to be misunderstood.","options":["she wants","wants she","does she want","she does want"],"correct":2,"explanation":"A negative adverbial (\"In no way\") at the front forces inversion with an auxiliary: <b>does she want</b>."},{"n":8,"part":"I. LANGUAGE USE (4.0 pts) – Questions 1-50 Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"The art exhibit featured a variety of sculptures, each ______ with precision by a renowned artist.","options":["crafted","to craft","craft","crafting"],"correct":0,"explanation":"A reduced passive participle clause: \"each (of which was) <b>crafted</b> with precision by…\"."},{"n":9,"part":"I. LANGUAGE USE (4.0 pts) – Questions 1-50 Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"It is important that she ______ her passport before traveling.","options":["would renew","renew","renews","renewed"],"correct":1,"explanation":"\"It is important that + subject + bare infinitive\" (present subjunctive): <b>renew</b>."},{"n":10,"part":"I. LANGUAGE USE (4.0 pts) – Questions 1-50 Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"After the financial crisis, many families were reduced ______ in temporary shelters, struggling to rebuild their lives from scratch.","options":["to living","to live","to be living","to be lived"],"correct":0,"explanation":"\"Be reduced <b>to</b> + V-ing\" = be forced into a worse state: \"reduced to living in temporary shelters\"."},{"n":11,"part":"I. LANGUAGE USE (4.0 pts) – Questions 1-50 Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Demands for tickets were high, ______ inflating resell prices to 300%.","options":["and so","thereby","resulting in","which consequently"],"correct":1,"explanation":"\"<b>Thereby</b> + V-ing\" = by that means, introducing the result of the preceding clause."},{"n":12,"part":"I. LANGUAGE USE (4.0 pts) – Questions 1-50 Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"One of the students was caught ______ during the exam and was removed from the room.","options":["having cheated","cheating","to cheat","to have cheated"],"correct":1,"explanation":"\"Catch somebody <b>doing</b> something\" — the passive keeps the V-ing form: \"was caught cheating\"."},{"n":13,"part":"I. LANGUAGE USE (4.0 pts) – Questions 1-50 Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"The company is said ______ victory over its rival through a smear campaign.","options":["to having achieved","to achieve","to have been achieving","to have achieved"],"correct":3,"explanation":"\"Is said <b>to have achieved</b>\": a perfect infinitive because the achievement is already complete."},{"n":14,"part":"I. LANGUAGE USE (4.0 pts) – Questions 1-50 Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Nobody is really what they seem, ______?","options":["is it","aren't they","isn't it","are they"],"correct":3,"explanation":"\"Nobody\" is negative and takes the plural pronoun \"they\", so the tag is positive: <b>are they</b>?"},{"n":15,"part":"I. LANGUAGE USE (4.0 pts) – Questions 1-50 Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"______, the acrobat couldn't make the final landing gracefully.","options":["Disappointed as we were","Much to our disappointment","Greatly were we disappointed","Despite our disappointment"],"correct":1,"explanation":"\"<b>Much to our disappointment</b>\" is the fixed phrase for a result that disappoints; \"Disappointed as we were\" would dangle, since the subject is the acrobat."},{"n":16,"part":"I. LANGUAGE USE (4.0 pts) – Questions 1-50 Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"The cult leader, along with his 2 closest associates, ______ a life sentence.","options":["was receiving","were to receive","was to receive","were receiving"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The subject is \"The cult leader\" (singular — \"along with\" does not change agreement) and \"<b>was to receive</b>\" = was due to receive."},{"n":17,"part":"I. LANGUAGE USE (4.0 pts) – Questions 1-50 Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"______ your school uniform? We're just going to the mall with our classmates.","options":["Need you have worn","Have you to wear","Couldn't you have worn","Don't you wear"],"correct":0,"explanation":"\"<b>Need you have worn</b>…?\" questions whether a past action was necessary — it was unnecessary because they are only going to the mall."},{"n":18,"part":"I. LANGUAGE USE (4.0 pts) – Questions 1-50 Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Max cheated his sister out of the inheritance money, ______ she would never forgive him.","options":["for that","for which","of that","of which"],"correct":1,"explanation":"\"Forgive someone <b>for</b> something\" → preposition + relative pronoun: \"<b>for which</b> she would never forgive him\"."},{"n":19,"part":"I. LANGUAGE USE (4.0 pts) – Questions 1-50 Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Your road to recovery is likely long. ______, you have the best healthcare service available right now.","options":["Saying that","Having said that","That said","To say that"],"correct":2,"explanation":"\"<b>That said</b>\" is the absolute phrase used to introduce a qualification of the previous statement; it needs no subject, unlike the participle form."},{"n":20,"part":"I. LANGUAGE USE (4.0 pts) – Questions 1-50 Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Potential delays ______, this airline service isn't so bad.","options":["notwithstanding","despite","regardless","though"],"correct":0,"explanation":"\"<b>Notwithstanding</b>\" can follow the noun it governs: \"Potential delays notwithstanding\" = despite potential delays."},{"n":21,"part":"I. LANGUAGE USE (4.0 pts) – Questions 1-50 Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"After working for several hours straight, Sarah was ______ and needed to take a break.","options":["running out of steam","pulling her weight","hitting the nail on the head","burning the midnight oil"],"correct":0,"explanation":"\"<b>Run out of steam</b>\" = lose energy or enthusiasm."},{"n":22,"part":"I. LANGUAGE USE (4.0 pts) – Questions 1-50 Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"The politician's promise to lower taxes turned out to be nothing but ______.","options":["a piece of cake","hot air","a bitter pill to swallow","a storm in a teacup"],"correct":1,"explanation":"\"<b>Hot air</b>\" = empty talk, promises that mean nothing."},{"n":23,"part":"I. LANGUAGE USE (4.0 pts) – Questions 1-50 Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"The chef prepared a ______ feast, featuring a variety of delicious dishes.","options":["tongue-in-cheek","fine-tooth","mouth-watering","lip-smacking"],"correct":2,"explanation":"\"<b>Mouth-watering</b>\" describes food that looks or smells delicious."},{"n":24,"part":"I. LANGUAGE USE (4.0 pts) – Questions 1-50 Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"The media has been reporting non-stop about this scandal for weeks. It's time to stop ______.","options":["flogging a dead horse","skinning a cat","crying wolf","counting sheep"],"correct":0,"explanation":"\"<b>Flog a dead horse</b>\" = keep going over something that is already finished or pointless."},{"n":25,"part":"I. LANGUAGE USE (4.0 pts) – Questions 1-50 Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Enjoy your holiday and keep me ______ about anything exciting!","options":["shared","signed","posted","framed"],"correct":2,"explanation":"\"Keep someone <b>posted</b>\" = keep someone informed of the latest news."},{"n":26,"part":"I. LANGUAGE USE (4.0 pts) – Questions 1-50 Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"You have been having terrible luck, I suggest you ______ and stop playing.","options":["tighten your purse strings","cut your losses","lower the stakes","cash in your chips"],"correct":1,"explanation":"\"<b>Cut your losses</b>\" = stop doing something before you lose even more."},{"n":27,"part":"I. LANGUAGE USE (4.0 pts) – Questions 1-50 Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"These ______ office cubicles are drab, plain, and depressing to work in.","options":["cookie-cutter","bread-and-butter","vanilla","eye candy"],"correct":0,"explanation":"\"<b>Cookie-cutter</b>\" = identical and unoriginal, matching \"drab, plain\"."},{"n":28,"part":"I. LANGUAGE USE (4.0 pts) – Questions 1-50 Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"That's not very prim and ______ of her to chew with her mouth wide open.","options":["proud","bright","polite","proper"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The fixed binomial is \"prim and <b>proper</b>\" = very correct in manners."},{"n":29,"part":"I. LANGUAGE USE (4.0 pts) – Questions 1-50 Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"I'm sorry, but I have to ______ my plans for tonight due to unexpected circumstances.","options":["turn down","put off","bring up","do without"],"correct":1,"explanation":"\"<b>Put off</b>\" = postpone; you postpone plans because of unexpected circumstances."},{"n":30,"part":"I. LANGUAGE USE (4.0 pts) – Questions 1-50 Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"The company decided to ______ 50 employees in order to reduce costs.","options":["lay off","cut down","give up","turn away"],"correct":0,"explanation":"\"<b>Lay off</b>\" = dismiss workers, typically to cut costs."},{"n":31,"part":"I. LANGUAGE USE (4.0 pts) – Questions 1-50 Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"The teacher asked the students to finish and ______ their assignments by Friday.","options":["give away","hand in","pass up","bring forth"],"correct":1,"explanation":"\"<b>Hand in</b>\" = submit work to a teacher."},{"n":32,"part":"I. LANGUAGE USE (4.0 pts) – Questions 1-50 Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"She had to ______ the courage to speak in front of such a large audience.","options":["evoke","accrue","spend","summon"],"correct":3,"explanation":"\"<b>Summon (up)</b> the courage\" is the collocation for gathering one's courage."},{"n":33,"part":"I. LANGUAGE USE (4.0 pts) – Questions 1-50 Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"The board was unconvinced by the idea at first, but after the demonstration, most of them ______.","options":["caved in","gave into","came round","keeled over"],"correct":2,"explanation":"\"<b>Come round</b>\" = change your mind and agree after initial reluctance."},{"n":34,"part":"I. LANGUAGE USE (4.0 pts) – Questions 1-50 Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Mickey is not telling us the whole story. There's more than he ______.","options":["showed off","let on","brought forth","put across"],"correct":1,"explanation":"\"<b>Let on</b>\" = reveal a secret; \"more than he lets on\" is the idiom."},{"n":35,"part":"I. LANGUAGE USE (4.0 pts) – Questions 1-50 Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Even though the house was built just a year ago, we are starting to see signs of paint ______ off.","options":["tearing","cracking","dusting","chipping"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Paint \"<b>chips</b> off\" (or peels/flakes off) when small pieces break away."},{"n":36,"part":"I. LANGUAGE USE (4.0 pts) – Questions 1-50 Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"The highway accident led to a terrible ______ of over 50 vehicles and 100 casualties including deaths.","options":["pileup","turnover","gridlock","backlash"],"correct":0,"explanation":"A \"<b>pileup</b>\" is a crash involving many vehicles."},{"n":37,"part":"I. LANGUAGE USE (4.0 pts) – Questions 1-50 Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"She ______ her gratitude for the opportunity to work on such an important project.","options":["espoused","extended","expanded","extorted"],"correct":1,"explanation":"\"<b>Extend</b> one's gratitude/thanks\" = offer or express thanks formally."},{"n":38,"part":"I. LANGUAGE USE (4.0 pts) – Questions 1-50 Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"The students eagerly participated in a ______, discussing various aspects of the novel.","options":["lively debate","heated argument","casual conversation","shop talk"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Eager participation and discussing \"various aspects of the novel\" describe a <b>lively debate</b>, not a quarrel or small talk."},{"n":39,"part":"I. LANGUAGE USE (4.0 pts) – Questions 1-50 Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"The team's ______ performance in the playoffs ended their tournament run prematurely.","options":["lackluster","hideous","lukewarm","stale"],"correct":0,"explanation":"\"<b>Lackluster</b> performance\" = dull and uninspired, the standard collocation for a poor showing."},{"n":40,"part":"I. LANGUAGE USE (4.0 pts) – Questions 1-50 Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"A.I. is bringing about a ___ shift in what is normally considered 'skilled, intellectual, creative' fields.","options":["paragon","paradigm","paramount","paradox"],"correct":1,"explanation":"\"<b>Paradigm</b> shift\" = a fundamental change in approach."},{"n":41,"part":"I. LANGUAGE USE (4.0 pts) – Questions 1-50 Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"It is ____ normal to live with your parents. You don't have to move out if you don't have the means.","options":["utterly","absolutely","exceptionally","incredibly"],"correct":1,"explanation":"\"<b>Absolutely</b> normal\" = completely normal; \"utterly\" collocates with negative adjectives."},{"n":42,"part":"I. LANGUAGE USE (4.0 pts) – Questions 1-50 Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Read your contract carefully before signing as the terms in there are legally ______.","options":["secure","enforced","binding","obligatory"],"correct":2,"explanation":"\"Legally <b>binding</b>\" = enforceable by law, the fixed collocation for contract terms."},{"n":43,"part":"I. LANGUAGE USE (4.0 pts) – Questions 1-50 Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Skeptics ____ to the view that 'alternative medicine' is just placebo without any medicinal properties.","options":["allude","amount","resign","subscribe"],"correct":3,"explanation":"\"<b>Subscribe to</b> a view\" = agree with or support an opinion."},{"n":44,"part":"I. LANGUAGE USE (4.0 pts) – Questions 1-50 Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"The company decided to ______ a new marketing strategy to boost sales and attract more customers.","options":["enforce","instill","implement","mandate"],"correct":2,"explanation":"\"<b>Implement</b> a strategy\" = put a plan into action."},{"n":45,"part":"I. LANGUAGE USE (4.0 pts) – Questions 1-50 Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"The hiker had to ______ a steep mountain to reach the summit.","options":["ascend","descend","traverse","wander"],"correct":0,"explanation":"To reach the summit you must <b>ascend</b> (climb up) the mountain."},{"n":46,"part":"I. LANGUAGE USE (4.0 pts) – Questions 1-50 Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"The detective's keen observation skills helped him ______ the mystery behind the missing diamond.","options":["unearth","underpin","unravel","underline"],"correct":2,"explanation":"\"<b>Unravel</b> a mystery\" = solve it by working out the tangled details."},{"n":47,"part":"I. LANGUAGE USE (4.0 pts) – Questions 1-50 Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"The author's prose is ____, so even first-time readers of the genre find it easy to follow the story.","options":["lucid","explicit","cohesive","plain"],"correct":0,"explanation":"\"<b>Lucid</b>\" = clearly expressed and easy to understand, matching \"easy to follow\"."},{"n":48,"part":"I. LANGUAGE USE (4.0 pts) – Questions 1-50 Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"The Amazon rainforest ______ over 3 million species of plants and animals, many of which unique to the region and under serious threat.","options":["claims","gloats","boasts","enjoys"],"correct":2,"explanation":"\"<b>Boast</b>\" = have something impressive: \"the rainforest boasts over 3 million species\"."},{"n":49,"part":"I. LANGUAGE USE (4.0 pts) – Questions 1-50 Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"The striking laborers ______ demands regarding their working conditions, healthcare, and paid leave.","options":["instigated","propagated","mitigated","stipulated"],"correct":3,"explanation":"\"<b>Stipulate</b> demands\" = state them clearly as conditions."},{"n":50,"part":"I. LANGUAGE USE (4.0 pts) – Questions 1-50 Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Most humans are instinctively ______ to snakes, which are a danger to our primate ancestors.","options":["averse","perverse","inverse","adverse"],"correct":0,"explanation":"\"<b>Averse to</b>\" = having a strong dislike of; \"adverse\" means harmful and does not take \"to\" with a person as subject."},{"n":51,"part":"I. LANGUAGE USE (4.0 pts) – Questions 51-60 Guided Cloze Test","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","q":"(51) goes the famous rhyme that ______ the fates of King Henry VIII's six wives.","options":["recounts","recalls","recollects","recants"],"correct":0,"explanation":"\"<b>Recount</b>\" = tell a story or describe events; the rhyme tells the fates of the six wives.","passageId":0},{"n":52,"part":"I. LANGUAGE USE (4.0 pts) – Questions 51-60 Guided Cloze Test","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","q":"(52) 'SIX' gives ______ and tune to the women who served as queens","options":["sound","voice","music","tone"],"correct":1,"explanation":"\"Give <b>voice</b> to\" = let someone express themselves; the show lets the queens tell their own stories.","passageId":0},{"n":53,"part":"I. LANGUAGE USE (4.0 pts) – Questions 51-60 Guided Cloze Test","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","q":"(53) The show does not, however, feature a character playing the king. ______, it is centered on the women","options":["Presumably","Rather","Particularly","In turn"],"correct":1,"explanation":"\"<b>Rather</b>\" introduces the alternative to what was just denied: not the king, but the women.","passageId":0},{"n":54,"part":"I. LANGUAGE USE (4.0 pts) – Questions 51-60 Guided Cloze Test","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","q":"(54) The entire 80-minute production takes place on a ______ that appears to be a concert stage.","options":["scenario","set","scenery","setting"],"correct":1,"explanation":"A \"<b>set</b>\" is the physical stage construction on which a show is performed.","passageId":0},{"n":55,"part":"I. LANGUAGE USE (4.0 pts) – Questions 51-60 Guided Cloze Test","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","q":"(55) 'SIX's take on the Tudor era is heavily fictionalized. ______ is clear the moment the actresses stepped out","options":["So it","So much","As such","That much"],"correct":3,"explanation":"\"<b>That much</b> is clear\" = at least that is obvious, referring back to the fictionalized take.","passageId":0},{"n":56,"part":"I. LANGUAGE USE (4.0 pts) – Questions 51-60 Guided Cloze Test","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","q":"(56) the stage, with modern ______ like bass guitar and keyboard, feels more at home at a pop concert","options":["devices","equipment","gadgets","instruments"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Bass guitar and keyboard are musical <b>instruments</b>.","passageId":0},{"n":57,"part":"I. LANGUAGE USE (4.0 pts) – Questions 51-60 Guided Cloze Test","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","q":"(57) Still, ______ the era and region the storyline takes place in, there are hints of the music from 500 years ago.","options":["in honour of","on behalf of","in relation to","on account of"],"correct":3,"explanation":"\"Still\" concedes the modern sound, then gives the reason there are period touches: <b>on account of</b> (= because of) the era and region the story is set in — hence the harpsichord and the German-folk polka. \"In honour of\" would mean paying tribute to a region, which does not fit.","passageId":0},{"n":58,"part":"I. LANGUAGE USE (4.0 pts) – Questions 51-60 Guided Cloze Test","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","q":"(58) paying ______ to 'pop queens' the likes of Beyoncé, Adele, and Ariana Grande.","options":["lip service","homage","respects","court"],"correct":1,"explanation":"\"Pay <b>homage</b> to\" = show respect or tribute to someone admired.","passageId":0},{"n":59,"part":"I. LANGUAGE USE (4.0 pts) – Questions 51-60 Guided Cloze Test","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","q":"(59) Many of the ______ loved how it was fast-paced and shorter than some other productions","options":["audience","spectators","viewers","participants"],"correct":0,"explanation":"People watching a theatre show are the <b>audience</b>; \"spectators\" watch sport and \"viewers\" watch TV.","passageId":0},{"n":60,"part":"I. LANGUAGE USE (4.0 pts) – Questions 51-60 Guided Cloze Test","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","q":"(60) though they didn't know what to expect ______ the show, they left highly impressed.","options":["in the wake of","by the end of","prior to","throughout"],"correct":2,"explanation":"\"<b>Prior to</b> the show\" = before it; they did not know what to expect beforehand but left impressed.","passageId":0},{"n":61,"part":"I. LANGUAGE USE (4.0 pts) – Questions 61-70 Open Cloze Test 1","section":"Open cloze","type":"text","q":"(61) Roughly two-thirds of the world's cocoa is grown in West Africa, ______ of it concentrating in Ivory Coast and Ghana","accept":["most","much"],"answer":"most","explanation":"\"<b>Most</b> of it\" — the largest share of West African cocoa comes from just two countries.","passageId":1},{"n":62,"part":"I. LANGUAGE USE (4.0 pts) – Questions 61-70 Open Cloze Test 1","section":"Open cloze","type":"text","q":"(62) farmers there have faced extreme weather brought by changing climate ______ for a few years","accept":["patterns","now"],"answer":"patterns","explanation":"\"Changing climate <b>patterns</b>\" is the collocation; the colon then lists the extreme weather those patterns bring.","passageId":1},{"n":63,"part":"I. LANGUAGE USE (4.0 pts) – Questions 61-70 Open Cloze Test 1","section":"Open cloze","type":"text","q":"(63) ______ has worsened crop disease and road conditions, which disrupts bean deliveries to ports.","accept":["This","That","It","Which"],"answer":"This","explanation":"A demonstrative \"<b>This</b>\" refers back to the extreme weather just described and starts the new sentence.","passageId":1},{"n":64,"part":"I. LANGUAGE USE (4.0 pts) – Questions 61-70 Open Cloze Test 1","section":"Open cloze","type":"text","q":"(64) Other cocoa-growing countries have been unable to fill the ______ because ramping up production is expensive","accept":["gap","void","shortfall","shortage","deficit"],"answer":"gap","explanation":"\"Fill the <b>gap</b>\" = make up for the missing supply left by the West African decline.","passageId":1},{"n":65,"part":"I. LANGUAGE USE (4.0 pts) – Questions 61-70 Open Cloze Test 1","section":"Open cloze","type":"text","q":"(65) ramping up production is expensive and ______; it takes years for newly planted trees to produce cocoa beans.","accept":["slow","lengthy","time-consuming","timeconsuming"],"answer":"slow","explanation":"The semicolon explains the word: \"it takes years\" → production is <b>slow</b>.","passageId":1},{"n":66,"part":"I. LANGUAGE USE (4.0 pts) – Questions 61-70 Open Cloze Test 1","section":"Open cloze","type":"text","q":"(66) This extensive shortfall of cocoa has ______ buyers scrambling, pushing prices up","accept":["left","sent","got","had","set"],"answer":"left","explanation":"\"Has <b>left</b> buyers scrambling\" = has put buyers in a state of rushing to find supply (leave + object + V-ing).","passageId":1},{"n":67,"part":"I. LANGUAGE USE (4.0 pts) – Questions 61-70 Open Cloze Test 1","section":"Open cloze","type":"text","q":"(67) Normally, if prices are very high, people start producing more of that commodity, but that is not the ______ with cocoa.","accept":["case"],"answer":"case","explanation":"\"That is not the <b>case</b> with…\" = that does not apply to cocoa.","passageId":1},{"n":68,"part":"I. LANGUAGE USE (4.0 pts) – Questions 61-70 Open Cloze Test 1","section":"Open cloze","type":"text","q":"(68) those farmers rarely benefit from the surging cost of cocoa on the ______, because they typically pre-sell the beans","accept":["market","exchange"],"answer":"market","explanation":"Cocoa prices are quoted \"on the <b>market</b>\"; farmers who pre-sold do not gain from market surges.","passageId":1},{"n":69,"part":"I. LANGUAGE USE (4.0 pts) – Questions 61-70 Open Cloze Test 1","section":"Open cloze","type":"text","q":"(69) they typically pre-sell the beans at agreed-upon prices in ______.","accept":["advance"],"answer":"advance","explanation":"\"Pre-sell … in <b>advance</b>\" — prices are agreed before the harvest.","passageId":1},{"n":70,"part":"I. LANGUAGE USE (4.0 pts) – Questions 61-70 Open Cloze Test 1","section":"Open cloze","type":"text","q":"(70) the amounts they earn for selling their cocoa cannot ______ up for the costs to replant, buy fertilizers, buy pesticides, etc.","accept":["make"],"answer":"make","explanation":"\"<b>Make</b> up for\" = compensate for the costs.","passageId":1},{"n":71,"part":"I. LANGUAGE USE (4.0 pts) – Questions 71-80 Open Cloze Test 2","section":"Open cloze","type":"text","q":"(71) Back then, the field hospital ______ was the ambulance. It brought the care to patients, not the other way around.","accept":["itself"],"answer":"itself","explanation":"Emphatic reflexive: the field hospital <b>itself</b> was the ambulance, as opposed to a vehicle going to it.","passageId":2},{"n":72,"part":"I. LANGUAGE USE (4.0 pts) – Questions 71-80 Open Cloze Test 2","section":"Open cloze","type":"text","q":"(72) Just look at the word. It has the same ______ as words like \"ambulatory\" or \"amble.\"","accept":["root","origin","stem","etymology"],"answer":"root","explanation":"Words sharing an origin share a <b>root</b> — \"ambul-\" = walk.","passageId":2},{"n":73,"part":"I. LANGUAGE USE (4.0 pts) – Questions 71-80 Open Cloze Test 2","section":"Open cloze","type":"text","q":"(73) ______ essence, the word means \"walking.\"","accept":["In"],"answer":"In","explanation":"\"<b>In</b> essence\" = fundamentally.","passageId":2},{"n":74,"part":"I. LANGUAGE USE (4.0 pts) – Questions 71-80 Open Cloze Test 2","section":"Open cloze","type":"text","q":"(74) The French term from which the English ______ was \"hôpital ambulant,\" but English dropped \"hôpital.\"","accept":["derives","comes","came","derived","originates","originated","stems","stemmed","evolved"],"answer":"derives","explanation":"\"The term from which the English <b>derives</b>\" = the French origin of the English word.","passageId":2},{"n":75,"part":"I. LANGUAGE USE (4.0 pts) – Questions 71-80 Open Cloze Test 2","section":"Open cloze","type":"text","q":"(75) ______ came the Crimean War (1853–1856).","accept":["Then","Next","Later"],"answer":"Then","explanation":"Sequencing the history: \"<b>Then</b> came the Crimean War\" (inversion after a fronted adverb).","passageId":2},{"n":76,"part":"I. LANGUAGE USE (4.0 pts) – Questions 71-80 Open Cloze Test 2","section":"Open cloze","type":"text","q":"(76) In ______ to that, the Crimean War was when people began to refer to ambulances as the conveyances","accept":["addition"],"answer":"addition","explanation":"\"In <b>addition</b> to that\" adds a second first for the Crimean War.","passageId":2},{"n":77,"part":"I. LANGUAGE USE (4.0 pts) – Questions 71-80 Open Cloze Test 2","section":"Open cloze","type":"text","q":"(77) the Crimean War was when people began to ______ to ambulances as the conveyances moving troops from the field","accept":["refer"],"answer":"refer","explanation":"\"<b>Refer</b> to X as Y\" = call X by the name Y.","passageId":2},{"n":78,"part":"I. LANGUAGE USE (4.0 pts) – Questions 71-80 Open Cloze Test 2","section":"Open cloze","type":"text","q":"(78) conveyances moving troops from the field to places where their injuries were ______.","accept":["treated","tended","dressed","attended"],"answer":"treated","explanation":"Injuries are \"<b>treated</b>\" — the wounded were moved to where they could receive care.","passageId":2},{"n":79,"part":"I. LANGUAGE USE (4.0 pts) – Questions 71-80 Open Cloze Test 2","section":"Open cloze","type":"text","q":"(79) Field hospitals couldn't possibly have ______ up with all of the battles.","accept":["kept"],"answer":"kept","explanation":"\"<b>Keep</b> up with\" = manage to stay level with; there were too many theaters of war.","passageId":2},{"n":80,"part":"I. LANGUAGE USE (4.0 pts) – Questions 71-80 Open Cloze Test 2","section":"Open cloze","type":"text","q":"(80) Field hospitals still exist today, of ______, but they're no longer called ambulances.","accept":["course"],"answer":"course","explanation":"\"Of <b>course</b>\" = naturally, obviously.","passageId":2},{"n":81,"part":"II. READING (2.5 pts) – Questions 81-90 Passage 1","section":"Reading","type":"mcq","q":"What is the most appropriate title for the passage?","options":["Traffic safety officer: Challenges and Opportunities","Road Safety without Road signs","Eliminating Europe's hidden traffic dangers","How cars have lost their status as 'king of the road'"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The whole passage is about Monderman removing signs, lights and markings (\"shared space\") and the roads becoming safer as a result.","passageId":3},{"n":82,"part":"II. READING (2.5 pts) – Questions 81-90 Passage 1","section":"Reading","type":"mcq","q":"What word means the CLOSEST to '<i>anarchical</i>' (section A)?","options":["confusing","orderly","empty","chaotic"],"correct":3,"explanation":"\"Anarchical\" = without order or rules; the intersection is \"stripped of all lights, signs, and road markings\", so it looks <b>chaotic</b>.","passageId":3},{"n":83,"part":"II. READING (2.5 pts) – Questions 81-90 Passage 1","section":"Reading","type":"mcq","q":"According to the text, what is TRUE about the concept of 'shared space'?","options":["It dictates certain behaviours from traffic participants.","It first gained popularity in some European countries.","It might need certain conditions to be used on highways.","It prioritizes pedestrians over vehicle drivers."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Section B: in shared space \"the design tells the driver what to do\" and \"makes it clear what sort of behavior is anticipated\". Cars and pedestrians are \"equal\", and highways are \"another matter altogether\".","passageId":3},{"n":84,"part":"II. READING (2.5 pts) – Questions 81-90 Passage 1","section":"Reading","type":"mcq","q":"What can be inferred about Monderman from his comments <u>(1), (2), and (3)</u>?","options":["He is passionate about his projects.","He is critical of the conventional traffic designs.","He is confident about the effectiveness of his designs.","He is curious about the behaviours of people in traffic."],"correct":2,"explanation":"In all three comments he takes it for granted that people will \"use their own brains\", stop for Grandma and wait for each other — he is <b>confident</b> his designs make people behave well.","passageId":3},{"n":85,"part":"II. READING (2.5 pts) – Questions 81-90 Passage 1","section":"Reading","type":"mcq","q":"What is NOT TRUE about Monderman's first experiment with the 'shared space' concept?","options":["He was sure it would be a success.","It arose from complaints by local people.","Driving awareness increased as a result.","It did not take long to yield results."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Section D calls it \"his first <b>nervous</b> foray into shared space\", so he was not sure of success; residents were \"upset\", people \"drove more carefully\" and speeds dropped \"within two weeks\".","passageId":3},{"n":86,"part":"II. READING (2.5 pts) – Questions 81-90 Passage 1","section":"Reading","type":"text","q":"Which section (A – E) contains the following information? a reference to reckless drivers","accept":["E"],"answer":"E","explanation":"Section E: \"the 15 percent of drivers who will behave badly no matter what the rules are\".","passageId":3},{"n":87,"part":"II. READING (2.5 pts) – Questions 81-90 Passage 1","section":"Reading","type":"text","q":"Which section (A – E) contains the following information? a concrete example of unnecessary signage","accept":["C"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Section C: a traffic sign with a picture of a cow on a road with cows in every direction — \"What do you expect to find here? Wallabies??\"","passageId":3},{"n":88,"part":"II. READING (2.5 pts) – Questions 81-90 Passage 1","section":"Reading","type":"text","q":"Which section (A – E) contains the following information? an authority's attempt to take the design to a larger scale","accept":["B"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Section B: \"The European Union has appointed a committee of experts, including Mr. Monderman, for a Europe-wide study.\"","passageId":3},{"n":89,"part":"II. READING (2.5 pts) – Questions 81-90 Passage 1","section":"Reading","type":"text","q":"Which section (A – E) contains the following information? a description of strange traffic settings","accept":["A"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Section A: \"several odd things immediately became clear … virtually naked, stripped of all lights, signs, and road markings … a bare brick square\".","passageId":3},{"n":90,"part":"II. READING (2.5 pts) – Questions 81-90 Passage 1","section":"Reading","type":"text","q":"Which section (A – E) contains the following information? influence of the engineer's background on his career choice","accept":["D"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Section D: his father was a headmaster, \"Hans liked to fiddle with machines\", he \"repaired the TV sets in our village\" and trained as an engineer.","passageId":3},{"n":91,"part":"II. READING (2.5 pts) – Questions 91-99 Passage 2","section":"Reading","type":"text","q":"Decide if the statement agrees with the information given in the excerpt — write <b>True</b>, <b>False</b> or <b>Not Given</b>: The view of the mind as a software predates that of the mind as a record to store information.","accept":["True","T"],"answer":"True","explanation":"Paragraph 1: the \"digital program\" view came \"much later\" than Locke and Hume, and only \"more recently\" did scientists argue the mind \"works by storing records of actual experiences\" — so the software view came first.","passageId":4},{"n":92,"part":"II. READING (2.5 pts) – Questions 91-99 Passage 2","section":"Reading","type":"text","q":"Decide if the statement agrees with the information given in the excerpt — write <b>True</b>, <b>False</b> or <b>Not Given</b>: Technology became more advanced as the result of growing complexity of the mind.","accept":["Not Given","NG","Not given","N"],"answer":"Not Given","explanation":"Paragraph 2 says societies get better at building technologies that \"capture some of what the human mind can do\", but nothing is said about the mind growing more complex as a cause.","passageId":4},{"n":93,"part":"II. READING (2.5 pts) – Questions 91-99 Passage 2","section":"Reading","type":"text","q":"Decide if the statement agrees with the information given in the excerpt — write <b>True</b>, <b>False</b> or <b>Not Given</b>: The wedding scenario best exemplifies the necessity of simulations in thinking.","accept":["False","F"],"answer":"False","explanation":"Paragraph 3: weddings are just one example — \"we could just as well have taken war, love, inertia, democracy, or anything\".","passageId":4},{"n":94,"part":"II. READING (2.5 pts) – Questions 91-99 Passage 2","section":"Reading","type":"text","q":"Decide if the statement agrees with the information given in the excerpt — write <b>True</b>, <b>False</b> or <b>Not Given</b>: Roleplaying in simulations can benefit decision-making in real life.","accept":["True","T"],"answer":"True","explanation":"Paragraph 4: \"You can role-play another person in the model and try to see what motivates their actions … before you respond in the real world.\"","passageId":4},{"n":95,"part":"II. READING (2.5 pts) – Questions 91-99 Passage 2","section":"Reading","type":"text","q":"Decide if the statement agrees with the information given in the excerpt — write <b>True</b>, <b>False</b> or <b>Not Given</b>: A simulation always requires participants to achieve some kind of victory.","accept":["False","F"],"answer":"False","explanation":"Paragraph 6: it is \"the existence of such win states that, in part, distinguishes games from other simulations\" — other simulations have no win state.","passageId":4},{"n":96,"part":"II. READING (2.5 pts) – Questions 91-99 Passage 2","section":"Reading","type":"text","q":"Decide if the statement agrees with the information given in the excerpt — write <b>True</b>, <b>False</b> or <b>Not Given</b>: The more freedom players are allowed to modify the game world, the more fun and engaging their experience is.","accept":["Not Given","NG","Not given","N"],"answer":"Not Given","explanation":"Paragraph 5 describes how players can modify game worlds, but never links the amount of freedom to how fun or engaging the game is.","passageId":4},{"n":97,"part":"II. READING (2.5 pts) – Questions 91-99 Passage 2","section":"Reading","type":"text","q":"Choose THREE features from the list below that best describe video game 'affordances' (answer with one letter per question, in any order — this is choice 1 of 3):<br>A. They facilitate problem solving skills in players.<br>B. They are particularly common in some genres of game.<br>C. They allow for modifications of the game's assets.<br>D. Players can exert influence on gameplay through them.<br>E. They connect game developers and players.<br>F. They might act as restrictions to certain classes of players.<br>G. They set a fixed path on which players progress.<br>H. The term was defined by game developers.<br>I. They invoke a sense of accomplishment in players.<br><b>First letter (1 of 3):</b>","accept":["A","D","F"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The three features are <b>A</b>, <b>D</b> and <b>F</b>, in any order. Affordances are what \"players must utilize their resources and think in terms of\" to reach their goals — \"the heart and soul of effective human thinking and learning\" — i.e. they develop problem-solving.","passageId":4},{"n":98,"part":"II. READING (2.5 pts) – Questions 91-99 Passage 2","section":"Reading","type":"text","q":"Choose THREE features from the list below that best describe video game 'affordances' (answer with one letter per question, in any order — this is choice 2 of 3):<br>A. They facilitate problem solving skills in players.<br>B. They are particularly common in some genres of game.<br>C. They allow for modifications of the game's assets.<br>D. Players can exert influence on gameplay through them.<br>E. They connect game developers and players.<br>F. They might act as restrictions to certain classes of players.<br>G. They set a fixed path on which players progress.<br>H. The term was defined by game developers.<br>I. They invoke a sense of accomplishment in players.<br><b>Second letter (2 of 3):</b>","accept":["A","D","F"],"answer":"D","explanation":"The three features are <b>A</b>, <b>D</b> and <b>F</b>, in any order. The game world is \"set in motion in particular directions by the players, and, thus, co-designed by them — in terms of such affordances\": players shape gameplay through them.","passageId":4},{"n":99,"part":"II. READING (2.5 pts) – Questions 91-99 Passage 2","section":"Reading","type":"text","q":"Choose THREE features from the list below that best describe video game 'affordances' (answer with one letter per question, in any order — this is choice 3 of 3):<br>A. They facilitate problem solving skills in players.<br>B. They are particularly common in some genres of game.<br>C. They allow for modifications of the game's assets.<br>D. Players can exert influence on gameplay through them.<br>E. They connect game developers and players.<br>F. They might act as restrictions to certain classes of players.<br>G. They set a fixed path on which players progress.<br>H. The term was defined by game developers.<br>I. They invoke a sense of accomplishment in players.<br><b>Third letter (3 of 3):</b>","accept":["A","D","F"],"answer":"F","explanation":"The three features are <b>A</b>, <b>D</b> and <b>F</b>, in any order. A stag \"is an affordance for skinning for such a player, but not for one who has not acquired said skill\" — affordances restrict players who lack the ability.","passageId":4},{"n":100,"part":"II. READING (2.5 pts) – Questions 100-105 Passage 3","section":"Reading","type":"text","q":"Gap (100) – missing paragraph: choose the paragraph (A – F) which fits.","accept":["C"],"answer":"C","explanation":"After the intro on wearables, C introduces the specific case — the Apple Watch feature \"to detect atrial fibrillation\" — which the next paragraph then defines.","passageId":5},{"n":101,"part":"II. READING (2.5 pts) – Questions 100-105 Passage 3","section":"Reading","type":"text","q":"Gap (101) – missing sentence: choose the sentence (G – K) which fits.","accept":["I"],"answer":"I","explanation":"Inside the definition of atrial fibrillation, I describes the condition: \"It can be constant, or intermittent, and becomes more common with age.\"","passageId":5},{"n":102,"part":"II. READING (2.5 pts) – Questions 100-105 Passage 3","section":"Reading","type":"text","q":"Gap (102) – missing paragraph: choose the paragraph (A – F) which fits.","accept":["F"],"answer":"F","explanation":"F asks \"So why wouldn't you want to know if you had it?\" and notes doctors welcoming wearables — which \"The problem is that this is mass screening via the back door\" then answers.","passageId":5},{"n":103,"part":"II. READING (2.5 pts) – Questions 100-105 Passage 3","section":"Reading","type":"text","q":"Gap (103) – missing sentence: choose the sentence (G – K) which fits.","accept":["K"],"answer":"K","explanation":"K concedes \"Admittedly, it could facilitate early disease detection\", and the next sentence contrasts: \"It, however, causes far more damage…\".","passageId":5},{"n":104,"part":"II. READING (2.5 pts) – Questions 100-105 Passage 3","section":"Reading","type":"text","q":"Gap (104) – missing paragraph: choose the paragraph (A – F) which fits.","accept":["A"],"answer":"A","explanation":"A gives the NHS advice \"don't screen for atrial fibrillation\" because there is \"no evidence that treatment benefits outweigh the risks\" for asymptomatic people — the US paper that follows supplies the numbers.","passageId":5},{"n":105,"part":"II. READING (2.5 pts) – Questions 100-105 Passage 3","section":"Reading","type":"text","q":"Gap (105) – missing paragraph: choose the paragraph (A – F) which fits.","accept":["D"],"answer":"D","explanation":"\"All this matters because…\" in D refers back to the screening statistics and false positives just given, before the closing paragraph on the SAFER trial.","passageId":5},{"n":106,"part":"III. WRITING (3.5 pts) – Questions 106-115 Word Formation","section":"Word form","type":"text","q":"(106) With the ______ of Latin as a lingua franca, however, these abbreviations can be confusing even to trained professionals. (APPEAR)","accept":["disappearance"],"answer":"disappearance","explanation":"A noun after \"the\" and the sense is that Latin is no longer the common language: its <b>disappearance</b>.","passageId":6},{"n":107,"part":"III. WRITING (3.5 pts) – Questions 106-115 Word Formation","section":"Word form","type":"text","q":"(107) they were each administered 5 milligrams of morphine, rather than 0.5, as the ______ was written without an initial zero. (DOSE)","accept":["dosage"],"answer":"dosage","explanation":"The noun for the amount prescribed: the <b>dosage</b> (written \".5\" instead of \"0.5\").","passageId":6},{"n":108,"part":"III. WRITING (3.5 pts) – Questions 106-115 Word Formation","section":"Word form","type":"text","q":"(108) The personal and economic costs of these ______ medical prescriptions are hard to quantify (INTERPRET)","accept":["misinterpreted"],"answer":"misinterpreted","explanation":"The prescriptions were wrongly read: a negative past participle as adjective, <b>misinterpreted</b>.","passageId":6},{"n":109,"part":"III. WRITING (3.5 pts) – Questions 106-115 Word Formation","section":"Word form","type":"text","q":"(109) the American Medical Association has publicly advocated against the use of Latin abbreviations and other relics of historical ______. (PHARMACY)","accept":["pharmacology","pharmaceutics"],"answer":"pharmacology","explanation":"A noun for the science/practice: relics of historical <b>pharmacology</b>.","passageId":6},{"n":110,"part":"III. WRITING (3.5 pts) – Questions 106-115 Word Formation","section":"Word form","type":"text","q":"(110) enforcing these changes might be difficult due to the ______ of the healthcare system where each institution has its own authority. (CENTRAL)","accept":["decentralization","decentralisation"],"answer":"decentralization","explanation":"Each institution having \"its own authority\" means the system is decentralized: the noun <b>decentralization</b>.","passageId":6},{"n":111,"part":"III. WRITING (3.5 pts) – Questions 106-115 Word Formation","section":"Word form","type":"text","q":"(111) Regulators who ______ potential areas of confusion, such as drug names have adopted certain changes (SEE)","accept":["foresee","foresaw"],"answer":"foresee","explanation":"Regulators who anticipate confusion in advance — <b>foresee</b> — act to change drug names.","passageId":6},{"n":112,"part":"III. WRITING (3.5 pts) – Questions 106-115 Word Formation","section":"Word form","type":"text","q":"(112) ______, in 1990, the antacid Losec was renamed Prilosec to differentiate it from Lasix, a diuretic. (LIKE)","accept":["Likewise"],"answer":"Likewise","explanation":"A sentence adverb introducing a similar example: <b>Likewise</b> = in the same way.","passageId":6},{"n":113,"part":"III. WRITING (3.5 pts) – Questions 106-115 Word Formation","section":"Word form","type":"text","q":"(113) the antacid Losec was renamed Prilosec to ______ it from Lasix, a diuretic. (DIFFERENCE)","accept":["differentiate"],"answer":"differentiate","explanation":"A verb after \"to\": <b>differentiate</b> X from Y = make them distinguishable.","passageId":6},{"n":114,"part":"III. WRITING (3.5 pts) – Questions 106-115 Word Formation","section":"Word form","type":"text","q":"(114) Switching to '______ prescriber order entry' systems seems to fix the infamous problem of illegible handwriting (COMPUTER)","accept":["computerized","computerised"],"answer":"computerized","explanation":"An adjective (past participle) describing the systems: <b>computerized</b> prescriber order entry (CPOE).","passageId":6},{"n":115,"part":"III. WRITING (3.5 pts) – Questions 106-115 Word Formation","section":"Word form","type":"text","q":"(115) seems to fix the ______ problem of illegible handwriting, but many systems still permit naked decimals (FAME)","accept":["infamous"],"answer":"infamous","explanation":"A problem that is well known for being bad is <b>infamous</b> (negative form of \"famous\").","passageId":6},{"n":116,"part":"III. WRITING (3.5 pts) – Questions 116-120 Error Correction","section":"Error correction","type":"text","q":"Identify the FIVE errors in the following passage and correct them. Line 1 \"a backbone → the backbone\" is the printed example and does not count.<br><br><b>Line 1:</b> Family relationships are often seen as a backbone of the society, playing<br><b>Line 2:</b> a pivotal role in shaping our values and identities. Over the years,<br><b>Line 3:</b> many families have grappled for maintaining close-knit relationships<br><b>Line 4:</b> amidst the hustle and bustle of modern life. Engaging in heart-to-heart<br><b>Line 5:</b> conversations, cherishing sharing memories, and keeping family traditions alive<br><b>Line 6:</b> have all been cited as crucial to fostering strong bonds. However, as<br><b>Line 7:</b> technology advances, the way we connect with our loved one has evolved,<br><b>Line 8:</b> sometimes in the expense of genuine face-to-face interactions. Thus, making time<br><b>Line 9:</b> for our family and nurturing these relationships remain more important than ever.<br><br><b>Error in line 3</b> — write the correction (no more than three words).","accept":["grappled with","with","grappled with maintaining","have grappled with"],"answer":"grappled with","explanation":"\"Grapple <b>with</b>\" (not \"for\") = struggle to deal with a problem."},{"n":117,"part":"III. WRITING (3.5 pts) – Questions 116-120 Error Correction","section":"Error correction","type":"text","q":"Identify the FIVE errors in the following passage and correct them. Line 1 \"a backbone → the backbone\" is the printed example and does not count.<br><br><b>Line 1:</b> Family relationships are often seen as a backbone of the society, playing<br><b>Line 2:</b> a pivotal role in shaping our values and identities. Over the years,<br><b>Line 3:</b> many families have grappled for maintaining close-knit relationships<br><b>Line 4:</b> amidst the hustle and bustle of modern life. Engaging in heart-to-heart<br><b>Line 5:</b> conversations, cherishing sharing memories, and keeping family traditions alive<br><b>Line 6:</b> have all been cited as crucial to fostering strong bonds. However, as<br><b>Line 7:</b> technology advances, the way we connect with our loved one has evolved,<br><b>Line 8:</b> sometimes in the expense of genuine face-to-face interactions. Thus, making time<br><b>Line 9:</b> for our family and nurturing these relationships remain more important than ever.<br><br><b>Error in line 5</b> — write the correction (no more than three words).","accept":["shared memories","shared","cherishing shared memories","cherishing shared"],"answer":"shared memories","explanation":"Memories that are held in common are \"<b>shared</b> memories\" (past participle as adjective), not \"sharing memories\"."},{"n":118,"part":"III. WRITING (3.5 pts) – Questions 116-120 Error Correction","section":"Error correction","type":"text","q":"Identify the FIVE errors in the following passage and correct them. Line 1 \"a backbone → the backbone\" is the printed example and does not count.<br><br><b>Line 1:</b> Family relationships are often seen as a backbone of the society, playing<br><b>Line 2:</b> a pivotal role in shaping our values and identities. Over the years,<br><b>Line 3:</b> many families have grappled for maintaining close-knit relationships<br><b>Line 4:</b> amidst the hustle and bustle of modern life. Engaging in heart-to-heart<br><b>Line 5:</b> conversations, cherishing sharing memories, and keeping family traditions alive<br><b>Line 6:</b> have all been cited as crucial to fostering strong bonds. However, as<br><b>Line 7:</b> technology advances, the way we connect with our loved one has evolved,<br><b>Line 8:</b> sometimes in the expense of genuine face-to-face interactions. Thus, making time<br><b>Line 9:</b> for our family and nurturing these relationships remain more important than ever.<br><br><b>Error in line 7</b> — write the correction (no more than three words).","accept":["loved ones","ones","our loved ones"],"answer":"loved ones","explanation":"The idiom is plural: \"our <b>loved ones</b>\" = the people we love."},{"n":119,"part":"III. WRITING (3.5 pts) – Questions 116-120 Error Correction","section":"Error correction","type":"text","q":"Identify the FIVE errors in the following passage and correct them. Line 1 \"a backbone → the backbone\" is the printed example and does not count.<br><br><b>Line 1:</b> Family relationships are often seen as a backbone of the society, playing<br><b>Line 2:</b> a pivotal role in shaping our values and identities. Over the years,<br><b>Line 3:</b> many families have grappled for maintaining close-knit relationships<br><b>Line 4:</b> amidst the hustle and bustle of modern life. Engaging in heart-to-heart<br><b>Line 5:</b> conversations, cherishing sharing memories, and keeping family traditions alive<br><b>Line 6:</b> have all been cited as crucial to fostering strong bonds. However, as<br><b>Line 7:</b> technology advances, the way we connect with our loved one has evolved,<br><b>Line 8:</b> sometimes in the expense of genuine face-to-face interactions. Thus, making time<br><b>Line 9:</b> for our family and nurturing these relationships remain more important than ever.<br><br><b>Error in line 8</b> — write the correction (no more than three words).","accept":["at the expense","at","at the expense of","sometimes at the expense"],"answer":"at the expense","explanation":"The fixed phrase is \"<b>at</b> the expense of\" = to the detriment of."},{"n":120,"part":"III. WRITING (3.5 pts) – Questions 116-120 Error Correction","section":"Error correction","type":"text","q":"Identify the FIVE errors in the following passage and correct them. Line 1 \"a backbone → the backbone\" is the printed example and does not count.<br><br><b>Line 1:</b> Family relationships are often seen as a backbone of the society, playing<br><b>Line 2:</b> a pivotal role in shaping our values and identities. Over the years,<br><b>Line 3:</b> many families have grappled for maintaining close-knit relationships<br><b>Line 4:</b> amidst the hustle and bustle of modern life. Engaging in heart-to-heart<br><b>Line 5:</b> conversations, cherishing sharing memories, and keeping family traditions alive<br><b>Line 6:</b> have all been cited as crucial to fostering strong bonds. However, as<br><b>Line 7:</b> technology advances, the way we connect with our loved one has evolved,<br><b>Line 8:</b> sometimes in the expense of genuine face-to-face interactions. Thus, making time<br><b>Line 9:</b> for our family and nurturing these relationships remain more important than ever.<br><br><b>Error in line 9</b> — write the correction (no more than three words).","accept":["remains","remains more important","relationships remains"],"answer":"remains","explanation":"The subject is the gerund phrase \"making time … and nurturing these relationships\", treated as one activity, so the verb is singular: <b>remains</b>."},{"n":121,"part":"III. WRITING (3.5 pts) – Questions 121-130 Sentence Transformation","section":"Sentence transformation","type":"text","q":"For the audience, the effortless performance of the acrobats was astonishing. (HOW)<br>→ The audience was ______ performed.","accept":["astonished at how effortlessly the acrobats","astonished by how effortlessly the acrobats","astonished at how effortlessly the acrobats had","astonished by how effortlessly the acrobats had"],"answer":"astonished at how effortlessly the acrobats","explanation":"\"Astonished at/by <b>how effortlessly</b> the acrobats performed\" — the adjective \"effortless\" becomes the adverb after \"how\"."},{"n":122,"part":"III. WRITING (3.5 pts) – Questions 121-130 Sentence Transformation","section":"Sentence transformation","type":"text","q":"Molly told me it is not a good idea to throw away the receipts. (HOLD)<br>→ 'You had ______.' Molly told me.","accept":["better hold on to the receipts","better hold onto the receipts","better hold on to your receipts","better hold onto your receipts","better hold on to those receipts","better hold onto those receipts"],"answer":"better hold on to the receipts","explanation":"\"You had <b>better hold on to</b> the receipts\" = you should keep them (advice), the opposite of throwing them away."},{"n":123,"part":"III. WRITING (3.5 pts) – Questions 121-130 Sentence Transformation","section":"Sentence transformation","type":"text","q":"Being uncharismatic made it hard for her to become a successful actress. (BARRED)<br>→ Her lack of ______ success in acting.","accept":["charisma barred her from achieving","charisma barred her from","charisma barred her from attaining","charisma barred her way to","charisma barred her from finding","charisma barred her from gaining"],"answer":"charisma barred her from achieving","explanation":"\"Her lack of <b>charisma barred her from (achieving)</b> success\" — bar somebody from something = prevent them from getting it."},{"n":124,"part":"III. WRITING (3.5 pts) – Questions 121-130 Sentence Transformation","section":"Sentence transformation","type":"text","q":"If we want to finish before the deadline, we have to work faster. (PICK)<br>→ The deadline cannot ______ the pace.","accept":["be met unless we pick up","be met if we don't pick up","be met unless we pick up the pace","be met without us picking up","be met without our picking up","be met unless we pick up on"],"answer":"be met unless we pick up","explanation":"\"The deadline cannot <b>be met unless we pick up</b> the pace\" — pick up the pace = work faster; \"unless\" = if … not."},{"n":125,"part":"III. WRITING (3.5 pts) – Questions 121-130 Sentence Transformation","section":"Sentence transformation","type":"text","q":"They will do whatever it takes to save the company. (STOP)<br>→ They will ______ the company going.","accept":["stop at nothing to keep","stop at nothing in order to keep","stop at nothing so as to keep"],"answer":"stop at nothing to keep","explanation":"\"<b>Stop at nothing</b> to do something\" = be willing to do anything to achieve it; \"keep the company going\" = save it."},{"n":126,"part":"III. WRITING (3.5 pts) – Questions 121-130 Sentence Transformation","section":"Sentence transformation","type":"text","q":"'You must tell me how the money got lost!' the boss demanded. (ACCOUNT)<br>→ The boss demanded that ______ money.","accept":["I account for the lost","I should account for the lost","I account for the missing","I should account for the missing","we account for the lost","we should account for the lost","we account for the missing","we should account for the missing","I accounted for the lost","I accounted for the missing"],"answer":"I account for the lost","explanation":"\"Demand that + subject + (should) + bare infinitive\": the boss demanded that I <b>account for</b> (explain) the lost money."},{"n":127,"part":"III. WRITING (3.5 pts) – Questions 121-130 Sentence Transformation","section":"Sentence transformation","type":"text","q":"There is a severe shortage of clean water in our community after the disaster. (DIRE)<br>→ Our community ______ after the disaster.","accept":["is in dire need of clean water","has been in dire need of clean water","is in dire shortage of clean water","is in dire straits for clean water"],"answer":"is in dire need of clean water","explanation":"\"Be in <b>dire need of</b> something\" = need it urgently; a severe shortage means the community badly needs clean water."},{"n":128,"part":"III. WRITING (3.5 pts) – Questions 121-130 Sentence Transformation","section":"Sentence transformation","type":"text","q":"The economy is in crisis, so it is very likely that many companies will go bankrupt. (VERGE)<br>→ Many companies are ______ to the economic crisis.","accept":["on the verge of bankruptcy due","on the verge of going bankrupt due","on the verge of bankruptcy owing","on the verge of going bankrupt owing","on the verge of bankruptcy thanks","on the verge of going bankrupt thanks"],"answer":"on the verge of bankruptcy due","explanation":"\"<b>On the verge of</b> bankruptcy / going bankrupt\" = very close to it; \"due to\" gives the cause."},{"n":129,"part":"III. WRITING (3.5 pts) – Questions 121-130 Sentence Transformation","section":"Sentence transformation","type":"text","q":"The manager covered up the mistake because he didn't want to be criticized. (FEAR)<br>→ The manager swept the mistake ______ criticism.","accept":["under the carpet for fear of","under the rug for fear of","under the carpet out of fear of","under the rug out of fear of","under the carpet in fear of","under the rug in fear of"],"answer":"under the carpet for fear of","explanation":"\"Sweep something <b>under the carpet</b>\" = cover it up; \"<b>for fear of</b> criticism\" = because he was afraid of being criticized."},{"n":130,"part":"III. WRITING (3.5 pts) – Questions 121-130 Sentence Transformation","section":"Sentence transformation","type":"text","q":"The touring company stopped to have some food before continuing their journey. (BITE)<br>→ Having ______ their journey.","accept":["had a bite to eat, the touring company continued","had a bite, the touring company continued","grabbed a bite to eat, the touring company continued","grabbed a bite, the touring company continued","had a bite to eat, the company continued","had a bite, the company continued","grabbed a bite to eat, the company continued","grabbed a bite, the company continued","had a bite to eat, they continued","had a bite, they continued","grabbed a bite to eat, they continued","grabbed a bite, they continued","had a bite to eat, the touring company went on with","had a bite, the touring company went on with"],"answer":"had a bite to eat, the touring company continued","explanation":"\"Having <b>had a bite (to eat)</b>, the touring company continued their journey\" — a perfect participle clause for the earlier action; \"a bite\" = a small meal."}]},{"id":"ptnk-2023-chuyen","year":2023,"track":"chuyen","title":"PTNK 2023 · Tiếng Anh Chuyên","subtitle":"Đề thi tuyển sinh lớp 10 · Trường Phổ thông Năng khiếu · 2023-2024","durationMin":120,"keySource":"official","source":"De-thi-Tieng-Anh-chuyen-2023-2024 THPTNK.pdf","passages":["As <i>The Autobiography of Benjamin Franklin</i> describes in detail, apprenticeships in trades from printing to blacksmithing and tailoring were <b><u>a fixture</u></b> in Colonial America. Then came the Industrial Revolution. Manufacturing adopted an assembly-line process. Tasks were divided and subdivided and subdivided again into tiny, repetitious routines or reduced to machine watching, requiring no particular hierarchy of skills. Except for certain construction trades, apprenticeship was largely <b><u>relegated</u></b> to a historical footnote. In the United States in 1989, only 263,000 workers out of a blue-collar force of 35 million were registered apprentices. Their average age was in the mid-20s.<br><br>As college – any kind of liberal arts diploma – came to be seen as the only ticket to success, the country continued to back away from apprenticeship. In high schools, so-called shop courses evolved into <b><u>a dumping ground</u></b> for students who couldn't cope with (or had little interest in) education aimed at college. The idea that schools should prepare students to fill a niche in an industrialised society was regarded as elitist and un-American.<br><br>But today, partly because of the shortage of skilled workers and the need to revive American industry, partly because of the failure of American secondary school education, apprenticeship and work-based learning are a hot-button issue. An apprenticeship program combining education and work to train thinking workers for tomorrow is now seen as one means for the United States to regain its position in the increasingly competitive world economy. This subject was very much on the agenda of President Bill Clinton's pre-Inaugural economic conference in Little Rock. \"The world will not long pay American line workers ten times as much to work half as hard as the competition in the Pacific Basin,\" says Marc Tucker of the National Center on Education and the Economy. \"The choice we have is to become a nation of high skills or low wages.\"","<i>You are going to read a review of a documentary film. Five paragraphs have been removed from the review. Choose from the paragraphs A–G the one which fits each gap (1–5). There are two extra paragraphs which you do not need to use. Then answer the True / False / Not Given questions that follow.</i><br><br><b>“A Crack in the Mountain” review: Stunning cave shows tourism's paradox</b><br><i>A poignant documentary tells the paradoxical story of a vast, remote cave in Vietnam that looks likely to turn into a major tourist attraction</i><br>Simon Ings, 17 May 2023 | The New Scientists<br><br>“Everyone on a bicycle wants to be on a motorbike. Everyone on a motorbike wants to be in a car. And everyone in a car wants to be in a helicopter,” says entrepreneur David English, a wry smile creeping across his face. “So off we go to the future.”<br><br>(1)____<br><br>But the arrival of a British caving expedition the previous April had signalled a big change. The team had come to explore a remote cave system, whose entrance had been found by a local man named Ho Khanh in 1990, but that had otherwise been ignored.<br><br>(2)____<br><br>With its jungles, rivers and waterfalls, Hang Son Doong is the largest dry cave (dry because it isn't underwater) in the world. “It doesn't feel like you're on planet Earth any more,” says Meredith Harvey, a visitor to the cave.<br><br>Now, the local government wants to run a cable car through the site, opening it up to 1000 tourists an hour. Conversations with UNESCO (the cave is in Phong Nha-Ke Bang National Park, which became a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 2003) have won a reprieve until 2030, but no one believes the site will remain untouched forever.<br><br>(3)____<br><br>Certainly. It is hard to imagine a rapidly growing economy writing off its most potentially lucrative wonder so a few wealthy tourists can enjoy its pristine charms.<br><br>It isn't unreasonable to want an adventure, or to make the most of your birthplace. Nor is it, after a lifetime of riding to work on a motorbike, to want your children to afford a car. It is what makes the tragedy of the commons a tragedy.<br><br>(4)____<br><br>The production values of the documentary are extraordinarily high and the cave expeditions appear very well managed. One might wish that Nguyen could simply be left alone to tailor the region's development according to the needs of local people.<br><br>(5)____<br><br>This is a film about a wicked problem, sure to despoil a wonderful location, if not today then tomorrow or the day after that. By then, if a way to solve this impossible equation is to be found, it will surely have been inspired by films as intelligent and passionate as this one.<br><br><b>A</b> But then, that is to forget the ravages of covid-19, which closed down 90 per cent of Phong Nha's small businesses, not to mention recent floods that brought what little activity remained to a standstill.<br><b>B</b> Of course, you can still watch the documentary for its beauty, and some credit for this goes to Oxalis Adventure, an expeditions and production company founded by Phong Nha-born entrepreneur Nguyen Chau, which has put a lot of money back into the local economy – and made it possible to film the documentary at all.<br><b>C</b> 'A Crack in the Mountain' plays its role in this dialogue by showcasing the stark reality without offering a silver bullet. Because, let's face it, these problems are complex. Just like any wicked problem, the answer lies in our hands.<br><b>D</b> Ten years ago, Phong Nha in Quang Binh province was one of the poorest regions of Vietnam. English arrived during flooding in 2010 and remembers the air of despondency. People fished the rivers and grew a little rice. Hunger was commonplace.<br><b>E</b> Following a 5-kilometre-long fault through limestone, the cavers discovered chambers that are each big enough to hold a skyscraper. In places, the ceilings are 200 metres high. Where the roof has fallen in, sunken forests sport rare tree ferns and other plants.<br><b>F</b> You may feel the story of Phong Nha strikes a chord. It's the age-old tug-of-war between economic prosperity and the environment, a balancing act that could leave you teetering on the edge. The community around Hang Son Doong is grappling with this, caught in the crosshairs of progress and preservation.<br><b>G</b> In “A Crack in the Mountain”, director Alastair Evans tells a story we have heard many times before. It is a “tragedy of the commons”, a term coined in 1968 by biologist Garrett Hardin for situations in which people use a shared resource for their own self-interest, leading to its eventual depletion. Will this happen here?","<b>Artificial Intelligence or Humanity?</b><br><br>Dr. Paplivia Russo from the ABS Institute of Technology believes that humans are in an unstable situation. She posits that artificial intelligence could become the (21)____ force in future societies, with human beings finding themselves at a (22)____. To remain competitive, she argues, they will have to (23)____ their capabilities, which can only be achieved by integrating more advanced technology within themselves, essentially becoming part-human, part-AI.<br><br>To pave the way for this integration, Dr. Russo has become the (24)____ of her own experiments. In the winter of 2018, she had a device embedded in her wrist that captures her movements and feelings, transmitting them as data to a computer system. Dr. Russo's partner agreed to a similar procedure, thus allowing their neural activities to be linked via a computer system. Dr. Russo envisions that human communication will be (25)____ by such technology. On her research agenda, an attempt will be (26)____ to implant a similar device into her brain, linking it directly with a computer.<br><br>Understandably, Dr. Russo's work has (27)____ significant media attention and sparked robust debate. Critics have (28)____ that her research is nothing more than a sophisticated form of self-promotion. However, Dr. Russo dismisses these allegations, asserting that she would never subject herself to such invasive procedures for the mere sake of publicity. She anticipates that once the technology she is developing is (29)____, it could be (30)____ in many ways, such as enabling individuals with paralysis to regain control over their movements.","New research from Stanford University suggested that we learn how to behave (0) <u>by</u> observing the actions (1)____ others. In the context of relationships, a child learns by observing the relationships around them, (2)____ as the one between their parents or siblings. They learn what is acceptable and what is not, (3)____ they acquire knowledge about sharing, empathy, and conflict resolution.<br><br>As adults, we continue to learn (4)____ relationships from our partners, friends, and even from television and movies. However, just (5)____ we learn from observing others doesn't (6)____ we don't make mistakes. Human relationships are complex and filled with nuances that can often lead to misunderstandings.<br><br>Communication is an integral part of any relationship. (7)____ effective communication, misunderstandings become more frequent, leading to frustration and conflict. Listening is just as important, if not (8)____ so, than talking in a relationship. It shows respect and understanding towards the other person's perspective.<br><br>In the (9)____, relationships are built on mutual respect, understanding, and communication. The quality of our relationships often determines our quality of life. Therefore, working (10)____ to nurture and maintain relationships is a worthy endeavor.","In the digital age, people often tune into the news without taking a moment to vet the sources of information, a habit that's (1)____ healthy than binging on a bag of potato chips. As the flow of information hurls at us, whatever the hour, it becomes paramount to sift (2)____ the vast pile of data. (3)____ one to pick up another unverified article and present it as fact, one would find oneself the laughing (4)____ at a party, caught up in a frenzy of social embarrassment. It's crucial to look (5)____ each piece of information, since it's not as easy as one might think to spot fake news. The digital world is (6)____ of dubious content, some of (7)____ as plausible as another. The media landscape might not be perfect, but the onus is on us, the consumers, to check out the credibility of one source versus the other, and to separate the (8)____ from the chaff. We can't always rely on journalists to dish (9)____ the truth; sometimes, we must take up that mantle ourselves. After all, one man's truth could be (10)____ man's fiction.","Life-forms present on Earth today have evolved from ancient common (0) <u>ancestors</u> (ANCESTRAL) through the generation of (1)____ (HEIR) variation and natural (2)____ (SELECT). Although some studies state that life may have begun as early as 4.1 billion years ago, it can be traced to fossils dated to 3.5–3.7 billion years ago, which is still only slightly (3)____ (YOUTH) than Earth, which (4)____ (GRAVITY) accreted into a planet about 4.5 billion years ago. But this is life as a whole. More than 99.9 percent of species that have ever lived are (5)____ (EXTINCTION). The several branches of science that reveal the common historical, functional, and chemical basis of the (6)____ (EVOLVE) of all life include electron microscopy, genetics, paleobiology (including palaeontology), and molecular biology.<br><br>The phenomenon of life can be approached in several ways: life as it is known and studied on planet Earth; life imaginable in principle; and life, by (7)____ (THESIS) that might exist elsewhere in the universe. As far as is known, life exists only on Earth. Most life-forms reside in a thin sphere that extends about 23 km (14 miles) from 3 km (2 miles) beneath the bottom of the ocean to the top of the troposphere; the relative (8)____ (THICK) is comparable to a coat of paint on a rubber ball. An (9)____ (ESTIMATION) 10 to 30 million distinguishable species currently (10)____ (HABITAT) this sphere of life, or biosphere.<br><br><i>(An extract from the Britannica Encyclopedia)</i>","<b>For questions 101-105, identify the five errors in the following passage and correct them. Indicate the line at which mistakes are found, and how to correct them. Write NO MORE THAN THREE WORDS for each error/correction.</b><br><br><b>Line</b><br>1 The Olympic Games, one of the world's most prestigious athletic spectacle,<br>2 have roots dating back from ancient Greece, approximately 776 BCE. Originally,<br>3 they were religious events held in Olympia, in honor of Zeus, the king of the Greek<br>4 gods. Revived in the modern era by Baron Pierre de Coubertin in 1896, in Athens,<br>5 Greece, they eventually became a platform promoted international peace and<br>6 friendship through sports.<br><br>7 The Olympics have evolved significantly overtime, including the introduction<br>8 of the Winter Games in 1924 and the Paralympics for athletes with disabilities in<br>9 1960. Without obstacles like political boycotts, terrorism, and pandemics, the<br>10 Olympic Games have persistently symbolised the unifying spirit of global<br>11 competitor and fair play."],"questions":[{"n":1,"part":"I. READING — PASSAGE 1 (8 pts) — Questions 1-8","section":"Reading","type":"mcq","passageId":0,"q":"What is the most appropriate title for this passage?","options":["\"The Evolution and Revival of Apprenticeship in the United States\"","\"The Decline of Traditional Education in the United States\"","\"The Influence of the Industrial Revolution on American Workforce\"","\"The Rise of Liberal Arts Education in America\""],"correct":0,"explanation":"The passage traces apprenticeship from Colonial America through its decline after the Industrial Revolution to its present revival as a \"hot-button issue\" — its whole evolution and comeback."},{"n":2,"part":"I. READING — PASSAGE 1 (8 pts) — Questions 1-8","section":"Reading","type":"mcq","passageId":0,"q":"What does the term \"<b><u>a fixture</u></b>\" mean in paragraph 1?","options":["A type of lighting device used in interior design","A person or thing long established in the same place or position","A component used to hold or fasten something securely","A scheduled event, such as a game or match, in sports"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Apprenticeships \"were a fixture in Colonial America\" — something long established and firmly part of life there."},{"n":3,"part":"I. READING — PASSAGE 1 (8 pts) — Questions 1-8","section":"Reading","type":"mcq","passageId":0,"q":"Which of the following words could best replace the word \"<b><u>relegated</u></b>\" in paragraph 1?","options":["amplified","eulogised","conserved","downgraded"],"correct":3,"explanation":"\"Relegated to a historical footnote\" means demoted to a minor, unimportant position — downgraded."},{"n":4,"part":"I. READING — PASSAGE 1 (8 pts) — Questions 1-8","section":"Reading","type":"mcq","passageId":0,"q":"What was the most important factor that initiated the decline of apprenticeships in the States?","options":["An upsurge in college presence and academic sector dominance","The Industrial Revolution's simplification of tasks","The rise of liberal arts education and college degree","Unchecked growth of apprentice-reliant industries and legislative laxity"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 1: \"Then came the Industrial Revolution\" — tasks were subdivided into tiny routines \"requiring no particular hierarchy of skills\", so apprenticeship became a historical footnote; college came later (\"continued to back away\")."},{"n":5,"part":"I. READING — PASSAGE 1 (8 pts) — Questions 1-8","section":"Reading","type":"mcq","passageId":0,"q":"Why did high school shop courses become perceived as \"<b><u>a dumping ground</u></b>\"?","options":["A national shift towards college education as the only success ticket","A decline in manual labour jobs and depreciation of vocational skills value","They were filled with students indifferent to or struggling with college","It was considered elitist and un-American to prepare students for an industrial niche"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Shop courses became \"a dumping ground for students who couldn't cope with (or had little interest in) education aimed at college\"."},{"n":6,"part":"I. READING — PASSAGE 1 (8 pts) — Questions 1-8","section":"Reading","type":"mcq","passageId":0,"q":"What impact could an apprenticeship program combining education and work have on the United States?","options":["Oversaturation of job market leading to graduate unemployment","Social disparities due to apprenticeship bias over traditional education","A potential fall in secondary education quality and college enrollment rates","Reassertion of U.S. global competitiveness through fostering a skilled workforce"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Such a program is \"seen as one means for the United States to regain its position in the increasingly competitive world economy\" by training skilled \"thinking workers for tomorrow\"."},{"n":7,"part":"I. READING — PASSAGE 1 (8 pts) — Questions 1-8","section":"Reading","type":"mcq","passageId":0,"q":"Which of the following is NOT a cause for apprenticeship and work-based learning becoming a \"hot-button\" issue?","options":["the States regaining its world economy position","a shortage of skilled workers","the need to revive American industry","the failure of American secondary school education"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 lists the causes: the shortage of skilled workers, the need to revive American industry and the failure of secondary school education; regaining its world economy position is the hoped-for result, not a cause."},{"n":8,"part":"I. READING — PASSAGE 1 (8 pts) — Questions 1-8","section":"Reading","type":"mcq","passageId":0,"q":"According to the passage, which of the following is NOT a cause-and-effect relationship?","options":["The Industrial Revolution led to fewer apprenticeships.","The shortage of skilled workers led to new interest in apprenticeship program.","The Industrial Revolution led to a college degree being seen as the ticket to success.","Elitist ideas cause workers to work half as hard as those in the Pacific Basin."],"correct":3,"explanation":"The passage never links elitist ideas to how hard workers work; Marc Tucker's remark about working \"half as hard\" is about pay versus effort, not elitism."},{"n":9,"part":"I. READING — PASSAGE 2 (7 pts) — Questions 9-15 (\"A Crack in the Mountain\" review)","section":"Reading","type":"text","passageId":1,"q":"Gap (1) — write the letter (A–G) of the missing paragraph.","accept":["D"],"answer":"D","explanation":"The gap sits between English's wry \"off we go to the future\" and \"But the arrival of a British caving expedition the previous April had signalled a big change\" — D sets up the poverty ten years ago (\"English arrived during flooding in 2010 … Hunger was commonplace\") that the expedition changed."},{"n":10,"part":"I. READING — PASSAGE 2 (7 pts) — Questions 9-15 (\"A Crack in the Mountain\" review)","section":"Reading","type":"text","passageId":1,"q":"Gap (2) — write the letter (A–G) of the missing paragraph.","accept":["E"],"answer":"E","explanation":"After the cave entrance found by Ho Khanh, E describes what the cavers discovered inside (\"chambers … big enough to hold a skyscraper\"), which leads straight into \"Hang Son Doong is the largest dry cave in the world\"."},{"n":11,"part":"I. READING — PASSAGE 2 (7 pts) — Questions 9-15 (\"A Crack in the Mountain\" review)","section":"Reading","type":"text","passageId":1,"q":"Gap (3) — write the letter (A–G) of the missing paragraph.","accept":["G"],"answer":"G","explanation":"Following \"no one believes the site will remain untouched forever\", G introduces the film's theme, the \"tragedy of the commons\", and ends with \"Will this happen here?\" — answered by the next paragraph's \"Certainly.\""},{"n":12,"part":"I. READING — PASSAGE 2 (7 pts) — Questions 9-15 (\"A Crack in the Mountain\" review)","section":"Reading","type":"text","passageId":1,"q":"Gap (4) — write the letter (A–G) of the missing paragraph.","accept":["B"],"answer":"B","explanation":"B (\"Of course, you can still watch the documentary for its beauty … Oxalis Adventure … Nguyen Chau\") introduces Nguyen and the company, which the next paragraph continues: \"The production values … One might wish that Nguyen could simply be left alone\"."},{"n":13,"part":"I. READING — PASSAGE 2 (7 pts) — Questions 9-15 (\"A Crack in the Mountain\" review)","section":"Reading","type":"text","passageId":1,"q":"Gap (5) — write the letter (A–G) of the missing paragraph.","accept":["A"],"answer":"A","explanation":"A's \"But then, that is to forget the ravages of covid-19\" is the counterpoint to the wish that Nguyen \"could simply be left alone to tailor the region's development\", showing why the problem is \"wicked\", as the closing paragraph says."},{"n":14,"part":"I. READING — PASSAGE 2 (7 pts) — Questions 9-15 (\"A Crack in the Mountain\" review)","section":"Reading","type":"text","passageId":1,"q":"Does the following statement agree with the information given in the passage? Write <b>True</b> if it agrees, <b>False</b> if it contradicts the information, or <b>Not Given</b> if there is no information on this.<br>The British expedition rediscover the cave system once explored by a local man in 1990 but since then abandoned.","accept":["False","F","FALSE"],"answer":"False","explanation":"The passage says the entrance \"had been found by a local man named Ho Khanh in 1990, but that had otherwise been ignored\" — he found the entrance, he did not explore the system and then abandon it, so the statement contradicts the text."},{"n":15,"part":"I. READING — PASSAGE 2 (7 pts) — Questions 9-15 (\"A Crack in the Mountain\" review)","section":"Reading","type":"text","passageId":1,"q":"Does the following statement agree with the information given in the passage? Write <b>True</b> if it agrees, <b>False</b> if it contradicts the information, or <b>Not Given</b> if there is no information on this.<br>That a few wealthy people can enjoy the wonder while the locals may not reap the benefits of rapid ecomomic growth contributes to what referred to as the tragedy of the commons.","accept":["False","F","FALSE"],"answer":"False","explanation":"The tragedy of the commons is the opposite: everyone using a shared resource for their own self-interest (a cable car, 1000 tourists an hour) until it is depleted. Keeping the cave for \"a few wealthy tourists\" is what a growing economy will not do — so the statement contradicts the passage."},{"n":16,"part":"I. READING — PASSAGE 4 (10 pts) — Questions 21-30 (Artificial Intelligence or Humanity?)","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":2,"q":"(21) ____","options":["dominant","distinctive","eminent","ubiquitous"],"correct":0,"explanation":"\"The dominant force\" is the fixed collocation for the power that controls a society; it contrasts with humans being \"at a disadvantage\" in the next clause."},{"n":17,"part":"I. READING — PASSAGE 4 (10 pts) — Questions 21-30 (Artificial Intelligence or Humanity?)","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":2,"q":"(22) ____","options":["difficulty","threat","crisis","disadvantage"],"correct":3,"explanation":"\"At a disadvantage\" is the idiom meaning in a weaker position; the others do not fit \"find themselves at a ___\" (in difficulty, under threat, in crisis)."},{"n":18,"part":"I. READING — PASSAGE 4 (10 pts) — Questions 21-30 (Artificial Intelligence or Humanity?)","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":2,"q":"(23) ____","options":["excel","endow","enhance","implement"],"correct":2,"explanation":"\"Enhance their capabilities\" = improve them, done by \"integrating more advanced technology\"; excel is intransitive here, endow needs \"with\", and implement takes a plan, not capabilities."},{"n":19,"part":"I. READING — PASSAGE 4 (10 pts) — Questions 21-30 (Artificial Intelligence or Humanity?)","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":2,"q":"(24) ____","options":["figure","researcher","study","subject"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The \"subject of an experiment\" is the person it is performed on — she had a device embedded in her own wrist."},{"n":20,"part":"I. READING — PASSAGE 4 (10 pts) — Questions 21-30 (Artificial Intelligence or Humanity?)","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":2,"q":"(25) ____","options":["cultivated","increased","expanded","transformed"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Linking people's neural activity by computer would radically change communication, so it will be \"transformed by such technology\"; communication is not \"increased\" or \"cultivated\" by a device."},{"n":21,"part":"I. READING — PASSAGE 4 (10 pts) — Questions 21-30 (Artificial Intelligence or Humanity?)","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":2,"q":"(26) ____","options":["taken","tried","made","done"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The collocation is \"make an attempt\"; in the passive, \"an attempt will be made to implant…\"."},{"n":22,"part":"I. READING — PASSAGE 4 (10 pts) — Questions 21-30 (Artificial Intelligence or Humanity?)","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":2,"q":"(27) ____","options":["provided","covered","drawn","made"],"correct":2,"explanation":"\"Draw (media) attention\" is the standard collocation for attracting attention; work cannot \"provide\" or \"cover\" attention."},{"n":23,"part":"I. READING — PASSAGE 4 (10 pts) — Questions 21-30 (Artificial Intelligence or Humanity?)","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":2,"q":"(28) ____","options":["denied","implied","questioned","pointed"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Critics \"implied that\" her research is self-promotion — they suggested it indirectly, which the next sentence calls \"these allegations\"; \"questioned that\" and \"pointed that\" are ungrammatical, and \"denied\" reverses the meaning."},{"n":24,"part":"I. READING — PASSAGE 4 (10 pts) — Questions 21-30 (Artificial Intelligence or Humanity?)","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":2,"q":"(29) ____","options":["perfected","maximised","modified","assimilated"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The technology is still being developed; \"once it is perfected\" (made fully reliable) it can be put to use — the others do not describe finishing development."},{"n":25,"part":"I. READING — PASSAGE 4 (10 pts) — Questions 21-30 (Artificial Intelligence or Humanity?)","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":2,"q":"(30) ____","options":["desirable","beneficial","enlightening","utopian"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The example that follows — helping people with paralysis regain movement — shows the technology being \"beneficial in many ways\" (useful, helpful)."},{"n":26,"part":"II. USE OF LANGUAGE — GRAMMAR AND VOCABULARY (15 pts) — Questions 31-60","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"I ______ the memories from my time in Japan, though I have been home for a year.","options":["was still cherishing","am still cherishing","had been still cherishing","have been still cherishing"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The memories are still being cherished now, so the present continuous fits; \"though I have been home for a year\" contrasts the present feeling with the time elapsed."},{"n":27,"part":"II. USE OF LANGUAGE — GRAMMAR AND VOCABULARY (15 pts) — Questions 31-60","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"We _______ the Jeju Island while we were in Korea, but sadly we ran out of time.","options":["visited","would visit","were visiting","would have visited"],"correct":3,"explanation":"\"Would have visited\" expresses an unrealised past intention — they planned to go but did not because they ran out of time."},{"n":28,"part":"II. USE OF LANGUAGE — GRAMMAR AND VOCABULARY (15 pts) — Questions 31-60","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"He ______ signing a lease for a site in Boston when one of his backers persuaded him to take a look at Silicon Valley.","options":["was going to","was due to","was to","was on the point of"],"correct":3,"explanation":"\"Be on the point of + V-ing\" means to be just about to do something; the other options need a bare infinitive, not \"signing\"."},{"n":29,"part":"II. USE OF LANGUAGE — GRAMMAR AND VOCABULARY (15 pts) — Questions 31-60","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"The email notification went off, and _______ out of his bed, though it was two in the morning.","options":["up got he","got up he","did he get up","up he got"],"correct":3,"explanation":"With a fronted adverb particle and a pronoun subject, the order is adverb + pronoun + verb: \"up he got\" (inversion happens only with noun subjects, e.g. \"up got John\")."},{"n":30,"part":"II. USE OF LANGUAGE — GRAMMAR AND VOCABULARY (15 pts) — Questions 31-60","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"After what he did, the authority decided to lock him up and ordered that _____.","options":["he not be discharged","he be not discharged","he wouldn't be discharged","he couldn't be discharged"],"correct":0,"explanation":"After \"order that\" the subjunctive is used; the negative subjunctive is \"not + base verb\": \"that he not be discharged\"."},{"n":31,"part":"II. USE OF LANGUAGE — GRAMMAR AND VOCABULARY (15 pts) — Questions 31-60","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"No _____ Sasha found she lost her keys than she rushed to the store she _____.","options":["Sooner did/ just left","later did/ did just leave","Sooner had/ had just left","Later had/ had just left"],"correct":2,"explanation":"\"No sooner had + S + past participle ... than ...\" is the fixed inverted structure; the store is one she \"had just left\" (past perfect, before the rushing)."},{"n":32,"part":"II. USE OF LANGUAGE — GRAMMAR AND VOCABULARY (15 pts) — Questions 31-60","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"We hear ______ about the tensions and difficulties of asking someone to be our lover, but _____ of the challenges of asking someone to be our friend.","options":["so much/ so few","so many/ so little","so many/ so few","so much/ so little"],"correct":3,"explanation":"\"Hear so much about\" and \"so little of\" — both refer to an uncountable amount of talk or information, so \"much/little\" rather than \"many/few\"."},{"n":33,"part":"II. USE OF LANGUAGE — GRAMMAR AND VOCABULARY (15 pts) — Questions 31-60","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"_______ a compartment for air and a breathing tube, the aqua lung has revolutionised undersea exploration.","options":["consisted","comprised","consisting of","comprising of"],"correct":2,"explanation":"A present participle clause describes the aqua lung: \"consisting of\" (consist takes \"of\"; \"comprise\" takes a direct object with no \"of\")."},{"n":34,"part":"II. USE OF LANGUAGE — GRAMMAR AND VOCABULARY (15 pts) — Questions 31-60","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"The butcher sliced fifty pounds of roast beef each morning _____ the deli down the street sold it all during lunch.","options":["because","since","before","so that"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The slicing happened in the morning and the selling at lunch — a time sequence, so \"before\" links the two events."},{"n":35,"part":"II. USE OF LANGUAGE — GRAMMAR AND VOCABULARY (15 pts) — Questions 31-60","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"The unannounced nature of the president's arrival suggests he is keen to keep _______.","options":["a low profile","a small key","a slight apperance","a modest image"],"correct":0,"explanation":"\"Keep a low profile\" is the idiom for avoiding public attention, which matches an unannounced arrival."},{"n":36,"part":"II. USE OF LANGUAGE — GRAMMAR AND VOCABULARY (15 pts) — Questions 31-60","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"The public had to admit to a(n) ____ respect for her, although they ____ disliked her.","options":["forceful/ extremely","adequate/ absolutely","grudging/ heartily","inevitable/ reluctantly"],"correct":2,"explanation":"\"Grudging respect\" is respect given unwillingly, which fits people who \"heartily disliked\" her; \"heartily dislike\" is the natural collocation."},{"n":37,"part":"II. USE OF LANGUAGE — GRAMMAR AND VOCABULARY (15 pts) — Questions 31-60","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"There's no ______ proof of his guilt. The jury has no choice but to ___ him.","options":["conclusive/ acquit","concrete/ discharge","sizeable/ free","tangible/ release"],"correct":0,"explanation":"\"Conclusive proof\" is the collocation, and a jury that cannot convict must \"acquit\" the defendant."},{"n":38,"part":"II. USE OF LANGUAGE — GRAMMAR AND VOCABULARY (15 pts) — Questions 31-60","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"The suggestion is _____ the initial idea; it's a much safer choice.","options":["nearly as controversial as","just as controversial as","almost as controversial as","nowhere near as controversial as"],"correct":3,"explanation":"\"A much safer choice\" means it is far less controversial, so \"nowhere near as controversial as\" is the only option expressing that big difference."},{"n":39,"part":"II. USE OF LANGUAGE — GRAMMAR AND VOCABULARY (15 pts) — Questions 31-60","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Sir Kent Robinson is a(n) ______ speaker who holds lectures around the globe.","options":["motivational, British, well-known","well-known, British, motivational","British, well-known, motivational","British, motivational, well-known"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Adjective order puts opinion first, then origin, then purpose/type: \"well-known\" (opinion), \"British\" (origin), \"motivational\" (purpose) speaker."},{"n":40,"part":"II. USE OF LANGUAGE — GRAMMAR AND VOCABULARY (15 pts) — Questions 31-60","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Hanyu Yuzuru won the World Figure Skating Championship four years _______.","options":["running","passing","following","rotating"],"correct":0,"explanation":"\"Four years running\" means four years in a row, consecutively."},{"n":41,"part":"II. USE OF LANGUAGE — GRAMMAR AND VOCABULARY (15 pts) — Questions 31-60","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"____ the chief engineer ____ to call, tell him the machine is expected to be installed by Friday.","options":["if/ happen","should/ happen","would/ happen","will/ happen"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Inverted conditional: \"Should the chief engineer happen to call\" = \"If the chief engineer should happen to call\"; \"if\" cannot be followed by the bare \"happen\" with no inversion."},{"n":42,"part":"II. USE OF LANGUAGE — GRAMMAR AND VOCABULARY (15 pts) — Questions 31-60","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Alice wondered when ____ the national park again.","options":["she will visit","will she visit","would she visit","she would visit"],"correct":3,"explanation":"A reported question keeps statement word order, and \"wondered\" (past) backshifts \"will\" to \"would\": \"when she would visit\"."},{"n":43,"part":"II. USE OF LANGUAGE — GRAMMAR AND VOCABULARY (15 pts) — Questions 31-60","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Ronaldo's second goal was _______ because he was off-side.","options":["banned","disqualified","disallowed","outlawed"],"correct":2,"explanation":"A goal ruled invalid by the referee is \"disallowed\"; players are disqualified, things are banned or outlawed."},{"n":44,"part":"II. USE OF LANGUAGE — GRAMMAR AND VOCABULARY (15 pts) — Questions 31-60","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Current policies _______, the government decided to try a new approach.","options":["were abandoned","be abandoned","being abandoned","had been abandoned"],"correct":2,"explanation":"An absolute participle clause with its own subject: \"Current policies being abandoned, the government ...\" — a finite verb would need a conjunction."},{"n":45,"part":"II. USE OF LANGUAGE — GRAMMAR AND VOCABULARY (15 pts) — Questions 31-60","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"I'd prefer it if you _____ the proposal for our meeting.","options":["could prepare","can prepare","prepare","prepared"],"correct":3,"explanation":"\"I'd prefer it if + S + past simple\" is the polite structure for a present/future wish, like \"I'd rather you did\"."},{"n":46,"part":"II. USE OF LANGUAGE — GRAMMAR AND VOCABULARY (15 pts) — Questions 31-60","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"When her parents passed away, she came _____ a small fortune, mostly in art collections and property.","options":["across","into","up with","off"],"correct":1,"explanation":"\"Come into (money/a fortune)\" means to inherit it."},{"n":47,"part":"II. USE OF LANGUAGE — GRAMMAR AND VOCABULARY (15 pts) — Questions 31-60","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Mary adamantly ______ to ______ her wrongdoings, persistently evading any sense of responsibility.","options":["denied/ accept","resisted/ accept","rejected/ acknowledge","refused/ acknowledge"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Only \"refuse\" takes a to-infinitive (\"refused to acknowledge\"); \"deny\" and \"resist\" take -ing, and \"reject\" takes a noun."},{"n":48,"part":"II. USE OF LANGUAGE — GRAMMAR AND VOCABULARY (15 pts) — Questions 31-60","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"The local authority claim to have _____ all the mosquitoes in the _______.","options":["exterminated/ area","annihilated/ region","demolished/ neighbourhood","abolished/ vinicity"],"correct":0,"explanation":"\"Exterminate\" is the verb used for killing pests such as mosquitoes; \"demolish\" is for buildings and \"abolish\" for laws or systems."},{"n":49,"part":"II. USE OF LANGUAGE — GRAMMAR AND VOCABULARY (15 pts) — Questions 31-60","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"The rescue team ____ the scene, trying to _____ out the best way to save any survivors.","options":["considered/ find","surveyed/ work","estimated/ figure","esteemed/ pick"],"correct":1,"explanation":"You \"survey\" (look carefully over) a scene, and \"work out\" the best way to do something."},{"n":50,"part":"II. USE OF LANGUAGE — GRAMMAR AND VOCABULARY (15 pts) — Questions 31-60","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"The poet said that his _____ came from ____.","options":["passion/ the nature","aspiration/ nature","inspiration/ nature","muse/ the nature"],"correct":2,"explanation":"A poet's \"inspiration\" comes from \"nature\"; \"nature\" in the sense of the natural world takes no article."},{"n":51,"part":"II. USE OF LANGUAGE — GRAMMAR AND VOCABULARY (15 pts) — Questions 31-60","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Now that you have more money, you'll be able to ____ a little; you have no excuses any more.","options":["live it up","make a break","cut and run","fly off the handle"],"correct":0,"explanation":"\"Live it up\" means to enjoy yourself and spend freely, which is what extra money allows; the other idioms mean escape or lose your temper."},{"n":52,"part":"II. USE OF LANGUAGE — GRAMMAR AND VOCABULARY (15 pts) — Questions 31-60","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"The commitee ______ been _______ over this matter for years now.","options":["has/ agreeing","have/ at oods","has/ at ease","have/ on dispute"],"correct":1,"explanation":"\"Be at odds over something\" means to disagree about it; the committee is treated as a plural group (\"have\"), and \"on dispute\" is not the correct preposition."},{"n":53,"part":"II. USE OF LANGUAGE — GRAMMAR AND VOCABULARY (15 pts) — Questions 31-60","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"I know it's _______ at first when you start a new job, but you'll soon ______.","options":["challenging/ follow your nose","problematic/ try your hand","hard/ find your feet","difficult/ show your teeth"],"correct":2,"explanation":"\"Find your feet\" means to become confident and settled in a new situation, the natural follow-up to a job being hard at first."},{"n":54,"part":"II. USE OF LANGUAGE — GRAMMAR AND VOCABULARY (15 pts) — Questions 31-60","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"After 15 years in prison, he lost his _____ on reality and descended _____ madness.","options":["handling/ toward","grip/ into","grasp/ to","footing/ in"],"correct":1,"explanation":"\"Lose one's grip on reality\" and \"descend into madness\" are the fixed collocations."},{"n":55,"part":"II. USE OF LANGUAGE — GRAMMAR AND VOCABULARY (15 pts) — Questions 31-60","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"If I can be ____ for a moment, there's a lot going on at home which means I might not have been in the best ____ of mind.","options":["honest/ form","faithful/ state","truthful/ shape","frank/ frame"],"correct":3,"explanation":"\"If I can be frank\" introduces an honest admission, and \"frame of mind\" is the fixed expression for one's mood."},{"n":56,"part":"II. USE OF LANGUAGE — CLOZE TEST 01 (10 pts) — Questions 61-70","section":"Open cloze","passageId":3,"q":"Blank (1): complete the blank with ONE most suitable word.","type":"text","accept":["of"],"answer":"of","explanation":"\"the actions of others\" — <b>of</b> shows possession: whose actions are observed."},{"n":57,"part":"II. USE OF LANGUAGE — CLOZE TEST 01 (10 pts) — Questions 61-70","section":"Open cloze","passageId":3,"q":"Blank (2): complete the blank with ONE most suitable word.","type":"text","accept":["such"],"answer":"such","explanation":"\"<b>such as</b> the one between their parents or siblings\" introduces an example of the relationships around them."},{"n":58,"part":"II. USE OF LANGUAGE — CLOZE TEST 01 (10 pts) — Questions 61-70","section":"Open cloze","passageId":3,"q":"Blank (3): complete the blank with ONE most suitable word.","type":"text","accept":["as","while"],"answer":"as","explanation":"\"They learn what is acceptable … <b>as/while</b> they acquire knowledge\" — a conjunction meaning \"at the same time that\"."},{"n":59,"part":"II. USE OF LANGUAGE — CLOZE TEST 01 (10 pts) — Questions 61-70","section":"Open cloze","passageId":3,"q":"Blank (4): complete the blank with ONE most suitable word.","type":"text","accept":["about"],"answer":"about","explanation":"\"learn <b>about</b> relationships from our partners\" — learn about a topic."},{"n":60,"part":"II. USE OF LANGUAGE — CLOZE TEST 01 (10 pts) — Questions 61-70","section":"Open cloze","passageId":3,"q":"Blank (5): complete the blank with ONE most suitable word.","type":"text","accept":["because"],"answer":"because","explanation":"\"just <b>because</b> we learn from observing others doesn't mean we don't make mistakes\" — the fixed pattern \"just because … doesn't mean …\"."},{"n":61,"part":"II. USE OF LANGUAGE — CLOZE TEST 01 (10 pts) — Questions 61-70","section":"Open cloze","passageId":3,"q":"Blank (6): complete the blank with ONE most suitable word.","type":"text","accept":["mean"],"answer":"mean","explanation":"\"just because … doesn't <b>mean</b> we don't make mistakes\" — a common structure meaning the first fact does not guarantee the second."},{"n":62,"part":"II. USE OF LANGUAGE — CLOZE TEST 01 (10 pts) — Questions 61-70","section":"Open cloze","passageId":3,"q":"Blank (7): complete the blank with ONE most suitable word.","type":"text","accept":["without"],"answer":"without","explanation":"\"<b>Without</b> effective communication, misunderstandings become more frequent\" — the sentence describes what happens in the absence of communication."},{"n":63,"part":"II. USE OF LANGUAGE — CLOZE TEST 01 (10 pts) — Questions 61-70","section":"Open cloze","passageId":3,"q":"Blank (8): complete the blank with ONE most suitable word.","type":"text","accept":["more"],"answer":"more","explanation":"\"just as important, if not <b>more</b> so, than talking\" — the fixed phrase \"if not more so\" means \"possibly even more\"."},{"n":64,"part":"II. USE OF LANGUAGE — CLOZE TEST 01 (10 pts) — Questions 61-70","section":"Open cloze","passageId":3,"q":"Blank (9): complete the blank with ONE most suitable word.","type":"text","accept":["end"],"answer":"end","explanation":"\"In the <b>end</b>\" — the idiom meaning \"finally, when everything is considered\"."},{"n":65,"part":"II. USE OF LANGUAGE — CLOZE TEST 01 (10 pts) — Questions 61-70","section":"Open cloze","passageId":3,"q":"Blank (10): complete the blank with ONE most suitable word.","type":"text","accept":["hard"],"answer":"hard","explanation":"\"working <b>hard</b> to nurture and maintain relationships\" — the collocation \"work hard\" (hard as an adverb)."},{"n":66,"part":"II. USE OF LANGUAGE — CLOZE TEST 02 (10 pts) — Questions 71-80","section":"Open cloze","passageId":4,"q":"For questions 1-10, complete each blank below with ONE most suitable word. Blank (1)","type":"text","answer":"less","accept":["less"],"explanation":"Comparative with \"than\": a habit that is <b>less</b> healthy than binging on potato chips."},{"n":67,"part":"II. USE OF LANGUAGE — CLOZE TEST 02 (10 pts) — Questions 71-80","section":"Open cloze","passageId":4,"q":"For questions 1-10, complete each blank below with ONE most suitable word. Blank (2)","type":"text","answer":"through","accept":["through"],"explanation":"The phrasal verb <b>sift through</b> means to examine a large amount of information carefully."},{"n":68,"part":"II. USE OF LANGUAGE — CLOZE TEST 02 (10 pts) — Questions 71-80","section":"Open cloze","passageId":4,"q":"For questions 1-10, complete each blank below with ONE most suitable word. Blank (3)","type":"text","answer":"were","accept":["were"],"explanation":"Inverted conditional: <b>Were one to pick up</b> … = If one were to pick up … (formal second conditional)."},{"n":69,"part":"II. USE OF LANGUAGE — CLOZE TEST 02 (10 pts) — Questions 71-80","section":"Open cloze","passageId":4,"q":"For questions 1-10, complete each blank below with ONE most suitable word. Blank (4)","type":"text","answer":"stock","accept":["stock"],"explanation":"The fixed expression <b>laughing stock</b> means a person everyone laughs at."},{"n":70,"part":"II. USE OF LANGUAGE — CLOZE TEST 02 (10 pts) — Questions 71-80","section":"Open cloze","passageId":4,"q":"For questions 1-10, complete each blank below with ONE most suitable word. Blank (5)","type":"text","answer":"into","accept":["into"],"explanation":"<b>Look into</b> something means to investigate or examine it."},{"n":71,"part":"II. USE OF LANGUAGE — CLOZE TEST 02 (10 pts) — Questions 71-80","section":"Open cloze","passageId":4,"q":"For questions 1-10, complete each blank below with ONE most suitable word. Blank (6)","type":"text","answer":"full","accept":["full"],"explanation":"<b>Be full of</b> dubious content — the world contains a great deal of it."},{"n":72,"part":"II. USE OF LANGUAGE — CLOZE TEST 02 (10 pts) — Questions 71-80","section":"Open cloze","passageId":4,"q":"For questions 1-10, complete each blank below with ONE most suitable word. Blank (7)","type":"text","answer":"it","accept":["it"],"explanation":"\"some of it\" — <b>content</b> is uncountable, so the pronoun is <b>it</b>: some of it is as plausible as another."},{"n":73,"part":"II. USE OF LANGUAGE — CLOZE TEST 02 (10 pts) — Questions 71-80","section":"Open cloze","passageId":4,"q":"For questions 1-10, complete each blank below with ONE most suitable word. Blank (8)","type":"text","answer":"wheat","accept":["wheat"],"explanation":"The idiom <b>separate the wheat from the chaff</b> means to separate the valuable from the worthless."},{"n":74,"part":"II. USE OF LANGUAGE — CLOZE TEST 02 (10 pts) — Questions 71-80","section":"Open cloze","passageId":4,"q":"For questions 1-10, complete each blank below with ONE most suitable word. Blank (9)","type":"text","answer":"out","accept":["out"],"explanation":"<b>Dish out</b> the truth — the phrasal verb means to give or hand out."},{"n":75,"part":"II. USE OF LANGUAGE — CLOZE TEST 02 (10 pts) — Questions 71-80","section":"Open cloze","passageId":4,"q":"For questions 1-10, complete each blank below with ONE most suitable word. Blank (10)","type":"text","answer":"another","accept":["another"],"explanation":"\"One man's truth could be <b>another</b> man's fiction\" — the familiar \"one man's X is another man's Y\" pattern."},{"n":76,"part":"III. WRITING — WORD FORMS (10 pts) — Questions 81-90 (LIFE ON EARTH)","section":"Word form","type":"text","passageId":5,"q":"through the generation of (1)____ variation and natural selection. (HEIR)","accept":["hereditary"],"answer":"hereditary","explanation":"An adjective is needed before the noun <b>variation</b>: <b>hereditary</b> variation is variation passed on from parents to offspring."},{"n":77,"part":"III. WRITING — WORD FORMS (10 pts) — Questions 81-90 (LIFE ON EARTH)","section":"Word form","type":"text","passageId":5,"q":"hereditary variation and natural (2)____. (SELECT)","accept":["selection"],"answer":"selection","explanation":"A noun after the adjective <b>natural</b>: <b>natural selection</b> is Darwin's fixed term for the mechanism of evolution."},{"n":78,"part":"III. WRITING — WORD FORMS (10 pts) — Questions 81-90 (LIFE ON EARTH)","section":"Word form","type":"text","passageId":5,"q":"which is still only slightly (3)____ than Earth (YOUTH)","accept":["younger"],"answer":"younger","explanation":"<b>than</b> signals a comparative adjective: <b>younger</b>. \"Slightly\" modifies the comparison."},{"n":79,"part":"III. WRITING — WORD FORMS (10 pts) — Questions 81-90 (LIFE ON EARTH)","section":"Word form","type":"text","passageId":5,"q":"which (4)____ accreted into a planet about 4.5 billion years ago. (GRAVITY)","accept":["gravitationally"],"answer":"gravitationally","explanation":"The word modifies the verb <b>accreted</b>, so an adverb is needed: <b>gravitationally</b>."},{"n":80,"part":"III. WRITING — WORD FORMS (10 pts) — Questions 81-90 (LIFE ON EARTH)","section":"Word form","type":"text","passageId":5,"q":"More than 99.9 percent of species that have ever lived are (5)____. (EXTINCTION)","accept":["extinct"],"answer":"extinct","explanation":"After <b>are</b> a predicative adjective is required: species that no longer exist are <b>extinct</b>."},{"n":81,"part":"III. WRITING — WORD FORMS (10 pts) — Questions 81-90 (LIFE ON EARTH)","section":"Word form","type":"text","passageId":5,"q":"the common historical, functional, and chemical basis of the (6)____ of all life (EVOLVE)","accept":["evolution"],"answer":"evolution","explanation":"After <b>the</b> and before <b>of</b> a noun is needed: the <b>evolution</b> of all life."},{"n":82,"part":"III. WRITING — WORD FORMS (10 pts) — Questions 81-90 (LIFE ON EARTH)","section":"Word form","type":"text","passageId":5,"q":"and life, by (7)____ that might exist elsewhere in the universe. (THESIS)","accept":["hypothesis"],"answer":"hypothesis","explanation":"A noun after <b>by</b>: life that exists <b>by hypothesis</b> (as a supposition) elsewhere in the universe."},{"n":83,"part":"III. WRITING — WORD FORMS (10 pts) — Questions 81-90 (LIFE ON EARTH)","section":"Word form","type":"text","passageId":5,"q":"the relative (8)____ is comparable to a coat of paint on a rubber ball. (THICK)","accept":["thickness"],"answer":"thickness","explanation":"After <b>the relative</b> and as subject of <b>is</b> a noun is needed: <b>thickness</b>."},{"n":84,"part":"III. WRITING — WORD FORMS (10 pts) — Questions 81-90 (LIFE ON EARTH)","section":"Word form","type":"text","passageId":5,"q":"An (9)____ 10 to 30 million distinguishable species currently inhabit this sphere of life. (ESTIMATION)","accept":["estimated"],"answer":"estimated","explanation":"The participle adjective <b>estimated</b> before a number means \"roughly calculated\": an estimated 10 to 30 million species."},{"n":85,"part":"III. WRITING — WORD FORMS (10 pts) — Questions 81-90 (LIFE ON EARTH)","section":"Word form","type":"text","passageId":5,"q":"10 to 30 million distinguishable species currently (10)____ this sphere of life, or biosphere. (HABITAT)","accept":["inhabit"],"answer":"inhabit","explanation":"The subject <b>species</b> needs a plural present-tense verb taking a direct object: species <b>inhabit</b> the biosphere."},{"n":86,"part":"III. WRITING — KEY WORD TRANSFORMATION (20 pts) — Questions 91-100","section":"Rewrite","q":"Attending the party on Friday night is not an option for me as the deadline for my assignment is approaching. (<b>CHOICE</b>)<br>As the deadline for my assignment is drawing ______ skip the party on Friday night.","type":"text","accept":["near, I have no choice but to","near, I have little choice but to","near, the only choice for me is to","near, my only choice is to","near, it leaves me no choice but to"],"answer":"near, I have no choice but to","explanation":"<b>Draw near</b> = approach; <b>have no choice but to do something</b> = be forced to do it, so \"not an option\" becomes \"I have no choice but to skip\"."},{"n":87,"part":"III. WRITING — KEY WORD TRANSFORMATION (20 pts) — Questions 91-100","section":"Rewrite","q":"She felt greatly relieved when she received the news of her promotion. (<b>INFORMED</b>)<br>Much to ______ her promotion.","type":"text","accept":["her relief, she was informed about","her relief, she was informed of","her relief, she got informed about","her relief, she got informed of"],"answer":"her relief, she was informed about","explanation":"<b>Much to somebody's relief</b> expresses the feeling; \"received the news of\" is rewritten in the passive as <b>be informed about/of</b>."},{"n":88,"part":"III. WRITING — KEY WORD TRANSFORMATION (20 pts) — Questions 91-100","section":"Rewrite","q":"John's aptitude for numbers enables him to solve complex mathematical problems effortlessly and quickly, completing them within seconds. (<b>HEAD</b>)<br>John ______ numbers, effortlessly solving complex mathematical problems in seconds.","type":"text","accept":["has a head for"],"answer":"has a head for","explanation":"<b>Have a head for something</b> = have a natural ability for it, which restates \"aptitude for numbers\"."},{"n":89,"part":"III. WRITING — KEY WORD TRANSFORMATION (20 pts) — Questions 91-100","section":"Rewrite","q":"During the pandemic, it was difficult to find enough masks and ventilators. (<b>SUPPLY</b>)<br>Masks and ventilators ______ occurred.","type":"text","accept":["were in short supply when the pandemic"],"answer":"were in short supply when the pandemic","explanation":"<b>Be in short supply</b> = be scarce, difficult to obtain; \"during the pandemic\" becomes \"when the pandemic occurred\"."},{"n":90,"part":"III. WRITING — KEY WORD TRANSFORMATION (20 pts) — Questions 91-100","section":"Rewrite","q":"Anne Hathaway truly excelled in sences that called for raw emotion and full-throttled singing, like the one in the Les Miserables. (<b>ELEMENT</b>)<br>Anne Hathaway ______ in scenes that called for raw emotion and full-throttled singing, like the one in the Les Miserables.","type":"text","accept":["was truly in her element","was in her element"],"answer":"was truly in her element","explanation":"<b>Be in one's element</b> = be doing what one is best at and enjoys most, so \"truly excelled\" becomes \"was (truly) in her element\"."},{"n":91,"part":"III. WRITING — KEY WORD TRANSFORMATION (20 pts) — Questions 91-100","section":"Rewrite","q":"The attendees of the party tolerated the unruly behavior of some individuals, despite feeling annoyed and uncomfortable. (<b>UP</b>)<br>The unruly behavior of some individuals at the party ______ the attendees, despite their annoyance and discomfort.","type":"text","accept":["was put up with by"],"answer":"was put up with by","explanation":"<b>Put up with</b> = tolerate; the phrasal verb keeps all its particles in the passive: \"was put up with by the attendees\"."},{"n":92,"part":"III. WRITING — KEY WORD TRANSFORMATION (20 pts) — Questions 91-100","section":"Rewrite","q":"As I stepped into the room, I was startled to discover that there was nothing left, no trace of furniture or belongings to be found. (<b>ABACK</b>)<br>When I entered the room, I ______ find it completely empty, devoid of any furniture or belongings.","type":"text","accept":["was taken aback to"],"answer":"was taken aback to","explanation":"<b>Be taken aback</b> = be startled or shocked, followed by a to-infinitive: \"was taken aback to find\"."},{"n":93,"part":"III. WRITING — KEY WORD TRANSFORMATION (20 pts) — Questions 91-100","section":"Rewrite","q":"I did not hear much that could surprise me in any way. (<b>FAR</b>)<br>Much of ______ surprising.","type":"text","accept":["what I heard was far from"],"answer":"what I heard was far from","explanation":"<b>Far from + adjective</b> = not at all; \"Much of what I heard was far from surprising\" means little of it surprised me."},{"n":94,"part":"III. WRITING — KEY WORD TRANSFORMATION (20 pts) — Questions 91-100","section":"Rewrite","q":"A professional receptionist always gives a friendly smile whenever a guest enters the hotel, so as to make them feel welcomed. (<b>IN</b>)<br>A professional receptionist always ______ to welcome guests when they enter the hotel.","type":"text","accept":["smiles in a friendly manner","smiles in a friendly way"],"answer":"smiles in a friendly manner","explanation":"\"Gives a friendly smile\" becomes the verb <b>smiles</b> plus the adverbial <b>in a friendly manner/way</b>, since \"friendly\" is an adjective with no -ly adverb form."},{"n":95,"part":"III. WRITING — KEY WORD TRANSFORMATION (20 pts) — Questions 91-100","section":"Rewrite","q":"Khanh found it difficult to accept that her dream job was no longer available. (<b>TERMS</b>)<br>Khanh had difficulty ______ losing her dream job.","type":"text","accept":["in coming to terms with","coming to terms with"],"answer":"in coming to terms with","explanation":"<b>Come to terms with something</b> = accept something unpleasant; <b>have difficulty (in) + V-ing</b> follows the pattern of the stem."},{"n":96,"part":"III. WRITING — ERROR IDENTIFICATION (5 pts) — Questions 101-105","section":"Error correction","type":"text","passageId":6,"q":"Line 2 contains one error (<b>from</b>). Write the correction.","accept":["to","from to","from -> to","back to"],"answer":"to","explanation":"The fixed phrase is <b>date back to</b> a time: \"roots dating back <b>to</b> ancient Greece\"."},{"n":97,"part":"III. WRITING — ERROR IDENTIFICATION (5 pts) — Questions 101-105","section":"Error correction","type":"text","passageId":6,"q":"Line 5 contains one error (<b>promoted</b>). Write the correction.","accept":["promoting","for promoting","to promote","which promotes","that promotes","which promoted","that promoted","promoted promoting","promoted -> promoting","promoted to promote","promoted -> to promote"],"answer":"promoting","explanation":"A platform does the promoting, so the reduced relative clause needs an active form: \"a platform <b>promoting</b> / <b>to promote</b> / <b>which promotes</b> international peace\". The past participle <b>promoted</b> would mean the platform was promoted by something else."},{"n":98,"part":"III. WRITING — ERROR IDENTIFICATION (5 pts) — Questions 101-105","section":"Error correction","type":"text","passageId":6,"q":"Line 7 contains one error (<b>overtime</b>). Write the correction.","accept":["over time","overtime over time","overtime -> over time"],"answer":"over time","explanation":"<b>Overtime</b> (one word) means extra working hours; the meaning here is \"gradually, as time passes\", which is <b>over time</b> (two words)."},{"n":99,"part":"III. WRITING — ERROR IDENTIFICATION (5 pts) — Questions 101-105","section":"Error correction","type":"text","passageId":6,"q":"Line 9 contains one error (<b>Without</b>). Write the correction.","accept":["despite","in spite of","without despite","without -> despite","without in spite of","without -> in spite of"],"answer":"despite","explanation":"The obstacles did exist — boycotts, terrorism and pandemics happened — and the Games kept their spirit anyway, so a concessive preposition is needed: <b>Despite</b> / <b>In spite of</b> obstacles like…"},{"n":100,"part":"III. WRITING — ERROR IDENTIFICATION (5 pts) — Questions 101-105","section":"Error correction","type":"text","passageId":6,"q":"Line 11 contains one error (<b>competitor</b>). Write the correction.","accept":["competition","competitors","competitor competition","competitor -> competition","competitor competitors","competitor -> competitors"],"answer":"competition","explanation":"\"The unifying spirit of global <b>competition</b> and fair play\" needs the abstract noun (or the plural <b>competitors</b>); a singular countable <b>competitor</b> cannot stand alone without an article."}],"omitted":["không sao chép được: I. READING — PASSAGE 3 (5 pts) — Questions 16-20 (A Different Kind of Love) (5 câu)"]},{"id":"ptnk-2022-kc","year":2022,"track":"kc","title":"PTNK 2022 · Tiếng Anh Không chuyên","subtitle":"Đề thi tuyển sinh lớp 10 · Trường Phổ thông Năng khiếu · 2022-2023","durationMin":60,"keySource":"official","source":"2022 Thi vào 10 Đề thi Anh chung Phổ thông Năng khiếu.pdf","passages":["<b>LIFE DURING THE PANDEMIC</b><br><i>By: Lynda Feustel (adapted)</i><br><br>1. Entering year two of (31)____ pandemic is strange. It barely seems a day since last March 2020, but it also seems like <b>a lifetime</b>. As an only child and introvert, shutting down my world was initially simple and relatively easy. My friends and I had been super busy with the school play, and while I was sad about it being canceled, I was struggling a lot during that show and desperately needed some time (32)____.<br><br>2. As March turned to April, <b>virtual</b> school began, and being alone really set in. I missed my friends and us being together. The isolation felt real with just my parents and me, even as we spent time together. My friends and I began meeting on Facetime every night (33)____ TV and just be together in some way. We laughed at insane jokes we made, had homework and therapy sessions over Facetime, and grew closer through digital and literal walls. The summer passed with in-person events together, and the virus <b>faded into the background</b> for a little while. We went to the beach and (34)____ out in people's backyards.<br><br>3. Then school came for us in a way none of us had ever expected. In hybrid school we were <b>separated</b>. Students had lessons, sports, and other activities while many of us were in quarantine. Teachers piled on work, and the virus grew more present again. Group texting put out hundreds of messages a day and again, meeting in person as a group (35)____. Being together on video and in person was the way of staying <b>cheered up</b>.<br><br>4. We are working and looking for some change as we are about to enter the second year of this pandemic. After all, life is hopeful.","<i>By: Lieutenant Brian Ellis (adapted)</i><br><br>1. One of the promises I have made to myself as a leader in recent years is to be more mindful of improving my surroundings every day. This happens mostly through the lessons of life, and pushing into areas <b>that</b> challenge my way of thinking or creating useful opportunities for me to strengthen my values. Any good gift is worth sharing, so I extend the list of books with some following sweet faces of love.<br><br>2. <b>Love is patient</b>: The opposite is impatience, a cause of hostility and frustration. According to <i>Challenging the Law Enforcement Organization</i> by Jack Enter, leaders have to make sure they are patient enough for others to go through the processes of learning from mistakes.<br><br>3. <b>Love is kind</b>: No employee will follow a controlling leader. Good leaders make people-centered policies because of the benefits and <b>force</b> these policies create. In <i>A Higher Standard of Leadership</i> by Keshavan Nair, it is suggested to look below the surface and identify the hidden needs of others to create a bond with those you are trying to serve.<br><br>4. <b>Love does not envy</b>: Envy can be subtle and indirect, and interferes with colleagues, family, and friends, according to <i>Envy and Leadership</i> by Mark Stein. Being truthful to yourself means understanding who you are and who you want to be. Being comfortable with the journey is part of the experience. Having more doesn't fix anything, and the idea of being content is the way to a satisfied heart.<br><br>5. <b>Love keeps no record of wrongs</b>: I'm no saint and neither are you. Be compassionate enough for problem solving, which also means you understand when it's time to turn around and reassess. Create an environment that is focused on problem solving rather than <b>people blaming</b> through learning and development.<br><br>6. <b>Love always trusts</b>: Trust is challenging at times, and it requires us to confront the things that stand in its way. When we have to protect ourselves from each other, the whole organization suffers. When trust thrives, the organization grows stronger <b>as a result</b>, as illustrated in <i>Leaders Eat Last: Why Some Teams Pull Together and Others Don't</i> by Simon Sinek.<br><br>7. All in all, love never fails. It will be the one constant that gives you an inspired life.","1. SARS, Ebola, and SARS-CoV-2: all three of these highly infectious viruses have caused global panic since 2002, and all three of them jumped to humans from wild animals living in dense tropical forests that we are slashing and burning to create land for crops, mining, and housing. The more we <b>clear</b>, the more we come into contact with wildlife which hosts illnesses likely transferred to humans.<br><br>2. Stopping deforestation will not only reduce natural disasters but also control the spread of a long list of dangerous diseases that have come from rain forest habitats - <b>Zika, Nipah, malaria, cholera, HIV</b>, and so on. A 2019 study found that a 10 percent increase in deforestation would raise malaria cases by 3.3 percent; that would be 7.4 million people worldwide. Meanwhile, an average of 28 million hectares of forest have been cut down annually since 2016, and there is no sign of a slowdown. <b>[A]</b><br><br>3. Societies can take numerous steps to prevent the destruction. Eating less meat, <b>which</b> improves our health anyway, will lessen demand for crops and pastures. Eating fewer processed foods will reduce the demand for palm oil, much of which is grown on land from tropical rain forests. Producing more food per hectare can boost supply without the need for more land. <b>[B]</b><br><br>4. In the meantime, governments should prohibit the sale of live wild animals in so-called wet markets, where pathogens have repeatedly crossed over into humans. The markets may be culturally important, but the risk is too great. Governments must also ban illegal wildlife trade, which can spread infectious agents far and wide. In addition, authorities must examine factory farms that pack thousands of animals together – the source of the 2009 swine flu outbreak that killed more than 10,000 people in the U.S. and many more people worldwide. <b>[C]</b><br><br>5. Ending deforestation and preventing pandemics would address six of the United Nations' 17 Sustainable Development Goals: the guarantee of healthy lives, zero hunger, gender equality, responsible consumption and production, sustainably managed land, and climate action. The COVID-19 pandemic is a catastrophe, but it calls for our attention on the human achievements by not overexploiting the natural world. <b>[D]</b><br><br><i>(Adapted from Scientific American)</i>"],"questions":[{"n":1,"type":"mcq","part":"Section 1: Pronunciation","section":"Phonetics","q":"Choose the word whose underlined part is pronounced differently from those of the other words.","options":["<u>h</u>uman","<u>h</u>idden","<u>h</u>elpful","<u>h</u>onest"],"correct":3,"explanation":"In <b>honest</b> the \"h\" is silent (/ˈɒnɪst/); in human, hidden and helpful it is pronounced /h/."},{"n":2,"type":"mcq","part":"Section 1: Pronunciation","section":"Phonetics","q":"Choose the word whose underlined part is pronounced differently from those of the other words.","options":["dec<u>i</u>de","def<u>i</u>ne","ins<u>i</u>st","n<u>i</u>ght"],"correct":2,"explanation":"<b>insist</b> has the short /ɪ/ sound; decide, define and night all have the diphthong /aɪ/."},{"n":3,"type":"mcq","part":"Section 1: Pronunciation","section":"Phonetics","q":"Choose the word whose underlined part is pronounced differently from those of the other words.","options":["look<u>ed</u>","laugh<u>ed</u>","declin<u>ed</u>","hop<u>ed</u>"],"correct":2,"explanation":"After a voiced sound (/n/) the ending is pronounced /d/ in <b>declined</b>; after the voiceless /k/, /f/ and /p/ it is /t/ in looked, laughed and hoped."},{"n":4,"type":"mcq","part":"Section 1: Pronunciation","section":"Stress","q":"Choose the word whose primary stress is placed on a syllable different from those in the other words.","options":["gather","prefer","surprise","define"],"correct":0,"explanation":"<b>GAther</b> is stressed on the first syllable; preFER, surPRISE and deFINE are stressed on the second."},{"n":5,"type":"mcq","part":"Section 1: Pronunciation","section":"Stress","q":"Choose the word whose primary stress is placed on a syllable different from those in the other words.","options":["disaster","delightful","difficult","distraction"],"correct":2,"explanation":"<b>DIfficult</b> is stressed on the first syllable; diSASter, deLIGHTful and disTRACtion are stressed on the second."},{"n":6,"type":"mcq","part":"Section 2: Grammar and Vocabulary · Part 1: Structure and meaning identification","section":"Language use","q":"When our family was on holiday, our neighbor helped us by ______ our garden plants.","options":["looking up","taking care","caring about","looking after"],"correct":3,"explanation":"<b>look after</b> = take care of. \"Taking care\" would need \"of\", and \"look up\"/\"care about\" do not fit plants."},{"n":7,"type":"mcq","part":"Section 2: Grammar and Vocabulary · Part 1: Structure and meaning identification","section":"Language use","q":"As an only child, Mary wishes that she ______ at least one sibling to play with every day.","options":["has","had had","would have","had"],"correct":3,"explanation":"A wish about the present takes the past simple: <b>wish + (that) + S + V-ed</b> → \"wishes that she had\"."},{"n":8,"type":"mcq","part":"Section 2: Grammar and Vocabulary · Part 1: Structure and meaning identification","section":"Language use","q":"In their lesson yesterday, Linda's tutor ______ follow the model essay rigidly to gain a good score.","options":["advised not to","advised her to not","advises her to","advised her not to"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Pattern <b>advise + object + not + to-infinitive</b>; \"yesterday\" requires the past tense \"advised\"."},{"n":9,"type":"mcq","part":"Section 2: Grammar and Vocabulary · Part 1: Structure and meaning identification","section":"Language use","q":"Nancy is a hard-working and smart student. Rarely ______ any homework exercise unfinished.","options":["she does not leave","does she leave","she leaves","she does leave"],"correct":1,"explanation":"A negative adverb like <b>Rarely</b> at the start of a sentence forces inversion: Rarely + auxiliary + subject + verb."},{"n":10,"type":"mcq","part":"Section 2: Grammar and Vocabulary · Part 1: Structure and meaning identification","section":"Language use","q":"If the Smiths follow a clean-eating meal plan, they ______ cut down on fat and sugar.","options":["have","had better to","must","ought"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Only <b>must</b> can take the bare infinitive \"cut\" directly; \"have\" and \"ought\" need \"to\", and \"had better\" is followed by a bare infinitive, not \"to\"."},{"n":11,"type":"mcq","part":"Section 2: Grammar and Vocabulary · Part 1: Structure and meaning identification","section":"Language use","q":"It's too cold out there in the park! Put on your ______ to keep warm.","options":["wool sweater","plaid skirt","denim shorts","short-sleeved blouse"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Cold weather calls for a <b>wool sweater</b>; a skirt, shorts or a short-sleeved blouse would not keep anyone warm."},{"n":12,"type":"mcq","part":"Section 2: Grammar and Vocabulary · Part 1: Structure and meaning identification","section":"Language use","q":"In our training session last week, we met our new football coach ______ here from a city club.","options":["having moved","has been moved","being moved","moving"],"correct":0,"explanation":"A reduced relative clause with a perfect participle: \"coach <b>having moved</b> here\" = who had moved here (before we met him)."},{"n":13,"type":"mcq","part":"Section 2: Grammar and Vocabulary · Part 1: Structure and meaning identification","section":"Language use","q":"John's grandfather is interested in history. He likes watching ______ every night.","options":["social media","documentaries","breaking news","journalism"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Someone interested in history watches <b>documentaries</b>; the other options are not history programmes one \"watches every night\"."},{"n":14,"type":"mcq","part":"Section 2: Grammar and Vocabulary · Part 1: Structure and meaning identification","section":"Language use","q":"Of the three movies I have watched, <i>3 Idiots</i> is ______.","options":["more interesting than","the most interesting","as interesting as","less interesting"],"correct":1,"explanation":"\"Of the three movies\" compares three items, so the superlative <b>the most interesting</b> is needed."},{"n":15,"type":"mcq","part":"Section 2: Grammar and Vocabulary · Part 1: Structure and meaning identification","section":"Language use","q":"IELTS is a/an ______ English test whose result is widely accepted in many countries.","options":["national","institutional","internationally","international"],"correct":3,"explanation":"An adjective is needed before the noun \"test\", and \"accepted in many countries\" points to <b>international</b>."},{"n":16,"type":"mcq","part":"Section 2: Grammar and Vocabulary · Part 1: Structure and meaning identification","section":"Language use","q":"On their Independence Day, American people love watching ______ marching along the streets.","options":["splendid fireworks","colorful balloons","exciting celebrations","long parades"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Only <b>parades</b> can be \"marching along the streets\"."},{"n":17,"type":"mcq","part":"Section 2: Grammar and Vocabulary · Part 1: Structure and meaning identification","section":"Language use","q":"City born and city bred, Jack has never seen how ______ are raised on the farm.","options":["cattle","villages","crops","plants"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Animals are <b>raised</b> on a farm; crops and plants are grown, and villages are not raised at all."},{"n":18,"type":"mcq","part":"Section 2: Grammar and Vocabulary · Part 1: Structure and meaning identification","section":"Language use","q":"My city gradually returned to normal ______ all lockdown restrictions were removed.","options":["whenever","after","so long as","as soon as"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The city returned to normal gradually, i.e. over time <b>after</b> the restrictions were removed; \"as soon as\" would contradict \"gradually\"."},{"n":19,"type":"mcq","part":"Section 2: Grammar and Vocabulary · Part 1: Structure and meaning identification","section":"Language use","q":"Lan really loves the song lyrics ______ her father wrote especially for her.","options":["who","whom","whose","which"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The relative pronoun refers to \"the song lyrics\" (a thing) and is the object of \"wrote\", so <b>which</b> is used."},{"n":20,"type":"mcq","part":"Section 2: Grammar and Vocabulary · Part 1: Structure and meaning identification","section":"Language use","q":"Our class will join a ______ campaign this summer. This is our school's community program.","options":["littering","garbage dump","natural resource","beach clean-up"],"correct":3,"explanation":"A school community programme would run a <b>beach clean-up campaign</b>; \"littering campaign\" would mean the opposite."},{"n":21,"type":"mcq","part":"Section 2: Grammar and Vocabulary · Part 1: Structure and meaning identification","section":"Language use","q":"A/an ______, or a huge tidal wave, can wash away a whole seaside village.","options":["earthquake","hurricane","tsunami","tornado"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The definition \"a huge tidal wave\" is that of a <b>tsunami</b>."},{"n":22,"type":"mcq","part":"Section 2: Grammar and Vocabulary · Part 1: Structure and meaning identification","section":"Language use","q":"Tom's mother gave him a ______ as a birthday gift.","options":["red new Nike cap","Nike new red cap","new Nike red cap","new red Nike cap"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Adjective order: opinion/age (new) → colour (red) → origin/brand (Nike) → noun: <b>new red Nike cap</b>."},{"n":23,"type":"mcq","part":"Section 2: Grammar and Vocabulary · Part 1: Structure and meaning identification","section":"Language use","q":"Replace the leaking faucets or we'll have an enormous ______ as we did last month.","options":["water bill","power cut","fuel shortage","energy crisis"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Leaking faucets waste water, so the result is an enormous <b>water bill</b>."},{"n":24,"type":"mcq","part":"Section 2: Grammar and Vocabulary · Part 1: Structure and meaning identification","section":"Language use","q":"On her first vacation to Korea, Ellen was very excited about the diverse ______ of this Asian country.","options":["culture","uniqueness","region","temple"],"correct":0,"explanation":"\"Diverse\" describes the variety of a country's <b>culture</b>; \"region\" and \"temple\" would need a plural, and \"uniqueness\" cannot be diverse."},{"n":25,"type":"mcq","part":"Section 2: Grammar and Vocabulary · Part 1: Structure and meaning identification","section":"Language use","q":"No one believed in Nick's story about his UFO ____ last night during his drive home.","options":["sighting","sampling","appearance","alienation"],"correct":0,"explanation":"<b>UFO sighting</b> is the fixed collocation for seeing a UFO."},{"n":26,"type":"mcq","part":"Section 2: Grammar and Vocabulary · Part 2: Error identification","section":"Error correction","q":"Find ONE error (A, B, C, or D) in the following sentence.<br>(A) Rainy weather normally (B) effects people's mood, so (C) they tend to avoid the (D) wet season if possible.","options":["Rainy","effects","they","wet season"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The verb meaning \"to influence\" is <b>affect</b>; \"effect\" is a noun. effects → affects."},{"n":27,"type":"mcq","part":"Section 2: Grammar and Vocabulary · Part 2: Error identification","section":"Error correction","q":"Find ONE error (A, B, C, or D) in the following sentence.<br>Jack will have (A) to take the online exam again because (B) he (C) did not have Internet (D) assess last week.","options":["to take","he","did not","assess"],"correct":3,"explanation":"\"Internet <b>access</b>\" (noun, the ability to connect) is meant; \"assess\" is a verb meaning to evaluate. assess → access."},{"n":28,"type":"mcq","part":"Section 2: Grammar and Vocabulary · Part 2: Error identification","section":"Error correction","q":"Find ONE error (A, B, C, or D) in the following sentence.<br>I (A) can never forget (B) my grandmother's old house (C) was located in a (D) beautifully designed neighborhood.","options":["can never","my grandmother's","was located","beautifully designed neighborhood"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The object of \"forget\" is \"my grandmother's old house\"; the following clause needs a relative pronoun or a participle: was located → <b>located</b> / which was located."},{"n":29,"type":"mcq","part":"Section 2: Grammar and Vocabulary · Part 2: Error identification","section":"Error correction","q":"Find ONE error (A, B, C, or D) in the following sentence.<br>A famous singer (A) had posted a message (B) which hurt her fans, (C) therefore, she had to post (D) an apology.","options":["had posted","which hurt","therefore,","an apology"],"correct":2,"explanation":"\"therefore\" is an adverb and cannot join two clauses with only a comma; a conjunction is needed: therefore, → <b>so</b>."},{"n":30,"type":"mcq","part":"Section 2: Grammar and Vocabulary · Part 2: Error identification","section":"Error correction","q":"Find ONE error (A, B, C, or D) in the following sentence.<br>Being (A) extreme excited (B) as soon as the football match (C) began, little Tom shouted and (D) jumped around.","options":["extreme","as soon as","began","jumped around"],"correct":0,"explanation":"An adverb is needed to modify the adjective \"excited\": extreme → <b>extremely</b>."},{"n":31,"type":"mcq","part":"Section 3: Reading Comprehension","section":"Cloze","passageId":0,"q":"Choose the best option for gap (31).","options":["an","the","a","Ø (no article)"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The pandemic is a specific one already known to the reader, so the definite article <b>the</b> is used."},{"n":32,"type":"mcq","part":"Section 3: Reading Comprehension","section":"Cloze","passageId":0,"q":"Choose the best option for gap (32).","options":["down","for","up","off"],"correct":3,"explanation":"<b>time off</b> = a break from work or activity; the writer \"desperately needed some time off\"."},{"n":33,"type":"mcq","part":"Section 3: Reading Comprehension","section":"Cloze","passageId":0,"q":"Choose the best option for gap (33).","options":["to watch","watch","watching","watched"],"correct":0,"explanation":"An infinitive of purpose: they met on Facetime every night <b>to watch</b> TV \"and just be together\"."},{"n":34,"type":"mcq","part":"Section 3: Reading Comprehension","section":"Cloze","passageId":0,"q":"Choose the best option for gap (34).","options":["hanged","would hang","hung","was hanging"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Past simple parallel to \"went\": \"We went to the beach and <b>hung</b> out\"; the past of \"hang out\" is \"hung\"."},{"n":35,"type":"mcq","part":"Section 3: Reading Comprehension","section":"Cloze","passageId":0,"q":"Choose the best option for gap (35).","options":["not being allowed","allowed","was not allowed","did not allow"],"correct":2,"explanation":"\"meeting in person as a group\" is the subject of a passive clause: it <b>was not allowed</b> during quarantine."},{"n":36,"type":"mcq","part":"Section 3: Reading Comprehension","section":"Reading","passageId":0,"q":"The phrase <b>a lifetime</b> in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to ____.","options":["once upon a time","a very long time","life expectancy","a human's life"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The contrast \"It barely seems a day … but it also seems like a lifetime\" means it feels like <b>a very long time</b>."},{"n":37,"type":"mcq","part":"Section 3: Reading Comprehension","section":"Reading","passageId":0,"q":"The word <b>virtual</b> in paragraph 2 can be best replaced by ____.","options":["distance","unreal","online","digital"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Virtual school during lockdown is school held <b>online</b> (over the internet)."},{"n":38,"type":"mcq","part":"Section 3: Reading Comprehension","section":"Reading","passageId":0,"q":"The phrase <b>faded into the background</b> in paragraph 2 can be best replaced by ____.","options":["spread","declined","disappeared","controlled"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The virus \"faded into the background for a little while\" — it <b>declined</b>, becoming less noticeable, but did not disappear (it \"grew more present again\" later)."},{"n":39,"type":"mcq","part":"Section 3: Reading Comprehension","section":"Reading","passageId":0,"q":"The word <b>separated</b> in paragraph 3 can be best replaced by ____.","options":["moved","divided","locked","isolated"],"correct":1,"explanation":"In hybrid school the students were <b>divided</b>: some had lessons while others were in quarantine."},{"n":40,"type":"mcq","part":"Section 3: Reading Comprehension","section":"Reading","passageId":0,"q":"The phrase <b>cheered up</b> in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to ____.","options":["very happy","inspired","upset","positive"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Staying cheered up = staying in good spirits, i.e. staying <b>positive</b>."},{"n":41,"type":"mcq","part":"Section 3: Reading Comprehension","section":"Reading","passageId":1,"q":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["Lessons of love for leaders","True leadership and true love","Leaders' sharing of their love","How love can change leaders"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The author, a leader, shares \"sweet faces of love\" (patient, kind, does not envy…) as lessons drawn from leadership books — <b>lessons of love for leaders</b>."},{"n":42,"type":"mcq","part":"Section 3: Reading Comprehension","section":"Reading","passageId":1,"q":"What does the word <b>that</b> in paragraph 1 refer to?","options":["lessons","life","areas","surroundings"],"correct":2,"explanation":"\"pushing into areas <b>that</b> challenge my way of thinking\" — the relative pronoun refers to \"areas\"."},{"n":43,"type":"mcq","part":"Section 3: Reading Comprehension","section":"Reading","passageId":1,"q":"According to paragraph 1, which of the following is TRUE of the author?","options":["He/She often creates opportunities and gives gifts.","He/She loves challenging people's leadership.","He/She often makes promises to other leaders.","He/She wants to inspire people around him/her."],"correct":3,"explanation":"\"Any good gift is worth sharing, so I extend the list of books…\" — the author wants to pass on what improved him/her, i.e. to <b>inspire people around him/her</b>."},{"n":44,"type":"mcq","part":"Section 3: Reading Comprehension","section":"Reading","passageId":1,"q":"What can be inferred about the processes of learning from mistakes in paragraph 2?","options":["They are wrong.","They take time.","They must be avoided.","They cause hostility."],"correct":1,"explanation":"Leaders must be \"patient enough for others to go through the processes of learning from mistakes\" — patience is needed because the process <b>takes time</b>."},{"n":45,"type":"mcq","part":"Section 3: Reading Comprehension","section":"Reading","passageId":1,"q":"What is stated in paragraph 3 about a controlling leader?","options":["He/She cannot reach his/her desired power.","He/She creates bad forces for his/her staff.","He/She does not have his/her staff's support.","He/She tries to serve others to win their hearts."],"correct":2,"explanation":"\"No employee will follow a controlling leader\" = he/she <b>does not have the staff's support</b>."},{"n":46,"type":"mcq","part":"Section 3: Reading Comprehension","section":"Reading","passageId":1,"q":"What can the word <b>force</b> in paragraph 3 be best replaced by?","options":["strength","influence","energy","attraction"],"correct":1,"explanation":"\"the benefits and force these policies create\" — the effect policies have on people is their <b>influence</b>."},{"n":47,"type":"mcq","part":"Section 3: Reading Comprehension","section":"Reading","passageId":1,"q":"What lesson or message can be inferred from paragraph 4?","options":["Interferences cause colleagues to envy.","A life of contentment is a happy life.","Truthfulness leads to others' happiness.","Understanding creates comfort and love."],"correct":1,"explanation":"\"Having more doesn't fix anything, and the idea of being content is the way to a satisfied heart\" — <b>contentment brings happiness</b>."},{"n":48,"type":"mcq","part":"Section 3: Reading Comprehension","section":"Reading","passageId":1,"q":"In paragraph 5, what is the phrase <b>people blaming</b> closest in meaning to?","options":["criticism","penalty","abuse","mistake"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Blaming people is finding fault with them, i.e. <b>criticism</b>, contrasted with \"problem solving\"."},{"n":49,"type":"mcq","part":"Section 3: Reading Comprehension","section":"Reading","passageId":1,"q":"In paragraph 6, why does the author use the phrase <b>as a result</b>?","options":["to give an example","to show an effect","to compare two things","to prove a true fact"],"correct":1,"explanation":"\"When trust thrives, the organization grows stronger as a result\" — the phrase introduces the <b>effect</b> of trust thriving."},{"n":50,"type":"mcq","part":"Section 3: Reading Comprehension","section":"Reading","passageId":1,"q":"According to the passage, which of these love lessons is NOT cited from a book author?","options":["Love is kind","Love is patient","Love keeps no record of wrongs","Love always trusts"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Paragraphs 2, 3 and 6 each name a book and author; paragraph 5 (<b>Love keeps no record of wrongs</b>) cites none."},{"n":51,"type":"mcq","part":"Section 3: Reading Comprehension","section":"Reading","passageId":2,"q":"What may be the best title for the passage?","options":["Measures to Prevent Animal-transmitted Diseases","National Solutions to Global Deforestation","How to Stop Deforestation in Worldwide Societies","Deforestation as a Major Cause of Pandemics"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The whole passage links clearing forests to viruses jumping from wild animals to humans and argues that ending deforestation prevents pandemics — <b>deforestation as a cause of pandemics</b>."},{"n":52,"type":"mcq","part":"Section 3: Reading Comprehension","section":"Reading","passageId":2,"q":"The word <b>clear</b> in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to _____.","options":["reduce pollutants","remove confusion","slash and burn","trap and kill"],"correct":2,"explanation":"\"forests that we are <b>slashing and burning</b> to create land… The more we clear\" — clearing forest = slashing and burning it."},{"n":53,"type":"mcq","part":"Section 3: Reading Comprehension","section":"Reading","passageId":2,"q":"Why does the author mention <b>Zika, Nipah, malaria, cholera, HIV</b> in paragraph 2?","options":["To warn people not to go into forest habitats when it is raining.","To give some examples as consequences of deforestation.","To predict the future dangers if deforestation takes place widely.","To describe the danger of these diseases to those who get them."],"correct":1,"explanation":"They illustrate \"a long list of dangerous diseases that have come from rain forest habitats\" — <b>examples</b> of what deforestation spreads."},{"n":54,"type":"mcq","part":"Section 3: Reading Comprehension","section":"Reading","passageId":2,"q":"It can be inferred from the 2019 study in paragraph 2 that malaria cases _____.","options":["have been a worry for people in many countries worldwide","have been rising faster than those of Zika, Nipah, cholera, and HIV","will never stop rising due to people's continuous deforestation","would probably rise to about 10% if deforestation rose to 30%"],"correct":3,"explanation":"\"a 10 percent increase in deforestation would raise malaria cases by 3.3 percent\" — so a 30% rise would give roughly 3 × 3.3 ≈ <b>10%</b>."},{"n":55,"type":"mcq","part":"Section 3: Reading Comprehension","section":"Reading","passageId":2,"q":"What does the word <b>which</b> in paragraph 3 refer to?","options":["eating less meat","destruction","numerous steps","meat"],"correct":0,"explanation":"\"Eating less meat, <b>which</b> improves our health anyway\" — the clause describes the action of eating less meat."},{"n":56,"type":"mcq","part":"Section 3: Reading Comprehension","section":"Reading","passageId":2,"q":"According to paragraph 3, _____ is one step to prevent the destruction.","options":["meaty food banning","reforestation","increased productivity","oily food removal"],"correct":2,"explanation":"\"Producing more food per hectare can boost supply without the need for more land\" = <b>increased productivity</b>. Eating less meat is not a ban, and reforestation is not mentioned."},{"n":57,"type":"mcq","part":"Section 3: Reading Comprehension","section":"Reading","passageId":2,"q":"What is implied in paragraph 4 about sales of live wild animals in wet markets?","options":["They must be removed out of all wet markets.","They are illegal and have been long banned.","Their disadvantages outweigh their advantages.","They are important as they make local culture."],"correct":2,"explanation":"\"The markets may be culturally important, but the risk is too great\" — the <b>disadvantages outweigh the advantages</b>."},{"n":58,"type":"mcq","part":"Section 3: Reading Comprehension","section":"Reading","passageId":2,"q":"The main idea of paragraph 4 is _____.","options":["government-based solutions","society-based solutions","impacts of deforestation","governments' development goals"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Every sentence says what \"governments\" or \"authorities\" should prohibit, ban or examine — <b>government-based solutions</b>."},{"n":59,"type":"mcq","part":"Section 3: Reading Comprehension","section":"Reading","passageId":2,"q":"Which of the following is NOT a United Nations' Sustainable Development Goal?","options":["Hunger removal","Responsible land use","Health insurance","Reduced production"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The goals listed are healthy lives, zero hunger, gender equality, responsible consumption and production, sustainably managed land and climate action — <b>reduced production</b> is not among them."},{"n":60,"type":"mcq","part":"Section 3: Reading Comprehension","section":"Reading","passageId":2,"q":"In which place can the following sentence best fit?<br><b>Reducing food waste can also vastly lessen the pressure to grow more crops.</b>","options":["[A]","[B]","[C]","[D]"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 lists what societies can do to reduce demand for land; \"also\" adds one more such step, so the sentence fits at <b>[B]</b>."},{"n":61,"type":"text","part":"Section 4: Writing · Part 1: Word formation","section":"Word form","q":"Vietnam was ___ successful in its early fight against the Covid-19 virus. (IMPRESS)","accept":["impressively"],"answer":"impressively","explanation":"An adverb is needed to modify the adjective \"successful\": <b>impressively</b>."},{"n":62,"type":"text","part":"Section 4: Writing · Part 1: Word formation","section":"Word form","q":"Most ___ parks are built in the suburbs, not in city centers. (INDUSTRY)","accept":["industrial"],"answer":"industrial","explanation":"An adjective before the noun \"parks\": <b>industrial parks</b> (khu công nghiệp)."},{"n":63,"type":"text","part":"Section 4: Writing · Part 1: Word formation","section":"Word form","q":"At colleges and universities, teachers are normally called \"___\". (INSTRUCT)","accept":["instructors"],"answer":"instructors","explanation":"A plural noun for people: teachers = <b>instructors</b>."},{"n":64,"type":"text","part":"Section 4: Writing · Part 1: Word formation","section":"Word form","q":"Mary's clothes are always in style because she wants to look ___. (FASHION)","accept":["fashionable"],"answer":"fashionable","explanation":"After \"look\" (linking verb) an adjective is needed: <b>fashionable</b>."},{"n":65,"type":"text","part":"Section 4: Writing · Part 1: Word formation","section":"Word form","q":"Young people should work hard to develop their country's ___. (ECONOMICAL)","accept":["economy"],"answer":"economy","explanation":"After the possessive \"country's\" a noun is needed: the country's <b>economy</b>."},{"n":66,"type":"text","part":"Section 4: Writing · Part 1: Word formation","section":"Word form","q":"In a written ___, it takes more time for test papers to be marked. (EXAMINE)","accept":["examination","exam"],"answer":"examination","explanation":"After \"a written\" a noun is needed: a written <b>examination</b>."},{"n":67,"type":"text","part":"Section 4: Writing · Part 1: Word formation","section":"Word form","q":"The 31st SEA Games event was both ___ and motivational to many people. (ENJOY)","accept":["enjoyable"],"answer":"enjoyable","explanation":"\"both ___ and motivational\" needs an adjective parallel to \"motivational\": <b>enjoyable</b>."},{"n":68,"type":"text","part":"Section 4: Writing · Part 1: Word formation","section":"Word form","q":"It is interesting that many useful products have been ___ by chance. (INVENTION)","accept":["invented"],"answer":"invented","explanation":"Present perfect passive \"have been + past participle\": <b>invented</b>."},{"n":69,"type":"text","part":"Section 4: Writing · Part 1: Word formation","section":"Word form","q":"___ speaking, teenagers are passionate fans of entertainment trends. (GENERAL)","accept":["Generally","generally"],"answer":"Generally","explanation":"The fixed adverbial phrase <b>Generally speaking</b> = in general."},{"n":70,"type":"text","part":"Section 4: Writing · Part 1: Word formation","section":"Word form","q":"___ to fluorescent bulbs, LED lights are up to 80% more energy-saving. (COMPARE)","accept":["Compared","compared"],"answer":"Compared","explanation":"The fixed participle phrase <b>Compared to/with</b> = in comparison with."},{"n":71,"type":"text","part":"Section 4: Writing · Part 2: Sentence transformation","section":"Sentence transformation","q":"It is possible that the Covid-19 is less threatening in many countries now.\n→ The Covid-19 virus ___","accept":["is likely to be less threatening in many countries now","may be less threatening in many countries now","might be less threatening in many countries now","could be less threatening in many countries now","is possibly less threatening in many countries now"],"answer":"is likely to be less threatening in many countries now","explanation":"\"It is possible that S + V\" → \"S <b>is likely to</b> V\" or \"S <b>may/might</b> V\"."},{"n":72,"type":"text","part":"Section 4: Writing · Part 2: Sentence transformation","section":"Sentence transformation","q":"They believe that hard work leads to success.\n→ It ___","accept":["is believed that hard work leads to success"],"answer":"is believed that hard work leads to success","explanation":"Impersonal passive with a reporting verb: \"They believe that…\" → \"<b>It is believed that</b>…\"."},{"n":73,"type":"text","part":"Section 4: Writing · Part 2: Sentence transformation","section":"Sentence transformation","q":"\"Don't go to bed late or you'll be late for school tomorrow,\" Tim's mother said to him.\n→ Tim's mother told ___ the next day.","accept":["him not to go to bed late or he would be late for school","him not to go to bed late or he'd be late for school"],"answer":"him not to go to bed late or he would be late for school","explanation":"Reported command: \"told + object + <b>not to</b> + V\"; \"you'll\" becomes \"he would\" and \"tomorrow\" becomes \"the next day\"."},{"n":74,"type":"text","part":"Section 4: Writing · Part 2: Sentence transformation","section":"Sentence transformation","q":"I'm dreaming about a vacation by the beach in this hot weather.\n→ I wish ___","accept":["I were having a vacation by the beach in this hot weather","I was having a vacation by the beach in this hot weather","I could have a vacation by the beach in this hot weather","I had a vacation by the beach in this hot weather"],"answer":"I were having a vacation by the beach in this hot weather","explanation":"A wish about the present takes the past (subjunctive \"were\"): \"I wish I <b>were having</b> a vacation…\"."},{"n":75,"type":"text","part":"Section 4: Writing · Part 2: Sentence transformation","section":"Sentence transformation","q":"Karen's best friend is studying abroad now. He can't attend Karen's 15th birthday.\n→ If Karen's best friend ___","accept":["were not studying abroad now, he could attend Karen's 15th birthday","weren't studying abroad now, he could attend Karen's 15th birthday","were not studying abroad now, he would be able to attend Karen's 15th birthday","were not studying abroad, he could attend Karen's 15th birthday","was not studying abroad now, he could attend Karen's 15th birthday","wasn't studying abroad now, he could attend Karen's 15th birthday"],"answer":"were not studying abroad now, he could attend Karen's 15th birthday","explanation":"Second conditional for an unreal present: \"If S <b>were not</b> V-ing, S <b>could</b> V\" — the facts are reversed in the if-clause and the result clause."},{"n":76,"type":"text","part":"Section 4: Writing · Part 2: Sentence transformation","section":"Sentence transformation","q":"My cousin rides his bike to work every day, but he thinks going by bus is safer.\n→ Riding ___","accept":["his bike to work every day, my cousin thinks going by bus is safer","his bike to work daily, my cousin thinks going by bus is safer"],"answer":"his bike to work every day, my cousin thinks going by bus is safer","explanation":"A participle clause with the same subject: \"<b>Riding</b> his bike to work every day, my cousin thinks…\"; the subject \"my cousin\" must follow the comma."},{"n":77,"type":"text","part":"Section 4: Writing · Part 2: Sentence transformation","section":"Sentence transformation","q":"Learning English through Walt Disney movies is easy for many children.\n→ Many children find ___","accept":["learning English through Walt Disney movies easy","it easy to learn English through Walt Disney movies","it easy learning English through Walt Disney movies"],"answer":"learning English through Walt Disney movies easy","explanation":"Pattern \"<b>find + object + adjective</b>\": find learning English … easy, or \"find it easy to learn…\"."},{"n":78,"type":"text","part":"Section 4: Writing · Part 2: Sentence transformation","section":"Sentence transformation","q":"If I am admitted into my dream high school, I will try my best for my academic goals.\n→ Should ___, I will try my best for my academic goals.","accept":["I be admitted into my dream high school","I be admitted to my dream high school"],"answer":"I be admitted into my dream high school","explanation":"Inverted first conditional: \"If I am admitted\" → \"<b>Should I be</b> admitted\" (should + subject + bare infinitive)."},{"n":79,"type":"text","part":"Section 4: Writing · Part 2: Sentence transformation","section":"Sentence transformation","q":"Larry joins some study groups on Facebook, so this social media platform is useful to him.\n→ Facebook ___","accept":["on which Larry joins some study groups, is useful to him","is useful to Larry because he joins some study groups on it","is a useful platform to Larry because he joins some study groups on it","where Larry joins some study groups, is useful to him","is useful to Larry as he joins some study groups on it","is a useful social media platform to Larry because he joins some study groups on it"],"answer":"on which Larry joins some study groups, is useful to him","explanation":"Either a relative clause \"Facebook, <b>on which</b> Larry joins some study groups, is useful to him\" or a reason clause \"Facebook is useful to Larry <b>because</b> he joins some study groups on it\"."},{"n":80,"type":"text","part":"Section 4: Writing · Part 2: Sentence transformation","section":"Sentence transformation","q":"The new supervisor just made a request. He asked us not to gossip during our meetings.\n→ The new supervisor requested that ___","accept":["we should not gossip during our meetings","we not gossip during our meetings","we shouldn't gossip during our meetings"],"answer":"we should not gossip during our meetings","explanation":"After \"request that\" the subjunctive is used: \"requested that we <b>(should) not gossip</b>…\"."}]},{"id":"ptnk-2022-chuyen","year":2022,"track":"chuyen","title":"PTNK 2022 · Tiếng Anh Chuyên","subtitle":"Đề thi tuyển sinh lớp 10 · Trường Phổ thông Năng khiếu · 2022-2023","durationMin":120,"keySource":"official","source":"2022 Thi vào 10 Đề thi Anh chuyên Phổ thông Năng khiếu.pdf","passages":["<b>New Test Shows If You Are a Shopaholic</b><br><br>A new shopaholic test could tell if you should leave your credit card at home when heading out to the mall.<br><br>The test makes it clear that there's shopping and then there's over-the-top purchasing that can wreak havoc on a person's life. People who become preoccupied with buying stuff and repeatedly spend money on items, regardless of need, are commonly referred to as <i>shopaholics</i>. Scientists call it <b>compulsive</b> buying.<br><br>The new test was administered along with a survey that revealed that nearly 9 percent of a sample of 550 university staff members, mostly women, would be considered compulsive buyers. Past studies had put the incidence of compulsive buying somewhere between 2 percent and 8 percent 15 years ago, and more recently, at nearly 6 percent, the researchers say. Other research has found that men are just as addicted to shopping as women.<br><br>\"We are living in a consumption-oriented society and have been spending ourselves into serious difficulty,\" researcher Kent Monroe, a marketing professor at the University of Illinois at Urbana-Champaign, told <i>LiveScience</i>. \"Compulsive buying is an addiction that can be harmful to the individual, families, relationships. It is not just something that only afflicts low-income people.\"<br><br>Monroe and his colleagues found that compulsive buying was linked to materialism, reduced self-esteem, depression, anxiety and stress. Compulsive shoppers had positive feelings associated with buying, and they also tended to hide purchases, return items, have more family arguments about purchases and have more maxed-out credit cards.<br><br>Previous tests for identifying problem buyers are lacking because they depend in large part on the consequences of shopping, such as financial difficulties and family strain over money matters, the researchers note. But for compulsive shoppers with higher income, money matters could be non-existent.<br><br>A dwindling bank account is just one of the upshots of <b>shopping 'til you drop</b>. Others include family conflicts, stress, depression and loss of self-esteem. The shopaholic test is just part of the answer.<br><br>\"There needs to be more research not only identifying people who have a tendency to buy compulsively, but also on developing education and self-help programs for people who are buying things they do not need or use,\" Monroe said. \"It can lead to a waste of resources and to deterioration in families and relationships.\"","<b>SAMOA</b><br><br>When reporting on foreign culture, the media often focus on what seems alien, and the Polynesian island of Samoa is certainly a target of this kind of reporting. Whenever a journalist comes looking for a story, they inevitably focus on the <i>fa'afafine</i> (Samoan men who dress as women) and (1)____ most other aspects of Samoan culture. Almost without exception, they (2)____ the valued place that a <i>fa'afafine</i> has in the community.<br><br><i>Fa'afafine</i> (3)____ as <i>in the manner of a woman</i>. In traditional Samoan society, when a family had a large number of male children, one would be selected to help his mother. The choice would be based on which son showed the most ability and interest in domestic (4)____, and he would then be dressed and raised as female. The choice was in no way based on the sexual (5)____ the family believed the son might have. It was a choice based on who was best (6)____ to a certain kind of labour. The <i>fa'afafine</i>'s abilities in the home and in producing crafts, combined with physical strength, were a useful (7)____ to Samoan communities. When the son grew up, he would marry and have children, as is expected of all Samoan men, but continue to (8)____ a female identity. Modern-day Samoa has strong Christian beliefs, as well as a firm sense of cultural (9)____, and many Samoans (10)____ the Western description of <i>fa'afafine</i> as homosexual.","<i>Read the text and fill the gaps with the correct sentence (A–F). There are two extra sentences you will not need.</i><br><br>A virtuous circle links conversation to the capacity for self-reflection. When we are secure in ourselves, we are able to really hear what other people have to say. At the same time, conversation with other people, both in intimate settings and in larger social groups, leads us to become better at inner dialogue.<br><br>But we have put this virtuous circle in peril. (1)____ Timothy D. Wilson, a psychologist at the University of Virginia, led a team that explored our capacity for solitude. People were asked to sit in a chair and think, without a device or a book. They were told that they would have from 6 to 15 minutes alone and that the only rules were that they had to stay seated and not fall asleep. In one experiment, many student subjects opted to give themselves mild electric shocks rather than sit alone with their thoughts.<br><br>People sometimes say to me that they can see how one might be disturbed when people turn to their phones when they are together. But surely there is no harm when people turn to their phones when they are by themselves? (2)____<br><br>But this way of dividing things up misses the essential connection between solitude and conversation. In solitude we learn to concentrate and imagine, to listen to ourselves. (3)____<br><br>Every technology asks us to confront human values. (4)____ If we are now ready to make face-to-face conversation a priority, it is easier to see what the next steps should be. We are not looking for simple solutions. We are looking for beginnings. Some of them may seem familiar by now, but they are no less challenging for that. Each addresses only a small piece of what silences us. Taken together, they can make a difference.<br><br>One start toward reclaiming conversation is to reclaim solitude. Some of the most crucial conversations you will ever have will be with yourself. Slow down sufficiently to make this possible. And make a practice of doing one thing at a time. Think of unitasking as the next big thing. (5)____<br><br><b>A</b> We need these skills to be fully present in conversation.<br><b>B</b> In every domain of life, it will increase performance and decrease stress.<br><b>C</b> This is easier to do without your phone in hand.<br><b>D</b> We turn time alone into a problem that needs to be solved with technology.<br><b>E</b> This is a good thing, because it causes us to reaffirm what they are.<br><b>F</b> If anything, it's our new form of being together.","<i>Five paragraphs have been removed from the extract. Choose from the paragraphs A–F the one that fits each gap (1–5). There is one extra paragraph which you do not need to use.</i><br><br><b>OMICRON VARIANT</b>: HOW WORRIED SHOULD WE BE ABOUT IT?<br><br>Since we first heard about Omicron, the variant has been found in many countries around the world where it is driving the most dramatic spike in COVID-19 cases we have yet seen. In the UK, there were over 30,000 new cases a day in the one-week period from 14 December. On 22 December, cases were nearly double the previous peak seen in January 2021, and Omicron was the dominant variant. Similar trends have also been seen in many other countries after the arrival of Omicron.<br><br>(1)____<br><br>The rapid spread of Omicron and the associated spike in COVID-19 cases in many countries suggests that the variant may be more transmissible. This concept is supported by pre-prints that show that Omicron is more infectious and has a shorter incubation period than the Delta variant. However, the rapid rise in Omicron cases may also be due to its ability to evade the protection of vaccine- or infection-induced immunity.<br><br>(2)____<br><br>Both Pfizer and Moderna have stated that two doses of their vaccines are significantly less effective against Omicron. Although a third dose was expected to increase protection, even two doses will likely still provide some protection against severe disease. There is also good evidence emerging that Omicron can evade immunity from a previous infection, even if that previous infection was with Delta or Beta, though it is not known if an Omicron infection would protect against a subsequent Delta infection. Modelling from Imperial College London estimates that Omicron is associated with a 5.41-fold increased risk of reinfection, compared to Delta.<br><br>(3)____<br><br>Recent non-peer-reviewed research suggests that the variant may change where in the body it grows best, with Omicron showing less replication in the lungs than Delta but much higher replication in the bronchi. It is possible that if Omicron does not replicate as much in the lungs, then it might cause less severe disease.<br><br>(4)____<br><br>Similar trends are now being observed in the UK, with both Imperial College London and the University of Edinburgh finding a reduction in the risks of hospitalisation with Omicron compared to Delta. In children, data from South Africa shows an increase in paediatric hospitalisations with Omicron. In Scotland there also appears to be a slight increase in the rate of hospitalisations in children with Omicron. None of this data has yet been peer reviewed and our full understanding of the clinical course of the disease and its severity will likely change as new and more full information becomes available over the coming weeks.<br><br>(5)____<br><br>In addition, it is expected that Omicron infections will still result in approximately 12 per cent of cases going on to develop the long-term debilitating symptoms of long COVID, for both adults and children. At the current level of over 200,000 COVID-19 cases per day in the UK (as of 4 January 2022), that would be 24,000 new cases of long COVID every day.<br><br><b>A</b> Recent research from South Africa that has not yet been peer reviewed suggests that Omicron may be more immune-evading than Delta, with increased risks of reinfection. This is beginning to be confirmed with laboratory experiments that have shown low to virtually no protection against infection with two vaccine doses. Protection was somewhat restored in people that had an mRNA vaccine booster, though breakthrough infections of boosted individuals have been reported. Imperial College London has modelled the vaccine efficacy of two doses of AstraZeneca as between 0 to 20 per cent, rising to 55 to 80 per cent after a third dose.<br><br><b>B</b> There is a reduced risk of hospitalisation for Omicron compared to the Delta variant. But WHO warns that it should not be dismissed as \"mild\". An increase in the number of COVID-19 related deaths because of the Omicron variant has been seen in many countries, especially where vaccination levels are low among vulnerable populations.<br><br><b>C</b> The four biggest questions surrounding Omicron are: is it more transmissible, is it more immune evading, does it cause different disease and do we need to do anything different to stop it. We do not have definitive answers to most of these questions, as we are still learning about the variant; however, several studies have recently been published as non-peer-reviewed pre-prints that may help to provide some answers.<br><br><b>D</b> An early report from South Africa has suggested the variant may produce more mild symptoms, a conclusion that has been met with much debate. Recent non-peer-reviewed research from South Africa looked at over 160,000 COVID-19 cases and found that Omicron infections were less likely to result in hospitalisation or severe disease in adults, compared to Delta. However, the authors note that this may be because many of the Omicron cases are reinfections, with immunity from previous infections conferring some protection.<br><br><b>E</b> Even if the severity of Omicron is lower than that of Delta, we know the variant can still cause severe and fatal illness. With the current rapid rise in cases, even a small rate of severe infections could result in a large number of hospitalisations that threaten overburdening the NHS.<br><br><b>F</b> Part of the reason for this immune evasion and possible increase in transmissibility is the large number of mutations Omicron has. Some mutations in the virus's spike protein are known to affect virus transmissibility and immune evasion. However, many are wondering if some of these mutations in Omicron have changed the severity of COVID-19 disease.","<i>Use the words on the right of the text to form ONE word that fits in the same numbered space in the text.</i><br><br><b>FLAVOURISTS</b><br><br>Do you ever wonder how some of your (0) <u>favourite</u> foods get their flavour? The answer lies with flavourists – experts who work with natural and (1)____ chemicals and ingredients to develop and improve how things taste.<br><br>In the past, people relied on natural herbs and spices to improve the flavour of their food. Cinnamon and thyme were used by early Egyptian (2)____. Other spice (3)____ were developed in the Middle Ages to stop meat from going bad.<br><br>Everything changed in 1858, when French scientist Theodore-Nicolas Gobley discovered the chemical that gives vanilla its flavour. This paved the way for the first artificial vanilla extract. The need for new taste (4)____ is driven by mass production of food. To get rid of microbes, many foods are heat-treated. This makes them safer to eat but it also destroys some of their natural taste. Flavourists aim to restore the lost flavours to make the food (5)____ tasty.<br><br>Almost every food you find in your supermarket will have been flavoured in some way. Today, flavour (6)____ is a multi-billion-pound (7)____ industry. To become a flavourist, you need to learn about herbs, spices and the different types of (8)____ oils, as well as how they taste when they are combined.<br><br>Finding unique flavours is as much an art as it is a science. Flavourists spend a lot of time blending different chemicals together to see which taste right. It can take up to 70 tries before the perfect (9)____ is found, with the right taste and (10)____ value at just the right price.<br><br>(0) FAVOUR · (1) SYNTHESISE · (2) CIVILISE · (3) MIX · (4) SENSE · (5) RESIST · (6) CREATE · (7) GLOBE · (8) ESSENCE · (9) COMBINE · (10) NUTRITION","<i>Complete the text. Use only ONE word for each gap.</i><br><br>I have and have had many names. Little Dog was what Grandma Lan called me. What made a woman who named herself and her daughter after flowers call her grandson a dog? A woman who watches (1)____ for her own, that's who. As you know, in the village where Grandma Lan (2)____ up, a child, often the smallest or weakest of the flock, (3)____ I was, is named after the most despicable things: demon, ghost child, pig snout, monkey-born, buffalo head, bastard — little dog being the more tender (4)____. Because evil spirits, roaming the land (5)____ healthy, beautiful children, would hear the name of something hideous and ghastly (6)____ called in for supper and pass over the house, (7)____ the child. To love something, then, is to name it after (8)____ so worthless it might be (9)____ untouched — and alive. A name, (10)____ as air, can also be a shield. A Little Dog shield.","<i>Complete the text. Use only ONE word for each gap.</i><br><br><b>BLAST FROM THE PAST</b><br><br>Researchers in France have managed to play the world's oldest-known conch shell horn – a musical (11)____ from more than 17,000 years ago. The shell, which is 31 centimetres long and 18 centimetres wide, once belonged to a type of large sea snail. It was discovered in a cave in southern France in 1931.<br><br>Researchers initially thought the shell was used (12)____ a cup by prehistoric people (13)____ Magdelanians. However, Gilles Tosello and his colleagues at the University of Toulouse recently took (14)____ look at it, using small cameras and a CT scanner machine ((15)____ uses X-rays to reveal details inside objects). They found that a hole at (16)____ end had been cut to form a mouthpiece. At the (17)____ end was a space for the player to insert their hand and modify the sound – not (18)____ to playing a French horn. A professional musician used the horn to make sounds that were close to the (19)____ notes C, C sharp and D.<br><br>The researchers are now using a 3D model of the shell to continue their search (20)____ its secrets.","<i>Identify the five mistakes in the following passage and correct them.</i><br><br>Line 1: To tip or not to tip, that is the question. And there is the one that causes British tourists more<br>Line 2: moments of embarrassment than almost any other issue. Furthermore, a handful of London's top<br>Line 3: hotels have come up with a cunning solution to spare the blushes. They've started adding a<br>Line 4: discretionary service charge for guests' bills. Despite the hotels insisting that the charge is optional,<br>Line 5: it is, in fact, disingenuous. We may make tipping embarrassing, but it's far more embarrassing to<br>Line 6: stand at a reception desk and demanding that the charge should be removed from the bill."],"questions":[{"n":1,"part":"Part 1. Reading","section":"Reading","type":"mcq","passageId":0,"q":"What is the main idea of the article?","options":["Our consumer society produces compulsive buyers, and the results can be devastating.","Many people overspend when they want to compensate for depression and stress.","More and more higher income people are becoming shopaholics.","Compulsive shopping is being recognized and studied by more and more researchers."],"correct":0,"explanation":"The article moves from \"a consumption-oriented society … spending ourselves into serious difficulty\" to the harm it causes: family conflicts, stress, depression, maxed-out credit cards."},{"n":2,"part":"Part 1. Reading","section":"Reading","type":"mcq","passageId":0,"q":"Which of the following best explains the meaning of the word <b>compulsive</b>?","options":["caused by a secret wish","resulting from an irresistible urge","relating to a psychological pain","driven by an emotional loss"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Compulsive buyers \"repeatedly spend money on items, regardless of need\" and it is called \"an addiction\" — an urge they cannot resist."},{"n":3,"part":"Part 1. Reading","section":"Reading","type":"mcq","passageId":0,"q":"Which of the following statements does <b>NOT</b> describe a compulsive buyer?","options":["My closet has unopened shopping bags in it.","Much of my life centres around buying things.","I buy things I did not plan to buy.","I buy things I need but they are more expensive than necessary."],"correct":3,"explanation":"Compulsive buying is spending \"regardless of need\" on things people \"do not need or use\"; buying needed items, even expensive ones, is not compulsive."},{"n":4,"part":"Part 1. Reading","section":"Reading","type":"mcq","passageId":0,"q":"Which of the following is reportedly the deep cause of compulsive buying behavior?","options":["need to seek pleasure or reduce feelings of stress","irresistibly strong temptation when going shopping","inability to control shopping and spending habits","financial difficulties and family strain over money matters"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Compulsive buying \"was linked to materialism, reduced self-esteem, depression, anxiety and stress\" and shoppers \"had positive feelings associated with buying\" — buying to feel good or relieve stress."},{"n":5,"part":"Part 1. Reading","section":"Reading","type":"mcq","passageId":0,"q":"What is the key point of paragraph 4?","options":["Compulsive spending is a growing trend among richer people.","There is an income difference between appropriate buyers and compulsive buyers.","Compulsive buying reflects our consumer culture with many negative consequences.","People ought to shop less if they want to prevent its harmful effects."],"correct":2,"explanation":"Monroe's quote: \"a consumption-oriented society … Compulsive buying is an addiction that can be harmful to the individual, families, relationships\"."},{"n":6,"part":"Part 1. Reading","section":"Reading","type":"mcq","passageId":0,"q":"Which of the following best describes the meaning of the phrase <b>shopping 'til you drop</b>?","options":["an exhaustive shopping list","a shrinking bank account","a pleasant shopping experience","an excessive buying phenomenon"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Shopping until you drop is shopping to excess; the passage lists its \"upshots\" — a dwindling bank account, family conflicts, stress, depression."},{"n":7,"part":"Part 1. Reading","section":"Reading","type":"mcq","passageId":0,"q":"Which of the following describes a serious flaw of a test designed to identify problem buyers by focusing only on financial difficulties and family strain over money matters?","options":["It overlooks compulsive shoppers with low income.","It makes no contribution to our understanding of shopaholicism.","It fails to identify compulsive shoppers with high income.","It blames high-income shopaholics for their problem."],"correct":2,"explanation":"\"For compulsive shoppers with higher income, money matters could be non-existent\" — so a test based on money trouble misses them."},{"n":8,"part":"Part 1. Reading","section":"Reading","type":"mcq","passageId":0,"q":"Future research can help compulsive buyers by providing information on","options":["the way family relationships can be repaired and rebuilt.","how to recognize and break the addiction.","the amount of money they overspend on luxurious stuff.","when a positive activity becomes a negative one."],"correct":1,"explanation":"Monroe calls for research \"identifying people who have a tendency to buy compulsively\" and \"developing education and self-help programs\" — recognising and breaking the habit."},{"n":9,"part":"Part 1. Reading","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":1,"q":"(1) ____","options":["oversee","overlook","supervise","manage"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Journalists focus on one thing and \"overlook\" (fail to notice, ignore) most other aspects of the culture."},{"n":10,"part":"Part 1. Reading","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":1,"q":"(2) ____","options":["misrepresent","ignore","mislead","translates"],"correct":0,"explanation":"They give a false picture of — \"misrepresent\" — the valued place the fa'afafine has; \"mislead\" takes a person as object and \"translates\" does not agree with \"they\"."},{"n":11,"part":"Part 1. Reading","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":1,"q":"(3) ____","options":["means","shows","reads","translates"],"correct":3,"explanation":"A foreign word \"translates as\" its English meaning; \"means\" would not take \"as\"."},{"n":12,"part":"Part 1. Reading","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":1,"q":"(4) ____","options":["chores","jobs","responses","products"],"correct":0,"explanation":"\"Domestic chores\" is the collocation for routine household tasks the son would help his mother with."},{"n":13,"part":"Part 1. Reading","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":1,"q":"(5) ____","options":["customs","values","preferences","demonstration"],"correct":2,"explanation":"\"Sexual preferences\" — the choice had nothing to do with the son's orientation, only with his aptitude for the work."},{"n":14,"part":"Part 1. Reading","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":1,"q":"(6) ____","options":["fitting","appropriate","suited","capable"],"correct":2,"explanation":"\"Best suited to\" a kind of labour; \"appropriate\" takes \"for\", \"capable\" takes \"of\"."},{"n":15,"part":"Part 1. Reading","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":1,"q":"(7) ____","options":["service","asset","instrument","means"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Someone whose abilities benefit a community is \"a useful asset to\" it."},{"n":16,"part":"Part 1. Reading","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":1,"q":"(8) ____","options":["transform","recall","reserve","retain"],"correct":3,"explanation":"He marries and has children but continues to \"retain\" (keep) a female identity."},{"n":17,"part":"Part 1. Reading","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":1,"q":"(9) ____","options":["identity","idea","valuation","custom"],"correct":0,"explanation":"\"A firm sense of cultural identity\" is the fixed collocation for a community's awareness of who it is."},{"n":18,"part":"Part 1. Reading","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":1,"q":"(10) ____","options":["distrust","resent","astonish","confuse"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Samoans \"resent\" (feel bitter about) the Western label of fa'afafine as homosexual, which contradicts their own understanding."},{"n":19,"part":"Part 1. Reading","section":"Reading","type":"text","passageId":2,"q":"Gap (1) — write the letter (A–F) of the missing sentence.","accept":["D"],"answer":"D","explanation":"The gap follows \"we have put this virtuous circle in peril\" and introduces Wilson's study of people who cannot bear solitude — we treat time alone as a problem to solve with technology."},{"n":20,"part":"Part 1. Reading","section":"Reading","type":"text","passageId":2,"q":"Gap (2) — write the letter (A–F) of the missing sentence.","accept":["F"],"answer":"F","explanation":"It completes the sceptic's view that phones alone do no harm: \"If anything, it's our new form of being together\" — which the next paragraph then rejects."},{"n":21,"part":"Part 1. Reading","section":"Reading","type":"text","passageId":2,"q":"Gap (3) — write the letter (A–F) of the missing sentence.","accept":["A"],"answer":"A","explanation":"\"These skills\" refers back to concentrating, imagining and listening to ourselves, which we need \"to be fully present in conversation\"."},{"n":22,"part":"Part 1. Reading","section":"Reading","type":"text","passageId":2,"q":"Gap (4) — write the letter (A–F) of the missing sentence.","accept":["E"],"answer":"E","explanation":"\"This is a good thing, because it causes us to reaffirm what they are\" — \"they\" are the human values every technology makes us confront."},{"n":23,"part":"Part 1. Reading","section":"Reading","type":"text","passageId":2,"q":"Gap (5) — write the letter (A–F) of the missing sentence.","accept":["B"],"answer":"B","explanation":"\"It\" is unitasking, the \"next big thing\"; in every domain it \"will increase performance and decrease stress\"."},{"n":24,"part":"Part 1. Reading","section":"Reading","type":"text","passageId":3,"q":"Gap (1) — write the letter (A–F) of the missing paragraph.","accept":["C"],"answer":"C","explanation":"After the opening on the case spike, C sets out the four big questions (transmissibility, immune evasion, disease, response) that the rest of the extract then answers in turn."},{"n":25,"part":"Part 1. Reading","section":"Reading","type":"text","passageId":3,"q":"Gap (2) — write the letter (A–F) of the missing paragraph.","accept":["A"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The preceding paragraph ends on Omicron's \"ability to evade … immunity\"; A gives the South African and lab evidence of immune evasion, and the next paragraph continues with vaccine effectiveness."},{"n":26,"part":"Part 1. Reading","section":"Reading","type":"text","passageId":3,"q":"Gap (3) — write the letter (A–F) of the missing paragraph.","accept":["F"],"answer":"F","explanation":"\"This immune evasion and possible increase in transmissibility\" sums up the two previous sections, and F's closing question about severity leads into the paragraph on where the virus replicates."},{"n":27,"part":"Part 1. Reading","section":"Reading","type":"text","passageId":3,"q":"Gap (4) — write the letter (A–F) of the missing paragraph.","accept":["D"],"answer":"D","explanation":"D reports the South African finding of milder disease; the next paragraph opens \"Similar trends are now being observed in the UK\"."},{"n":28,"part":"Part 1. Reading","section":"Reading","type":"text","passageId":3,"q":"Gap (5) — write the letter (A–F) of the missing paragraph.","accept":["E"],"answer":"E","explanation":"E warns that lower severity can still mean many hospitalisations, and the following paragraph adds \"In addition\" the burden of long COVID; B is the extra paragraph."},{"n":29,"part":"Part 2. Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"______ 80 per cent of the population in this village grow their own food on small plots of land.","options":["Some","A few","Most","Many"],"correct":0,"explanation":"\"Some\" before a number means \"approximately\": some 80 per cent = about 80 per cent."},{"n":30,"part":"Part 2. Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"With a monthly salary of 200 dollars, Allen was well-off ______ with most of his friends in the neighborhood.","options":["in comparing","comparing","being compared","compared"],"correct":3,"explanation":"\"Compared with/to\" is the fixed participle phrase used to make a comparison."},{"n":31,"part":"Part 2. Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"All children, ______ their race or ethnicity, deserve the same quality of care and affection in their first years of life.","options":["what's the matter with","no matter what is","no matter with","no matter what"],"correct":3,"explanation":"\"No matter what + noun\" = regardless of; no verb is needed before a noun phrase."},{"n":32,"part":"Part 2. Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"She already knew the result of the fight, but ______ she wanted to hear him admit his defeat.","options":["instead","even","still","so"],"correct":2,"explanation":"\"Still\" = nevertheless: despite knowing the result, she still wanted to hear it."},{"n":33,"part":"Part 2. Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"______ referred to by these theorists as \"the perfect social order\" turned out to be just an illusion.","options":["What is","What has","It is","Being"],"correct":0,"explanation":"A nominal \"what\" clause acts as the subject: \"What is referred to … as … turned out to be …\"."},{"n":34,"part":"Part 2. Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"I want to talk about child hunger in my own country, the United States, where one in six households with children are ______.","options":["insecurity food","food insecure","food insecurity","insecure food"],"correct":1,"explanation":"After \"are\" we need an adjective phrase; \"food insecure\" (lacking reliable access to food) is the established compound adjective."},{"n":35,"part":"Part 2. Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"The human species have come ______ the planet on which more than 7.9 billion humans live.","options":["very closely to destroy","very closely to destroying","very close to destroying","very close to destroy"],"correct":2,"explanation":"\"Come close to + V-ing\" = almost do something; \"close\" here is the adjective, and \"to\" is a preposition followed by a gerund."},{"n":36,"part":"Part 2. Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Mr. Grinde is 45 years old and ______ 5,000 dollars every month for the last 5 years for his retirement.","options":["has been saving","saved","had saved","was saving"],"correct":0,"explanation":"\"For the last 5 years\" with an action continuing up to now takes the present perfect continuous."},{"n":37,"part":"Part 2. Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Some of the most horrifying war crimes were denied ______.","options":["happening","to have happened","to be happening","to happen"],"correct":1,"explanation":"In the passive, \"X was denied to have happened\" (\"they denied that the crimes had happened\") needs the perfect infinitive for an earlier event."},{"n":38,"part":"Part 2. Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"As an Asian woman with brown skin, she finds herself working in a field where women are still ______.","options":["very minority","much the minority","very much minority","very much the minority"],"correct":3,"explanation":"\"Be (very much) in/the minority\" is the idiom; \"very much\" intensifies the noun phrase \"the minority\"."},{"n":39,"part":"Part 2. Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"______ money you are saving, you are completely justified in enjoying a lovely reward every so often.","options":["How much more","Given how much","Not how much","No matter how"],"correct":1,"explanation":"\"Given how much money you are saving\" = considering how much you save; \"no matter how\" would need \"how much money\"."},{"n":40,"part":"Part 2. Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Social media and networking tools (like LinkedIn) have ______ to increase the number of connections we can develop and maintain.","options":["made possible for us","made us possible","made possible","made it possible"],"correct":3,"explanation":"\"Make it possible to do something\" — the dummy object \"it\" stands for the infinitive that follows."},{"n":41,"part":"Part 2. Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"This is an experience from which she emerges ______ more than ever of her destiny as a visionary writer and leader.","options":["convincing","convincingly","convinced","having convinced"],"correct":2,"explanation":"\"Emerge convinced of\" — a past-participle adjective describing her state; she is convinced of her destiny."},{"n":42,"part":"Part 2. Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"If they ask him \"Why are you called Copper?\", he just laughs and never explains, which makes his friends want to know the reason ______.","options":["all the way","all the same","all the more","all the time"],"correct":2,"explanation":"\"All the more\" = even more (because of that): his silence makes them even more curious."},{"n":43,"part":"Part 2. Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"War can destroy families and leave survivors permanently ______.","options":["scarred","to be scarring","scarring","to be scarred"],"correct":0,"explanation":"\"Leave somebody + past participle\": the survivors are scarred (marked) by the war."},{"n":44,"part":"Part 2. Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"After the military attack in 2003, the country went through a long period of political instability, which is likely to last for years ______.","options":["to come","in coming","coming","that will come"],"correct":0,"explanation":"\"For years to come\" is the fixed phrase meaning for many future years."},{"n":45,"part":"Part 2. Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"If I have money to spare, I can be extravagant, but when, as is usually the case, I am hard up, then I am ______.","options":["the meanest man imaginable","the man meanest imaginable","the most imaginable mean man","the meanest imaginable man"],"correct":0,"explanation":"\"Imaginable\" follows the noun after a superlative: \"the meanest man imaginable\" = the meanest man you could imagine."},{"n":46,"part":"Part 2. Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"He was considered a true ______ to a tradition capable of transforming challenges into opportunities for growth.","options":["legacy","heir","hero","inheritor"],"correct":1,"explanation":"\"Heir to a tradition\" — the person who carries on a tradition; \"inheritor\" takes \"of\"."},{"n":47,"part":"Part 2. Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Today, in an area of roughly 3 square miles, Little Saigon is ______ more than 4,000 Vietnamese American businesses and 200 restaurants.","options":["shelter to","residence of","home to","headquarters of"],"correct":2,"explanation":"A place \"is home to\" the businesses located there — the standard collocation."},{"n":48,"part":"Part 2. Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"The concern for human dignity is given vivid ______ in the Christian teachings about how to perform charity work.","options":["personification","expression","voice","example"],"correct":1,"explanation":"\"Give expression to\" / \"be given expression in\" = be expressed; an idea finds vivid expression in the teachings."},{"n":49,"part":"Part 2. Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"These ruthless killers are not beasts, and therefore are not completely ______ of emotions or morals.","options":["devoid","destitute","desperate","incapacitated"],"correct":0,"explanation":"\"Devoid of\" = completely lacking; \"destitute\" is about poverty and \"desperate\" takes \"for\"."},{"n":50,"part":"Part 2. Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Dana White, a communications director at a technology nonprofit in California, saw her social ______ shrink to a precious few during the pandemic.","options":["webs","assemblies","meetings","circles"],"correct":3,"explanation":"\"Social circles\" are the groups of people one knows and mixes with."},{"n":51,"part":"Part 2. Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Thanks to the pandemic, she has come to ______ the intimate friendships that blossomed the past year, including with a neighbor who sang with her on her stoop every night at 7 p.m.","options":["savor","revel","acclaim","exult"],"correct":0,"explanation":"\"Savor\" = enjoy fully and appreciate; \"revel\" and \"exult\" need \"in\" and \"acclaim\" means praise publicly."},{"n":52,"part":"Part 2. Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"After his mother's death, he found ______ in the Buddhist rituals of mourning.","options":["sympathy","solace","relief","condolence"],"correct":1,"explanation":"\"Find solace in\" = find comfort in something during grief."},{"n":53,"part":"Part 2. Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Money is a small but often effective weapon to keep life's hardships and tragedies ______.","options":["at length","out of the way","out of reach","at bay"],"correct":3,"explanation":"\"Keep something at bay\" = prevent it from coming close or harming you."},{"n":54,"part":"Part 2. Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"The process of writing a memoir forced me to revisit painful memories, to ______ old wounds I had covered up for years.","options":["rekindle","revive","reopen","recall"],"correct":2,"explanation":"\"Reopen old wounds\" is the idiom for bringing back painful past experiences."},{"n":55,"part":"Part 2. Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"After the terrorist attack in New York, a ______ of \"you're either with us or you're against us\" became dominant.","options":["thinking","mentality","temperament","temper"],"correct":1,"explanation":"A \"mentality\" is a characteristic way of thinking; \"a mentality of us versus them\"."},{"n":56,"part":"Part 2. Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Ms. Joyce has spent much of her career as a psychiatrist ______ patients with life-threatening medical conditions to accept psychiatric care.","options":["instructing","consulting","counseling","directing"],"correct":2,"explanation":"\"Counsel somebody to do something\" = advise professionally; a psychiatrist counsels patients."},{"n":57,"part":"Part 2. Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"While her book describes the issue facing the country clearly and in great detail, it is regrettably ______ on solutions and specific ideas for achieving change.","options":["lacking","missing","short","inadequate"],"correct":2,"explanation":"\"Be short on something\" = not have enough of it; \"lacking\" takes \"in\"."},{"n":58,"part":"Part 2. Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"The old doctor said he had lost ______ of the number of patients he had saved in his life.","options":["count","sight","memory","file"],"correct":0,"explanation":"\"Lose count of\" = no longer know how many there have been."},{"n":59,"part":"Part 2. Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Some critics have argued that he is too ______ for the past and has too much reverence for it.","options":["yearning","sentimental","longing","nostalgic"],"correct":3,"explanation":"\"Nostalgic for the past\" — the adjective that takes \"for\"; \"sentimental\" takes \"about\"."},{"n":60,"part":"Part 2. Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"After winning \"American Idol\" in 2000, this farmwoman continued to ______ to success in the next two decades.","options":["sing her voice","sing her lips","sing her fame","sing her way"],"correct":3,"explanation":"\"Verb + one's way to\" (sing / work / fight one's way to success) = reach it by doing that action."},{"n":61,"part":"Part 2. Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Contrary to current trends, sometimes you do need to ______ your feelings, especially when planning your actions.","options":["compress","depress","oppress","repress"],"correct":3,"explanation":"\"Repress one's feelings\" = hold them back; \"oppress\" is used of people, \"compress\" of physical things."},{"n":62,"part":"Part 2. Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"The girl ______ away the dead leaves to reveal the tiny shoot of a new plant.","options":["scratched","scraped","grated","clawed"],"correct":1,"explanation":"\"Scrape away\" = remove by pulling something across a surface; \"grated\" is for cheese and \"clawed\" implies violence."},{"n":63,"part":"Part 3. Word formation","section":"Word form","type":"text","passageId":4,"q":"(1) experts who work with natural and ______ chemicals and ingredients (SYNTHESISE)","accept":["synthetic"],"answer":"synthetic","explanation":"An adjective is needed to pair with \"natural\": synthetic = artificially made."},{"n":64,"part":"Part 3. Word formation","section":"Word form","type":"text","passageId":4,"q":"(2) Cinnamon and thyme were used by early Egyptian ______. (CIVILISE)","accept":["civilizations","civilisations"],"answer":"civilizations","explanation":"A plural noun after \"early Egyptian\": the civilisations (societies) of ancient Egypt."},{"n":65,"part":"Part 3. Word formation","section":"Word form","type":"text","passageId":4,"q":"(3) Other spice ______ were developed in the Middle Ages to stop meat from going bad. (MIX)","accept":["mixtures"],"answer":"mixtures","explanation":"The verb \"were\" requires a plural noun: spice mixtures."},{"n":66,"part":"Part 3. Word formation","section":"Word form","type":"text","passageId":4,"q":"(4) The need for new taste ______ is driven by mass production of food. (SENSE)","accept":["sensations"],"answer":"sensations","explanation":"\"Taste sensations\" — the plural noun for the experiences of taste."},{"n":67,"part":"Part 3. Word formation","section":"Word form","type":"text","passageId":4,"q":"(5) Flavourists aim to restore the lost flavours to make the food ______ tasty. (RESIST)","accept":["irresistibly"],"answer":"irresistibly","explanation":"An adverb modifies the adjective \"tasty\", and the sense is positive: irresistibly tasty."},{"n":68,"part":"Part 3. Word formation","section":"Word form","type":"text","passageId":4,"q":"(6) Today, flavour ______ is a multi-billion-pound industry. (CREATE)","accept":["creation"],"answer":"creation","explanation":"A noun is needed as subject: \"flavour creation\" is the activity of making flavours."},{"n":69,"part":"Part 3. Word formation","section":"Word form","type":"text","passageId":4,"q":"(7) a multi-billion-pound ______ industry (GLOBE)","accept":["global"],"answer":"global","explanation":"An adjective before \"industry\": a global (worldwide) industry."},{"n":70,"part":"Part 3. Word formation","section":"Word form","type":"text","passageId":4,"q":"(8) the different types of ______ oils (ESSENCE)","accept":["essential"],"answer":"essential","explanation":"\"Essential oils\" are the concentrated plant oils used for flavour and scent."},{"n":71,"part":"Part 3. Word formation","section":"Word form","type":"text","passageId":4,"q":"(9) It can take up to 70 tries before the perfect ______ is found. (COMBINE)","accept":["combination"],"answer":"combination","explanation":"A singular noun after \"the perfect\": the perfect combination of chemicals."},{"n":72,"part":"Part 3. Word formation","section":"Word form","type":"text","passageId":4,"q":"(10) with the right taste and ______ value at just the right price (NUTRITION)","accept":["nutritional"],"answer":"nutritional","explanation":"An adjective modifies \"value\": nutritional value."},{"n":73,"part":"Part 4. Open Cloze","section":"Open cloze","type":"text","passageId":5,"q":"(1) ____","accept":["out"],"answer":"out","explanation":"\"Watch out for\" = look out for, protect: a woman who watches out for her own family."},{"n":74,"part":"Part 4. Open Cloze","section":"Open cloze","type":"text","passageId":5,"q":"(2) ____","accept":["grew"],"answer":"grew","explanation":"\"Grow up\" = spend one's childhood; past simple after \"where Grandma Lan\"."},{"n":75,"part":"Part 4. Open Cloze","section":"Open cloze","type":"text","passageId":5,"q":"(3) ____","accept":["as"],"answer":"as","explanation":"\"As I was\" = which I was: the smallest or weakest of the flock, as I was."},{"n":76,"part":"Part 4. Open Cloze","section":"Open cloze","type":"text","passageId":5,"q":"(4) ____","accept":["one"],"answer":"one","explanation":"\"One\" stands in for \"name\": little dog being the more tender one of these names."},{"n":77,"part":"Part 4. Open Cloze","section":"Open cloze","type":"text","passageId":5,"q":"(5) ____","accept":["for"],"answer":"for","explanation":"\"Roaming the land for\" = searching the land for healthy, beautiful children."},{"n":78,"part":"Part 4. Open Cloze","section":"Open cloze","type":"text","passageId":5,"q":"(6) ____","accept":["being"],"answer":"being","explanation":"\"Hear something being called\" — the passive participle: they hear the hideous name being called in for supper."},{"n":79,"part":"Part 4. Open Cloze","section":"Open cloze","type":"text","passageId":5,"q":"(7) ____","accept":["sparing"],"answer":"sparing","explanation":"The spirits pass over the house, \"sparing\" (leaving unharmed) the child — a participle of result."},{"n":80,"part":"Part 4. Open Cloze","section":"Open cloze","type":"text","passageId":5,"q":"(8) ____","accept":["something"],"answer":"something","explanation":"\"Name it after something so worthless\" — an indefinite pronoun completed by \"so worthless\"."},{"n":81,"part":"Part 4. Open Cloze","section":"Open cloze","type":"text","passageId":5,"q":"(9) ____","accept":["left"],"answer":"left","explanation":"\"Be left untouched\" = be allowed to remain untouched; passive with \"left\"."},{"n":82,"part":"Part 4. Open Cloze","section":"Open cloze","type":"text","passageId":5,"q":"(10) ____","accept":["thin"],"answer":"thin","explanation":"\"Thin as air\" — a name is as thin (insubstantial) as air, yet can still be a shield."},{"n":83,"part":"Part 4. Open Cloze","section":"Open cloze","type":"text","passageId":6,"q":"(11) ____","accept":["instrument"],"answer":"instrument","explanation":"A conch shell horn is \"a musical instrument\" from 17,000 years ago."},{"n":84,"part":"Part 4. Open Cloze","section":"Open cloze","type":"text","passageId":6,"q":"(12) ____","accept":["as"],"answer":"as","explanation":"\"Used as a cup\" — \"as\" introduces the function something served."},{"n":85,"part":"Part 4. Open Cloze","section":"Open cloze","type":"text","passageId":6,"q":"(13) ____","accept":["called","of"],"answer":"called","explanation":"\"Prehistoric people called Magdelanians\" (or \"people of Magdelanians\" per the key) names the group."},{"n":86,"part":"Part 4. Open Cloze","section":"Open cloze","type":"text","passageId":6,"q":"(14) ____","accept":["a"],"answer":"a","explanation":"\"Take a look at\" — the article completes the fixed expression."},{"n":87,"part":"Part 4. Open Cloze","section":"Open cloze","type":"text","passageId":6,"q":"(15) ____","accept":["that","which"],"answer":"that","explanation":"A relative pronoun introduces the clause describing the CT scanner: a machine that/which uses X-rays."},{"n":88,"part":"Part 4. Open Cloze","section":"Open cloze","type":"text","passageId":6,"q":"(16) ____","accept":["one"],"answer":"one","explanation":"\"A hole at one end … at the other end\" — the pair \"one … the other\"."},{"n":89,"part":"Part 4. Open Cloze","section":"Open cloze","type":"text","passageId":6,"q":"(17) ____","accept":["other"],"answer":"other","explanation":"\"At the other end\" completes the contrast with \"at one end\"."},{"n":90,"part":"Part 4. Open Cloze","section":"Open cloze","type":"text","passageId":6,"q":"(18) ____","accept":["dissimilar"],"answer":"dissimilar","explanation":"\"Not dissimilar to\" = quite similar to; the horn works much like a French horn."},{"n":91,"part":"Part 4. Open Cloze","section":"Open cloze","type":"text","passageId":6,"q":"(19) ____","accept":["musical"],"answer":"musical","explanation":"The notes C, C sharp and D are \"musical notes\"."},{"n":92,"part":"Part 4. Open Cloze","section":"Open cloze","type":"text","passageId":6,"q":"(20) ____","accept":["for"],"answer":"for","explanation":"\"Search for something\" — the noun \"search\" takes the preposition \"for\"."},{"n":93,"part":"Part 5. Key Word Transformation","section":"Sentence transformation","type":"text","q":"Can we really justify the idea that human lives matter more than nonhuman lives? (WEIGHT)<br>→ Is it possible for us to really justify the idea that human lives ______ nonhuman lives?","accept":["carry more weight than","is given more weight than","are given more weight than","should carry more weight than","carry much more weight than"],"answer":"carry more weight than","explanation":"\"Carry weight\" = matter, be important; \"human lives carry more weight than nonhuman lives\" keeps the comparison."},{"n":94,"part":"Part 5. Key Word Transformation","section":"Sentence transformation","type":"text","q":"The sheer magnitude of the project was bewildering. (SCALE)<br>→ I was ______ of the project.","accept":["bewildered by the sheer scale","bewildered at the sheer scale"],"answer":"bewildered by the sheer scale","explanation":"Magnitude → scale; the adjective \"bewildering\" becomes the passive \"I was bewildered by\"."},{"n":95,"part":"Part 5. Key Word Transformation","section":"Sentence transformation","type":"text","q":"Misfortune can turn even wealthy people into beggars on the street overnight. (REDUCED)<br>→ Even wealthy people ______ on the street overnight by misfortune.","accept":["can be reduced to begging","can be reduced to beggars","may be reduced to begging","may be reduced to beggars","could be reduced to begging","could be reduced to beggars"],"answer":"can be reduced to begging","explanation":"\"Be reduced to begging / to beggars\" = be brought down to that state; the passive with \"by misfortune\" mirrors \"misfortune can turn\"."},{"n":96,"part":"Part 5. Key Word Transformation","section":"Sentence transformation","type":"text","q":"We often refused to accept new ideas as we grew old. (IMMUNE)<br>→ We often ______ as we grew old.","accept":["became immune to new ideas","found ourselves immune to new ideas","grew immune to new ideas","were immune to new ideas"],"answer":"became immune to new ideas","explanation":"\"Be/become immune to something\" = be unaffected by it; refusing new ideas = becoming immune to them."},{"n":97,"part":"Part 5. Key Word Transformation","section":"Sentence transformation","type":"text","q":"We keep meeting accidentally, don't we? (RUNNING)<br>→ We are ______ other, aren't we?","accept":["always running into each","constantly running into each","forever running into each"],"answer":"always running into each","explanation":"\"Run into somebody\" = meet by chance; \"are always + V-ing\" expresses a repeated happening."},{"n":98,"part":"Part 5. Key Word Transformation","section":"Sentence transformation","type":"text","q":"Apart from their age, they are two absolutely different people. (COMMON)<br>→ The two of ______ apart from their age.","accept":["them have nothing in common","them have got nothing in common","them have nothing at all in common","them have absolutely nothing in common"],"answer":"them have nothing in common","explanation":"\"Have nothing in common\" = share no similarities; \"the two of them\" is the subject."},{"n":99,"part":"Part 5. Key Word Transformation","section":"Sentence transformation","type":"text","q":"She may seem quiet and reserved but her music is bursting with strong emotions. (HOWEVER)<br>→ ______ her music is bursting with emotions.","accept":["However quiet and reserved she may seem,","However quiet and reserved she may seem","However quiet and reserved she seems,","However quiet and reserved she seems","However quiet and reserved she might seem,","However quiet and reserved she might seem"],"answer":"However quiet and reserved she may seem,","explanation":"\"However + adjective + subject + verb\" = no matter how …: a concessive clause replacing \"she may seem … but\"."},{"n":100,"part":"Part 5. Key Word Transformation","section":"Sentence transformation","type":"text","q":"Plastic is such an integral product for convenience in our daily lives that we simply take it for granted. (PAUSE)<br>→ We seldom ______ since it is such an integral product for convenience in our daily lives.","accept":["give ourselves a pause for thought about plastic","pause to appreciate plastic","pause to think about plastic","give a pause for thought about plastic","pause to give a thought to plastic","pause to consider plastic"],"answer":"give ourselves a pause for thought about plastic","explanation":"Taking something for granted = never pausing to think about or appreciate it; \"pause for thought\" is the key phrase."},{"n":101,"part":"Part 5. Key Word Transformation","section":"Sentence transformation","type":"text","q":"The artist often tells his students that freedom, not money, is the meaning of success. (SO MUCH)<br>→ The artist often tells his students that success is not ______ freedom.","accept":["so much about money as","so much about money as about","so much a matter of money as","so much a matter of money as of"],"answer":"so much about money as","explanation":"\"Not so much X as Y\" = Y rather than X: success is not so much about money as (about) freedom."},{"n":102,"part":"Part 5. Key Word Transformation","section":"Sentence transformation","type":"text","q":"Even though we tried to follow Peter and Bob as closely as we could, we soon got separated. (LONG)<br>→ We tried to follow Peter and Bob as closely as we could but it ______ us to get separated.","accept":["didn't take long for","did not take long for","was not long before it was time for","wasn't long before it was time for","did not take very long for","didn't take very long for"],"answer":"didn't take long for","explanation":"\"It didn't take long for somebody to do something\" = it happened soon; matches \"we soon got separated\"."},{"n":103,"part":"Part 6. Error Identification and Correction","section":"Error correction","type":"text","passageId":7,"q":"Line 1: \"And <b>there</b> is the one that causes British tourists more moments of embarrassment\" — write the correction.","accept":["it","that","it is","that is"],"answer":"it","explanation":"The pronoun refers back to \"the question\" of tipping: \"it/that is the one that causes …\"; \"there is\" would introduce something new."},{"n":104,"part":"Part 6. Error Identification and Correction","section":"Error correction","type":"text","passageId":7,"q":"Line 2: \"<b>Furthermore</b>, a handful of London's top hotels have come up with a cunning solution\" — write the correction.","accept":["However","However,"],"answer":"However","explanation":"The hotels' solution contrasts with the embarrassment just described, so a contrast linker \"However\" is needed, not the additive \"Furthermore\"."},{"n":105,"part":"Part 6. Error Identification and Correction","section":"Error correction","type":"text","passageId":7,"q":"Line 4: \"They've started adding a discretionary service charge <b>for</b> guests' bills.\" — write the correction.","accept":["in","in guests' bills","in guests' bill","to","to guests' bills"],"answer":"in","explanation":"A charge is added \"in\" (or \"to\") a bill, not \"for\" it — the official key gives \"in guests' bill\"."},{"n":106,"part":"Part 6. Error Identification and Correction","section":"Error correction","type":"text","passageId":7,"q":"Line 5: \"We may <b>make</b> tipping embarrassing, but it's far more embarrassing to stand at a reception desk\" — write the correction.","accept":["find"],"answer":"find","explanation":"\"Find something embarrassing\" = consider it embarrassing; \"make\" would mean we cause it to be so."},{"n":107,"part":"Part 6. Error Identification and Correction","section":"Error correction","type":"text","passageId":7,"q":"Line 6: \"to stand at a reception desk and <b>demanding</b> that the charge should be removed from the bill\" — write the correction.","accept":["demand"],"answer":"demand","explanation":"Parallel structure after \"to\": \"to stand … and demand\" — both verbs share the infinitive."}]},{"id":"ptnk-2021-kc","year":2021,"track":"kc","title":"PTNK 2021 · Tiếng Anh Không chuyên","subtitle":"Đề thi tuyển sinh lớp 10 · Trường Phổ thông Năng khiếu · 2021-2022","durationMin":60,"keySource":"official","source":"2021 Thi vào 10 Đề thi Anh chung Phổ thông Năng khiếu.pdf","passages":["<b>Elves</b><br><br>Elves have been a popular subject in fiction for centuries, ranging from W. Shakespeare's play \"A Midsummer Night's Dream\" to the classic fantasy novels of J. R. R. Tolkien 300 years later. But it's only recently that <b>elves have been confined to plays</b>, books, and fairy tales. In centuries past, belief in the existence of fairies and elves was common among both adults and children.<br><br>Like fairies, elves were said to be magical, tiny shape changers. Shakespeare's elves were tiny, winged creatures that lived in, and playfully flitted around flowers. English male elves were described as looking like little old men, though elf maidens were invariably young and beautiful. Like men of the time, elves lived in kingdoms found in forests, meadows, or hollowed-out tree trunks. <b>[A]</b><br><br>Elves, fairies and leprechauns are closely related in folklore, though elves specifically seem to have sprung from early Norse mythology. By the 1500s, people began incorporating elf folklore into stories and legends about fairies, and by 1800, fairies and elves were widely considered to be simply different names for the same magical creatures. <b>[B]</b><br><br>As with fairies, elves eventually developed a reputation for pranks and mischief, and strange daily occurrences were often attributed to them. For example, when the hair on a person or horse became tangled and knotted, such \"elf locks\" were blamed on elves. <b>[C]</b><br><br>Though elves were sometimes friendly toward humans, they were also known to take terrible revenge on any human who offends them. They may steal babies, cattle, milk, and bread or enchant and hold young men in their spell for years at a time.<br><br>Another type of elf <b>emerged</b>, one with a somewhat different nature and form than the mischievous and tiny ones of old. Some elves, such as those depicted in J.R.R. Tolkien's \"Lord of the Rings\" trilogy, are slender, human-sized, and beautiful, with fine, almost angelic, features. Tolkien's characters were drawn largely from his research into Scandinavian folklore, and therefore it's not surprising that his elves might be tall and blond. Though not immortal, these elves were said to live hundreds of years. They have also become a staple of modern fantasy fiction. <b>[D]</b><br><br>In either form, elves are strongly associated with magic and nature. As with fairies, elves were said to secretly steal healthy human babies and replace them with their own kind. There were even legends instructing parents on how to get their real child back from its elven <b>abductors</b>.<br><br>Each generation seems to have their own use for elves in their stories. Just as leprechauns have historically been associated with one type of work (shoemaking), it is perhaps not surprising that many common (and commercial) images of elves depict them as industrious workers; think, for example, of Santa Claus' toy making elves. Folklore, like language and culture, is constantly evolving, and elves will likely always be with us, in one form or another.","<b>Life Expectancy</b><br><br><b>0</b> Life expectancy can be defined as the number of years someone is expected to live and has been the subject of many studies. The most recent studies into changes in life expectancy in many countries of the world show that it has been increasing for many years. In fact, life expectancy is higher today than it has ever been in the past. This is a very positive development for those who are interested in diet, health and lifestyle. It would appear that, when judged by changes in life expectancy, health in our modern society is improving all the time and is not, as is often suggested, getting worse.<br><br><b>1</b> In adults, the main change is a decline in circulatory disease, especially heart disease. Heart disease is the principal cause of death in the world. The mortality rate from this type of disease has decreased dramatically over the last 15 years in many countries, and it is likely that this <b>trend</b> will continue. The second principal cause of death is cancer. However, mortality over the last 15 years from cancer has either stabilised in countries such as Japan and Switzerland, or has decreased slightly in countries such as Italy, the USA and the UK.<br><br><b>2</b> Of course, the increase in life expectancy during the last 100 years is not due to any one factor. On the whole, improvements in medical care as well as better <b>prevention</b> of illnesses have both played a role in the increase. By prevention we mean better food availability, variety and balance, more appropriate nutrition, and understanding of the importance of exercise, and a reduction in smoking and alcohol consumption.<br><br><b>3</b> It is obvious that many factors have been involved in making our lives safer and healthier. This conclusion is by no means new. Hippocrates stated something similar over 2,000 years ago. In the 5th century BC, he said: \"Positive health requires knowledge of man's primary constitution and the powers of various foods, both those natural to them and those resulting from human skills. But eating alone is not enough for good health. There must be exercise, of which the effects must likewise be known.\" Interestingly, Hippocrates <b>incorporated</b> almost every element that we now consider important for improving health and increasing our chances of longer life.<br><br><b>4</b> A recent study has shown that there are four types of behaviour which can actually increase our average life expectancy. The research found that those who exercised regularly, ate five portions of fruit and vegetables a day, didn't smoke, and consumed alcohol in moderation, lived an average of 14 years longer than those who didn't adopt any of these behaviours. Finally, it is interesting to note that in virtually every population in the world, women live longer than men – by an average of 7 years. To date, no-one has come up with an exact explanation for this phenomenon.","<b>Healthy eating in schools is celebrated</b><br><br>On 11 March, International School Meals Day (ISMD) was marked, highlighting how important it is for children to have (1)____ to healthy food in order to learn (2)____. The theme for this year was \"eat for the health of it\", to encourage young people and teachers to talk about the role food (3)____ in their lives, and (4)____ it helps them to learn. (5)____ shows that healthy foods can help students to focus.<br><br>ISMD was started in 2013 and is (6)____ by the charity Children in Scotland. The programme is (7)____ by organisations from around the world, including the Scottish Government, the Welsh Assembly and the World Food Programme.<br><br>Organisations across the world, including in Colombia, Finland, Iraq and Yemen, (8)____ on social media about (9)____ healthy school meals for students. In the UK, a project called The Breakfast Club Cart will begin in 20 primary and secondary schools. It will allow pupils to (10)____ a free breakfast from a mobile cart when they arrive at school.","<b>NOISE IS BAD FOR US</b><br><br>Did you know that, even when you are (0) <b><u>asleep</u></b>, your body continues to (1)____ to noise? Loud noises raise your levels of stress hormones such as adrenaline. This is probably left over from our ancestors, who needed to be alert to anything that might (2)____ them, even while they were sleeping, so that they could wake up immediately and escape from (3)____ situations.<br><br>(4)____, producing stress hormones constantly can cause a number of serious (5)____ and other health problems. Heart attacks, strokes and high blood (6)____ are among the most life-threatening. Moreover, it is believed that too much noise can cause social problems, since it makes people angry and aggressive. According to recent (7)____ studies, you can't get used to noise. Even if you don't notice it, it is still affecting you both (8)____ and psychologically. So, whether it's heavy traffic, car alarms or (9)____ neighbours, watch out! Noise can be very (10)____!"],"questions":[{"n":1,"part":"Part 1. Pronunciation","section":"Phonetics","type":"mcq","q":"Choose the word whose underlined part differs from the other three in pronunciation.","options":["in<u>s</u>ect","in<u>s</u>ert","in<u>s</u>ist","in<u>s</u>ure"],"correct":3,"explanation":"In <b>insure</b> the \"s\" is /ʃ/ (/ɪnˈʃʊə/); in insect, insert and insist it is /s/."},{"n":2,"part":"Part 1. Pronunciation","section":"Phonetics","type":"mcq","q":"Choose the word whose underlined part differs from the other three in pronunciation.","options":["surf<u>a</u>ce","r<u>a</u>cial","rest<u>a</u>te","eng<u>a</u>ge"],"correct":0,"explanation":"In <b>surface</b> the \"a\" is the weak /ɪ/ (/ˈsɜːfɪs/); in racial, restate and engage it is /eɪ/."},{"n":3,"part":"Part 1. Pronunciation","section":"Phonetics","type":"mcq","q":"Choose the word whose underlined part differs from the other three in pronunciation.","options":["fu<u>n</u>ction","si<u>n</u>gle","inve<u>n</u>tion","fi<u>n</u>ger"],"correct":2,"explanation":"In <b>invention</b> the \"n\" is /n/ before /ʃ/; in function, single and finger it is the velar /ŋ/ before /k/ or /g/."},{"n":4,"part":"Part 1. Pronunciation","section":"Stress","type":"mcq","q":"Choose the word that differs from the other three in the position of primary stress.","options":["helpful","global","distract","monkey"],"correct":2,"explanation":"<b>Distract</b> is stressed on the second syllable (dis-TRACT); helpful, global and monkey are stressed on the first."},{"n":5,"part":"Part 1. Pronunciation","section":"Stress","type":"mcq","q":"Choose the word that differs from the other three in the position of primary stress.","options":["professor","waterfall","assistant","confusion"],"correct":1,"explanation":"<b>Waterfall</b> is stressed on the first syllable (WA-ter-fall); professor, assistant and confusion are stressed on the second."},{"n":6,"part":"Part 2. Reading","section":"Reading","type":"mcq","passageId":0,"q":"What, according to the article, is true about elves?","options":["Centuries ago people believed in them.","They are identical to fairies.","They sprung from leprechauns.","They may cause hair to get tangled."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 1: \"In centuries past, belief in the existence of fairies and elves was common among both adults and children.\""},{"n":7,"part":"Part 2. Reading","section":"Reading","type":"mcq","passageId":0,"q":"Which of the following is closest in meaning to <b>elves have been confined to plays</b> in paragraph 1?","options":["Elves appear only in literature.","Restrictions have been put on them.","They cannot go wherever they want.","They still like to play tricks."],"correct":0,"explanation":"\"Confined to plays, books, and fairy tales\" is contrasted with the past belief that elves really existed — today they exist only in literature."},{"n":8,"part":"Part 2. Reading","section":"Reading","type":"mcq","passageId":0,"q":"What was one characteristic of Shakespeare's elves?","options":["They were mostly male.","They were unattractive.","The females were good-looking.","They were better-looking."],"correct":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 2: \"elf maidens were invariably young and beautiful.\""},{"n":9,"part":"Part 2. Reading","section":"Reading","type":"mcq","passageId":0,"q":"What, according to the author, is the origin of elves?","options":["They are the ancestors of leprechauns.","Their roots lie in Scandinavian folklore.","Shakespeare first brought them to life.","They belong to early Norse Mythology."],"correct":3,"explanation":"Paragraph 3: \"elves specifically seem to have sprung from early Norse mythology.\""},{"n":10,"part":"Part 2. Reading","section":"Reading","type":"mcq","passageId":0,"q":"What does the author claim about Tolkien's elves?","options":["They liked to create trouble.","They were immortal.","They liked to play tricks.","They were the size of normal people."],"correct":3,"explanation":"Paragraph 6 describes Tolkien's elves as \"slender, human-sized, and beautiful\" and \"not immortal\"."},{"n":11,"part":"Part 2. Reading","section":"Reading","type":"mcq","passageId":0,"q":"Which of the following is closest in meaning to <b>emerged</b> in paragraph 6?","options":["appeared","united","followed","took place"],"correct":0,"explanation":"\"Another type of elf emerged\" means a new kind of elf appeared or came into being."},{"n":12,"part":"Part 2. Reading","section":"Reading","type":"mcq","passageId":0,"q":"What are elves associated with?","options":["youth and beauty","nature and magic","deformity and birthmarks","tangled hair"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 7: \"In either form, elves are strongly associated with magic and nature.\""},{"n":13,"part":"Part 2. Reading","section":"Reading","type":"mcq","passageId":0,"q":"Which of the following is closest in meaning to <b>abductors</b> in paragraph 7?","options":["kidnappers","villains","associates","benefactors"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The elves \"steal healthy human babies\" — an abductor is someone who takes a person away by force, i.e. a kidnapper."},{"n":14,"part":"Part 2. Reading","section":"Reading","type":"mcq","passageId":0,"q":"What does the article claim about Santa's elves?","options":["They are leprechauns.","They are always around.","They work hard.","They are very commercial."],"correct":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 8: images of elves \"depict them as industrious workers; think, for example, of Santa Claus' toy making elves.\""},{"n":15,"part":"Part 2. Reading","section":"Reading","type":"mcq","passageId":0,"q":"At which point in the article would the following sentence best fit?<br><b>\"A baby born with a birthmark or deformity was said to be elf-marked.\"</b>","options":["[A]","[B]","[C]","[D]"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The sentence gives another example of a strange occurrence blamed on elves, so it follows the \"elf locks\" example in the paragraph about pranks and mischief, at [C]."},{"n":16,"part":"Part 2. Reading","section":"Reading","type":"text","passageId":1,"q":"Match the heading (A–G) with paragraph <b>1</b>. Write the letter.<br>Headings: A Not a new idea; B Specific things we can do to live longer; C More people getting ill; D Research shows we are living longer (example: paragraph 0); E No-one knows why we live longer; F Few deaths due to disease; G Stopping illness before it happens.","accept":["F","Few deaths due to disease"],"answer":"F","explanation":"Paragraph 1 is about the decline in deaths from heart disease and cancer, so \"Few deaths due to disease\" (F) fits."},{"n":17,"part":"Part 2. Reading","section":"Reading","type":"text","passageId":1,"q":"Match the heading (A–G) with paragraph <b>2</b>. Write the letter.<br>Headings: A Not a new idea; B Specific things we can do to live longer; C More people getting ill; D Research shows we are living longer (example: paragraph 0); E No-one knows why we live longer; F Few deaths due to disease; G Stopping illness before it happens.","accept":["G","Stopping illness before it happens"],"answer":"G","explanation":"Paragraph 2 explains \"better prevention of illnesses\", i.e. stopping illness before it happens (G)."},{"n":18,"part":"Part 2. Reading","section":"Reading","type":"text","passageId":1,"q":"Match the heading (A–G) with paragraph <b>3</b>. Write the letter.<br>Headings: A Not a new idea; B Specific things we can do to live longer; C More people getting ill; D Research shows we are living longer (example: paragraph 0); E No-one knows why we live longer; F Few deaths due to disease; G Stopping illness before it happens.","accept":["A","Not a new idea"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Paragraph 3 says \"This conclusion is by no means new\" and cites Hippocrates 2,000 years ago, so \"Not a new idea\" (A)."},{"n":19,"part":"Part 2. Reading","section":"Reading","type":"text","passageId":1,"q":"Match the heading (A–G) with paragraph <b>4</b>. Write the letter.<br>Headings: A Not a new idea; B Specific things we can do to live longer; C More people getting ill; D Research shows we are living longer (example: paragraph 0); E No-one knows why we live longer; F Few deaths due to disease; G Stopping illness before it happens.","accept":["B","Specific things we can do to live longer"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Paragraph 4 lists four behaviours (exercise, five portions of fruit and vegetables, not smoking, moderate alcohol) that increase life expectancy (B)."},{"n":20,"part":"Part 2. Reading","section":"Reading","type":"mcq","passageId":1,"q":"According to the passage, life expectancy is","options":["at a low point.","not really understood.","the highest it's ever been.","not related to diet, health and lifestyle."],"correct":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 0: \"life expectancy is higher today than it has ever been in the past.\""},{"n":21,"part":"Part 2. Reading","section":"Reading","type":"mcq","passageId":1,"q":"Heart disease in adults","options":["has been rising for the last 15 years.","is as serious as cancer.","has only been monitored for 15 years.","causes the highest number of deaths."],"correct":3,"explanation":"Paragraph 1: \"Heart disease is the principal cause of death in the world.\""},{"n":22,"part":"Part 2. Reading","section":"Reading","type":"mcq","passageId":1,"q":"The word closest in meaning to <b>trend</b> in paragraph 2 is","options":["disease.","tendency.","increase.","rate."],"correct":1,"explanation":"A trend is a general direction of change — a tendency; here the tendency for heart-disease mortality to fall."},{"n":23,"part":"Part 2. Reading","section":"Reading","type":"mcq","passageId":1,"q":"According to the passage, deaths from cancer","options":["used to be a less serious problem in the USA.","have neither increased nor decreased in Italy.","are increasing in Japan.","have remained the same in Switzerland."],"correct":3,"explanation":"Paragraph 1: cancer mortality \"has either stabilised in countries such as Japan and Switzerland, or has decreased slightly in countries such as Italy, the USA and the UK.\""},{"n":24,"part":"Part 2. Reading","section":"Reading","type":"mcq","passageId":1,"q":"An example of <b>prevention</b> is","options":["drinking less alcohol.","living a less demanding lifestyle.","improving medical care.","eating mostly one type of food."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 defines prevention as including \"a reduction in smoking and alcohol consumption\"; medical care is listed separately."},{"n":25,"part":"Part 2. Reading","section":"Reading","type":"mcq","passageId":1,"q":"The word closest in meaning to <b>incorporated</b> in paragraph 4 is","options":["included.","criticized.","explained.","rejected."],"correct":0,"explanation":"To incorporate something is to include it as part of a whole — Hippocrates included almost every element we now consider important."},{"n":26,"part":"Part 2. Reading","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":2,"q":"Choose the word or phrase that best fits blank (1).","options":["chance","access","choice","entry"],"correct":1,"explanation":"\"Have access to healthy food\" — <b>access to</b> is the fixed collocation."},{"n":27,"part":"Part 2. Reading","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":2,"q":"Choose the word or phrase that best fits blank (2).","options":["justly","rightly","properly","exactly"],"correct":2,"explanation":"\"Learn properly\" — properly means correctly/in the right way; the other adverbs do not collocate with learn here."},{"n":28,"part":"Part 2. Reading","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":2,"q":"Choose the word or phrase that best fits blank (3).","options":["does","plays","makes","acts"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The collocation is \"the role (that) food <b>plays</b> in their lives\" — play a role."},{"n":29,"part":"Part 2. Reading","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":2,"q":"Choose the word or phrase that best fits blank (4).","options":["what","where","how","when"],"correct":2,"explanation":"\"Talk about … and how it helps them to learn\" — the way in which food helps learning."},{"n":30,"part":"Part 2. Reading","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":2,"q":"Choose the word or phrase that best fits blank (5).","options":["Inspection","Examination","Study","Research"],"correct":3,"explanation":"\"Research shows that…\" — research is the uncountable noun for scientific investigation and takes a singular verb."},{"n":31,"part":"Part 2. Reading","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":2,"q":"Choose the word or phrase that best fits blank (6).","options":["run","set","drawn","put"],"correct":0,"explanation":"\"Is run by the charity Children in Scotland\" — <b>run</b> means managed/operated."},{"n":32,"part":"Part 2. Reading","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":2,"q":"Choose the word or phrase that best fits blank (7).","options":["planned","supported","developed","established"],"correct":1,"explanation":"\"Supported by organisations from around the world, including the Scottish Government…\" — the organisations back the programme."},{"n":33,"part":"Part 2. Reading","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":2,"q":"Choose the word or phrase that best fits blank (8).","options":["mailed","sent","posted","delivered"],"correct":2,"explanation":"\"Posted on social media\" — <b>post</b> is the verb used for publishing on social media."},{"n":34,"part":"Part 2. Reading","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":2,"q":"Choose the word or phrase that best fits blank (9).","options":["donating","helping","giving","providing"],"correct":3,"explanation":"\"About providing healthy school meals for students\" — provide something for someone."},{"n":35,"part":"Part 2. Reading","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":2,"q":"Choose the word or phrase that best fits blank (10).","options":["take","bring","fetch","need"],"correct":0,"explanation":"\"Allow pupils to take a free breakfast from a mobile cart\" — pick up/receive it."},{"n":36,"part":"Part 3. Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"If I were you, I ______ eat much chocolate.","options":["won't","mustn't","didn't","wouldn't"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Second conditional: \"If I were you, I wouldn't…\" — would + bare infinitive in the main clause."},{"n":37,"part":"Part 3. Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"It is four years since that runner ______ a medal in an athletic event.","options":["has won","last won","had won","last wins"],"correct":1,"explanation":"\"It is + period + since + past simple\": the last time he won was four years ago."},{"n":38,"part":"Part 3. Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"My plane ______ at exactly 10 am, but I must be at the airport at least an hour earlier.","options":["leaves","has to leave","will have left","is about to leave"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Present simple is used for timetables and scheduled departures: \"My plane leaves at 10 am.\""},{"n":39,"part":"Part 3. Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Jack is not only an excellent pianist but a talented singer ______.","options":["either","both","again","as well"],"correct":3,"explanation":"\"Not only … but … as well\" — <b>as well</b> (= too) goes at the end of the clause."},{"n":40,"part":"Part 3. Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Those women, ______ are my students, work at the local hospital.","options":["their children","both their children","whose children","that their children"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Non-defining relative clause with possessive: \"whose children are my students\"."},{"n":41,"part":"Part 3. Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"While she ______ to convince him of her innocence, he flatly refused to listen.","options":["tried","was tried","has been trying","was trying"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Past continuous after \"while\" for the action in progress when the past-simple action (\"refused\") happened."},{"n":42,"part":"Part 3. Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"The accident which happened this morning ______ to the police.","options":["reported","was reported","is reporting","has reported"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Passive: the accident cannot report itself — \"was reported to the police\"."},{"n":43,"part":"Part 3. Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"\"Would you like some more tea?\" \"Yes please, but only ______.\"","options":["a little","any","little","a few"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Tea is uncountable, and the speaker wants a small amount: \"only a little\"."},{"n":44,"part":"Part 3. Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Do you know ______ Bob is coming to the show tomorrow?","options":["as long as","what if","what time is","whether"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Reported yes/no question: \"Do you know whether Bob is coming…?\""},{"n":45,"part":"Part 3. Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"______, there has been no increase in bus fares since last year.","options":["It was surprising","There was a surprise","Surprisingly enough","It is enough of a surprise"],"correct":2,"explanation":"A sentence adverb introduces a comment on the whole statement: \"Surprisingly enough, there has been no increase…\"."},{"n":46,"part":"Part 3. Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"A true friend is someone you can always trust and ______.","options":["rely on","interfere with","insist on","provide with"],"correct":0,"explanation":"\"Trust and rely on\" — rely on someone means depend on them."},{"n":47,"part":"Part 3. Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Unless ______ increases, the factory will close down.","options":["extension","improvement","production","arrangement"],"correct":2,"explanation":"A factory survives if its <b>production</b> (output) increases."},{"n":48,"part":"Part 3. Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Hugo has been in a bad ______ since he broke up with Mary.","options":["mood","problem","courage","economy"],"correct":0,"explanation":"\"In a bad mood\" is the fixed expression for feeling unhappy or irritable."},{"n":49,"part":"Part 3. Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"It's time something was done for the millions of children who are ______ to death.","options":["hungry","starving","thirsty","committing"],"correct":1,"explanation":"\"Starving to death\" is the collocation — dying from lack of food."},{"n":50,"part":"Part 3. Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"I wish I could go abroad, but my passport has ______.","options":["expired","stolen","run out","robbed"],"correct":0,"explanation":"A passport <b>expires</b> when it is no longer valid; \"stolen\" would need the passive (has been stolen)."},{"n":51,"part":"Part 3. Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Dave survived the accident, but his car was ______.","options":["injured","damaged","spoilt","distracted"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Things are <b>damaged</b>; people are injured."},{"n":52,"part":"Part 3. Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"That green jacket is ______ what I've been looking for. How much is it?","options":["exactly","correctly","accurately","rightly"],"correct":0,"explanation":"\"Exactly what I've been looking for\" — exactly emphasises a precise match."},{"n":53,"part":"Part 3. Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Daniel is very depressed as he has been ______ since the factory closed.","options":["out of date","out of work","out of order","out of bounds"],"correct":1,"explanation":"\"Out of work\" means unemployed — he lost his job when the factory closed."},{"n":54,"part":"Part 3. Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"The police didn't arrest him because they had no ______ that he was involved in the crime.","options":["charge","accusation","proof","approval"],"correct":2,"explanation":"\"Have no proof that…\" — evidence showing he was involved."},{"n":55,"part":"Part 3. Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"You must have ______ in your abilities if you want to do this job well.","options":["qualifications","satisfaction","efficiency","confidence"],"correct":3,"explanation":"\"Have confidence in your abilities\" — confidence in is the collocation."},{"n":56,"part":"Part 4. Error Identification","section":"Error correction","type":"mcq","q":"Choose the underlined part that needs correction.<br>The rate (A) <u>which</u> an animal (B) <u>loses heat</u> (C) <u>depends on</u> the relationship between its size (D) <u>and</u> its body surface area.","options":["A","B","C","D"],"correct":0,"explanation":"\"The rate <b>at which</b> an animal loses heat\" — the relative clause needs the preposition: at which / at the rate."},{"n":57,"part":"Part 4. Error Identification","section":"Error correction","type":"mcq","q":"Choose the underlined part that needs correction.<br>The solution (A) <u>to</u> these problems is (B) <u>eating little</u> but often, (C) <u>which</u> explains why small animals often (D) <u>leads</u> busy lives.","options":["A","B","C","D"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The subject \"small animals\" is plural, so the verb must be <b>lead</b>, not \"leads\"."},{"n":58,"part":"Part 4. Error Identification","section":"Error correction","type":"mcq","q":"Choose the underlined part that needs correction.<br>Without (A) <u>maths</u>, people can (B) <u>be fooled</u> (C) <u>into</u> picking the (D) <u>worst</u> of two options.","options":["A","B","C","D"],"correct":3,"explanation":"With only two options the comparative is needed: \"the <b>worse</b> of two options\"."},{"n":59,"part":"Part 4. Error Identification","section":"Error correction","type":"mcq","q":"Choose the underlined part that needs correction.<br>(A) <u>As</u> the years pass, these diaries will help you (B) <u>remembering</u> brilliant moments and (C) <u>spot</u> long-term changes (D) <u>in nature</u>.","options":["A","B","C","D"],"correct":1,"explanation":"\"Help somebody (to) do something\" — it should be \"help you <b>remember</b>\", parallel with \"spot\"."},{"n":60,"part":"Part 4. Error Identification","section":"Error correction","type":"mcq","q":"Choose the underlined part that needs correction.<br>Dinosaurs (A) <u>ruled</u> our planet (B) <u>for such a long time</u> and (C) <u>were</u> very (D) <u>adapting</u>.","options":["A","B","C","D"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The adjective needed is <b>adaptable</b> (able to adapt), not the participle \"adapting\"."},{"n":61,"part":"Part 5. Word Formation","section":"Word form","type":"text","passageId":3,"q":"Use the word given to form ONE word that fits blank (1). (ACT)","accept":["react"],"answer":"react","explanation":"Your body continues to <b>react</b> (respond) to noise — verb after \"continues to\"."},{"n":62,"part":"Part 5. Word Formation","section":"Word form","type":"text","passageId":3,"q":"Use the word given to form ONE word that fits blank (2). (THREAT)","accept":["threaten"],"answer":"threaten","explanation":"A verb is needed after \"might\": anything that might <b>threaten</b> them."},{"n":63,"part":"Part 5. Word Formation","section":"Word form","type":"text","passageId":3,"q":"Use the word given to form ONE word that fits blank (3). (DANGER)","accept":["dangerous"],"answer":"dangerous","explanation":"An adjective is needed before \"situations\": <b>dangerous</b> situations."},{"n":64,"part":"Part 5. Word Formation","section":"Word form","type":"text","passageId":3,"q":"Use the word given to form ONE word that fits blank (4). (FORTUNE)","accept":["Unfortunately","unfortunately"],"answer":"Unfortunately","explanation":"A sentence adverb with a negative meaning opens the paragraph: <b>Unfortunately</b>, producing stress hormones constantly can cause…"},{"n":65,"part":"Part 5. Word Formation","section":"Word form","type":"text","passageId":3,"q":"Use the word given to form ONE word that fits blank (5). (ILL)","accept":["illnesses"],"answer":"illnesses","explanation":"A plural noun after \"a number of serious\": <b>illnesses</b>."},{"n":66,"part":"Part 5. Word Formation","section":"Word form","type":"text","passageId":3,"q":"Use the word given to form ONE word that fits blank (6). (PRESS)","accept":["pressure"],"answer":"pressure","explanation":"\"High blood <b>pressure</b>\" — the medical condition."},{"n":67,"part":"Part 5. Word Formation","section":"Word form","type":"text","passageId":3,"q":"Use the word given to form ONE word that fits blank (7). (SCIENCE)","accept":["scientific"],"answer":"scientific","explanation":"An adjective before \"studies\": recent <b>scientific</b> studies."},{"n":68,"part":"Part 5. Word Formation","section":"Word form","type":"text","passageId":3,"q":"Use the word given to form ONE word that fits blank (8). (PHYSICAL)","accept":["physically"],"answer":"physically","explanation":"An adverb parallel with \"psychologically\": affecting you both <b>physically</b> and psychologically."},{"n":69,"part":"Part 5. Word Formation","section":"Word form","type":"text","passageId":3,"q":"Use the word given to form ONE word that fits blank (9). (CONSIDER)","accept":["inconsiderate"],"answer":"inconsiderate","explanation":"Negative adjective describing bad neighbours, alongside heavy traffic and car alarms: <b>inconsiderate</b> neighbours."},{"n":70,"part":"Part 5. Word Formation","section":"Word form","type":"text","passageId":3,"q":"Use the word given to form ONE word that fits blank (10). (HARM)","accept":["harmful"],"answer":"harmful","explanation":"An adjective after \"very\": noise can be very <b>harmful</b>."},{"n":71,"part":"Part 6. Key Word Transformation","section":"Sentence transformation","type":"text","q":"Complete the second sentence so that it has a similar meaning to the first, using the word given (no more than FIVE words).<br>Owen doesn't plan to write that report. (INTENTION)<br>→ Owen ______ that report.","accept":["has no intention of writing"],"answer":"has no intention of writing","explanation":"\"Have no intention of + V-ing\" = not plan to do something."},{"n":72,"part":"Part 6. Key Word Transformation","section":"Sentence transformation","type":"text","q":"Complete the second sentence so that it has a similar meaning to the first, using the word given (no more than FIVE words).<br>You ought to tell Martin what happened. (BETTER)<br>→ You ______ Martin know what happened.","accept":["had better let","'d better let"],"answer":"had better let","explanation":"\"Ought to\" ≈ \"had better\" (advice), and \"let somebody know\" = tell somebody."},{"n":73,"part":"Part 6. Key Word Transformation","section":"Sentence transformation","type":"text","q":"Complete the second sentence so that it has a similar meaning to the first, using the word given (no more than FIVE words).<br>Paul finds it difficult to look for a suitable job. (HAS)<br>→ Paul ______ for a suitable job.","accept":["has difficulty in looking","has difficulty looking"],"answer":"has difficulty in looking","explanation":"\"Find it difficult to do\" = \"have difficulty (in) doing\"."},{"n":74,"part":"Part 6. Key Word Transformation","section":"Sentence transformation","type":"text","q":"Complete the second sentence so that it has a similar meaning to the first, using the word given (no more than FIVE words).<br>The kids continued to play tennis despite the fact that it was raining. (CARRIED)<br>→ The kids ______ despite the fact that it was raining.","accept":["carried on playing tennis"],"answer":"carried on playing tennis","explanation":"\"Carry on + V-ing\" = continue doing."},{"n":75,"part":"Part 6. Key Word Transformation","section":"Sentence transformation","type":"text","q":"Complete the second sentence so that it has a similar meaning to the first, using the word given (no more than FIVE words).<br>Although Henry thought of the plan, he didn't get the credit for it. (CAME)<br>→ Even ______ the plan, he didn't get the credit for it.","accept":["though Henry came up with"],"answer":"though Henry came up with","explanation":"\"Even though\" = although, and \"come up with\" = think of (an idea/plan)."},{"n":76,"part":"Part 6. Key Word Transformation","section":"Sentence transformation","type":"text","q":"Complete the second sentence so that it has a similar meaning to the first, using the word given (no more than FIVE words).<br>It was such a high shelf that the little girl couldn't reach it. (LOW)<br>→ The shelf ______ for the little girl to reach.","accept":["was not low enough","wasn't low enough"],"answer":"was not low enough","explanation":"\"Such a high shelf that she couldn't reach\" → \"not low enough for her to reach\" (adjective + enough + for + to-infinitive)."},{"n":77,"part":"Part 6. Key Word Transformation","section":"Sentence transformation","type":"text","q":"Complete the second sentence so that it has a similar meaning to the first, using the word given (no more than FIVE words).<br>They will work this weekend, but only if you pay them extra. (UNLESS)<br>→ This weekend, they ______ are paid extra.","accept":["will not work unless they","won't work unless they"],"answer":"will not work unless they","explanation":"\"Only if you pay them\" → \"unless they are paid\": unless = if … not."},{"n":78,"part":"Part 6. Key Word Transformation","section":"Sentence transformation","type":"text","q":"Complete the second sentence so that it has a similar meaning to the first, using the word given (no more than FIVE words).<br>You can use margarine instead of butter in this recipe. (USED)<br>→ Margarine ______ place of butter in this recipe.","accept":["can be used in"],"answer":"can be used in","explanation":"Passive with modal: \"can be used\", and \"in place of\" = instead of."},{"n":79,"part":"Part 6. Key Word Transformation","section":"Sentence transformation","type":"text","q":"Complete the second sentence so that it has a similar meaning to the first, using the word given (no more than FIVE words).<br>People say he is the richest man in the region. (SAID)<br>→ He ______ richest man in the region.","accept":["is said to be the"],"answer":"is said to be the","explanation":"Personal passive of reporting verbs: \"People say he is…\" → \"He is said to be…\"."},{"n":80,"part":"Part 6. Key Word Transformation","section":"Sentence transformation","type":"text","q":"Complete the second sentence so that it has a similar meaning to the first, using the word given (no more than FIVE words).<br>He will probably be late for school tomorrow. (UNLIKELY)<br>→ He ______ time for school tomorrow.","accept":["is unlikely to be on"],"answer":"is unlikely to be on","explanation":"\"Probably be late\" → \"is unlikely to be on time\": be unlikely to + infinitive."}]},{"id":"ptnk-2021-chuyen","year":2021,"track":"chuyen","title":"PTNK 2021 · Tiếng Anh Chuyên","subtitle":"Đề thi tuyển sinh lớp 10 · Trường Phổ thông Năng khiếu · 2021-2022","durationMin":120,"keySource":"official","source":"2021 Thi vào 10 Đề thi Anh chuyên Phổ thông Năng khiếu kèm đáp án.pdf","passages":["<b>The Development of the Courier Service</b><br><br>In ancient times, runners, pigeons and riders on horseback were used to deliver messages. Before there were mechanized courier services, foot messengers physically ran miles to their destinations. To this day, there are marathons directly related to actual historical messenger routes. In cities today, there are often bicycle couriers or motorcycle couriers, but for products requiring delivery over greater distance networks, this may often include trucks, railways and aircraft. <b>§1.</b><br><br>The same-day courier market expanded with the help of taxi companies, but soon expanded into dedicated motorcycle dispatch riders with the taxi companies setting up separate arms to their companies to cover the courier work. During the late 1970s small provincial and regional companies were popping up throughout the country. Today, there are many large companies offering next-day courier services, including worldwide couriers such as APC Overnight, FedEx, DHL, UPS and TNT. There are many \"specialist\" couriers usually for the transportation of items such as <b>freight</b>/palettes, sensitive documents and liquids. <b>§2.</b><br><br>There are thousands upon thousands of independent couriers and localized companies, offering next-day and same-day services. Since the turn of the millennium, there has been a noticeable increase in owner-drivers, self-employed couriers, operating mainly from home with a sole vehicle. Advantages of this rather than working for an established same-day courier firm are that they are able to offer far better rates to their customers. Self-employed couriers come from varied employment backgrounds from non-skilled through to highly qualified tradespeople. Motorbike couriers still exist, but mainly in and around cities, where there is often congestion, as they are much cheaper to run in heavy traffic. <b>§3.</b><br><br>Large couriers often require an account to be held (and this can include daily scheduled collections). Senders are therefore primarily in the commercial/industrial sector (and not the general public); some couriers such as DHL do, however, allow public sending (at higher cost than regular senders).<br><br>The courier industry has long held an important place in United States commerce and been involved in important moments in the nation's history, such as westward migration and the gold rush. Wells Fargo was founded in 1852 and rapidly became the preeminent package delivery company. The company specialized in shipping gold, packages and newspapers throughout the West, making a Wells Fargo office in every camp and settlement a necessity for commerce and connections to home. Shortly afterward, the Pony Express was established to move packages more quickly than the traditional method, which was the stagecoach routes. It also illustrated the demand for timely deliveries across the nation, a concept that continued to evolve with the railroads, automobiles and interstate highways and which has emerged into today's courier industry. <b>§4.</b><br><br>The courier industry in the United States is a $59 billion industry, with 86% of the business shared by only four companies, including DHL, FedEx and UPS. The remaining 14% shared among almost 11,900 other small businesses ranging in size from 1 employee to over 600. These businesses comprise of mostly same-day deliveries and are strong offline businesses and strong online businesses like Naparex and USA Couriers.","The UK Government has said it (1)____ to allow self-driving cars on public roads for the first time later this year. Drivers of cars (2)____ with an automated lane-keeping system (ALKS) will be allowed to take their hands (3)____ the steering wheel in certain situations.<br><br>ALKS uses cameras and sensors to monitor the speed of nearby vehicles and (4)____ a safe distance. Under new plans, drivers will be able to use the system on motorways when (5)____ is moving at less than 37 miles per hour. However, the driver must still be ready to take control when requested or (6)____ the car will automatically slow down.<br><br>The Department for Transport says that self-driving cars could make the roads safer and reduce accidents caused by human (7)____. According to Mike Hawes of the Society of Motor Manufacturers and Traders, up to 47,000 accidents could be (8)____ over the next decade thanks to self-driving systems. (9)____, Thatcham Research, which conducts car-safety tests, has (10)____ people that ALKS still requires a lot of human involvement.","<b>Early Football</b><br><br>Documented evidence of what is possibly the oldest activity resembling football can be found in a Chinese military manual written during the Warring States Period in about the 476BC-221BC.<br><br>(1)____ During the Han Dynasty (206BC-220AD), <i>cuju</i> games were standardized and rules were established. Variations of this game later spread to Japan and Korea, known as <i>kemari</i> and <i>chuk-guk</i> respectively. By the Chinese Tang Dynasty (618-907), the feather-stuffed ball was replaced by an air-filled ball and <i>cuju</i> games had become professionalized, with many players making a living playing <i>cuju</i>. Also, two different types of goal posts emerged. (2)____<br><br>The Ancient Greeks and Romans are known to have played many ball games, some of which involved the use of the feet. The Roman writer Cicero describes the case of a man who was killed whilst having a shave when a ball was kicked into a barber's shop. The Roman game <i>harpastum</i> is believed to have been adapted from a team game known as <i>pheninda</i> that is mentioned by Greek playwright, Antiphanes (388-311BC) and later referred to by Clement of Alexandria. (3)____<br><br>There are a number of references to traditional, ancient, and/or prehistoric ball games, played by indigenous peoples in many different parts of the world. (4)____ There are later accounts of an Inuit game played on ice, called <i>aqsaqtuk</i>. (5)____ In 1610, William Strachey of the Jamestown settlement, Virginia recorded a game played by Native Americans, called <i>pahsaheman</i>. In Victoria, Australia, indigenous people played a game called <i>Marn Grook</i> (\"ball game\"). An 1878 book by Robert Brough-Smyth, <i>The Aborigines of Victoria</i>, quotes a man called Richard Thomas as saying, in about 1841, that he had witnessed Aboriginal people playing the game: \"Mr Thomas describes how the foremost player will drop kick a ball made from the skin of a possum and how other players leap into the air in order to catch it.\" (6)____<br><br><b>A.</b> One was made by setting up posts with a net between them and the other consisted of just one goal post in the middle of the field.<br><b>B.</b> They often look much different in different places.<br><b>C.</b> This game appears to have resembled rugby.<br><b>D.</b> It is widely believed that <i>Marn Grook</i> had an influence on the development of Australian football.<br><b>E.</b> Each match began with two teams facing each other in parallel lines, before attempting to kick the ball through each other team's line and then at a goal.<br><b>F.</b> Although this is true only in certain societies.<br><b>G.</b> For example, in 1586, men from a ship commanded by an English explorer named John Davis went ashore to play a form of football with Inuit (Eskimo) people in Greenland.<br><b>H.</b> It describes a practice known as <i>cuju</i> (literally \"kick ball\"), which originally involved kicking a leather ball through a hole in a piece of silk cloth strung between two 30-foot (9.1 m) poles.","<i>You are going to read an article about New York's mayoral race. Seven parts have been removed from the extract. Choose from the paragraphs A-H the one which fits each gap (1-7). There is one extra paragraph which you do not need to use. Write the letter of the missing paragraph in the corresponding numbered box provided.</i><br><br><b>UNCERTAINTY AND THE CITY</b><br><i>Next month's primary is the first in a generation to be run while the city seems to be shrinking</i><br><br>Times Square is the only neighbourhood in New York City where regulations require a minimum amount of display lighting. Before the pandemic, tourists flocked there to gawk at the dazzling lights or to take selfies with someone dressed as Elmo or Spiderman, or maybe with the guitar-strumming Naked Cowboy (who in fact wears Y-fronts).<br><br>(1)____<br><br>On promises to make New York's lights shine brighter than ever, about a dozen Democrats are running for mayor, known as the second-toughest job in America. They are elbowing one another for attention from the city's diminished media. New Yorkers are still struggling to keep track of their names, much less the fine points of policy that (sort of) distinguish them. The candidate leading in the polls is the one with the most name recognition, Andrew Yang, who competed for the 2020 Democratic nomination for president, and who is positioning himself as the field's tech-savvy moderate.<br><br>(2)____<br><br>Typically mayoral candidates find themselves arguing about how to manage New York's growth. This is the first election for a generation in which they are confronting the hazards of shrinkage. Reliable numbers are hard to come by, but even before the epidemic the population was declining very slightly.<br><br>(3)____<br><br>With so many commuters vanishing, thousands of small businesses have closed their doors, taking one out of every eight jobs with them. On once-busy commercial streets, storefronts are empty and dark. Though Broadway is set to reopen in September, the hospitality industry has taken a beating during the pandemic: more than seven in ten of those working in the sector lost work last year. Across the city, employment is not expected to recover its pre-pandemic levels before 2024.<br><br>(4)____<br><br>He compared gun violence to a virus. “If we do not inoculate against it now, it will spread and spread and it will mean the death of countless New Yorkers and the city we have built.” Fearmongering, perhaps, but also a sign of how fearful many constituents are. When Mr Adams recently strolled down Brooklyn's Metropolitan Avenue, which he once patrolled, many passers-by shared their concerns about violence with him. “I'm afraid to walk to work,” said one Fed-Ex worker.<br><br>(5)____<br><br>Another New York specific problem is the subway, beset before the pandemic by delays caused by an ageing signal system and now seeing a decline of two-thirds in daily riders, which was 5.6m before COVID-19 struck. New York's governor actually has more control over the subway, which only makes the next mayor's task harder: the city will not rebound if the subway doesn't. Not only the virus but fear of crime is keeping New Yorkers from going down into the tunnels. The current mayor, Bill de Blasio, has felt it necessary to create a travel-buddy scheme for city employees.<br><br>(6)____<br><br>Each candidate has a plan for the city's recovery. Ray McGuire, a former Citigroup executive, says through measures like salary subsidies to small businesses he will bring back 500,000 jobs. He ticks a lot of boxes. He had a modest upbringing by a single mother, but at Citi he advised on transactions even bigger than the city's $99bn budget.<br><br>(7)____<br><br>Mr Yang has some intriguing ideas, and New York's mayors are supposed to do things that other cities copy. But he stands apart mainly because the city likes a star: Ed Koch, Rudy Giuliani and Michael Bloomberg were all well-known before they became mayors. Mr Yang has said he would hire Kathryn Garcia, an experienced problem-solver in city government, to help him run the place. Like a true New Yorker, Ms Garcia, who is also a candidate, shot back that Mr Yang could work for her.<br><br><b>A</b> He was quick to condemn the shooting, saying “everything is contingent upon whether our streets and our subways are safe”. The Democratic primary is on June 22nd, and it is likely to be decisive. There are two Republican candidates running, but Democrats outnumber Republicans in the city seven to one.<br><b>B</b> Crime is still much lower than it was at its height three decades ago, but New Yorkers list it among their top three priorities for the next mayor, along with stopping the spread of COVID-19 and kick-starting the economy. Eric Adams, Brooklyn's borough president and a former policeman, is second in most polls and has made combating crime a focus.<br><b>C</b> For the first time in a mayoral primary, city voters will be able to rank up to five candidates in order of preference. When the Board of Elections begins tabulating the results, if no candidate receives more than 50 percent of first-choice votes, all votes for the lowest-performing candidate will be eliminated, and those voters' second-choice picks will be counted instead. The cycle continues until one winner remains.<br><b>D</b> But for decades until the mid-1990s, New Yorkers and visitors alike avoided this “Crossroads of the World” because of its reputation for crime and drugs. On May 8th, when a gunfight erupted in Times Square and a toddler and two visitors were wounded, New Yorkers feared, not the first time, that their city was sliding back into its old ways.<br><b>E</b> Several progressive candidates, including Dianne Morales, a former non-profit executive, are calling for new programmes and higher taxes on the wealthy. But the city cannot raise taxes without the state government's approval, and New York City already faces the highest income-tax rate in the country.<br><b>F</b> Last year it shrank by perhaps 300,000 people (an estimate from CBS, a broadcaster, based on postal changes of address). Perhaps more worrying, the Partnership for New York City, an industry group, surveyed the city's big employers and found that only 10% of Manhattan's office workers had returned to their desks by March. Employers expect only 46% of their people back at their desks by September, and they expect 56% will work remotely at least part of the time after that.<br><b>G</b> New Yorkers have more reason than usual to master fine distinctions among all the candidates because this is the first big-city election to be decided by ranked-choice voting. Voters will be able to express their preference, in order, for up to five candidates. But whom to rank where?<br><b>H</b> Crime is again confronting many American cities. But the next mayor will also face problems that are peculiar to New York. One is how to raise taxes enough to finance more spending without prompting more Wall Street firms to leave town. Several, including Blackstone, a private equity giant, have already opened offices in Florida, where taxes are far lower.","<b>THE 1,000-YEAR BENDER</b><br><br>Even if you are teetotal, you cannot deny that humans, as a species, like to drink. We consume wine, beer, cider, spirits… in fact, the (0) <u>fermented</u> product of almost anything we can turn into alcohol. Our (1)____ for this toxic substance, the cause of so much trouble, is something of a mystery. Maybe it is enough to say that we drink because it makes us feel good. But to understand our love of alcohol we need a bigger, more (2)____, explanation.<br><br>The story of alcohol is one of an intimate relationship between humans and yeasts, an affair that began millions of years ago and is still playing out today. We like to cast ourselves as the star of this drama, but in fact yeasts are the (3)____ lead character. Ours is a symbiotic connection – a mutually beneficial (4)____. It is also one in which the balance of power is constantly shifting. If anything, the yeasts seem to have had the (5)____ hand, at least since our ancestors began brewing their own grog. We cultivate yeasts, ensuring they survive and thrive, and in return we get, at best, a good night out and a (6)____ the next morning. Once upon a time, however, yeast and alcohol may have offered us more (7)____ rewards.<br><br>Today the costs of our love of alcohol often (8)____ any benefits. But, being a story of evolution, it doesn’t end there. Already some humans have acquired (9)____ changes that encourage them to drink less. If this trend continues, it is possible that one day this long and (10)____ relationship will reach a kind of tenuous truce.<br><br><i>0. FERMENT · 1. FOND · 2. EVOLVE · 3. SING · 4. PARTNER · 5. UP · 6. HANG · 7. SIGNIFY · 8. WEIGH · 9. GENE · 10. TEMPEST</i>","COVID AND COMMUNITY<br><br>Graphic detail looked at the economic impact of COVID-19 (1)____ suicide rates (April 24th). The reported decrease in suicide, perhaps surprising (2)____ many people’s financial hardship, could be explained by the work of Emile Durkheim. In “Suicide”, the late-19th-century French sociologist studied people who (3)____ their own life and how they interacted (4)____ society. He described four types of suicide, one of which is egoistic suicide, when an individual does not feel a (5)____ of belonging. It is well known that in (6)____ of war suicide rates drop. This is generally ascribed (7)____ a “we’re all in it together” sentiment in a unified fight (8)____ a common foe. It is plausible that the (9)____ on COVID has united societies in this way and suicide rates have fallen as a (10)____.","When was the last time you had a good night’s sleep? By that I mean: you said goodbye to the waking world an hour or two (1)____ than usual, left your phone in another room and didn’t set an (2)____ for the next morning. Sounds good, doesn’t it? But in reality, it’s something few of us ever indulge (3)____.<br><br>You might even feel like our sleeping hours are (4)____ threat. Celebs and self-proclaimed lifestyle gurus often boast about how (5)____ they sleep – productivity comes (6)____. And how many of us are guilty of bringing our smartphones into bed, where a few TikToks or tweets quickly turn into hours of lost sleep? And let’s not get into how the pandemic has wreaked (7)____ with our sleeping patterns.<br><br>Unfortunately, while we all struggle to get a decent rest, scientists are discovering just (8)____ important sleep is. Of course, we all know the consequences of (9)____ an all-nighter, but it seems that even just an hour of lost sleep can have a profound effect (10)____ our overall health and wellbeing.","<i>Identify the five mistakes in the following passage and correct them.</i><br><br><b>Line 1</b> Alongside the growth in modern institutional distance learning have come Web-based or<br><b>Line 2</b> facilitated personal educational services, including e-tutoring, e-mentoring, and research<br><b>Line 3</b> assistance. In addition, they are many educational assistance companies that help parents choose<br><b>Line 4</b> and contact local tutors for their children while the companies handle the contracts. The use of<br><b>Line 5</b> distance learning programs and tutoring services have increased particularly among parents<br><b>Line 6</b> whose homeschool their children. Many universities have some online tutoring services for<br><b>Line 7</b> remedial help with reading, writing, and basic mathematics, and some even have online mentor<br><b>Line 8</b> programs to help doctoral candidates through the dissertation process. Finally, many Web-based<br><b>Line 9</b> personal-assistant companies offered a range of services to adults seeking continuing education<br><b>Line 10</b> or professional development."],"questions":[{"n":1,"part":"Part 1. Reading — Passage 1 (The Development of the Courier Service)","section":"Reading","type":"mcq","passageId":0,"q":"How did the courier market expand?","options":["by using bicycles","with the help of cab companies","by using motorcycles","with the use of trucks"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 states: \"The same-day courier market expanded with the help of taxi companies\" — a cab company is a taxi company."},{"n":2,"part":"Part 1. Reading — Passage 1 (The Development of the Courier Service)","section":"Reading","type":"mcq","passageId":0,"q":"What is the meaning of the word <b>freight</b> in paragraph 2?","options":["valuables","edibles","wastes","cargo"],"correct":3,"explanation":"\"Freight\" means goods transported in bulk, i.e. cargo; the passage pairs it with \"palettes\", which carry cargo."},{"n":3,"part":"Part 1. Reading — Passage 1 (The Development of the Courier Service)","section":"Reading","type":"mcq","passageId":0,"q":"Who were the forerunners of our modern day courier services?","options":["marathon racers","foot messengers","pigeons","riders"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 1: \"Before there were mechanized courier services, foot messengers physically ran miles to their destinations.\""},{"n":4,"part":"Part 1. Reading — Passage 1 (The Development of the Courier Service)","section":"Reading","type":"mcq","passageId":0,"q":"What does the passage say about self-employed couriers?","options":["One person does everything.","They are highly qualified employees.","They are mostly skilled laborers.","They are not as dependable."],"correct":0,"explanation":"They are \"owner-drivers, self-employed couriers, operating mainly from home with a sole vehicle\" — one person owns, drives and runs the business."},{"n":5,"part":"Part 1. Reading — Passage 1 (The Development of the Courier Service)","section":"Reading","type":"mcq","passageId":0,"q":"At which of the numbered points in the passage would the following sentence best fit?<br><i>\"And of course they are much faster than using cars.\"</i>","options":["§1","§2","§3","§4"],"correct":2,"explanation":"§3 follows the sentence about motorbike couriers in congested cities being \"much cheaper to run in heavy traffic\"; the added sentence continues that comparison with cars."},{"n":6,"part":"Part 1. Reading — Passage 1 (The Development of the Courier Service)","section":"Reading","type":"mcq","passageId":0,"q":"What is true about the sole vehicle company?","options":["Its service is slower.","Its service is faster.","It is more expensive.","It gives better prices."],"correct":3,"explanation":"Paragraph 3: the advantage of the owner-driver with a sole vehicle is that \"they are able to offer far better rates to their customers.\""},{"n":7,"part":"Part 1. Reading — Passage 1 (The Development of the Courier Service)","section":"Reading","type":"mcq","passageId":0,"q":"According to the passage, DHL","options":["only services the commercial sector.","is not used by the general public.","services the industrial customers.","has higher prices for non-commercial customers."],"correct":3,"explanation":"Paragraph 4: \"some couriers such as DHL do, however, allow public sending (at higher cost than regular senders).\""},{"n":8,"part":"Part 1. Reading — Passage 1 (The Development of the Courier Service)","section":"Reading","type":"mcq","passageId":0,"q":"Which provided the first delivery services in the U.S.?","options":["DHL","the Pony Express","Wells Fargo","the stagecoaches"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The Pony Express was set up \"to move packages more quickly than the traditional method, which was the stagecoach routes\" — so stagecoaches came first."},{"n":9,"part":"Part 1. Reading — Passage 1 (The Development of the Courier Service)","section":"Reading","type":"mcq","passageId":0,"q":"What is true about present day courier service?","options":["They employ 600 people.","It has become a giant industry.","There are only 4 companies left.","Its forerunner was the Pony Express."],"correct":1,"explanation":"The last paragraph calls it \"a $59 billion industry\" with four giants plus almost 11,900 small businesses — a huge industry."},{"n":10,"part":"Part 1. Reading — Passage 1 (The Development of the Courier Service)","section":"Reading","type":"mcq","passageId":0,"q":"The author feels that","options":["the courier industry is an important part of American commerce.","courier companies have helped build the highway system.","courier companies should do more online business.","there aren't enough \"specialist\" courier companies."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 5 opens: \"The courier industry has long held an important place in United States commerce.\""},{"n":11,"part":"Part 1. Reading — Passage 2 (self-driving cars, multiple-choice cloze)","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":1,"q":"Choose the word or phrase that best fits blank (1).","options":["targets","aims","designs","goals"],"correct":1,"explanation":"\"Aims to\" + infinitive means intends to; \"target\", \"design\" and \"goal\" are not used this way with an infinitive."},{"n":12,"part":"Part 1. Reading — Passage 2 (self-driving cars, multiple-choice cloze)","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":1,"q":"Choose the word or phrase that best fits blank (2).","options":["furnished","supplied","geared","fitted"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Cars are \"fitted with\" equipment such as a lane-keeping system — the standard collocation for installed technology."},{"n":13,"part":"Part 1. Reading — Passage 2 (self-driving cars, multiple-choice cloze)","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":1,"q":"Choose the word or phrase that best fits blank (3).","options":["away","on","off","out"],"correct":2,"explanation":"\"Take your hands off the steering wheel\" — \"off\" expresses removing the hands from a surface."},{"n":14,"part":"Part 1. Reading — Passage 2 (self-driving cars, multiple-choice cloze)","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":1,"q":"Choose the word or phrase that best fits blank (4).","options":["keep","control","lay","hold"],"correct":0,"explanation":"\"Keep a safe distance\" is the fixed collocation for maintaining space between vehicles."},{"n":15,"part":"Part 1. Reading — Passage 2 (self-driving cars, multiple-choice cloze)","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":1,"q":"Choose the word or phrase that best fits blank (5).","options":["car","vehicle","means","traffic"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The system may be used on motorways when \"traffic is moving\" slowly — uncountable \"traffic\" takes a singular verb and fits the context of speed limits."},{"n":16,"part":"Part 1. Reading — Passage 2 (self-driving cars, multiple-choice cloze)","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":1,"q":"Choose the word or phrase that best fits blank (6).","options":["other","else","so","not"],"correct":1,"explanation":"\"Or else\" means otherwise: the driver must take control when asked, or else the car will slow down automatically."},{"n":17,"part":"Part 1. Reading — Passage 2 (self-driving cars, multiple-choice cloze)","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":1,"q":"Choose the word or phrase that best fits blank (7).","options":["defect","fault","error","flaw"],"correct":2,"explanation":"\"Human error\" is the fixed phrase for mistakes made by people, the usual cause of road accidents."},{"n":18,"part":"Part 1. Reading — Passage 2 (self-driving cars, multiple-choice cloze)","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":1,"q":"Choose the word or phrase that best fits blank (8).","options":["caused","occurred","prevented","restrained"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Self-driving systems are said to make roads safer, so accidents could be \"prevented\" over the next decade."},{"n":19,"part":"Part 1. Reading — Passage 2 (self-driving cars, multiple-choice cloze)","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":1,"q":"Choose the word or phrase that best fits blank (9).","options":["Furthermore","However","Therefore","Besides"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The sentence introduces a contrasting, cautious view after the positive claims, so \"However\" is needed."},{"n":20,"part":"Part 1. Reading — Passage 2 (self-driving cars, multiple-choice cloze)","section":"Cloze","type":"mcq","passageId":1,"q":"Choose the word or phrase that best fits blank (10).","options":["warned","alarmed","alerted","threatened"],"correct":0,"explanation":"\"Warn somebody that…\" — Thatcham Research warned people that ALKS still needs human involvement; \"alert\" is not followed by a that-clause in this way."},{"n":21,"part":"Part 1. Reading — Passage 3 (Early Football, gapped sentences A-H)","section":"Reading","passageId":2,"q":"Read the text and fill the gaps with the correct sentence (A-H). Write the letter of the missing sentence. There are two extra sentences you will not need. Gap (1)____ — which sentence (A-H) is missing?","type":"text","accept":["H"],"answer":"H","explanation":"Sentence H — \"It describes a practice known as cuju…\" — \"It\" refers to the Chinese military manual in the previous sentence, and introduces cuju before the Han Dynasty sentence develops it."},{"n":22,"part":"Part 1. Reading — Passage 3 (Early Football, gapped sentences A-H)","section":"Reading","passageId":2,"q":"Read the text and fill the gaps with the correct sentence (A-H). Write the letter of the missing sentence. There are two extra sentences you will not need. Gap (2)____ — which sentence (A-H) is missing?","type":"text","accept":["A"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Sentence A explains the \"two different types of goal posts\" just mentioned: one with a net between posts, the other a single post in the middle of the field."},{"n":23,"part":"Part 1. Reading — Passage 3 (Early Football, gapped sentences A-H)","section":"Reading","passageId":2,"q":"Read the text and fill the gaps with the correct sentence (A-H). Write the letter of the missing sentence. There are two extra sentences you will not need. Gap (3)____ — which sentence (A-H) is missing?","type":"text","accept":["C"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Sentence C — \"This game appears to have resembled rugby\" — refers back to the Roman game harpastum, adapted from the Greek pheninda, closing the Greek and Roman paragraph."},{"n":24,"part":"Part 1. Reading — Passage 3 (Early Football, gapped sentences A-H)","section":"Reading","passageId":2,"q":"Read the text and fill the gaps with the correct sentence (A-H). Write the letter of the missing sentence. There are two extra sentences you will not need. Gap (4)____ — which sentence (A-H) is missing?","type":"text","accept":["G"],"answer":"G","explanation":"Sentence G gives an example (\"For example, in 1586…\") of indigenous ball games, and its mention of Inuit people leads into the \"later accounts of an Inuit game\" that follow."},{"n":25,"part":"Part 1. Reading — Passage 3 (Early Football, gapped sentences A-H)","section":"Reading","passageId":2,"q":"Read the text and fill the gaps with the correct sentence (A-H). Write the letter of the missing sentence. There are two extra sentences you will not need. Gap (5)____ — which sentence (A-H) is missing?","type":"text","accept":["E"],"answer":"E","explanation":"Sentence E describes how \"each match began\" — the rules of the Inuit game aqsaqtuk just mentioned — before the text moves on to the 1610 Native American game."},{"n":26,"part":"Part 1. Reading — Passage 3 (Early Football, gapped sentences A-H)","section":"Reading","passageId":2,"q":"Read the text and fill the gaps with the correct sentence (A-H). Write the letter of the missing sentence. There are two extra sentences you will not need. Gap (6)____ — which sentence (A-H) is missing?","type":"text","accept":["D"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Sentence D concludes the description of Marn Grook by noting its widely believed influence on Australian football, which fits after the Richard Thomas quotation."},{"n":27,"part":"Part 1. Reading — Passage 4 (UNCERTAINTY AND THE CITY, gapped paragraphs A-H)","section":"Reading","type":"text","passageId":3,"q":"Gap (1) — write the letter (A-H) of the missing paragraph.","accept":["D"],"answer":"D","explanation":"The gap follows the Times Square description and precedes “On promises to make New York's lights shine brighter” — D continues with “this ‘Crossroads of the World’” (Times Square) and the May 8th shooting there, introducing the fear of the city sliding back."},{"n":28,"part":"Part 1. Reading — Passage 4 (UNCERTAINTY AND THE CITY, gapped paragraphs A-H)","section":"Reading","type":"text","passageId":3,"q":"Gap (2) — write the letter (A-H) of the missing paragraph.","accept":["A"],"answer":"A","explanation":"“He was quick to condemn the shooting” refers back to Mr Yang and the Times Square gunfight of paragraph D; A then gives the primary date, and the next paragraph continues the election theme."},{"n":29,"part":"Part 1. Reading — Passage 4 (UNCERTAINTY AND THE CITY, gapped paragraphs A-H)","section":"Reading","type":"text","passageId":3,"q":"Gap (3) — write the letter (A-H) of the missing paragraph.","accept":["F"],"answer":"F","explanation":"“Last year it shrank by perhaps 300,000 people” picks up “the population was declining very slightly”, and the office-worker figures lead into “With so many commuters vanishing”."},{"n":30,"part":"Part 1. Reading — Passage 4 (UNCERTAINTY AND THE CITY, gapped paragraphs A-H)","section":"Reading","type":"text","passageId":3,"q":"Gap (4) — write the letter (A-H) of the missing paragraph.","accept":["B"],"answer":"B","explanation":"B introduces Eric Adams, a former policeman focused on crime, and the following paragraph opens with “He compared gun violence to a virus” — “He” is Mr Adams."},{"n":31,"part":"Part 1. Reading — Passage 4 (UNCERTAINTY AND THE CITY, gapped paragraphs A-H)","section":"Reading","type":"text","passageId":3,"q":"Gap (5) — write the letter (A-H) of the missing paragraph.","accept":["H"],"answer":"H","explanation":"H moves from crime to “problems that are peculiar to New York”, and the next paragraph begins “Another New York specific problem is the subway”."},{"n":32,"part":"Part 1. Reading — Passage 4 (UNCERTAINTY AND THE CITY, gapped paragraphs A-H)","section":"Reading","type":"text","passageId":3,"q":"Gap (6) — write the letter (A-H) of the missing paragraph.","accept":["G"],"answer":"G","explanation":"G says voters must “master fine distinctions among all the candidates” and ends “But whom to rank where?”, which is answered by “Each candidate has a plan for the city's recovery” and the candidate profiles that follow."},{"n":33,"part":"Part 1. Reading — Passage 4 (UNCERTAINTY AND THE CITY, gapped paragraphs A-H)","section":"Reading","type":"text","passageId":3,"q":"Gap (7) — write the letter (A-H) of the missing paragraph.","accept":["E"],"answer":"E","explanation":"After Ray McGuire's plan, E describes the progressive candidates' plans (Dianne Morales, higher taxes), and the passage then moves on to Mr Yang's ideas. C (the ranked-choice mechanics) is the unused extra paragraph."},{"n":34,"part":"Part 2. Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"\"Do you think Greg was at the gym on Sunday?\" \"He _____. It's closed on weekends.\"","options":["shouldn't have been","could be","can't have been","didn't need to be"],"correct":2,"explanation":"<b>Can't have been</b> expresses a confident deduction about the past that something was impossible: the gym is closed on weekends, so he could not have been there."},{"n":35,"part":"Part 2. Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"The mayor announced his plan _____ a new library.","options":["that will construct","for the construction of","to be constructed","constructed"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The noun <b>plan</b> is followed by <b>for</b> + noun phrase: \"a plan for the construction of a new library\". \"To be constructed\" would wrongly make the plan itself the thing being built."},{"n":36,"part":"Part 2. Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"The state of the economy is poor. _____, the unemployment rate is rising.","options":["Since","Seeing that","Thanks to","Consequently"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Two separate sentences need a linking adverb, not a conjunction or preposition: <b>Consequently</b> (= as a result) introduces the effect of the poor economy."},{"n":37,"part":"Part 2. Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Do you know what time it is? You _____ to be here an hour ago!","options":["supposed","were supposing","had supposed","were supposed"],"correct":3,"explanation":"<b>Be supposed to</b> + infinitive expresses what was expected or arranged; the past passive form \"were supposed to be here\" is required with \"an hour ago\"."},{"n":38,"part":"Part 2. Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"I've been buying all my clothes from second-hand stores _____.","options":["today","now","lately","all day"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The present perfect continuous describes a recent habit, which goes with <b>lately</b> (= recently). \"Today\" and \"all day\" describe a single day, not a repeated action."},{"n":39,"part":"Part 2. Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"The fantastic view was _____ me to buy this apartment.","options":["that it convinced","what convinced","how I was convinced","which convinced"],"correct":1,"explanation":"A nominal relative clause with <b>what</b> (= the thing that) completes the sentence: \"The fantastic view was what convinced me to buy this apartment.\""},{"n":40,"part":"Part 2. Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Dean has two sisters, both _____ work abroad.","options":["of whose","of whom","of them","of who"],"correct":1,"explanation":"After a comma, \"both of\" + relative pronoun joins the two clauses; the object form <b>whom</b> is required after a preposition. \"Both of them work abroad\" would need a full stop or \"and\"."},{"n":41,"part":"Part 2. Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Magie didn't seem _____ concerned when Jack told her about the snake in her bedroom.","options":["at least","in least","leastways","the least bit"],"correct":3,"explanation":"<b>Not the least bit</b> + adjective means \"not at all\": she didn't seem the least bit concerned. \"At least\" has a different meaning (a minimum)."},{"n":42,"part":"Part 2. Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"I've made my decision. _____ you say will change my mind!","options":["Whatever","Nothing","No matter","Anything"],"correct":1,"explanation":"<b>Nothing you say will change my mind</b>: the subject is \"Nothing (that) you say\", and the sentence must be negative to fit \"I've made my decision\". \"Whatever\" would give a positive meaning."},{"n":43,"part":"Part 2. Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Bob _____ as a baggage handler at the airport until he can find something more suitable.","options":["works","is working","worked","has worked"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The present continuous <b>is working</b> describes a temporary situation, signalled by \"until he can find something more suitable\"."},{"n":44,"part":"Part 2. Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Although not everyone is a fan of Justin Bieber, most people agree he is worthy of his _____ success.","options":["influential","aspiring","phenomenal","resourceful"],"correct":2,"explanation":"<b>Phenomenal success</b> is a fixed collocation meaning extraordinary, remarkable success."},{"n":45,"part":"Part 2. Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"My brother _____ a total transformation during adolescence.","options":["underwent","undermined","underlined","underpinned"],"correct":0,"explanation":"<b>Undergo a transformation</b> means to experience a change; \"underwent\" is the past form."},{"n":46,"part":"Part 2. Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"There is _____ parking for both staff and customers.","options":["single","hard-pressed","mere","ample"],"correct":3,"explanation":"<b>Ample parking</b> means more than enough parking space; the other adjectives do not collocate with \"parking\"."},{"n":47,"part":"Part 2. Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"A therapist is trying to help the couple _____ with the strain of the situation.","options":["appreciate","fester","condone","cope"],"correct":3,"explanation":"<b>Cope with</b> means to deal successfully with something difficult, here \"the strain of the situation\"."},{"n":48,"part":"Part 2. Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"William _____ to becoming a powerful politician and has already started to make his way in the world of politics.","options":["persists","determines","aspires","perseveres"],"correct":2,"explanation":"<b>Aspire to</b> + V-ing/noun means to have a strong ambition to achieve something: he aspires to becoming a powerful politician."},{"n":49,"part":"Part 2. Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Will you _____ all these books and papers to my office, please?","options":["fetch","bring","take","set"],"correct":2,"explanation":"<b>Take</b> is used for carrying something away from the speaker to another place (\"to my office\"); \"bring\" means carrying it towards the speaker."},{"n":50,"part":"Part 2. Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"\"Is Leah OK?\" \"Yes, we spent hours _____ about her problem and she seems to feel better now.\"","options":["talking","speaking","discussing","listening"],"correct":0,"explanation":"<b>Talk about</b> something is the natural collocation; \"discuss\" takes a direct object without \"about\", and \"listen\" goes with \"to\"."},{"n":51,"part":"Part 2. Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"You will find the stockbrokers' offices in the city's central _____ district.","options":["business","industrial","rural","provincial"],"correct":0,"explanation":"<b>Central business district</b> (CBD) is the fixed term for the commercial centre of a city, where stockbrokers' offices are found."},{"n":52,"part":"Part 2. Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"I stayed up all night because I was completely _____ the book I was reading.","options":["absorbed in","acquainted with","appealed to","spontaneous about"],"correct":0,"explanation":"<b>Be absorbed in</b> something means to be so interested in it that you notice nothing else, which explains staying up all night."},{"n":53,"part":"Part 2. Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"You'll have to reverse out of this road – it's a _____ and we can't go any further.","options":["next-door","run-down","built-up","dead-end"],"correct":3,"explanation":"A <b>dead end</b> is a road that is closed at one end, so you cannot go any further and must reverse."},{"n":54,"part":"Part 2. Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Her _____ success at the polls is the best indicator of her popularity.","options":["unprecedented","unabridged","unrequited","unrelenting"],"correct":0,"explanation":"<b>Unprecedented success</b> means success never seen before; \"unabridged\" describes texts, \"unrequited\" love, and \"unrelenting\" pressure or effort."},{"n":55,"part":"Part 2. Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"The little boy was _____ by the storekeeper, who charged him twice the normal price for a soda.","options":["set down","ripped off","torn up","shaken out"],"correct":1,"explanation":"<b>Rip someone off</b> means to cheat someone by charging too much, as the storekeeper did by doubling the price."},{"n":56,"part":"Part 2. Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"Jackie lives on the fourth floor of a _____ in London's West End.","options":["bungalow","block of flats","detached house","terraced house"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Only a <b>block of flats</b> has a fourth floor; a bungalow is single-storey and houses do not usually have four floors."},{"n":57,"part":"Part 2. Grammar and Vocabulary","section":"Language use","type":"mcq","q":"I'm sure your cat will soon get used to her new _____.","options":["surrounded","surrounding","surround","surroundings"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The plural noun <b>surroundings</b> means the environment or place around you; \"get used to her new surroundings\" is the standard phrase."},{"n":58,"part":"Part 3. Word formation (THE 1,000-YEAR BENDER)","section":"Word form","type":"text","passageId":4,"q":"Our (1)____ for this toxic substance, the cause of so much trouble, is something of a mystery. (FOND)","accept":["fondness"],"answer":"fondness","explanation":"A noun is needed after \"Our\" and before \"for\": <b>fondness</b> (for something) = a liking for it."},{"n":59,"part":"Part 3. Word formation (THE 1,000-YEAR BENDER)","section":"Word form","type":"text","passageId":4,"q":"But to understand our love of alcohol we need a bigger, more (2)____, explanation. (EVOLVE)","accept":["evolutionary"],"answer":"evolutionary","explanation":"After \"more\" and before the noun \"explanation\" an adjective is needed: <b>evolutionary</b> — the text later calls this \"a story of evolution\"."},{"n":60,"part":"Part 3. Word formation (THE 1,000-YEAR BENDER)","section":"Word form","type":"text","passageId":4,"q":"We like to cast ourselves as the star of this drama, but in fact yeasts are the (3)____ lead character. (SING)","accept":["unsung"],"answer":"unsung","explanation":"An adjective before \"lead character\" with a negative sense: <b>unsung</b> = not praised or celebrated, though deserving to be — yeasts are the real, unrecognised star."},{"n":61,"part":"Part 3. Word formation (THE 1,000-YEAR BENDER)","section":"Word form","type":"text","passageId":4,"q":"Ours is a symbiotic connection – a mutually beneficial (4)____. (PARTNER)","accept":["partnership"],"answer":"partnership","explanation":"A noun after the adjective \"mutually beneficial\" naming the relationship: <b>partnership</b>."},{"n":62,"part":"Part 3. Word formation (THE 1,000-YEAR BENDER)","section":"Word form","type":"text","passageId":4,"q":"If anything, the yeasts seem to have had the (5)____ hand, at least since our ancestors began brewing their own grog. (UP)","accept":["upper"],"answer":"upper","explanation":"The idiom \"have the <b>upper</b> hand\" = to have the advantage or control — the balance of power favours the yeasts."},{"n":63,"part":"Part 3. Word formation (THE 1,000-YEAR BENDER)","section":"Word form","type":"text","passageId":4,"q":"We cultivate yeasts, ensuring they survive and thrive, and in return we get, at best, a good night out and a (6)____ the next morning. (HANG)","accept":["hangover"],"answer":"hangover","explanation":"A noun after \"a … the next morning\": a <b>hangover</b> is the headache and sickness felt after drinking too much alcohol."},{"n":64,"part":"Part 3. Word formation (THE 1,000-YEAR BENDER)","section":"Word form","type":"text","passageId":4,"q":"Once upon a time, however, yeast and alcohol may have offered us more (7)____ rewards. (SIGNIFY)","accept":["significant"],"answer":"significant","explanation":"An adjective is needed after \"more\" to describe \"rewards\": <b>significant</b> = important, considerable."},{"n":65,"part":"Part 3. Word formation (THE 1,000-YEAR BENDER)","section":"Word form","type":"text","passageId":4,"q":"Today the costs of our love of alcohol often (8)____ any benefits. (WEIGH)","accept":["outweigh"],"answer":"outweigh","explanation":"A verb is needed with \"the costs\" as subject and \"any benefits\" as object: <b>outweigh</b> = to be greater or more important than."},{"n":66,"part":"Part 3. Word formation (THE 1,000-YEAR BENDER)","section":"Word form","type":"text","passageId":4,"q":"Already some humans have acquired (9)____ changes that encourage them to drink less. (GENE)","accept":["genetic"],"answer":"genetic","explanation":"An adjective before the noun \"changes\": <b>genetic</b> changes — changes in the genes, fitting \"a story of evolution\"."},{"n":67,"part":"Part 3. Word formation (THE 1,000-YEAR BENDER)","section":"Word form","type":"text","passageId":4,"q":"If this trend continues, it is possible that one day this long and (10)____ relationship will reach a kind of tenuous truce. (TEMPEST)","accept":["tempestuous"],"answer":"tempestuous","explanation":"An adjective parallel to \"long\" describing \"relationship\": <b>tempestuous</b> = stormy, full of strong emotion and conflict — hence the need for a \"truce\"."},{"n":68,"part":"Part 4. Open Cloze — Passage 1 (COVID AND COMMUNITY)","section":"Open cloze","passageId":5,"q":"Complete the text. Use only ONE word for each gap. Gap (1)","type":"text","answer":"on","accept":["on"],"explanation":"“the economic impact of COVID-19 <b>on</b> suicide rates” — <b>impact on</b> something is the fixed preposition."},{"n":69,"part":"Part 4. Open Cloze — Passage 1 (COVID AND COMMUNITY)","section":"Open cloze","passageId":5,"q":"Complete the text. Use only ONE word for each gap. Gap (2)","type":"text","answer":"given","accept":["given","considering"],"explanation":"“perhaps surprising <b>given</b>/<b>considering</b> many people’s financial hardship” — both mean “taking into account”."},{"n":70,"part":"Part 4. Open Cloze — Passage 1 (COVID AND COMMUNITY)","section":"Open cloze","passageId":5,"q":"Complete the text. Use only ONE word for each gap. Gap (3)","type":"text","answer":"took","accept":["took"],"explanation":"“people who <b>took</b> their own life” — the idiom <b>take one’s own life</b> = commit suicide; past tense because Durkheim studied them in the 19th century."},{"n":71,"part":"Part 4. Open Cloze — Passage 1 (COVID AND COMMUNITY)","section":"Open cloze","passageId":5,"q":"Complete the text. Use only ONE word for each gap. Gap (4)","type":"text","answer":"with","accept":["with"],"explanation":"“how they interacted <b>with</b> society” — <b>interact with</b> is the fixed collocation."},{"n":72,"part":"Part 4. Open Cloze — Passage 1 (COVID AND COMMUNITY)","section":"Open cloze","passageId":5,"q":"Complete the text. Use only ONE word for each gap. Gap (5)","type":"text","answer":"sense","accept":["sense"],"explanation":"“does not feel a <b>sense</b> of belonging” — <b>a sense of belonging</b> is a fixed phrase."},{"n":73,"part":"Part 4. Open Cloze — Passage 1 (COVID AND COMMUNITY)","section":"Open cloze","passageId":5,"q":"Complete the text. Use only ONE word for each gap. Gap (6)","type":"text","answer":"time","accept":["time","times"],"explanation":"“in <b>time(s)</b> of war suicide rates drop” — <b>in time(s) of war/crisis</b> = during periods of war."},{"n":74,"part":"Part 4. Open Cloze — Passage 1 (COVID AND COMMUNITY)","section":"Open cloze","passageId":5,"q":"Complete the text. Use only ONE word for each gap. Gap (7)","type":"text","answer":"to","accept":["to"],"explanation":"“This is generally ascribed <b>to</b> a ‘we’re all in it together’ sentiment” — <b>ascribe something to</b> a cause."},{"n":75,"part":"Part 4. Open Cloze — Passage 1 (COVID AND COMMUNITY)","section":"Open cloze","passageId":5,"q":"Complete the text. Use only ONE word for each gap. Gap (8)","type":"text","answer":"against","accept":["against"],"explanation":"“a unified fight <b>against</b> a common foe” — you <b>fight against</b> an enemy (foe)."},{"n":76,"part":"Part 4. Open Cloze — Passage 1 (COVID AND COMMUNITY)","section":"Open cloze","passageId":5,"q":"Complete the text. Use only ONE word for each gap. Gap (9)","type":"text","answer":"war","accept":["war"],"explanation":"“the <b>war</b> on COVID has united societies” — echoes the “times of war” idea; <b>the war on</b> + a problem (cf. the war on drugs)."},{"n":77,"part":"Part 4. Open Cloze — Passage 1 (COVID AND COMMUNITY)","section":"Open cloze","passageId":5,"q":"Complete the text. Use only ONE word for each gap. Gap (10)","type":"text","answer":"consequence","accept":["consequence","result"],"explanation":"“suicide rates have fallen as a <b>consequence</b>/<b>result</b>” — <b>as a consequence/result</b> = therefore."},{"n":78,"part":"Part 4. Open Cloze — Passage 2 (sleep)","section":"Open cloze","passageId":6,"q":"Blank (1)____: write ONE word that best fits the gap.","type":"text","accept":["earlier"],"answer":"earlier","explanation":"Comparative with \"than usual\": going to sleep \"an hour or two earlier than usual\"."},{"n":79,"part":"Part 4. Open Cloze — Passage 2 (sleep)","section":"Open cloze","passageId":6,"q":"Blank (2)____: write ONE word that best fits the gap.","type":"text","accept":["alarm"],"answer":"alarm","explanation":"\"set an alarm\" is the collocation — the article \"an\" requires a noun beginning with a vowel sound."},{"n":80,"part":"Part 4. Open Cloze — Passage 2 (sleep)","section":"Open cloze","passageId":6,"q":"Blank (3)____: write ONE word that best fits the gap.","type":"text","accept":["in"],"answer":"in","explanation":"\"indulge in something\" — the phrasal verb takes the preposition \"in\"; here the object \"something\" is fronted, leaving \"indulge in\" at the end."},{"n":81,"part":"Part 4. Open Cloze — Passage 2 (sleep)","section":"Open cloze","passageId":6,"q":"Blank (4)____: write ONE word that best fits the gap.","type":"text","accept":["under"],"answer":"under","explanation":"\"under threat\" is a fixed phrase meaning at risk or endangered."},{"n":82,"part":"Part 4. Open Cloze — Passage 2 (sleep)","section":"Open cloze","passageId":6,"q":"Blank (5)____: write ONE word that best fits the gap.","type":"text","accept":["little"],"answer":"little","explanation":"The gurus boast about \"how little they sleep\" — sleeping very few hours is presented as a badge of productivity."},{"n":83,"part":"Part 4. Open Cloze — Passage 2 (sleep)","section":"Open cloze","passageId":6,"q":"Blank (6)____: write ONE word that best fits the gap.","type":"text","accept":["first"],"answer":"first","explanation":"\"productivity comes first\" — it is their top priority, ahead of sleep."},{"n":84,"part":"Part 4. Open Cloze — Passage 2 (sleep)","section":"Open cloze","passageId":6,"q":"Blank (7)____: write ONE word that best fits the gap.","type":"text","accept":["havoc"],"answer":"havoc","explanation":"\"wreak havoc with/on something\" is the collocation meaning to cause great damage or disorder."},{"n":85,"part":"Part 4. Open Cloze — Passage 2 (sleep)","section":"Open cloze","passageId":6,"q":"Blank (8)____: write ONE word that best fits the gap.","type":"text","accept":["how"],"answer":"how","explanation":"\"just how important sleep is\" — an indirect exclamative clause with \"how\" + adjective."},{"n":86,"part":"Part 4. Open Cloze — Passage 2 (sleep)","section":"Open cloze","passageId":6,"q":"Blank (9)____: write ONE word that best fits the gap.","type":"text","accept":["pulling"],"answer":"pulling","explanation":"\"pull an all-nighter\" means to stay up all night; after the preposition \"of\" the gerund \"pulling\" is needed."},{"n":87,"part":"Part 4. Open Cloze — Passage 2 (sleep)","section":"Open cloze","passageId":6,"q":"Blank (10)____: write ONE word that best fits the gap.","type":"text","accept":["on"],"answer":"on","explanation":"\"have an effect on something\" — \"effect\" collocates with the preposition \"on\"."},{"n":88,"part":"Part 5: Key Word Transformation","section":"Sentence transformation","type":"text","q":"Complete the second sentence so that it has a similar meaning to the first sentence, using the word given. Do not change the word given. You must use between THREE and EIGHT words, including the word given.<br><br>Simon would found a new company whenever he ran into difficulties. <b>TO</b><br>Whenever Simon ran into difficulties, ________ up a new company.","accept":["he used to set","used to set"],"answer":"he used to set","explanation":"<b>Used to</b> + verb expresses a past habit (would found), and <b>set up</b> means found/establish a company."},{"n":89,"part":"Part 5: Key Word Transformation","section":"Sentence transformation","type":"text","q":"Complete the second sentence so that it has a similar meaning to the first sentence, using the word given. Do not change the word given. You must use between THREE and EIGHT words, including the word given.<br><br>Larry sought to dissuade them from selling the ranch. <b>TALK</b><br>Larry tried ________ the ranch.","accept":["to talk them out of selling"],"answer":"to talk them out of selling","explanation":"<b>Talk somebody out of doing something</b> means to persuade them not to do it, i.e. dissuade."},{"n":90,"part":"Part 5: Key Word Transformation","section":"Sentence transformation","type":"text","q":"Complete the second sentence so that it has a similar meaning to the first sentence, using the word given. Do not change the word given. You must use between THREE and EIGHT words, including the word given.<br><br>“I’ll take you anywhere, no matter where it is,” Jim promised her. <b>WHEREVER</b><br>Jim promised to ________ wanted to go.","accept":["take her wherever she"],"answer":"take her wherever she","explanation":"<b>Wherever</b> = anywhere, no matter where; reported speech shifts you → her and I → he."},{"n":91,"part":"Part 5: Key Word Transformation","section":"Sentence transformation","type":"text","q":"Complete the second sentence so that it has a similar meaning to the first sentence, using the word given. Do not change the word given. You must use between THREE and EIGHT words, including the word given.<br><br>He is sure to figure out what happened eventually. <b>MATTER</b><br>It’s only ________ out what happened.","accept":["a matter of time before he figures"],"answer":"a matter of time before he figures","explanation":"<b>It's only a matter of time before</b> + clause means something is certain to happen eventually."},{"n":92,"part":"Part 5: Key Word Transformation","section":"Sentence transformation","type":"text","q":"Complete the second sentence so that it has a similar meaning to the first sentence, using the word given. Do not change the word given. You must use between THREE and EIGHT words, including the word given.<br><br>I was forced to confess to cheating in the exam. <b>MADE</b><br>They ________ up to cheating in the exam.","accept":["made me own"],"answer":"made me own","explanation":"<b>Make somebody do something</b> (bare infinitive) = force; <b>own up to</b> = confess to."},{"n":93,"part":"Part 5: Key Word Transformation","section":"Sentence transformation","type":"text","q":"Complete the second sentence so that it has a similar meaning to the first sentence, using the word given. Do not change the word given. You must use between THREE and EIGHT words, including the word given.<br><br>Mark didn’t want to be discovered, so he carried out his plan in the middle of the night. <b>FOR</b><br>Mark carried out his plan in the middle of the night ________ out.","accept":["for fear of being found"],"answer":"for fear of being found","explanation":"<b>For fear of + V-ing</b> expresses the reason for avoiding something; <b>be found out</b> = be discovered."},{"n":94,"part":"Part 5: Key Word Transformation","section":"Sentence transformation","type":"text","q":"Complete the second sentence so that it has a similar meaning to the first sentence, using the word given. Do not change the word given. You must use between THREE and EIGHT words, including the word given.<br><br>Most people won’t tolerate being lied to. <b>UP</b><br>Very ________ being lied to.","accept":["few people will put up with"],"answer":"few people will put up with","explanation":"<b>Put up with</b> = tolerate; \"most people won't\" becomes \"very few people will\"."},{"n":95,"part":"Part 5: Key Word Transformation","section":"Sentence transformation","type":"text","q":"Complete the second sentence so that it has a similar meaning to the first sentence, using the word given. Do not change the word given. You must use between THREE and EIGHT words, including the word given.<br><br>He advised me so half-heartedly that I was left wondering what to do. <b>ADVICE</b><br>He gave me such ________ a loss as to what I should do.","accept":["half-hearted advice that I was at","half hearted advice that I was at"],"answer":"half-hearted advice that I was at","explanation":"<b>Such + (adj) + uncountable noun + that</b> mirrors so + adverb + that; <b>be at a loss</b> = not know what to do."},{"n":96,"part":"Part 5: Key Word Transformation","section":"Sentence transformation","type":"text","q":"Complete the second sentence so that it has a similar meaning to the first sentence, using the word given. Do not change the word given. You must use between THREE and EIGHT words, including the word given.<br><br>It’s a pity they can’t abolish military service. <b>ONLY</b><br>If ________ with military service.","accept":["only they could do away"],"answer":"only they could do away","explanation":"<b>If only + past modal (could)</b> expresses a present wish or regret; <b>do away with</b> = abolish."},{"n":97,"part":"Part 5: Key Word Transformation","section":"Sentence transformation","type":"text","q":"Complete the second sentence so that it has a similar meaning to the first sentence, using the word given. Do not change the word given. You must use between THREE and EIGHT words, including the word given.<br><br>“Your new hairstyle looks great, Sylvia!” I said. <b>COMPLIMENTED</b><br>I ________ new hairstyle.","accept":["complimented Sylvia on her"],"answer":"complimented Sylvia on her","explanation":"<b>Compliment somebody on something</b> is the fixed pattern for praising someone; \"your\" becomes \"her\" in reported form."},{"n":98,"part":"Part 6: Error Identification and Correction","section":"Error correction","type":"text","passageId":7,"q":"Mistake 1 is in <b>line 3</b>: \"In addition, <b>they</b> are many educational assistance companies…\". Write the correction (or \"mistake -> correction\").","accept":["there","they -> there","they - there","they → there","they there"],"answer":"there","explanation":"Existence is expressed with <b>there are</b>, not a pronoun subject: \"there are many educational assistance companies\"."},{"n":99,"part":"Part 6: Error Identification and Correction","section":"Error correction","type":"text","passageId":7,"q":"Mistake 2 is in <b>line 5</b>: \"The use of distance learning programs and tutoring services <b>have</b> increased…\". Write the correction (or \"mistake -> correction\").","accept":["has","have -> has","have - has","have → has","have has"],"answer":"has","explanation":"The head of the subject is the singular noun <b>The use</b>, so the verb must be singular: \"The use … has increased\"."},{"n":100,"part":"Part 6: Error Identification and Correction","section":"Error correction","type":"text","passageId":7,"q":"Mistake 3 is in <b>line 6</b>: \"…among parents <b>whose</b> homeschool their children.\" Write the correction (or \"mistake -> correction\").","accept":["who","whose -> who","whose - who","whose → who","whose who","that","whose -> that"],"answer":"who","explanation":"The relative clause needs a subject pronoun for <b>parents</b> (\"parents who homeschool their children\"); <b>whose</b> is possessive and would need a noun after it."},{"n":101,"part":"Part 6: Error Identification and Correction","section":"Error correction","type":"text","passageId":7,"q":"Mistake 4 is in <b>line 7</b>: \"…some even have online <b>mentor</b> programs…\". Write the correction (or \"mistake -> correction\").","accept":["mentoring","mentor -> mentoring","mentor - mentoring","mentor → mentoring","mentor mentoring"],"answer":"mentoring","explanation":"In parallel with \"online tutoring services\" (line 6), the programs are <b>mentoring programs</b> — the gerund names the activity, while <b>mentor</b> is the person."},{"n":102,"part":"Part 6: Error Identification and Correction","section":"Error correction","type":"text","passageId":7,"q":"Mistake 5 is in <b>line 9</b>: \"…many Web-based personal-assistant companies <b>offered</b> a range of services…\". Write the correction (or \"mistake -> correction\").","accept":["offer","offered -> offer","offered - offer","offered → offer","offered offer"],"answer":"offer","explanation":"The whole passage describes the present situation in the present simple (\"have come\", \"help\", \"have\"), so the past tense <b>offered</b> is inconsistent — it should be <b>offer</b>."}]}];
if (typeof module !== 'undefined' && module.exports) module.exports = { PTNK_EXAMS };
